version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) The apparatus labelled S is used for", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000054", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 54, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A crystallisation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000055", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 55, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "B filtration", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000056", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 56, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "C fractional distillation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000057", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 57, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "D simple distillation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000058", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 58, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) (i) Which method of separation should be used to obtain sand from a mixture containing salt, sand and water?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000059", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 59, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A crystallisation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000060", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 60, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "B filtration", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000061", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 61, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "C fractional distillation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000062", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 62, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "D simple distillation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000063", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 63, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Which method of separation should be used to obtain pure water from a mixture containing salt, sand and water?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000064", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 64, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A crystallisation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000065", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 65, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "B filtration", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000066", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 66, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "C fractional distillation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000067", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 67, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "D simple distillation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000068", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 68, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) Which method of separation should be used to obtain copper(II) sulfate from a mixture containing copper(II) sulfate and water?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000069", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 69, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A crystallisation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000070", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 70, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "B filtration", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000071", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 71, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "C fractional distillation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000072", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 72, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "D simple distillation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000073", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 73, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) Food colourings contain one or more food dyes.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000074", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 74, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A student used paper chromatography to separate the dyes contained in food colourings. She placed spots of three known food colourings (E, F and G) and one unknown food colouring (H) on the chromatography paper.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000075", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 75, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The diagram shows the appearance of the paper before and after her experiment.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000076", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 76, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Describe how the student should complete the experiment after placing the four spots on the paper.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000079", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 79, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(3)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000080", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 80, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Suggest why food colouring F did not move during the experiment.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000081", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 81, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) How many food dyes are there in food colouring E?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000082", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 82, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iv) How many known food dyes are there in food colouring H?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000083", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 83, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(v) Dyes are often identified by their $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ values.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000084", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 84, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Record the results for the dye in G and calculate its $ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000086", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 86, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 2 = 14 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000088", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 88, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "3 This question is about ways of preventing iron nails from rusting.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000089", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 89, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) This experiment is set up with three iron nails.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000090", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 90, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "tube 1", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000092", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 92, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "tube 2", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000094", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 94, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "tube 3", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000096", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 96, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) What is the name of the main compound in rust?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000097", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 97, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(1)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000098", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 98, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Why does the nail in tube 1 not rust?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000099", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 99, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(1)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000100", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 100, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) What is the purpose of the layer of oil in tube 2?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000101", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 101, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(1)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000102", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 102, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) Zinc can be used to coat iron nails to prevent them from rusting.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000103", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 103, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) What is the name of this process?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000104", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 104, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) If the layer of zinc on the nail is scratched, sacrificial protection prevents the iron from rusting.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000105", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 105, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Explain, with the help of two ionic half-equations, how this type of sacrificial protection works.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000106", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 106, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Use symbols from the box in your equations. You may use each symbol once, more than once or not at all.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000107", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 107, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) Electroplating is another method of rust prevention.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000109", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 109, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "This apparatus can be used to electroplate an iron nail.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000110", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 110, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Equation 1 shows the reaction at the copper rod.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000112", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 112, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Equation 1", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000113", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 113, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Name this type of reaction, giving a reason for your answer.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000115", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 115, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "type of reaction reason...", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000116", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 116, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Equation 2 shows the reaction at the iron nail.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000117", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 117, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Equation 2", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000118", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 118, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Use equations 1 and 2 to explain why the colour of the copper(II) sulfate solution does not change during the experiment.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000120", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 120, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "4 This question is about elements in Group 1 of the Periodic Table.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000121", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 121, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) Which statement about lithium is correct?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000122", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 122, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A It is a good electrical conductor and forms an acidic oxide", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000123", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 123, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "B It is a poor electrical conductor and forms an acidic oxide", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000124", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 124, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "C It is a good electrical conductor and forms a basic oxide", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000125", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 125, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "D It is a poor electrical conductor and forms a basic oxide", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000126", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 126, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) A small piece of sodium is added to a large trough of water.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000127", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 127, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) State two observations that could be made.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000128", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 128, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Complete the equation for this reaction by inserting the appropriate state symbols.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000129", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 129, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) Potassium reacts in a similar way to sodium, but is more reactive.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000131", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 131, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "State one observation that could be made when a small piece of potassium is added to a large trough of water, but would not be observed with sodium.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000132", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 132, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(d) Explain why elements in Group 1 have similar reactions.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000133", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 133, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 4 = 7 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000134", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 134, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "5 This question is about elements in Group 7 of the Periodic Table.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000135", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 135, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) Complete the table to show the physical state at room temperature of fluorine and astatine, and the colour of liquid bromine.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000136", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 136, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(2)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000137", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 137, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) Chlorine reacts with hydrogen to form hydrogen chloride.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000139", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 139, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A piece of magnesium ribbon is added to hydrogen chloride in three separate experiments under different conditions.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000140", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 140, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The table below shows the observations made under these different conditions.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000141", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 141, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Write the formulae of two ions formed in the solution produced in experiment 2.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000143", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 143, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Positive ion...", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000144", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 144, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Negative ion...", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000145", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 145, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Identify the gas formed in experiment 2 and give a test for it.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000146", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 146, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) Silver nitrate solution and dilute nitric acid are added to the solution produced in experiment 2.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000147", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 147, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "State what is observed and name the substance responsible for this observation.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000148", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 148, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Explain why dilute nitric acid is added.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000149", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 149, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "observation..", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000150", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 150, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "substance responsible", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000151", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 151, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "explanation...", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000152", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 152, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iv) Explain why there is no reaction in experiment 3.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000153", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 153, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 5 = 10 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000154", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 154, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "6 Crude oil is an important source of organic compounds.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000155", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 155, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) The diagram shows how crude oil is separated into fractions in the oil industry.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000156", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 156, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) What happens to the crude oil in A?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000158", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 158, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Most of the compounds in crude oil are hydrocarbons.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000159", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 159, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "What is meant by the term hydrocarbons?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000160", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 160, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) Compare the hydrocarbons in fractions D and F in terms of", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000161", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 161, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "boiling point", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000162", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 162, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "size of molecules", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000163", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 163, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "viscosity", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000164", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 164, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) Some of the fractions are catalytically cracked. The general equation for some reactions in this process is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000165", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 165, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) State two conditions used in catalytic cracking.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000167", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 167, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) How does the bonding in an alkene molecule differ from the bonding in an alkane molecule?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000168", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 168, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) The chemical equation for one cracking reaction is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000169", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 169, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Deduce the molecular formula of Q.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000171", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 171, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) The compound with molecular formula $ \\mathrm{C_{3} H_{6}} $ can be used to make a polymer.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000172", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 172, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Give the name of the compound $ \\mathrm{C_{3} H_{6}} $", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000173", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 173, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Complete the table of information about this compound.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000174", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 174, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) Complete this structure to show the part of the polymer formed from two molecules of $ \\mathrm{C_{3} H_{6}} $", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000176", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 176, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "7 Hydrogen peroxide solution decomposes very slowly at room temperature.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000178", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 178, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The equation for this reaction is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000179", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 179, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Very few bubbles can be seen in the solution because of the slow decomposition.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000181", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 181, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The rate of this reaction is greatly increased by adding a catalyst.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000182", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 182, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) A student added a solid to some hydrogen peroxide solution to see if the solid acted as a catalyst.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000183", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 183, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "He noticed that a lot of bubbles formed, and that the solid was still present at the end of the reaction.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000184", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 184, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Outline a method to show that the solid acted as a catalyst and not as a reactant.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000185", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 185, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) The student investigated the effect that changing the concentration of the hydrogen peroxide solution has on the rate of the reaction.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000186", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 186, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "He used solid manganese(IV) oxide as the catalyst in each experiment.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000187", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 187, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "This is the method he used.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000188", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 188, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "pour some hydrogen peroxide solution into a conical flask on a top-pan balance", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000189", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 189, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "add the catalyst and place some cotton wool loosely in the neck of the flask", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000190", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 190, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "record the balance reading and start a timer", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000191", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 191, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "record the balance reading every minute until the mass no longer changes", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000192", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 192, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "repeat the experiment several times using different concentrations of hydrogen peroxide solution", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000193", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 193, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) State one property of each substance that the student should keep the same in each experiment.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000194", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 194, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "hydrogen peroxide solution", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000195", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 195, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "manganese(IV) oxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000196", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 196, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) What is the purpose of the cotton wool?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000197", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 197, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) The graph shows the results of one of the student''s experiments.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000198", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 198, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Balance reading in grams", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000199", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 199, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Why does the balance reading decrease during the experiment?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000201", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 201, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) What does the slope of the curve indicate about the reaction?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000202", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 202, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) How long does the reaction take to complete?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000203", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 203, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(d) The results of some of the student''s other experiments are shown on this graph.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000204", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 204, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Balance reading in grams", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000205", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 205, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Which one of the experiments, R, S or T, was the fastest?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000207", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 207, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) The concentration of the hydrogen peroxide solution in experiment S was $ 0. 4 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3}. $ Use the graph to deduce the concentration of the hydrogen peroxide solution in experiment T.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000208", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 208, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "State how you deduced your answer.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000209", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 209, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(e) Another student repeated the investigation.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000210", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 210, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "She recorded the time for the total mass of the beaker and contents to decrease by 0.50 g in each experiment. She then converted the times to relative rates of reaction.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000211", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 211, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The table shows the concentrations she used and the relative rates of reaction she calculated.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000212", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 212, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Plot a graph of these results on the grid.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000214", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 214, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Draw a straight line of best fit through the points.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000215", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 215, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Relative rate of reaction", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000216", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 216, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Concentration of hydrogen peroxide in mol/dm $ ^{3} $", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000218", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 218, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(f) Explain, in terms of particles, why the rate of a reaction increases as the concentration of a reactant increases.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000219", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 219, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 7 = 16 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000220", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 220, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "BLANK PAGE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000221", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 221, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "8 (a) The flow chart shows how ammonia is made using the Haber process.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000222", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 222, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) State one raw material that is used as the source of", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000224", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 224, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Identify the catalyst and state the pressure, in atmospheres, used in the Haber process. (2)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000225", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 225, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "catalyst", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000226", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 226, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "pressure", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000227", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 227, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) Which substances pass from the cooler to the heated catalyst?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000228", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 228, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A ammonia, hydrogen and nitrogen", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000229", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 229, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "B hydrogen only", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000230", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 230, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "C hydrogen and nitrogen", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000231", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 231, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "D nitrogen only", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000232", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 232, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iv) When ammonia leaves the cooler it is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000233", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 233, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iv) When ammonia leaves the co", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000234", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 234, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A an aqueous solution", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000235", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 235, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "B a gas", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000236", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 236, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "C a liquid", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000237", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 237, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "D a solid", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000238", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 238, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) Hydrazine $ \\left( \\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{4} \\right) $ is a useful compound that can be manufactured from ammonia.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000239", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 239, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Hydrogen peroxide can be used to convert ammonia to hydrazine.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000240", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 240, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Balance the equation for this reaction.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000241", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 241, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) The bonding in ammonia and hydrazine can be represented by dot and cross diagrams. The diagram for ammonia has been drawn.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000243", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 243, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "All the bonds in hydrazine are single bonds. Complete the diagram for hydrazine. Show only the outer electrons.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000244", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 244, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) Hydrazine was used as the fuel in the first rocket-powered fighter aircraft in World War II. It is now used as a propellant in spacecraft. It slowed the descent of the Phoenix spacecraft as it landed on Mars.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000246", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 246, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The equations for its use as a rocket fuel and as a propellant are shown in the table.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000247", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 247, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) How does the information in the table show that both reactions are exothermic?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000249", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 249, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Why is it not correct to describe hydrazine as a fuel when it is used as a propellant?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000250", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 250, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(d) Some spacecraft use MMH, a compound similar to hydrazine, as a propellant. MMH has the composition by mass of 26.1% carbon, 60.9% nitrogen and 13.0% hydrogen.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000251", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 251, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Calculate the empirical formula of MMH.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000252", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 252, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "empirical formula.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000253", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 253, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) The $ M_{r} $ of MMH is 46", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000254", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 254, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "What is the molecular formula of MMH?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000255", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 255, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 8 = 15 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000256", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 256, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "9 Molybdenum (Mo) is a metal. It is often used to make an alloy with iron.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000257", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 257, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Like iron, it is extracted from its oxide. Unlike iron, it occurs mainly as its sulfide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000258", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 258, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) Molybdenum sulfide is converted into molybdenum oxide by heating in air. The equation for this reaction is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000259", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 259, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Why is molybdenum said to be oxidised in this reaction?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000261", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 261, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) The sulfur dioxide formed in the reaction could form acid rain if it escaped into the atmosphere.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000262", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 262, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Write a chemical equation for the formation of an acid from sulfur dioxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000263", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 263, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) The table shows the melting points of molybdenum oxide and sulfur dioxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000264", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 264, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The melting point indicates the type of bonding and structure in a compound.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000266", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 266, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) What is the type of bonding in a molecule of sulfur dioxide?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000267", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 267, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Explain why the melting point of sulfur dioxide is low.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000268", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 268, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) The melting point of molybdenum oxide suggests that it has ionic bonding. However, it is often represented as a molecular structure.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000269", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 269, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Deduce the molecular formula of molybdenum oxide as shown in this structure.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000270", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 270, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) The metallic structure of molybdenum gives it some typical properties.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000272", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 272, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Describe the metallic structure of molybdenum.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000273", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 273, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Explain why molybdenum is a good conductor of electricity.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000274", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 274, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) Explain why molybdenum is malleable.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000275", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 275, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 9 = 12 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000276", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 276, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "10 This apparatus can be used to investigate the reduction of metal oxides.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000277", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 277, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The mass of the metal oxide is measured before and after heating it in hydrogen.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000279", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 279, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The results can be used to determine the formula of the oxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000280", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 280, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) The hydrogen gas burns as it leaves the tube.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000281", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 281, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) What substance is formed when hydrogen burns in air?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000282", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 282, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Why is it important to relight the flame if it goes out?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000283", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 283, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) These are the results for one experiment.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000284", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 284, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Mass of solid before heating = 4.2 g", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000285", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 285, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Mass of solid after heating = 3.4 g", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000286", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 286, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "These results may not be sufficient to find the mass of metal for use in determining the formula of the metal oxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000287", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 287, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "What further practical steps should be taken to confirm that an accurate value for the mass of metal has been obtained?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000288", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 288, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) In an experiment using a different metal oxide, a mass of 2.8 g of metal is obtained from 3.6 g of the metal oxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000289", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 289, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Calculate the mass of oxygen in the sample of the metal oxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000290", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 290, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Calculate the amount, in moles, of oxygen atoms in the sample of the metal oxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000291", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 291, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) The formula of the metal oxide is MO, where M is the symbol of the metal. Deduce the amount, in moles, of M in the sample of the metal oxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000292", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 292, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iv) What is the relative atomic mass of M?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000293", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 293, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "relative atomic mass of M=", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000294", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 294, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 10 = 10 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000295", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 295, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "TOTAL FOR PAPER=120 MARKS", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000296", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 296, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "BLANK PAGE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000297", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 297, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "BLANK PAGE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000298", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 298, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "BLANK PAGE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000299", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 299, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}], "schemaVersion": "1.0"}', NULL, '2026-09-13 12:53:10.591143+00');
INSERT INTO public.documents VALUES ('6e666887-9049-4e56-a93d-f605300a216a', 'f9b3ad01-e82b-5449-be6d-12a21d9c4188', '1.0', 1, 'MARK_SCHEME', 'corpus/igcse-chemistry-4ch0-1c-2014junr/MS.md', 'MS.md', 'text/markdown', '95544412b9e1c744d7ae91d8c4ffee4db5419d62140371c809bf4e998820ce15', 'SHA-256', 1, 49, 49, 17, 'glm-ocr-markdown', '1.1.0', '2026-09-13 12:52:49.079238+00', '{"pages": [{"width": null, "height": null, "pageNumber": 1}], "source": {"uri": "corpus/igcse-chemistry-4ch0-1c-2014junr/MS.md", "checksum": "95544412b9e1c744d7ae91d8c4ffee4db5419d62140371c809bf4e998820ce15", "fileName": "MS.md", "mimeType": "text/markdown", "checksumAlgorithm": "SHA-256"}, "tables": [{"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["1", "a", "i", "steam", "Accept gas/vapour", "1"], ["ii", "ice", "Accept solid", "1"], ["iii", "ice", "Accept solid", "1"], ["", "b", "i", "D(melting)", "", "1"], ["ii", "B(condensing)", "", "1"], ["", "c", "", "D(solid to gas)", "", "1"], ["", "d", "i", "exothermic", "", "1"], ["ii", "H2O(g)→H2O(l)", "Accept multiples and fractions", "1"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n1 | a | i | steam | Accept gas/vapour | 1\nii | ice | Accept solid | 1\niii | ice | Accept solid | 1\n | b | i | D(melting) |  | 1\nii | B(condensing) |  | 1\n | c |  | D(solid to gas) |  | 1\n | d | i | exothermic |  | 1\nii | H2O(g)→H2O(l) | Accept multiples and fractions | 1", "row_count": 9, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000020", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 20, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["2", "a", "i", "B(filtration)", "", "1"], ["ii", "C(fractional distillation)", "", "1"], ["", "b", "i", "B(filtration)", "", "1"], ["ii", "D(simple distillation)", "", "1"], ["iii", "A(crystallisation)", "", "1"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n2 | a | i | B(filtration) |  | 1\nii | C(fractional distillation) |  | 1\n | b | i | B(filtration) |  | 1\nii | D(simple distillation) |  | 1\niii | A(crystallisation) |  | 1", "row_count": 6, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000022", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 22, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["2", "c", "i", "place paper in beaker/container/solvent/water solvent level below spots/starting line leave until solvent/water/liquid AND rises/reaches (near) top (of paper) / solvent (front) reaches level shown OR leave until dyes/spots separate (allow to) dry / cover/seal container", "Any three for 1 each", "3"], ["", "", "ii", "insoluble/did not dissolve (in water/solvent)", "", "1"], ["", "", "iii", "2", "", "1"], ["", "", "iv", "1", "", "1"], ["", "", "v", "4670", "Accept value in range 44.5-48.5Accept value in range 69-70Award 1 for M1 and M2 both correct but recorded in cm", "111"], ["", "", "", "0.67", "Accept value in range 0.63-0.7(0)ECF from values recorded (even if>1)", "1"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n2 | c | i | place paper in beaker/container/solvent/water solvent level below spots/starting line leave until solvent/water/liquid AND rises/reaches (near) top (of paper) / solvent (front) reaches level shown OR leave until dyes/spots separate (allow to) dry / cover/seal container | Any three for 1 each | 3\n |  | ii | insoluble/did not dissolve (in water/solvent) |  | 1\n |  | iii | 2 |  | 1\n |  | iv | 1 |  | 1\n |  | v | 4670 | Accept value in range 44.5-48.5Accept value in range 69-70Award 1 for M1 and M2 both correct but recorded in cm | 111\n |  |  | 0.67 | Accept value in range 0.63-0.7(0)ECF from values recorded (even if>1) | 1", "row_count": 7, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000023", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 23, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["3", "a", "i", "(hydrated) iron(III) oxide / ferric oxide", "Accept (hydrated) iron oxide\nReject iron with other oxidation states\nIgnore formulae even if incorrect", "1"], ["ii", "no water / moisture", "Accept drying agent absorbs water\nPrevents water from reaching nail reacting with nail\nReject no air/oxygen", "1"], ["iii", "to keep air/oxygen out (of water)", "Accept to keep air/oxygen from dissolving in water/away from nail", "1"], ["", "b", "i", "galvanising", "Ignore sacrificial protection", "1"], ["ii", "zinc more reactive than iron / above iron in reactivity series / better reducing agent than iron / loses electrons more readily\nzinc reacts/corrodes/oxidises (instead of iron)\nZn $\\rightarrow$ Zn$^{2+}$ + 2e (occurs)\nFe $\\rightarrow$ Fe$^{2+}$ + 2e does not occur\nOR\nFe$^{2+}$ + 2e $\\rightarrow$ Fe (occurs)", "Accept converse statements\nReject zinc rusts\nAccept zinc reacts first/before iron", "4"], ["", "", "If neither M3 nor M4 given, award 1 mark for Zn + Fe$^{2+}$ $\\rightarrow$ Zn$^{2+}$ + Fe", ""], ["", "", "", "", ""], ["", "", "", "", ""]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n3 | a | i | (hydrated) iron(III) oxide / ferric oxide | Accept (hydrated) iron oxide\nReject iron with other oxidation states\nIgnore formulae even if incorrect | 1\nii | no water / moisture | Accept drying agent absorbs water\nPrevents water from reaching nail reacting with nail\nReject no air/oxygen | 1\niii | to keep air/oxygen out (of water) | Accept to keep air/oxygen from dissolving in water/away from nail | 1\n | b | i | galvanising | Ignore sacrificial protection | 1\nii | zinc more reactive than iron / above iron in reactivity series / better reducing agent than iron / loses electrons more readily\nzinc reacts/corrodes/oxidises (instead of iron)\nZn $\\rightarrow$ Zn$^{2+}$ + 2e (occurs)\nFe $\\rightarrow