dence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000090", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 90, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["", "NH4Cl(s)", "NH4Cl(aq)"], ["A", "colourless", "colourless"], ["B", "colourless", "white"], ["C", "white", "colourless"], ["D", "white", "white"]], "text": " | NH4Cl(s) | NH4Cl(aq)\nA | colourless | colourless\nB | colourless | white\nC | white | colourless\nD | white | white", "row_count": 5, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000098", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 3, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 98, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["the molecules that are cracked are hydrocarbons", ""], ["catalytic cracking uses iron as the catalyst", ""], ["cracking is used because of different demands for hydrocarbons", ""], ["cracking reactions are examples of addition reactions", ""], ["cracking produces molecules with shorter chains", ""], ["CH4+2O2→CO2+2H2O is an equation for a cracking reaction", ""]], "text": "the molecules that are cracked are hydrocarbons | \ncatalytic cracking uses iron as the catalyst | \ncracking is used because of different demands for hydrocarbons | \ncracking reactions are examples of addition reactions | \ncracking produces molecules with shorter chains | \nCH4+2O2→CO2+2H2O is an equation for a cracking reaction | ", "row_count": 6, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000136", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 2, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 136, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["volume reading at start in $ \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $", ""], ["volume reading at end in $ \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $", ""], ["change in volume in $ \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $", ""]], "text": "volume reading at start in $ \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ | \nvolume reading at end in $ \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ | \nc