max 3 marks", "5"], ["M1 Poly(ethene) is cheaper than polymers from corn starch", "IGNORE durable", ""], ["M2 Poly(ethene) is stronger than polymers from corn starch\nM3 Poly(ethene) frees up land to grow food crops", "", ""]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n9 | (a)(i) | M1(Empirical formula)CH2 | ALLOW sub and super script numbers for M1 and M2 | 2\nM2(General formula)CnH2n | ALLOW letters other than n\nALLOW capital letters | \nAny two from: |  | 2\nM1 each member differs from the next by a CH2 group OWTTE\nM2(each member has) same functional group\nM3(each member has) similar/same chemical properties / similar/same (chemical) reactions\nM4 trend in physical properties (between successive members) | ACCEPT react in similar/same way\nACCEPT named physical property, e.g. boiling point\nREJECT similar/same physical properties | \n(b)(i) | (i)(ii) | addition | ALLOW additional\nREJECT condensation | 1\nM1 Single bond between the two carbons, 4 hydrogens joined by single bonds\nM2 trailing bonds through the brackets and the n to the right | ALLOW n in any position outside the bracket to the right of the structure.\nALLOW capital N | \n(iii) | (iii) | Any 5 points from: | If only advantages or disadvantages given, max 3 marks | 5\nM1 Poly(ethene) is cheaper than polymers from corn starch | IGNORE durable | \nM2 Poly(ethene) is stronger than polymers from corn starch\nM3 Poly(ethene) frees up land to grow food crops |  | ", "row_count": 10, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000029", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 29, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["", "M4 Poly(ethene) comes from (cracking of certain fractions from) crude oil\nM5 Poly(ethene) is non-renewable OR ethene is a finite source\nM6 Poly(ethene) is inert\nM7 poly(ethene) is non-biodegradable\nM8 poly(ethene) takes longer to decompose\nM9 Disposal of poly(ethene) is a problem (in landfill)\nM10 Poly(ethene) causes problems with litter\nM11 Burning poly(ethene) (could) create toxic fumes/greenhouse gases", "", ""], ["(c)", "Chemical structure of poly(ethene)", "Must show every bond.\nIGNORE bond angles\nIGNORE n\nIGNORE brackets\nREJECT trailing bonds", "1"]], "text": " | M4 Poly(ethene) comes from (cracking of certain fractions from) crude oil\nM5 Poly(ethene) is non-renewable OR ethene is a finite source\nM6 Poly(ethene) is inert\nM7 poly(ethene) is non-biodegradable\nM8 poly(ethene) takes longer to decompose\nM9 Disposal of poly(ethene) is a problem (in landfill)\nM10 Poly(ethene) causes problems with litter\nM11 Burning poly(ethene) (could) create toxic fumes/greenhouse gases |  | \n(c) | Chemical structure of poly(ethene) | Must show every bond.\nIGNORE bond angles\nIGNORE n\nIGNORE brackets\nREJECT trailing bonds | 1", "row_count": 2, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000030", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 30, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["10(a)(i)", "M1 four electrons between the carbon and each oxygen", "", "2"], ["M2 rest of molecule correct", "M2 dep on M1", ""], ["M1 shared pair(s) of electrons", "", "2"], ["(b)(i)", "M2 attracted to (two) nuclei", "REJECT nucleus. Must be plural for M2.M2 dep on mention of electrons in M1", ""], ["M1 Graphite has delocalised electrons", "IGNORE sea of electronsIGNORE free electronsIGNORE number of electrons", "2"], ["M2 (delocalised electron(s)) can move or flow (throughout the structure)", "IGNORE references to carrying a charge or currentIGNORE references to layersM2 dep on mentioning electrons in M1Any mention of ions scores 0", ""], ["M1 (diamond) giant covalent", "ALLOW macromolecularALLOW giant structure if M2 is scoredIGNORE tetrahedral structureREJECT molecules of diamond", "5"], ["M2 (in melting diamond) covalent bonds are broken", "ALLOW description of covalent bonds", ""], ["M3(C60)(simple) molecular structure", "ALLOW molecules of C60", ""], ["M4(in melting C60) intermolecular forces (of attraction) are overcome", "", ""], ["", "M5 more energy is needed to break covalent bonds(in diamond) than intermolecular forces(in C60)", "ALLOW strong covalent bonds and weak intermolecular forces(or attraction)", ""]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n10(a)(i) | M1 four electrons between the carbon and each oxygen |  | 2\nM2 rest of molecule correct | M2 dep on M1 | \nM1 shared pair(s) of electrons |  | 2\n(b)(i) | M2 attracted to (two) nuclei | REJECT nucleus. Must be plural for M2.M2 dep on mention of electrons in M1 | \nM1 Graphite has delocalised electrons | IGNORE sea of electronsIGNORE free electronsIGNORE number of electrons | 2\nM2 (delocalised electron(s)) can move or flow (throughout the structure) | IGNORE references to carrying a charge or currentIGNORE references to layersM2 dep on mentioning electrons in M1Any mention of ions scores 0 | \nM1 (diamond) giant covalent | ALLOW macromolecularALLOW giant structure if M2 is scoredIGNORE tetrahedral structureREJECT molecules of diamond | 5\nM2 (in melting diamond) covalent bonds are broken | ALLOW description of covalent bonds | \nM3(C60)(simple) molecular structure | ALLOW molecules of C60 | \nM4(in melting C60) intermolecular forces (of attraction) are overcome |  | \n | M5 more energy is needed to break covalent bonds(in diamond) than intermolecular forces(in C60) | ALLOW strong covalent bonds and weak intermolecular forces(or attraction) | ", "row_count": 12, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000031", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 31, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["", "", "ACCEPT breaking bonds in C60 if intermolecular forces clearly mentioned\nMention of intermolecular forces in diamond no M2 or M5\nMention of breaking covalent bonds in C60 no M4 or M5", ""]], "text": " |  | ACCEPT breaking bonds in C60 if intermolecular forces clearly mentioned\nMention of intermolecular forces in diamond no M2 or M5\nMention of breaking covalent bonds in C60 no M4 or M5 | ", "row_count": 1, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000032", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 32, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["11(a)", "4 CuO(s)+CH4(g)→4 Cu(s)+CO2(g)+2H2O(l/g)M1 correct balancingM2 correct state symbols", "ALLOW multiples and fractions", "2"], ["(b)(i)", "M1 Mass copper 3.18g and mass oxygen 0.40gM2 Moles copper=3.18/63.5OR0.0500molesM3 Moles oxygen=0.40/16OR0.025molesM4 Ratio of moles Cu:O is2:1", "M2 and M3 allow ecf from M1M4 is dep on M2 and M3", "4"], ["(ii)", "Any one from:M1 Use a safety screenM2 Position the class some distance from the apparatusM3 Do the experiment in a fume cupboardM4 Set fire to the (excess) methane gas straight away", "ALLOW tie hair backALLOW wear heat-proof gloves", "1"], ["(c)(i)", "(Iron(III)oxide)loses oxygen", "ALLOW iron loses oxygenIGNORE any reference to electrons.", "1"], ["(ii)", "Carbon monoxide is poisonous/toxic OR carbon monoxide reduces the ability of the blood to carry oxygen", "ALLOW carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin in the blood", "1"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n11(a) | 4 CuO(s)+CH4(g)→4 Cu(s)+CO2(g)+2H2O(l/g)M1 correct balancingM2 correct state symbols | ALLOW multiples and fractions | 2\n(b)(i) | M1 Mass copper 3.18g and mass oxygen 0.40gM2 Moles copper=3.18/63.5OR0.0500molesM3 Moles oxygen=0.40/16OR0.025molesM4 Ratio of moles Cu:O is2:1 | M2 and M3 allow ecf from M1M4 is dep on M2 and M3 | 4\n(ii) | Any one from:M1 Use a safety screenM2 Position the class some distance from the apparatusM3 Do the experiment in a fume cupboardM4 Set fire to the (excess) methane gas straight away | ALLOW tie hair backALLOW wear heat-proof gloves | 1\n(c)(i) | (Iron(III)oxide)loses oxygen | ALLOW iron loses oxygenIGNORE any reference to electrons. | 1\n(ii) | Carbon monoxide is poisonous/toxic OR carbon monoxide reduces the ability of the blood to carry oxygen | ALLOW carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin in the blood | 1", "row_count": 6, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000033", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 33, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["(iii)", "calculate $ M_{r} $ of $ Fe_{2}O_{3} $\ncalculate the amount, in moles, of $ Fe_{2}O_{3} $\ncalculate the amount, in moles, of Fe\ncalculate the mass in tonnes of Fe\nExample calculation\nM1 $ M_{r} $ of $ Fe_{2}O_{3} $ = 160\nM2 $ n(Fe_{2}O_{3}) $ = 30.0 $ \\times10^{6} $ $ \\div $ 160 OR 187,500 moles\nM3 $ n(Fe) $ = 187,500 $ \\times2 $ OR 375,000 moles\nM4 375,000 $ \\times56 $ = 21 tonnes", "Correct answer of 21 tonnes scores 4 marks with or without working\nALLOW ecf from M1 (incorrect $ M_{r} $ )\nALLOW working in megamoles\nALLOW ecf from M1\nALLOW working in megamoles\nALLOW ECF from M2\nALLOW ecf from M3", "4"], ["(iv)", "M1 840,000g is 70,000 moles of carbon\nM2 therefore need 23,333 moles $ Fe_{2}O_{3} $ (but we have 25,000 which is an excess)\nOR\nM1 Need 75,000 moles carbon\nM2 900,000g of carbon is needed (and have 840,000g of carbon so iron(III) oxide is in excess as carbon is the limiting reactant)\nOR\nM1 need 75,000 moles of carbon\nM2 have 840,000 $ \\div $ 12 OR 70,000 moles of carbon (so iron(III) oxide is in excess as carbon is the limiting reactant)", "", "2"]], "text": "(iii) | calculate $ M_{r} $ of $ Fe_{2}O_{3} $\ncalculate the amount, in moles, of $ Fe_{2}O_{3} $\ncalculate the amount, in moles, of Fe\ncalculate the mass in tonnes of Fe\nExample calculation\nM1 $ M_{r} $ of $ Fe_{2}O_{3} $ = 160\nM2 $ n(Fe_{2}O_{3}) $ = 30.0 $ \\times10^{6} $ $ \\div $ 160 OR 187,500 moles\nM3 $ n(Fe) $ = 187,500 $ \\times2 $ OR 375,000 moles\nM4 375,000 $ \\times56 $ = 21 tonnes | Correct answer of 21 tonnes scores 4 marks with or without working\nALLOW ecf from M1 (incorrect $ M_{r} $ )\nALLOW working in megamoles\nALLOW ecf from M1\nALLOW working in megamoles\nALLOW ECF from M2\nALLOW ecf from M3 | 4\n(iv) | M1 840,000g is 70,000 moles of carbon\nM2 therefore need 23,333 moles $ Fe_{2}O_{3} $ (but we have 25,000 which is an excess)\nOR\nM1 Need 75,000 moles carbon\nM2 900,000g of carbon is needed (and have 840,000g of carbon so iron(III) oxide is in excess as carbon is the limiting reactant)\nOR\nM1 need 75,000 moles of carbon\nM2 have 840,000 $ \\div $ 12 OR 70,000 moles of carbon (so iron(III) oxide is in excess as carbon is the limiting reactant) |  | 2", "row_count": 2, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000034", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 34, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}], "figures": [], "version": 1, "sections": [{"level": 2, "title": "Mark Scheme (Results)", "sectionId": "s001", "elementIds": ["e000003", "e000004"], "pageNumber": 1}, {"level": 2, "title": "Edexcel and BTEC Qualifications", "sectionId": "s002", "elementIds": ["e000006"], "pageNumber": 1}, {"level": 2, "title": "Pearson: helping people progress, everywhere", "sectionId": "s003", "elementIds": ["e000008", "e000009"], "pageNumber": 1}, {"level": 2, "title": "General Marking Guidance", "sectionId": "s004", "elementIds": ["e000011", "e000012", "e000013", "e000014", "e000015", "e000016", "e000017", "e000018"], "pageNumber": 1}], "equations": [], "pageCount": 1, "documentId": "2969c0ec-34a1-58ab-aaa0-e9bbe16da6f2", "provenance": {"engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "application": "syllabai-parser", "extractedAt": "2026-09-13T12:54:22.306976112Z", "engineVersion": "1.1.0", "schemaVersion": "1.0", "extractionParams": {"pageBoundaries": "none-in-source", "sourceLineCount": 169, "upstreamConverter": "zai-glm-ocr", "entityDecodedLines": 0, "signedUrlFigureRefs": 0, "normalizedHtmlEntities": true}}, "textBlocks": [{"role": "paragraph", "text": "PMT", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000000", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 0, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Pearson", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000001", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 1, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "Mark Scheme (Results)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000002", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 2, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "January 2020", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000003", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 3, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Pearson Edexcel International GCSE in Chemistry (4CH1) Paper 1CR", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000004", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 4, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "Edexcel and BTEC Qualifications", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000005", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 5, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Edexcel and BTEC qualifications are awarded by Pearson, the UK''s largest awarding body. We provide a wide range of qualifications including academic, vocational, occupational and specific programmes for employers. For further information visit our qualifications websites at www.edexcel.com or www.btec.co.uk. Alternatively, you can get in touch with us using the details on our contact us page at www.edexcel.com/contactus.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000006", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 6, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "Pearson: helping people progress, everywhere", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000007", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 7, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Pearson aspires to be the world''s leading learning company. Our aim is to help everyone progress in their lives through education. We believe in every kind of learning, for all kinds of people, wherever they are in the world. We''ve been involved in education for over 150 years, and by working across 70 countries, in 100 languages, we have built an international reputation for our commitment to high standards and raising achievement through innovation in education. Find out more about how we can help you and your students at: www.pearson.com/uk", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000008", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 8, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "January 2020 Publications Code 4CH1_1CR_msc_20200305 All the material in this publication is copyright $ \\textcircled{c} $ Pearson Education Ltd 2020", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000009", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 9, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "General Marking Guidance", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000010", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 10, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "All candidates must receive the same treatment. Examiners must mark the first candidate in exactly the same way as they mark the last.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000011", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 11, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Mark schemes should be applied positively. Candidates must be rewarded for what they have shown they can do rather than penalised for omissions.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000012", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 12, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Examiners should mark according to the mark scheme not according to their perception of where the grade boundaries may lie.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000013", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 13, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "There is no ceiling on achievement. All marks on the mark scheme should be used appropriately.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000014", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 14, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "All the marks on the mark scheme are designed to be awarded. Examiners should always award full marks if deserved, i.e. if the answer matches the mark scheme. Examiners should also be prepared to award zero marks if the candidate''s response is not worthy of credit according to the mark scheme.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000015", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 15, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Where some judgement is required, mark schemes will provide the principles by which marks will be awarded and exemplification may be limited.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000016", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 16, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "When examiners are in doubt regarding the application of the mark scheme to a candidate''s response, the team leader must be consulted.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000017", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 17, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Crossed out work should be marked UNLESS the candidate has replaced it with an alternative response.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000018", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 18, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}], "schemaVersion": "1.0"}', NULL, '2026-09-13 12:54:40.554927+00');
