(\\mathrm {C H} _ {3} \\mathrm {C O O}) _ {2} + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000164", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "equation", "reading_order": 164, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}], "pageCount": 1, "documentId": "afe3708e-14d5-5112-ad60-09bfdcf8c3d2", "provenance": {"engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "application": "syllabai-parser", "extractedAt": "2026-09-13T13:02:33.660736635Z", "engineVersion": "1.1.0", "schemaVersion": "1.0", "extractionParams": {"pageBoundaries": "none-in-source", "sourceLineCount": 219, "upstreamConverter": "zai-glm-ocr", "entityDecodedLines": 0, "signedUrlFigureRefs": 0, "normalizedHtmlEntities": true}}, "textBlocks": [{"role": "heading", "text": "Instructions", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000001", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 1, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Use black ink or ball-point pen.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000002", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 2, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Fill in the boxes at the top of this page with your name, centre number and candidate number.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000003", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 3, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Answer all questions.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000004", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 4, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Answer the questions in the spaces provided", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000005", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 5, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "there may be more space than you need.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000006", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 6, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Show all the steps in any calculations and state the units.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000007", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 7, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "Information", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000008", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 8, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "The total mark for this paper is 70.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000009", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 9, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "The marks for each question are shown in brackets", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000010", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 10, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "use this as a guide as to how much time to spend on each question.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000011", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 11, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "Advice", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000012", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 12, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Read each question carefully before you start to answer it.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000013", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 13, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Write your answers neatly and in good English.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000014", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 14, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Try to answer every question.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000015", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 15, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Check your answers if you have time at the end.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000016", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 16, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Turn over", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000017", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 17, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "The lanthanoids (atomic numbers 58-71) and the actinoids (atomic numbers 90-103) have been omitted.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000019", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 19, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The relative atomic masses of copper and chlorine have not been rounded to the nearest whole number.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000020", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 20, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "Answer ALL questions.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000021", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 21, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Some questions must be answered with a cross in a box. If you change your mind about an answer, put a line through the box and then mark your new answer with a cross.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000022", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 22, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "1 This question is about the unsaturated hydrocarbon, ethene.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000023", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 23, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The displayed formula of ethene is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000024", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 24, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) (i) State the meaning of the term hydrocarbon.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000026", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 26, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Give the reason why ethene is described as unsaturated.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000027", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 27, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) Ethene is bubbled through bromine water until there is no further colour change. Which of these is the appearance of the solution formed?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000028", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 28, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A colourless", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000029", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 29, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "B orange", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000030", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 30, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "C purple", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000031", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 31, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "D red", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000032", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 32, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) Ethanol is produced industrially by the reaction between ethene and steam.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000033", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 33, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The equation for the reaction is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000034", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 34, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) State the temperature and pressure used in this reaction.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000035", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 35, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "temperature", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000037", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 37, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "pressure", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000038", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 38, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Give the molecular formula of ethanol.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000039", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 39, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 1 = 7 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000040", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 40, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "2 This question is about gases in the air.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000041", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 41, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The pie chart represents the percentages of gases in dry, unpolluted air.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000042", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 42, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Gases with percentages of less than 1% in air are called trace gases.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000043", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 43, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) (i) Which of these is gas Z?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000045", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 45, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A hydrogen", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000046", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 46, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "B methane", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000047", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 47, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "C neon", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000048", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 48, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "D nitrogen", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000049", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 49, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Which of these is the approximate percentage of oxygen in dry, unpolluted air?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000050", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 50, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A 0.04%", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000051", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 51, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "B 0.9%", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000052", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 52, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "C 21%", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000053", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 53, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "D 35%", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000054", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 54, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) One of the trace gases is carbon dioxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000055", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 55, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Identify two reactions that produce carbon dioxide by placing a tick ( $ \\surd $ ) in two boxes.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000056", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 56, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Name an environmental problem that is caused by the percentage of carbon dioxide increasing in the atmosphere.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000058", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 58, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) Name the trace gas with the highest percentage in dry, unpolluted air.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000059", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 59, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) Rainwater is acidic because carbon dioxide dissolves in water to form carbonic acid.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000060", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 60, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Acid rain is more acidic than rainwater because acidic pollutant gases also dissolve in water.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000061", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 61, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Give the name of the acid that forms when nitrogen dioxide dissolves in water.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000062", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 62, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Name another pollutant gas that also forms acid rain.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000063", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 63, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 2 = 8 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000064", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 64, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "BLANK PAGE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000065", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 65, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "3 This question is about solubility.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000066", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 66, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) The graph shows the solubilities of copper(II) chloride and sodium chloride at different temperatures.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000067", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 67, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Determine the temperature at which copper(II) chloride and sodium chloride have the same solubility.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000070", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 70, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Show on the graph how you obtained your answer.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000071", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 71, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) A saturated solution of copper(II) chloride in 100g of water is cooled from $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ to $ 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000073", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 73, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Determine the mass, in grams, of copper(II) chloride that crystallises.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000074", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 74, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "mass of copper(II) chloride =", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000075", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 75, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) A student uses this method to determine the solubility of potassium chloride in water at room temperature.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000076", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 76, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "record the mass of an empty evaporating basin", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000077", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 77, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "pour some saturated potassium chloride solution into the evaporating basin", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000078", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 78, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "record the mass of the evaporating basin and saturated potassium chloride solution", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000079", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 79, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "heat the evaporating basin to remove all the water", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000080", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 80, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "record the mass of the evaporating basin and the dry potassium chloride", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000081", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 81, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The table shows the student''s results.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000082", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 82, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Calculate the mass of dry potassium chloride obtained.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000084", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 84, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Calculate the mass of water removed.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000085", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 85, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) Calculate the solubility of potassium chloride in grams per 100 grams of water.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000086", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 86, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iv) Suggest why the student''s method is not suitable for determining the solubility of hydrated copper(II) sulfate.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000088", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 88, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 3 = 9 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000089", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 89, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "4 This question is about the reactions of Group 1 metals with water.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000090", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 90, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) A teacher adds a piece of sodium to some water containing universal indicator.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000091", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 91, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The equation for this reaction is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000093", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 93, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The sodium floats on the surface of the water and the universal indicator changes colour because an alkaline solution is formed.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000095", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 95, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Give two other observations.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000096", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 96, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Give the final colour of the universal indicator.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000097", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 97, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) The diagram represents an atom of lithium and an atom of sodium.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000098", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 98, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "lithium", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000100", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 100, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "sodium", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000102", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 102, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Give a reason why lithium and sodium have similar reactions with water.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000103", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 103, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Explain why lithium is less reactive than sodium.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000104", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 104, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) The teacher adds 0.150g of lithium to an excess of water and collects the hydrogen gas produced.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000105", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 105, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The equation for the reaction is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000106", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 106, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The teacher collects $ 2 5 4 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of hydrogen gas at room temperature and pressure (rtp).", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000108", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 108, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Show by calculation that 1 mol of hydrogen gas has a volume of approximately $ 2 4 0 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ at rtp.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000109", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 109, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "BLANK PAGE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000110", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 110, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "5 Hydrogen peroxide solution decomposes to give water and oxygen gas.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000111", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 111, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The equation for this reaction is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000112", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 112, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) Three different solids are catalysts for the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide solution.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000114", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 114, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A student is given hydrogen peroxide solution and a sample of each of the solid catalysts.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000115", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 115, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The student has a timer, a measuring cylinder, a balance and the apparatus shown in the diagram.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000116", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 116, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Describe a method the student could use to find which of the three solids is the most effective catalyst for the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide solution.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000118", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 118, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) The diagram shows the reaction profile for the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide without a catalyst.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000119", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 119, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Label the diagram to show the activation energy $ ( E_{\\mathrm{a}}) $ and the enthalpy change $ (\\Delta H) $ for this reaction.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000121", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 121, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) On the diagram, draw a curve to show the reaction profile for the same reaction when a catalyst is used.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000122", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 122, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 5 = 8 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000123", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 123, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "BLANK PAGE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000124", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 124, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "6 This question is about the electrolysis of copper(II) sulfate solution.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000125", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 125, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) The diagram shows the apparatus used for the electrolysis.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000126", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 126, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A student records the total increase in mass of the negative electrode every minute for 8 minutes.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000128", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 128, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The table shows the results.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000129", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 129, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Plot the student''s results.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000131", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 131, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Draw a curve of best fit.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000132", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 132, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "Increase in mass in grams", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000133", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 133, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) Explain the shape of the graph.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000135", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 135, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) The product at the positive electrode is oxygen gas.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000136", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 136, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) The student repeats the electrolysis using different apparatus.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000137", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 137, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Describe how the student should collect a sample of pure oxygen at the positive electrode.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000139", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 139, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Give an ionic half-equation for the formation of oxygen.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000140", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 140, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) The wire used to connect the power supply to the electrodes is made of copper metal.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000141", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 141, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The diagram shows the arrangement of the ions in a metal.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000142", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 142, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "metal ion", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000145", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 145, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Metals that are malleable can also be stretched to form long, thin wires. Suggest why metals can be stretched to form wires.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000146", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 146, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Explain why metals conduct electricity.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000147", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 147, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 6 = 12 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000148", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 148, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "7 This question is about esters.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000149", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 149, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Ester A reacts with water to form ethanoic acid and ethanol.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000150", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 150, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The displayed formulae of the reactants and products are shown in this equation", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000151", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 151, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The molar enthalpy change $ (\\Delta H) $ for the reaction is 0 kJ/mol.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000153", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 153, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) (i) Draw a ring around the functional group in ester A.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000154", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 154, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Give the name of ester A.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000155", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 155, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) Describe a chemical test, other than using an indicator, to show that the reaction mixture contains ethanoic acid.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000156", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 156, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) Explain why the molar enthalpy change $ (\\Delta H) $ for the reaction between ester A and water is 0 kJ/mol.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000157", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 157, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "In your answer, refer to the bonds broken and the bonds formed.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000158", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 158, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) A mixture of ester A and water is left in a sealed container until the reaction mixture reaches dynamic equilibrium.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000159", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 159, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Describe what is meant by dynamic equilibrium.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000160", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 160, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Explain why adding a catalyst does not change the position of equilibrium.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000161", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 161, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(d) The ethanoic acid produced in the reaction is completely neutralised by $ 2 2. 7 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of $ 0. 1 5 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ barium hydroxide solution.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000162", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 162, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The equation for the neutralisation reaction is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000163", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 163, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Calculate the amount, in moles, of ethanoic acid neutralised Give your answer to 3 significant figures.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000165", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 165, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(e) The structures of two organic compounds are shown.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000166", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 166, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "These compounds react together to form a polymer.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000169", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 169, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Give the repeat unit of the polymer formed.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000170", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 170, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 7 = 15 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000171", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 171, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "TOTAL FOR PAPER = 70 MARKS", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000172", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 172, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "BLANK PAGE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000173", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 173, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "BLANK PAGE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000174", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 174, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}], "schemaVersion": "1.0"}', NULL, '2026-09-13 13:02:53.831703+00');
INSERT INTO public.documents VALUES ('0061fba9-55b2-41d5-8448-fe9a3221f7a5', '9047f3ba-c1ac-5e04-8bc1-08908601955a', '1.0', 1, 'MARK_SCHEME', 'corpus/igcse-chemistry-4ch0-2c-2022jan/MS.md', 'MS.md', 'text/markdown', '7649654514db73f8a21e0fe0c5ed1c410762ba6dbaa63e618e7afac4bf402450', 'SHA-256', 1, 33, 33, 11, 'glm-ocr-markdown', '1.1.0', '2026-09-13 13:02:33.723027+00', '{"pages": [{"width": null, "height": null, "pageNumber": 1}], "source": {"uri": "corpus/igcse-chemistry-4ch0-2c-2022jan/MS.md", "checksum": "7649654514db73f8a21e0fe0c5ed1c410762ba6dbaa63e618e7afac4bf402450", "fileName": "MS.md", "mimeType": "text/markdown", "checksumAlgorithm": "SHA-256"}, "tables": [{"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["1(a)(i)(ii)", "M1 a compound/substance/molecule containing hydrogen/H and carbon/C (atoms)", "no M1 if reference to element containing C and H OR C and H molecules", "2"], ["M2 only", "M2 dep on M1 or near miss", ""], ["ethene has a double bond", "", "1"], ["(b)", "A colourlessA is the only correct answer because alkenes such as ethene react with bromine water forming a colourless solutionB is not correct as bromine water is orange before it reacts with etheneC is not correct as the solution produced is not purpleD is not correct as the solution produced is not red", "", "1"], ["(c)(i)", "temperature=300℃pressure=60-70 atmospheres", "ACCEPT any value or range of values between250和350℃If no unit given assume it is CelsiusACCEPT equivalent temperatures in other units provided the unit is givenACCEPT any value or range of values between60和70atmIf no unit given assume it is atmACCEPT equivalent pressures in other units provided the unit is given", "2"], ["(ii)", "C2H6O", "IGNORE size of numbersREJECTC2H5OH", "1"], ["Total for question=7"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n1(a)(i)(ii) | M1 a compound/substance/molecule containing hydrogen/H and carbon/C (atoms) | no M1 if reference to element containing C and H OR C and H molecules | 2\nM2 only | M2 dep on M1 or near miss | \nethene has a double bond |  | 1\n(b) | A colourlessA is the only correct answer because alkenes such as ethene react with bromine water forming a colourless solutionB is not correct as bromine water is orange before it reacts with etheneC is not correct as the solution produced is not purpleD is not correct as the solution produced is not red |  | 1\n(c)(i) | temperature=300℃pressure=60-70 atmospheres | ACCEPT any value or range of values between250和350℃If no unit given assume it is CelsiusACCEPT equivalent temperatures in other units provided the unit is givenACCEPT any value or range of values between60和70atmIf no unit given assume it is atmACCEPT equivalent pressures in other units provided the unit is given | 2\n(ii) | C2H6O | IGNORE size of numbersREJECTC2H5OH | 1\nTotal for question=7", "row_count": 8, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000024", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 24, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["2(a)(i)", "D nitrogen", "", "1"], ["D is the only correct answer as nitrogen is the most abundant gas in unpolluted air.A is not correct as there are only trace amounts of hydrogen in unpolluted air.B is not correct as there are only trace amounts of methane in unpolluted air.C is not correct as there are only trace amounts of neon in unpolluted air.", "1"], ["C 21%C is the only correct answer as the percentage of oxygen in the air is 21%A is not the correct answer as the percentage of oxygen in the air is not 0.04%B is not the correct answer as the percentage of oxygen in the air is not 0.9%D is not the correct answer as the percentage of oxygen in the air is not 35%"], ["(b)(i)", "cracking an alkane", "", "ALLOW greenhouse effectALLOW an effect of global warming/climate change e.g. wildfires/melting of polar ice caps/flooding", "2"], ["complete combustion of an alkane", "✓", "1"], ["reaction between magnesium and hydrochloric acid", ""], ["rusting of iron", ""], ["thermal decomposition of copper(II) carbonate", "✓"], ["Any one from:M1 global warmingM2 climate changeargon", "1"], ["(c)(i)", "nitric acid", "ALLOW HNO3/ nitrous acid/HNO2", "1"], ["sulfur dioxide", "ALLOW SO2ALLOW other oxides of nitrogen", "1"], ["Total for question = 8"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n2(a)(i) | D nitrogen |  | 1\nD is the only correct answer as nitrogen is the most abundant gas in unpolluted air.A is not correct as there are only trace amounts of hydrogen in unpolluted air.B is not correct as there are only trace amounts of methane in unpolluted air.C is not correct as there are only trace amounts of neon in unpolluted air. | 1\nC 21%C is the only correct answer as the percentage of oxygen in the air is 21%A is not the correct answer as the percentage of oxygen in the air is not 0.04%B is not the correct answer as the percentage of oxygen in the air is not 0.9%D is not the correct answer as the percentage of oxygen in the air is not 35%\n(b)(i) | cracking an alkane |  | ALLOW greenhouse effectALLOW an effect of global warming/climate change e.g. wildfires/melting of polar ice caps/flooding | 2\ncomplete combustion of an alkane | ✓ | 1\nreaction between magnesium and hydrochloric acid | \nrusting of iron | \nthermal decomposition of copper(II) carbonate | ✓\nAny one from:M1 global warmingM2 climate changeargon | 1\n(c)(i) | nitric acid | ALLOW HNO3/ nitrous acid/HNO2 | 1\nsulfur dioxide | ALLOW SO2ALLOW other oxides of nitrogen | 1\nTotal for question = 8", "row_count": 13, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000025", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 25, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["3(a)(i)(ii)", "M1 working shown on graphM256(℃)", "ALLOW any value between 56 and 57 inclusive.", "2"], ["M131-13M218(g)", "ALLOW ecf if one incorrect reading in M1correct answer of 18g with or without working scores2", "2"], ["5.1(g)", "", "1"], ["(b)(i)(ii)(iii)", "15(g)", "", "1"], ["M15.1÷15OR0.34(g)ORanswer to(i)÷answer to(ii)M234(g)ORanswer toM1×100", "correct answer of 34(g)with or without working scores2", "2"], ["Any one fromM1(hydrated) copper(II)sulfate would become anhydrous copper sulfateM2(hydrated) copper(II)sulfate would lose water", "ALLOW the(hydrated) crystals would decompose", "1"], ["Total for question=9"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n3(a)(i)(ii) | M1 working shown on graphM256(℃) | ALLOW any value between 56 and 57 inclusive. | 2\nM131-13M218(g) | ALLOW ecf if one incorrect reading in M1correct answer of 18g with or without working scores2 | 2\n5.1(g) |  | 1\n(b)(i)(ii)(iii) | 15(g) |  | 1\nM15.1÷15OR0.34(g)ORanswer to(i)÷answer to(ii)M234(g)ORanswer toM1×100 | correct answer of 34(g)with or without working scores2 | 2\nAny one fromM1(hydrated) copper(II)sulfate would become anhydrous copper sulfateM2(hydrated) copper(II)sulfate would lose water | ALLOW the(hydrated) crystals would decompose | 1\nTotal for question=9", "row_count": 8, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000026", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 26, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["4(a)(i)", "Any two from:", "", "2"], ["M1 sodium moves (on the surface)", "", ""], ["M2 sodium turns into a sphere/ball", "ALLOW sodium melts", ""], ["M3 effervescence", "ALLOW fizzing / bubbles", ""], ["M4 sodium gets smaller", "ALLOW sodium disappears / dissolves", ""], ["M5 white trail seen", "IGNORE references to a flame", ""], ["(ii) blue / purple", "IGNORE qualifiers", "1"], ["(b)(i)", "same number of electrons/one electron in the outer shell.", "ALLOW same number of valence electrons/one valence electron", "1"], ["M1 lithium (atom) has a smaller atomic radius", "ALLOW lithium is smaller (atom)/lithium (atom) has fewer shells /energy levels", "3"], ["M2 the outer shell electron is more strongly attracted to the nucleus OWTTE", "ALLOW outer electron in lithium (atom) is closer to the nucleus", ""], ["M3 so is less easily lost", "ALLOW reverse argument for sodium", ""], ["(c)M1 amount lithium = 0.150÷7 OR 0.0214 molM2 amount hydrogen = 0.0107 molM3 254÷0.0107M4 23,738", "penalise 1 sig fig in M1ALLOW M1÷2ALLOW 2 or more sig figs in answers to M1 and M2ALLOW 2 or more sig figs if all working showncorrect answers in the range 23,707 to 24,910 cm3 to 3 or more significant figures, without working scores4 marks.", "4"], ["Total for question = 11"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n4(a)(i) | Any two from: |  | 2\nM1 sodium moves (on the surface) |  | \nM2 sodium turns into a sphere/ball | ALLOW sodium melts | \nM3 effervescence | ALLOW fizzing / bubbles | \nM4 sodium gets smaller | ALLOW sodium disappears / dissolves | \nM5 white trail seen | IGNORE references to a flame | \n(ii) blue / purple | IGNORE qualifiers | 1\n(b)(i) | same number of electrons/one electron in the outer shell. | ALLOW same number of valence electrons/one valence electron | 1\nM1 lithium (atom) has a smaller atomic radius | ALLOW lithium is smaller (atom)/lithium (atom) has fewer shells /energy levels | 3\nM2 the outer shell electron is more strongly attracted to the nucleus OWTTE | ALLOW outer electron in lithium (atom) is closer to the nucleus | \nM3 so is less easily lost | ALLOW reverse argument for sodium | \n(c)M1 amount lithium = 0.150÷7 OR 0.0214 molM2 amount hydrogen = 0.0107 molM3 254÷0.0107M4 23,738 | penalise 1 sig fig in M1ALLOW M1÷2ALLOW 2 or more sig figs in answers to M1 and M2ALLOW 2 or more sig figs if all working showncorrect answers in the range 23,707 to 24,910 cm3 to 3 or more significant figures, without working scores4 marks. | 4\nTotal for question = 11", "row_count": 14, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000027", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 27, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["5(a)", "A description that refers to any five of the following points", "", "5"], ["M1 add hydrogen peroxide to the conical flask and add catalyst", "", ""], ["M2 start the timer", "", ""], ["M3 record volume of gas produced in a given time/record the time for certain volume of gas to be produced", "", ""], ["M4 repeat with same mass of a different catalyst", "", ""], ["M5 and with same volume of hydrogen peroxide", "", ""], ["M6 plot the results on a graph and calculate gradient (for each catalyst)", "", ""], ["M7 the most effective catalyst gives the fastest rate of reaction OWTTE", "", ""], ["(b)(i)", "M1 a vertical line from the level of the hydrogen peroxide to the top of the curve labelled activation energy or $E_{\\mathrm{a}}$", "the line does not need an arrow head", "2"], ["M2 a vertical line from the level of the hydrogen peroxide to the level of the water and oxygen labelled enthalpy change or $\\Delta H$", "ALLOW double headed arrow\nREJECT arrow pointing down", ""], ["curve starting from hydrogen peroxide level, below the peak of the original curve, and ending at water and oxygen level", "ALLOW double headed arrow\nREJECT arrow pointing up", ""], ["(ii)", "", "1"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n5(a) | A description that refers to any five of the following points |  | 5\nM1 add hydrogen peroxide to the conical flask and add catalyst |  | \nM2 start the timer |  | \nM3 record volume of gas produced in a given time/record the time for certain volume of gas to be produced |  | \nM4 repeat with same mass of a different catalyst |  | \nM5 and with same volume of hydrogen peroxide |  | \nM6 plot the results on a graph and calculate gradient (for each catalyst) |  | \nM7 the most effective catalyst gives the fastest rate of reaction OWTTE |  | \n(b)(i) | M1 a vertical line from the level of the hydrogen peroxide to the top of the curve labelled activation energy or $E_{\\mathrm{a}}$ | the line does not need an arrow head | 2\nM2 a vertical line from the level of the hydrogen peroxide to the level of the water and oxygen labelled enthalpy change or $\\Delta H$ | ALLOW double headed arrow\nREJECT arrow pointing down | \ncurve starting from hydrogen peroxide level, below the peak of the original curve, and ending at water and oxygen level | ALLOW double headed arrow\nREJECT arrow pointing up | \n(ii) |  | 1", "row_count": 13, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000028", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 28, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["6(a)(i)", "all points plotted ± half a square", "ALLOW line becomes horizontal as there is no copper sulfate solution left", "1"], ["curved line of best fit", "1"], ["An explanation that links the following two points\nM1 curve shows increasing mass (of negative electrode) because (more) copper deposits/forms\nM2 line becomes horizontal because there are no more copper(II) ions left in the solution/all the copper has been deposited/formed OWTTE", "2"], ["(b)(i)", "A description that refers to two of the following points", "ALLOW fill a test tube/measuring cylinder with water", "2"], ["M1 fill a test tube/measuring cylinder with copper sulfate solution", "2"], ["M2 place the tube over the positive electrode\nM3 collect gas/oxygen by displacement of solution/water"], ["(ii)", "2H2O→O2+4H++4e(-)", "M2 dep on M1", "2"], ["M1 all formulae correct"], ["M2 correct balancing of correct formulae"], ["(c)(i)", "M1 layers (of atoms or ions)", "ALLOW sheets/rows", "2"], ["M2 can slide over one another", "M2 dep on M1\nALLOW atoms/ions slide over each other for M2", "2"], ["M1 delocalised electrons", "IGNORE sea of electrons/free electrons"], ["(ii)", "M2 are free to move (throughout the structure)", "M2 dep on mention of electrons in M1", "0 marks if reference to ions moving"], ["", "0 marks if reference to ions moving"], ["Total for question = 12"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n6(a)(i) | all points plotted ± half a square | ALLOW line becomes horizontal as there is no copper sulfate solution left | 1\ncurved line of best fit | 1\nAn explanation that links the following two points\nM1 curve shows increasing mass (of negative electrode) because (more) copper deposits/forms\nM2 line becomes horizontal because there are no more copper(II) ions left in the solution/all the copper has been deposited/formed OWTTE | 2\n(b)(i) | A description that refers to two of the following points | ALLOW fill a test tube/measuring cylinder with water | 2\nM1 fill a test tube/measuring cylinder with copper sulfate solution | 2\nM2 place the tube over the positive electrode\nM3 collect gas/oxygen by displacement of solution/water\n(ii) | 2H2O→O2+4H++4e(-) | M2 dep on M1 | 2\nM1 all formulae correct\nM2 correct balancing of correct formulae\n(c)(i) | M1 layers (of atoms or ions) | ALLOW sheets/rows | 2\nM2 can slide over one another | M2 dep on M1\nALLOW atoms/ions slide over each other for M2 | 2\nM1 delocalised electrons | IGNORE sea of electrons/free electrons\n(ii) | M2 are free to move (throughout the structure) | M2 dep on mention of electrons in M1 | 0 marks if reference to ions moving\n | 0 marks if reference to ions moving\nTotal for question = 12", "row_count": 16, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000030", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 30, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["7(a)(i)", "Chemical structure of ethyl ethanoate", "REJECT any other atoms in the ring.", "1"], ["(ii) ethyl ethanoate", "ALLOW ethylethanoate", "1"], ["ALLOW ethyl acetate"], ["(iii) M1 add a metal such as magnesium, aluminium, zinc or ironM2 effervescence/bubbles/fizzing", "must be a named metal REJECT Group 1 metals", "2"], ["ACCEPT test gas with lighted splint which pops"], ["M2 dep on reference to adding a metal"], ["ORM1 add a carbonateM2 effervescence/bubbles/fizzing", "ACCEPT any named carbonateACCEPT test gas with limewater which goes cloudy/milkyM2 dep on M1"], ["(b)", "M1 C-O and one/two O-H bonds are broken and formedM2 so the same amount of energy is needed to break the bonds in the reactants as is given off when the bonds in the products are formed", "ALLOW the same bonds are broken and formed", "2"], ["ALLOW energy of bonds formed equals energy of bonds broken"], ["(c)(i)", "M1 the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the backward reactionM2 the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant", "", "2"], ["REJECT concentration of the reactants and products are the same"], ["(ii)M1 a catalyst increases the rate of forward and backward reactionsM2 equally", "M2 dep on M1", "2"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n7(a)(i) | Chemical structure of ethyl ethanoate | REJECT any other atoms in the ring. | 1\n(ii) ethyl ethanoate | ALLOW ethylethanoate | 1\nALLOW ethyl acetate\n(iii) M1 add a metal such as magnesium, aluminium, zinc or ironM2 effervescence/bubbles/fizzing | must be a named metal REJECT Group 1 metals | 2\nACCEPT test gas with lighted splint which pops\nM2 dep on reference to adding a metal\nORM1 add a carbonateM2 effervescence/bubbles/fizzing | ACCEPT any named carbonateACCEPT test gas with limewater which goes cloudy/milkyM2 dep on M1\n(b) | M1 C-O and one/two O-H bonds are broken and formedM2 so the same amount of energy is needed to break the bonds in the reactants as is given off when the bonds in the products are formed | ALLOW the same bonds are broken and formed | 2\nALLOW energy of bonds formed equals energy of bonds broken\n(c)(i) | M1 the rate of the forward reaction equals the rate of the backward reactionM2 the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant |  | 2\nREJECT concentration of the reactants and products are the same\n(ii)M1 a catalyst increases the rate of forward and backward reactionsM2 equally | M2 dep on M1 | 2", "row_count": 13, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000031", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 31, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["7(d)", "example calculation\nM1 moles barium hydroxide=(0.150×22.75)÷1000\nOR0.0034125\nM2 moles ethanoic acid=0.006825\nM3 moles ethanoic acid to 3 significant figures=0.00683", "M1 needs to be given to at least 3 sig figs\nALLOW M1×2\nALLOW answer to M2 to 3 significant figures.\ncorrect answer of0.00683 moles with or without working scores3 marks\nACCEPT answer in standard form\nACCEPT 0.00682 if M1 given to 3 sig figs\n6.83 with or without working scores2 marks", "3"], ["(e)", "correct answer scores2 marks\nChemical structure\nM1 three carbons from the dicarboxylic acid and two from the diol and the ester linkage\nM2 -OH lost from the dicarboxylic acid and -H lost from the diol", "ALLOW structure without extension bonds\nIGNORE brackets and n and +H2O\nALLOW -OH lost from diol and -H lost from dicarboxylic acid", "2"], ["Total for question=15"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n7(d) | example calculation\nM1 moles barium hydroxide=(0.150×22.75)÷1000\nOR0.0034125\nM2 moles ethanoic acid=0.006825\nM3 moles ethanoic acid to 3 significant figures=0.00683 | M1 needs to be given to at least 3 sig figs\nALLOW M1×2\nALLOW answer to M2 to 3 significant figures.\ncorrect answer of0.00683 moles with or without working scores3 marks\nACCEPT answer in standard form\nACCEPT 0.00682 if M1 given to 3 sig figs\n6.83 with or without working scores2 marks | 3\n(e) | correct answer scores2 marks\nChemical structure\nM1 three carbons from the dicarboxylic acid and two from the diol and the ester linkage\nM2 -OH lost from the dicarboxylic acid and -H lost from the diol | ALLOW structure without extension bonds\nIGNORE brackets and n and +H2O\nALLOW -OH lost from diol and -H lost from dicarboxylic acid | 2\nTotal for question=15", "row_count": 4, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000032", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 32, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}], "figures": [], "version": 1, "sections": [{"level": 2, "title": "Edexcel and BTEC Qualifications", "sectionId": "s001", "elementIds": ["e000007"], "pageNumber": 1}, {"level": 2, "title": "Pearson: helping people progress, everywhere", "sectionId": "s002", "elementIds": ["e000009", "e000010", "e000011", "e000012", "e000013", "e000014"], "pageNumber": 1}, {"level": 2, "title": "General Marking Guidance", "sectionId": "s003", "elementIds": ["e000016", "e000017", "e000018", "e000019", "e000020", "e000021", "e000022", "e000023", "e000029"], "pageNumber": 1}], "equations": [], "pageCount": 1, "documentId": "9047f3ba-c1ac-5e04-8bc1-08908601955a", "provenance": {"engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "application": "syllabai-parser", "extractedAt": "2026-09-13T13:02:33.723027338Z", "engineVersion": "1.1.0", "schemaVersion": "1.0", "extractionParams": {"pageBoundaries": "none-in-source", "sourceLineCount": 54, "upstreamConverter": "zai-glm-ocr", "entityDecodedLines": 0, "signedUrlFigureRefs": 0, "normalizedHtmlEntities": true}}, "textBlocks": [{"role": "paragraph", "text": "PMT", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000000", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 0, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Pearson Edexcel", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000001", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 1, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Mark Scheme (Results)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000002", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 2, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "January 2022", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000003", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 3, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Pearson Edexcel International GCSE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000004", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 4, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "In Chemistry (4CH1) Paper 2C", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000005", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 5, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "Edexcel and BTEC Qualifications", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000006", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 6, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Edexcel and BTEC qualifications are awarded by Pearson, the UK''s largest awarding body. We provide a wide range of qualifications including academic, vocational, occupational and specific programmes for employers. For further information visit our qualifications websites at www.edexcel.com or www.btec.co.uk. Alternatively, you can get in touch with us using the details on our contact us page at www.edexcel.com/contactus.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000007", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 7, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "Pearson: helping people progress, everywhere", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000008", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 8, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Pearson aspires to be the world''s leading learning company. Our aim is to help everyone progress in their lives through education. We believe in every kind of learning, for all kinds of people, wherever they are in the world. We''ve been involved in education for over 150 years, and by working across 70 countries, in 100 languages, we have built an international reputation for our commitment to high standards and raising achievement through innovation in education. Find out more about how we can help you and your students at: www.pearson.com/uk", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000009", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 9, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "January 2022", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000010", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 10, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Question Paper Log Number P70702A", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000011", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 11, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Publications Code 4CH1_2C_2201_MS", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000012", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 12, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "All the material in this publication is copyright", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000013", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 13, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "$ \\textcircled{c} $ Pearson Education Ltd 2022", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000014", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 14, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "General Marking Guidance", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000015", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 15, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "All candidates must receive the same treatment. Examiners must mark the first candidate in exactly the same way as they mark the last.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000016", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 16, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Mark schemes should be applied positively. Candidates must be rewarded for what they have shown they can do rather than penalised for omissions.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000017", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 17, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Examiners should mark according to the mark scheme not according to their perception of where the grade boundaries may lie.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000018", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 18, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "There is no ceiling on achievement. All marks on the mark scheme should be used appropriately.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000019", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 19, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "All the marks on the mark scheme are designed to be awarded. Examiners should always award full marks if deserved, i.e. if the answer matches the mark scheme. Examiners should also be prepared to award zero marks if the candidate''s response is not worthy of credit according to the mark scheme.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000020", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 20, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Where some judgement is required,mark schemes will provide the principles by which marks will be awarded and exemplification may be limited.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000021", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 21, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "When examiners are in doubt regarding the application of the mark scheme to a candidate''s response,the team leader must be consulted.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000022", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 22, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Crossed out work should be marked UNLESS the candidate has replaced it with an alternative response.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000023", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 23, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Total for question = 8", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000029", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 29, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}], "schemaVersion": "1.0"}', NULL, '2026-09-13 13:02:53.867812+00');
