{2 +} + 2 e ^ {-} \\rightarrow M g", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000084", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "equation", "reading_order": 84, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"text": "C = 40 \\% H = 6.7 \\% O = 53.3 \\%", "latex": "C = 40 \\% H = 6.7 \\% O = 53.3 \\%", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000089", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "equation", "reading_order": 89, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"text": "2 \\mathrm {H C O O H} + \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {C O} _ {3} \\rightarrow", "latex": "2 \\mathrm {H C O O H} + \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {C O} _ {3} \\rightarrow", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000094", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "equation", "reading_order": 94, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"text": "\\mathrm {H C O O H} + \\mathrm {C} _ {3} \\mathrm {H} _ {7} \\mathrm {O H} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {H C O O C} _ {3} \\mathrm {H} _ {7} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}", "latex": "\\mathrm {H C O O H} + \\mathrm {C} _ {3} \\mathrm {H} _ {7} \\mathrm {O H} \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {H C O O C} _ {3} \\mathrm {H} _ {7} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000097", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "equation", "reading_order": 97, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"text": "\\mathrm {T i O} _ {2} + 2 \\mathrm {C l} _ {2} + \\mathrm {C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {T i C l} _ {4} + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2}", "latex": "\\mathrm {T i O} _ {2} + 2 \\mathrm {C l} _ {2} + \\mathrm {C} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {T i C l} _ {4} + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2}", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000115", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "equation", "reading_order": 115, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"text": "[ 1 \\mathrm {t o n n e} = 1 0 ^ {6} \\mathrm {g} M _ {\\mathrm {r}} \\mathrm {o f} \\mathrm {T i O} _ {2} = 8 0 ]", "latex": "[ 1 \\mathrm {t o n n e} = 1 0 ^ {6} \\mathrm {g} M _ {\\mathrm {r}} \\mathrm {o f} \\mathrm {T i O} _ {2} = 8 0 ]", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000118", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "equation", "reading_order": 118, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"text": "\\mathrm {T i C l} _ {4} + 2 \\mathrm {M g} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {T i} + 2 \\mathrm {M g C l} _ {2}", "latex": "\\mathrm {T i C l} _ {4} + 2 \\mathrm {M g} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {T i} + 2 \\mathrm {M g C l} _ {2}", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000123", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "equation", "reading_order": 123, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"text": "\\mathrm {C H} _ {4} + 2 \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} \\quad \\Delta H = - 8 9 0 \\mathrm {k J / m o l}", "latex": "\\mathrm {C H} _ {4} + 2 \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} \\quad \\Delta H = - 8 9 0 \\mathrm {k J / m o l}", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000141", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "equation", "reading_order": 141, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"text": "\\mathrm {C} - \\mathrm {H} \\mathrm {b o n d} \\mathrm {e n e r g y} =", "latex": "\\mathrm {C} - \\mathrm {H} \\mathrm {b o n d} \\mathrm {e n e r g y} =", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000147", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "equation", "reading_order": 147, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}], "pageCount": 1, "documentId": "50791a3d-0583-5c34-91ae-6f68d3197e06", "provenance": {"engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "application": "syllabai-parser", "extractedAt": "2026-09-13T13:02:34.978400844Z", "engineVersion": "1.1.0", "schemaVersion": "1.0", "extractionParams": {"pageBoundaries": "none-in-source", "sourceLineCount": 203, "upstreamConverter": "zai-glm-ocr", "entityDecodedLines": 0, "signedUrlFigureRefs": 0, "normalizedHtmlEntities": true}}, "textBlocks": [{"role": "heading", "text": "Instructions", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000001", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 1, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Use black ink or ball-point pen.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000002", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 2, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Fill in the boxes at the top of this page with your name, centre number and candidate number.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000003", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 3, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Answer all questions.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000004", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 4, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Answer the questions in the spaces provided", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000005", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 5, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "there may be more space than you need.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000006", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 6, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Show all the steps in any calculations and state the units.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000007", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 7, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "Information", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000008", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 8, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "The total mark for this paper is 70.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000009", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 9, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "The marks for each question are shown in brackets", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000010", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 10, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "use this as a guide as to how much time to spend on each question.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000011", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 11, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "Advice", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000012", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 12, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Read each question carefully before you start to answer it.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000013", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 13, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Write your answers neatly and in good English.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000014", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 14, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Try to answer every question.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000015", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 15, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "list_item", "text": "Check your answers if you have time at the end.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000016", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 16, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Turn over", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000017", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 17, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "$ ^{*} $ The lanthanoids (atomic numbers 58-71) and the actinoids (atomic numbers 90-103) have been omitted.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000019", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 19, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The relative atomic masses of copper and chlorine have not been rounded to the nearest whole number.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000020", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 20, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "Answer ALL questions.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000021", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 21, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Some questions must be answered with a cross in a box. If you change your mind about an answer, put a line through the box and then mark your new answer with a cross.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000022", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 22, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "1 This question is about gases in the atmosphere.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000023", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 23, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The box gives the names of some gases.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000024", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 24, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) Choose gases from the box to answer these questions.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000026", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 26, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Identify the least reactive gas in the atmosphere.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000027", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 27, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Identify the most abundant gas in the atmosphere.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000028", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 28, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) Identify the gas that is not normally found in the atmosphere.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000029", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 29, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) State an environmental problem caused by increasing amounts of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000030", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 30, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) Describe a test for carbon dioxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000031", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 31, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 1 = 6 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000032", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 32, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "2 (a) The diagram shows two pieces of apparatus, X and Y.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000033", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 33, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Y", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000036", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 36, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Give the name of each piece of apparatus.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000037", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 37, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) In a titration, a student adds $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of barium hydroxide solution to a conical flask.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000038", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 38, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Give a reason why it is better to use Y rather than X.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000039", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 39, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) The student uses methyl orange indicator in the titration.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000040", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 40, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) State the colour of methyl orange in barium hydroxide solution.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000041", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 41, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Give a reason why universal indicator is not suitable for use in a titration.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000042", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 42, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) The student adds some dilute nitric acid to a burette and does the titration.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000043", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 43, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The equation for the reaction is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000044", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 44, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The student finds that $ 2 1. 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of nitric acid of concentration $ 0. 6 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ neutralises $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of barium hydroxide solution.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000046", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 46, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Calculate the concentration, in mol/dm $ ^{3} $ , of the barium hydroxide solution.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000047", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 47, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(d) State why sulfuric acid would not be a suitable acid to use in this titration.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000049", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 49, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 2 = 9 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000050", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 50, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "3 This question is about Group 7,the halogens.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000051", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 51, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) Which halogen has the palest colour?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000052", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 52, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A astatine", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000053", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 53, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "B bromine", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000054", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 54, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "fluorine", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000055", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 55, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "D iodine", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000056", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 56, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) Which halogen is a solid at room temperature?", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000057", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 57, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "A astatine", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000058", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 58, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "chlorine", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000059", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 59, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "B bromine", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000060", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 60, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "D fluorine", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000061", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 61, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) The table shows the electronic configurations of a fluorine atom and a chlorine atom.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000062", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 62, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Explain the relative reactivities of fluorine and chlorine using the information in the table.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000064", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 64, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(d) Lithium reacts with chlorine to form lithium chloride.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000065", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 65, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Write a chemical equation for the reaction of lithium with chlorine.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000066", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 66, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Describe tests to show that the product of the reaction is lithium chloride.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000067", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 67, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "4 This question is about magnesium and magnesium compounds.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000068", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 68, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) Magnesium burns in oxygen to form magnesium oxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000069", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 69, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "State two observations that would be seen during the reaction.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000070", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 70, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) The diagram shows the electron configurations of a magnesium atom and an oxygen atom.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000071", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 71, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "magnesium atom", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000073", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 73, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "oxygen atom", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000075", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 75, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Describe the changes in the electronic configurations when magnesium reacts with oxygen to form the ionic compound magnesium oxide, MgO", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000076", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 76, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) Magnesium can be produced by the electrolysis of molten magnesium chloride.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000077", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 77, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) State why magnesium cannot be produced by heating magnesium oxide with carbon.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000078", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 78, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Explain the different ways that magnesium and magnesium chloride conduct electricity.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000079", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 79, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "magnesium", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000080", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 80, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "magnesium chloride", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000081", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 81, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(d) During the electrolysis of molten magnesium chloride, magnesium is formed at the negative electrode.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000082", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 82, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The ionic half-equation for the reaction is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000083", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 83, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) State why this is a reduction reaction.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000085", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 85, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Write an ionic half-equation for the formation of chlorine at the positive electrode.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000086", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 86, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 4 = 11 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000087", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 87, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "5 (a) An organic compound has this percentage composition by mass.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000088", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 88, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Show that the empirical formula of the compound is $ \\mathrm{C H_{2} O} $", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000090", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 90, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Draw the structural formula of a compound with the molecular formula $ \\mathrm{C_{2} H_{4}O_{2}} $", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000091", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 91, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) Methanoic acid (HCOOH) reacts with sodium carbonate solution to give three products.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000092", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 92, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Complete the equation for this reaction.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000093", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 93, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) State what you would observe in this reaction.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000095", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 95, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) Methanoic acid also reacts with propanol to form an ester.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000096", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 96, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Give the name of the ester that forms.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000098", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 98, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The equation for the reaction is", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000099", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 99, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) State what is meant by the $ \\rightleftharpoons $ symbol.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000100", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 100, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(iii) When this reaction occurs in a sealed container, the reaction can reach dynamic equilibrium.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000101", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 101, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Give one characteristic of a reaction at dynamic equilibrium.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000102", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 102, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(d) A polyester forms when butanedioic acid reacts with ethanediol.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000103", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 103, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The diagram shows the repeat unit of the polyester that forms.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000104", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 104, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Give the name of this type of polymerisation.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000106", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 106, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Draw the structural formulae of the two monomers used to make this polyester.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000107", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 107, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 5 = 12 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000108", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 108, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "BLANK PAGE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000109", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 109, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "6 Titanium is an important metal in industry.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000110", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 110, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Titanium dioxide $ \\left( \\mathrm{T i O}_{2} \\right) $ can be converted into titanium metal in two stages.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000111", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 111, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Stage 1 titanium dioxide is converted into titanium(IV) chloride $ \\left( \\mathrm{T i C l}_{4} \\right) $", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000112", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 112, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Stage 2 titanium(IV) chloride is converted into titanium", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000113", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 113, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) This is the equation for the reaction in stage 1.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000114", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 114, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Calculate the volume, in $ \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ of chlorine gas at rtp needed to react completely with 20 tonnes of titanium dioxide.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000116", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 116, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Give your answer in standard form.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000117", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 117, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "[molar volume of chlorine gas at rtp=24 dm $ ^{3} $]", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000119", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 119, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) In stage 2, titanium(IV) chloride vapour is passed through molten magnesium in a container filled with argon.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000121", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 121, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "This is the equation for the reaction in stage 2.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000122", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 122, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Explain why the container is filled with argon rather than air.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000124", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 124, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) Aeroplanes are made of an alloy containing aluminium and titanium.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000125", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 125, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Explain why the alloy is stronger than pure titanium metal.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000126", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 126, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "You may include diagrams in your answer.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000127", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 127, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 6 = 9 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000128", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 128, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "BLANK PAGE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000129", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 129, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "7 A student uses this apparatus to find the heat energy supplied by a liquid fuel.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000130", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 130, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The student burns some fuel to heat the water in the copper container and measures the change in temperature.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000132", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 132, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(a) The student notices that the bottom of the container turns black.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000133", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 133, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Give the name of the black substance that forms on the bottom of the container.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000134", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 134, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(b) In one experiment, the student burns 0.92 g of ethanol.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000135", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 135, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The student calculates that the heat energy absorbed by the water is 18.2 kJ.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000136", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 136, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "Show that the results of this experiment give an approximate value for the enthalpy of combustion of ethanol of $ \\Delta H=-9 0 0 \\mathrm{k J / m o l}. $", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000137", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 137, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "[M $ _{r} $ of ethanol=46]", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000138", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 138, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(c) The data book value of $ \\Delta H $ for the combustion of ethanol is $ -1 3 6 7 \\mathrm{k J / m o l} $ Give two reasons why the student''s value is much lower than the data book value.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000139", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 139, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(d) The equation shows the combustion of methane.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000140", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 140, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "This is the equation showing the displayed formulae.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000142", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 142, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "The table shows the bond energies for O=O, C=O and O—H", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000144", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 144, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(i) Calculate the bond energy of the C—H bond, using information from the equation and the table.", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000146", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 146, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(ii) Complete the energy level diagram to show the products and $ \\Delta H. $", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000148", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 148, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "(Total for Question 7 = 11 marks)", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000150", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 150, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "paragraph", "text": "TOTAL FOR PAPER = 70 MARKS", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000151", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": null, "reading_order": 151, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"role": "heading", "text": "BLANK PAGE", "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000152", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "element_type": "text_block", "heading_level": 2, "reading_order": 152, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}], "schemaVersion": "1.0"}', NULL, '2026-09-13 13:02:54.154346+00');
INSERT INTO public.documents VALUES ('afe14601-2b77-46a1-a29b-2c52df209520', '5ff5aef7-21db-5ed9-bfc3-c48add89d7db', '1.0', 1, 'MARK_SCHEME', 'corpus/igcse-chemistry-4ch0-2c-2022jun/MS.md', 'MS.md', 'text/markdown', '111f670feae5dd7a0020408425cede2552b6f80b11944af06f5d2fdbd8f7eef3', 'SHA-256', 1, 33, 33, 11, 'glm-ocr-markdown', '1.1.0', '2026-09-13 13:02:35.031131+00', '{"pages": [{"width": null, "height": null, "pageNumber": 1}], "source": {"uri": "corpus/igcse-chemistry-4ch0-2c-2022jun/MS.md", "checksum": "111f670feae5dd7a0020408425cede2552b6f80b11944af06f5d2fdbd8f7eef3", "fileName": "MS.md", "mimeType": "text/markdown", "checksumAlgorithm": "SHA-256"}, "tables": [{"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["1", "(a)(i)", "argon", "ALLOW Ar", "1"], ["(ii)", "nitrogen", "ALLOW N2/N", "1"], ["(iii)", "hydrogen", "ALLOW H2/H", "1"], ["(b)", "climate change/global warming/oceans becoming more acidic", "ALLOW greenhouse effectALLOW effects of global warming e.g. melting of polar ice caps/flooding/wild firesIGNORE acid rainREJECT references to ozone layer", "1"], ["(c)", "M1 bubble/pass/add the gas/carbon dioxide into limewaterM2(limewater) turns cloudy/milky", "ALLOW white precipitateM2 dep on mention of limewaterREJECT addition of extra reagents for both marks", "2"], ["", "", "", "Total 6"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n1 | (a)(i) | argon | ALLOW Ar | 1\n(ii) | nitrogen | ALLOW N2/N | 1\n(iii) | hydrogen | ALLOW H2/H | 1\n(b) | climate change/global warming/oceans becoming more acidic | ALLOW greenhouse effectALLOW effects of global warming e.g. melting of polar ice caps/flooding/wild firesIGNORE acid rainREJECT references to ozone layer | 1\n(c) | M1 bubble/pass/add the gas/carbon dioxide into limewaterM2(limewater) turns cloudy/milky | ALLOW white precipitateM2 dep on mention of limewaterREJECT addition of extra reagents for both marks | 2\n |  |  | Total 6", "row_count": 7, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000023", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 23, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["2(a)(i)(ii)", "M1(X) measuring cylinder", "", "2"], ["M2(Y) pipette", "ALLOW graduated pipette", ""], ["(volume measurement with Y is) more precise ORA", "ALLOW(volume measurement with Y is) more accurate ORA\nALLOW(Y gives) a(more) exact volume/exactly 25cm3", "1"], ["(b)(i)(ii)", "yellow", "", "1"], ["there is no clear end point/colour change is gradual(at the end point)/no sharp colour change OWTTE", "ALLOWit has a range of colours", "1"], ["(c)", "M1moles ofHNO3=21.5×0.6(00)1000", "correct answer with or without working scores3", "3"], ["M2moles ofBa(OH)2=0.0129÷2OR0.00645", "ALLOWecf onM2", ""], ["M3conc.ofBa(OH)2=0.00645×1000=0.258(mol/dm3)25", "ACCEPT alternative methods\n0.516 scores2\n1.032 scores2\nALLOW2 sig figs correctly rounded throughout\nPenalise rounding to1 sig fig once only", ""], ["barium sulfate is insoluble/does not dissolve/forms a precipitate", "", "1"], ["", "", "Total9"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n2(a)(i)(ii) | M1(X) measuring cylinder |  | 2\nM2(Y) pipette | ALLOW graduated pipette | \n(volume measurement with Y is) more precise ORA | ALLOW(volume measurement with Y is) more accurate ORA\nALLOW(Y gives) a(more) exact volume/exactly 25cm3 | 1\n(b)(i)(ii) | yellow |  | 1\nthere is no clear end point/colour change is gradual(at the end point)/no sharp colour change OWTTE | ALLOWit has a range of colours | 1\n(c) | M1moles ofHNO3=21.5×0.6(00)1000 | correct answer with or without working scores3 | 3\nM2moles ofBa(OH)2=0.0129÷2OR0.00645 | ALLOWecf onM2 | \nM3conc.ofBa(OH)2=0.00645×1000=0.258(mol/dm3)25 | ACCEPT alternative methods\n0.516 scores2\n1.032 scores2\nALLOW2 sig figs correctly rounded throughout\nPenalise rounding to1 sig fig once only | \nbarium sulfate is insoluble/does not dissolve/forms a precipitate |  | 1\n |  | Total9", "row_count": 11, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000024", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 24, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["3(a)", "C fluorineA is incorrect as astatine is blackB is incorrect as bromine is brownD is incorrect as iodine is dark grey", "", "1"], ["(b)", "A astatineB is incorrect as bromine is a liquidC is incorrect as chlorine is a gasD is incorrect as fluorine is a gas", "", "1"], ["(c)", "An explanation that links the following four pointsM1 fluorine is more reactive than chlorine ORA M2 the outer shell is closer to the nucleus in fluorine/fluorine has fewer shells/fluorine has a smaller atomic radius ORA M3 there is a stronger attraction to the nucleus for an electron in fluorine ORA M4 so fluorine accepts an electron more readily ORA", "ALLOW reactivity decreases down the group ORAALLOW a fluorine atom is smaller than a chlorine atom ORAALLOW there is less shielding in fluorine ORA", "4"], ["(d)(i)", "2Li+Cl2→2LiCl", "ALLOW multiples or fractionsIGNORE state symbols even if incorrectACCEPT2Li+Cl-REJECT any charges on Li or Cl2", "1"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n3(a) | C fluorineA is incorrect as astatine is blackB is incorrect as bromine is brownD is incorrect as iodine is dark grey |  | 1\n(b) | A astatineB is incorrect as bromine is a liquidC is incorrect as chlorine is a gasD is incorrect as fluorine is a gas |  | 1\n(c) | An explanation that links the following four pointsM1 fluorine is more reactive than chlorine ORA M2 the outer shell is closer to the nucleus in fluorine/fluorine has fewer shells/fluorine has a smaller atomic radius ORA M3 there is a stronger attraction to the nucleus for an electron in fluorine ORA M4 so fluorine accepts an electron more readily ORA | ALLOW reactivity decreases down the group ORAALLOW a fluorine atom is smaller than a chlorine atom ORAALLOW there is less shielding in fluorine ORA | 4\n(d)(i) | 2Li+Cl2→2LiCl | ALLOW multiples or fractionsIGNORE state symbols even if incorrectACCEPT2Li+Cl-REJECT any charges on Li or Cl2 | 1", "row_count": 5, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000025", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 25, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["(ii)", "A description that refers to the following five points\nTest for lithium ions\nM1 flame test\nM2 red (flame)\nTest for chloride ions\nM3 add nitric acid\nM4 add silver nitrate (solution)\nM5 white precipitate", "ACCEPT description of flame test\nALLOW crimson/scarlet\nREJECT brick red/orange red\nM2 dep on M1\nREJECT incorrect acid e.g. HCl or H2SO4 for M3 only\nALLOW acidified silver nitrate for M3 and M4\nM5 dep on addition of silver nitrate", "5"], ["", "", "", "Total 12"]], "text": "(ii) | A description that refers to the following five points\nTest for lithium ions\nM1 flame test\nM2 red (flame)\nTest for chloride ions\nM3 add nitric acid\nM4 add silver nitrate (solution)\nM5 white precipitate | ACCEPT description of flame test\nALLOW crimson/scarlet\nREJECT brick red/orange red\nM2 dep on M1\nREJECT incorrect acid e.g. HCl or H2SO4 for M3 only\nALLOW acidified silver nitrate for M3 and M4\nM5 dep on addition of silver nitrate | 5\n |  |  | Total 12", "row_count": 2, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000026", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 26, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["4(a)", "M1 bright/white light OR bright/white flame", "", "2"], ["M2 white powder/solid/ash", "ALLOW white smoke\nALLOW grey powder/solid/ash\nREJECT white precipitate", "2"], ["(b)", "A description that refers to the following two points", "", ""], ["M1 magnesium/Mg loses two electrons/becomes 2.8\nM2 oxygen/O gains two electrons/becomes 2.8", "ACCEPT magnesium gives two electrons to oxygen for M1 and M2\nBoth marks can be scored from diagrams showing correct electronic configurations of the ions.", ""], ["(c)(i)", "magnesium is more reactive/higher in the reactivity series (than carbon)/magnesium is a better reducing agent (than carbon) ORA", "ALLOW carbon cannot displace magnesium", "1"], ["An explanation that links the following four points", "", "4"], ["(ii)", "M1 (magnesium) has delocalised electrons", "REJECT reference to ions or atoms moving for M2\nions are free to move when (magnesium chloride) is molten/in solution OR (magnesium chloride) cannot conduct when solid", ""], ["M2 electrons can move\nM3 (magnesium chloride) can only conduct when molten/in solution OR (magnesium chloride) cannot conduct when solid\nM4 ions are free to move", "REJECT reference to electrons moving for M4", ""], ["(d)(i)", "magnesium ions/Mg2+ gains electrons", "ALLOW electrons are gained", "1"], ["2Cl-→Cl2+2e(-)", "REJECT magnesium/Mg gains electrons\nREJECT reference to loss or gain of oxygen", ""], ["(ii)", "", "ALLOW 2Cl-→2e(-)→Cl2", "1"], ["", "ALLOW multiples or fractions\nIGNORE state symbols even if incorrect", ""], ["", "", "", "Total 11"]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n4(a) | M1 bright/white light OR bright/white flame |  | 2\nM2 white powder/solid/ash | ALLOW white smoke\nALLOW grey powder/solid/ash\nREJECT white precipitate | 2\n(b) | A description that refers to the following two points |  | \nM1 magnesium/Mg loses two electrons/becomes 2.8\nM2 oxygen/O gains two electrons/becomes 2.8 | ACCEPT magnesium gives two electrons to oxygen for M1 and M2\nBoth marks can be scored from diagrams showing correct electronic configurations of the ions. | \n(c)(i) | magnesium is more reactive/higher in the reactivity series (than carbon)/magnesium is a better reducing agent (than carbon) ORA | ALLOW carbon cannot displace magnesium | 1\nAn explanation that links the following four points |  | 4\n(ii) | M1 (magnesium) has delocalised electrons | REJECT reference to ions or atoms moving for M2\nions are free to move when (magnesium chloride) is molten/in solution OR (magnesium chloride) cannot conduct when solid | \nM2 electrons can move\nM3 (magnesium chloride) can only conduct when molten/in solution OR (magnesium chloride) cannot conduct when solid\nM4 ions are free to move | REJECT reference to electrons moving for M4 | \n(d)(i) | magnesium ions/Mg2+ gains electrons | ALLOW electrons are gained | 1\n2Cl-→Cl2+2e(-) | REJECT magnesium/Mg gains electrons\nREJECT reference to loss or gain of oxygen | \n(ii) |  | ALLOW 2Cl-→2e(-)→Cl2 | 1\n | ALLOW multiples or fractions\nIGNORE state symbols even if incorrect | \n |  |  | Total 11", "row_count": 14, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000027", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 27, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["Question number", "Answer", "Notes", "Marks"], ["5", "(a)(i)", "M1", "40.0/12", "6.7/1", "53.3/16", "0 marks for division by atomic numbers or upside-down calculation", "2"], ["M2", "3.33", "6.7", "3.33", "ALLOW any number of sig figs except 1", ""], ["(ii)", "AND1", "", "ACCEPT alternative methods", ""], ["CH3COOHORH-C-C", "ACCEPT HCOOCH3ORH-C-C", "1"], ["(b)(i)", "(i)", "2HCOOH+Na2CO3→2HCOONa+CO2+H2O", "", "2"], ["M1 CO2+H2O", "IGNORE numbers in front of CO2 and/or H2O if only M1 scored", ""], ["M2 HCOONa and equation correctly balanced", "REJECT NaCOOHALLOW NaHCOO", ""], ["(ii)", "(ii)", "bubbles/ fizzing/ effervescence", "IGNORE gas given offALLOW sodium carbonate disappears/dissolves", "1"], ["propyl methanoate", "spelling must be correctALLOW propyl formate", "1"], ["(ii)", "(ii)", "reversible reaction", "ALLOW reaction which goes both waysIGNORE equilibrium", "1"], ["", "", ""]], "text": "Question number | Answer | Notes | Marks\n5 | (a)(i) | M1 | 40.0/12 | 6.7/1 | 53.3/16 | 0 marks for division by atomic numbers or upside-down calculation | 2\nM2 | 3.33 | 6.7 | 3.33 | ALLOW any number of sig figs except 1 | \n(ii) | AND1 |  | ACCEPT alternative methods | \nCH3COOHORH-C-C | ACCEPT HCOOCH3ORH-C-C | 1\n(b)(i) | (i) | 2HCOOH+Na2CO3→2HCOONa+CO2+H2O |  | 2\nM1 CO2+H2O | IGNORE numbers in front of CO2 and/or H2O if only M1 scored | \nM2 HCOONa and equation correctly balanced | REJECT NaCOOHALLOW NaHCOO | \n(ii) | (ii) | bubbles/ fizzing/ effervescence | IGNORE gas given offALLOW sodium carbonate disappears/dissolves | 1\npropyl methanoate | spelling must be correctALLOW propyl formate | 1\n(ii) | (ii) | reversible reaction | ALLOW reaction which goes both waysIGNORE equilibrium | 1\n |  | ", "row_count": 12, "confidence": 1.0, "element_id": "e000028", "page_number": 1, "bounding_box": null, "column_count": 4, "element_type": "table", "reading_order": 28, "source_engine": "glm-ocr-markdown", "source_engine_version": "1.1.0"}, {"rows": [["(iii)", "forward and backward reactions occur at the same rate OWTTE\nOR\nconcentrations of reactants and products remain constant/stay the same/do not change", "1"], ["", "", "ALLOW\namounts/moles/ratios of reactants and products remain constant\nREJECT concentrations of reactants and products are equal/the same"], ["(d)(i)(ii)", "condensation (polymerisation)", "1\n2"], ["", "M1\nHO-C-CH2CH2-C-OH", "M2\nHO-CH2CH2-OH", ""], ["", "", "", "Total 12"]], "text": "(iii) | forward and backward reactions occur at the same rate OWTTE\nOR\nconcentrations of reactants and products remain constant/stay the same/do not change | 1\n |  | ALLOW\namounts/moles/ratios of reactants and products remain constant\nREJECT concentrations of reactants and products are equal/the same\n(d)(i)(ii) | condensation (polymerisation) | 1\n2\n | M1\nHO-C-CH2CH2-C-OH | M2\nHO-CH2CH2-OH | \n |  |  | Total 12", "row_co