(Total for Question 2 = 6 marks)
3 Lead(II) sulfate, $ \mathrm{P b S O_{4}} $ , is an insoluble salt.
It can be made as a precipitate from a solution of lead(II) nitrate, $ \mathrm{P b ( N O_{3} )_{2}} $
(a) (i) Identify a substance that could be added to lead(II) nitrate solution to form a precipitate of lead(II) sulfate.
(ii) Write a chemical equation for the reaction between lead(II) nitrate and the substance you identified in (a)(i).
(iii) Outline how you would produce a pure, dry sample of lead(II) sulfate from the reaction mixture in (a)(ii).
(b) A solution of lead(II) nitrate can be made by reacting solid lead(II) carbonate with dilute nitric acid.
The equation for this reaction is:', 1, 1, '["e000044", "e000045", "e000046", "e000047", "e000048", "e000049", "e000050", "e000051", "e000052", "e000053", "e000054", "e000055", "e000056", "e000057", "e000058", "e000059", "e000060", "e000061", "e000062", "e000063", "e000064"]', 283, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-13 12:57:41.870904+00');
INSERT INTO public.document_chunks VALUES ('ff01aa9e-4747-457c-aa74-7dad3d86c9e4', '0cd9f7fd-1fcb-4880-b246-e0b945f01812', 4, '\mathrm {P b C O} _ {3} (\mathrm {s}) + 2 \mathrm {H N O} _ {3} (\mathrm {a q}) \rightarrow \mathrm {P b} (\mathrm {N O} _ {3}) _ {2} (\mathrm {a q}) + \mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\mathrm {g}) + \mathrm {H} _ {2} \mathrm {O} (\mathrm {l})
State two observations you would make when dilute nitric acid is added to solid lead(II) carbonate.
(Total for Question 3 = 8 marks)
4 When magnesium is burned in air, it reacts with oxygen, $ \mathrm{O_{2}} $ , to form magnesium oxide, MgO A class of students investigated the relationship between the mass of magnesium burned and the mass of magnesium oxide formed.
Each student was given a different mass of clean magnesium to heat.
The students used the following method.
Weigh a crucible and lid
Place the magnesium ribbon in the crucible, replace the lid, and reweigh
Heat the crucible as shown in the diagram until the magnesium burns
Lift the lid from time to time until there is no sign of further reaction
Allow the crucible and lid to cool and reweigh
Repeat the heating, cooling and reweighing until two consecutive masses are the same
Calculate the mass of magnesium oxide formed
(a) (i) Why is it necessary to lift the lid from time to time while heating?', 1, 1, '["e000065", "e000066", "e000067", "e000068", "e000069", "e000070", "e000071", "e000072", "e000073", "e000075", "e000076", "e000077", "e000078", "e000079"]', 300, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-13 12:57:41.871056+00');
INSERT INTO public.document_chunks VALUES ('8505dd0b-cbcb-461f-af0a-f45bd9d1c56d', '0cd9f7fd-1fcb-4880-b246-e0b945f01812', 5, '(ii) Why is it necessary to repeat the heating until two consecutive masses are the same?
(1)
(b) Show how the mass of magnesium oxide formed can be calculated from the readings obtained.
(c) The results of each experiment are given in the table.
Mass of magnesium in g | Mass of magnesium oxide in g
0.24 | 0.40
0.26 | 0.64
0.42 | 0.70
0.62 | 1.04
0.70 | 1.20
0.80 | 1.33
(i) Plot the results on the grid and draw a straight line of best fit.
(ii) Draw a circle around the anomalous result.
(iii) Use your graph to find the mass of magnesium oxide formed when 0.48 g of magnesium is burned.
5 The apparatus shown can be used to electrolyse aqueous sodium chloride in the laboratory.
(a) Gases are evolved at both electrodes.
(i) Describe a chemical test to show that the gas evolved at the positive electrode is chlorine.
(ii) Identify gas A.
(b) Some of the solution formed after the electrolysis was tested with the indicator phenolphthalein. The indicator turned pink Explain this result.
(c) The equation for the reaction taking place at the positive electrode is:
2 \mathrm {C l} ^ {-} (\mathrm {a q}) \rightarrow \mathrm {C l} _ {2} (\mathrm {g}) + 2 \mathrm {e} ^ {-}', 1, 1, '["e000080", "e000081", "e000082", "e000083", "e000084", "e000085", "e000087", "e000088", "e000089", "e000091", "e000092", "e000093", "e000094", "e000095", "e000096"]', 296, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-13 12:57:41.871215+00');
INSERT INTO public.document_chunks VALUES ('c6902f64-1200-4feb-acb5-6268f1fad059', '0cd9f7fd-1fcb-4880-b246-e0b945f01812', 6, 'Ten faradays (10 F) of electricity were passed through an aqueous solution of sodium chloride.
(i) Calculate the amount, in moles, of chlorine formed.
(ii) Calculate the volume of chlorine formed.
(One mole of a gas occupies $ 2 4 \mathrm{~ d m}^{3} $ at this temperature and pressure)
6 Compound X is a blue, crystalline solid. It contains copper(II) ions $ \mathrm{(C u^{2+})} $ , sulfate ions $ \mathrm{(S O_{4}^{2-})} $ and water of crystallisation.
(a) A student dissolved some of compound X in water and then added aqueous sodium hydroxide solution. She obtained a blue precipitate.
Give the formula of the blue precipitate formed in the reaction.
(b) Another student tested a solution of compound X for sulfate ions using dilute hydrochloric acid, followed by a few drops of barium chloride solution. She obtained a white precipitate.
Why is the dilute hydrochloric acid necessary in this test?
(c) The empirical formula of compound X is $ \mathrm{C u S O_{9} H_{1 0}} $ Write the formula of compound X to show its water of crystallisation.
(d) Compound X gives a blue-green colour in a flame test.
Outline how you would carry out a flame test.
(Total for Question 6 = 5 marks)', 1, 1, '["e000097", "e000098", "e000099", "e000100", "e000101", "e000102", "e000103", "e000104", "e000105", "e000106", "e000107", "e000108", "e000109"]', 298, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-13 12:57:41.87135+00');
