3 A student added some pieces of iron to a boiling tube containing dilute hydrochloric acid. She observed fizzing and the formation of a solution, X.
(a) Identify the gas that causes the fizzing and describe a test for it.
(b) Solution X contains chloride ions.
(i) The student confirmed this by adding some silver nitrate solution. She observed a white precipitate of silver chloride.
Give the formula of the white precipitate, and name the other solution she should have added before the silver nitrate solution.
Formula of white precipitate.
Other solution.
(ii) Complete the word equation for the reaction in this test.
iron chloride + silver nitrate $ \rightarrow $ silver chloride +', 1, 1, '["e000037", "e000038", "e000039", "e000040", "e000041", "e000042", "e000043", "e000044", "e000045", "e000046", "e000047", "e000048", "e000049"]', 254, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-13 12:58:26.455887+00');
INSERT INTO public.document_chunks VALUES ('47833876-4cf0-4fea-84c3-f3cf7577e778', '5cabf8fa-9227-4c90-aca6-416b54eeb726', 3, '(c) Solution X also contains ions of iron. The student thought that these ions had the formula $ \mathrm{F e}^{2+} $ or $ \mathrm{F e}^{3+} $ What reagent should she add to decide whether solution X contains $ \mathrm{F e}^{2+} $ or $ \mathrm{F e}^{3+} $ ions? State the result of the test in each case.
Reagent...
Result with $ \mathrm{F e}^{2+} $ ions...
Result with $ \mathrm{F e}^{3+} $ ions...
(Total for Question 3 = 8 marks)
4 A teacher added some of the Group 1 elements to separate samples of water.
(a) State two observations that could be made when a small piece of sodium is added to a large trough containing water.
(b) In another experiment she added a small piece of a different Group 1 element and noticed that the reaction was less vigorous.
Which element did she add in this experiment?
(c) In another experiment she added a small piece of potassium to a large trough containing water. This time she observed a lilac flame.
(i) Identify the gas that burned.
(ii) Give the formula of the ion that caused the flame to be lilac.', 1, 1, '["e000050", "e000051", "e000052", "e000053", "e000054", "e000055", "e000056", "e000057", "e000058", "e000059", "e000060", "e000061"]', 263, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-13 12:58:26.456084+00');
INSERT INTO public.document_chunks VALUES ('a99de807-87df-47d8-b04c-ce44cda4f1dd', '5cabf8fa-9227-4c90-aca6-416b54eeb726', 4, '(d) When the Group 1 elements react with water, each of their atoms loses an electron from its outer shell. For sodium and potassium, these processes can be represented by the equations
\begin{array}{l} \cdot \mathrm {N a} \rightarrow \mathrm {N a} ^ {+} + \mathrm {e} ^ {-} \\ \cdot \mathrm {K} \rightarrow \mathrm {K} ^ {+} + \mathrm {e} ^ {-} \\ \end{array}
Explain, by referring to the electronic configurations of sodium and potassium, why potassium is more reactive than sodium.
(Total for Question 4 = 9 marks)
5 Fractional distillation and cracking are important steps in processing crude oil.
(a) Place ticks ( $ \surd $ ) in the columns to show which statements apply to each step. You may place a tick in one column, in both columns or in neither column.
The first one has been done for you.
Statement | Fractional distillation | Cracking
Crude oil is heated | √ | 
A catalyst may be used |  | 
Alkenes are formed |  | 
Decomposition reactions occur |  | 
Fuels are obtained |  | 
Separation is the main purpose |  |
(b) The formula $ \mathrm{C H_{3} C H_{2} C H_{2} C H_{2} C H_{3}} $ represents one of the compounds in crude oil.
(i) Give the molecular formula of this compound.', 1, 1, '["e000062", "e000063", "e000064", "e000065", "e000066", "e000067", "e000068", "e000069", "e000070", "e000071"]', 298, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-13 12:58:26.456291+00');
INSERT INTO public.document_chunks VALUES ('1de7a8ea-cfbc-41c3-ad67-820ae9bf09ca', '5cabf8fa-9227-4c90-aca6-416b54eeb726', 5, '(ii) Give the displayed formula of this compound.
(iii) Give the empirical formula of this compound.
