magnesium ribbon added | bubbles | no change
blue litmus paper added | goes red | stays blue', 1, 1, '["e000046", "e000047", "e000048", "e000049", "e000050", "e000051", "e000052", "e000053", "e000054", "e000055", "e000056", "e000057", "e000058", "e000059", "e000060"]', 291, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-13 12:58:26.574225+00');
INSERT INTO public.document_chunks VALUES ('c891ce4c-9b5b-42e7-aa29-0ba71d5a2157', 'ac8055f3-157b-49a8-987d-93d1e8a76016', 5, '(i) What substance is responsible for the bubbles?
(ii) State one change to the magnesium ribbon that could be seen after adding it to solution X.
(iii) What does the colour change of the litmus paper show about solution X?
(iv) Why does the litmus paper stay blue in solution Y?
(Total for Question 2 = 12 marks)
3 Tungsten is a useful metal. It has the chemical symbol W.
(a) One method of extracting tungsten involves heating a tungsten compound $ \mathrm{(W O_{3})} $ with hydrogen.
(i) Suggest the chemical name of $ \mathrm{W O}_{3} $
(ii) Balance the equation for the reaction between $ \mathrm{W O}_{3} $ and hydrogen.
\mathrm {W O} _ {3} + \dots \dots
(iii) Why is this reaction described as reduction?
(b) Scheelite is an ore of tungsten.
The main compound in scheelite has the percentage composition by mass $ \mathrm{C a}=1 3. 9 \% $ $ \mathrm{W}=6 3. 9 \% $ $ \mathrm{O}=2 2. 2 \% $
Calculate the empirical formula of this compound.
(c) Tungsten can also be obtained by reacting tungsten fluoride with hydrogen.
The equation for this reaction is
\mathrm {W F} _ {6} + 3 \mathrm {H} _ {2} \rightarrow \mathrm {W} + 6 \mathrm {H F}', 1, 1, '["e000061", "e000062", "e000063", "e000064", "e000065", "e000066", "e000067", "e000068", "e000069", "e000070", "e000071", "e000072", "e000073", "e000074", "e000075", "e000076", "e000077"]', 289, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-13 12:58:26.574403+00');
INSERT INTO public.document_chunks VALUES ('66622534-925f-41ce-ac64-e5b853de3fa5', 'ac8055f3-157b-49a8-987d-93d1e8a76016', 6, '(i) In an experiment, a chemist used 59.6 g of tungsten fluoride.
What is the maximum mass of tungsten he could obtain from 59.6 g of tungsten fluoride?
Relative formula mass of tungsten fluoride = 298
(ii) Starting with a different mass of tungsten fluoride, he calculates that the mass of tungsten formed should be 52.0 g. In his experiment he actually obtains 47.5 g of tungsten.
What is the percentage yield of tungsten in this experiment?
4 A student investigated the neutralisation of acids by measuring the temperature changes when alkalis were added to acids of known concentrations.
He used this apparatus to add different volumes of sodium hydroxide solution to a fixed volume of dilute nitric acid.
He used this method.
measure the temperature of 25.0 $ cm^{3} $ of the acid in the polystyrene cup
