5.0 | 2.5
6.0 | 3.0
7.0 | 3.0
8.0 | 3.0
(i) Plot the student''s results.
(ii) Draw two straight lines of best fit, ignoring the anomalous result.
Volume of silver nitrate added in $ \mathrm{c m}^{3} $
(iii) Suggest a mistake the student made to cause the anomalous result.
(iv) Give a reason why the last three heights are the same.
(c) The equation for the reaction between copper(II) chloride and silver nitrate is', 1, 1, '["e000101", "e000102", "e000103", "e000104", "e000105", "e000106", "e000107", "e000108", "e000109", "e000110", "e000112", "e000113", "e000114", "e000115"]', 275, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-13 13:01:26.216849+00');
INSERT INTO public.document_chunks VALUES ('709142e0-7f02-45ef-9d73-4aa5f4822e9b', 'a0f7da10-1869-4442-be9c-2b82d2845952', 8, '\mathrm {C u C l} _ {2} (\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \mathrm {A g N O} _ {3} (\mathrm {a q}) \rightarrow 2 \mathrm {A g C l} (\mathrm {s}) + \mathrm {C u} \left(\mathrm {N O} _ {3}\right) _ {2} (\mathrm {a q})
A student measures $ 2 5. 0 \mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of $ 0. 5 0 0 \mathrm{m o l} / \mathrm{d m}^{3} $ copper(II) chloride solution and reacts it with silver nitrate solution.
(i) Name a piece of apparatus suitable for measuring $ 2 5. 0 \mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of copper(II) chloride solution.
