 score2 are:\n0.6/2.4/4.8/0.12/12", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "12(c)(i): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "12(c)(i): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "12(c)(i): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "12(c)(i): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: 13(a)(i)(ii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: 3.78(g)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (iii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M2n(H2O)=3.78÷18/=0.21", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M3x=n(H2O)÷n(ZnSO4)=7", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: CSQ on(i)和(ii)Do not penalise non-integer values of xCorrect final answer with no working=1Correct final answer with some correct working=3", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (b)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "13(c): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "June 2012", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": "Monday 21 May 2012 – Morning", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH0/1C", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "6fa6e7ba-8094-5e7d-925e-0674ce769e05"}, "warnings": ["unclassified heading: THE PERIODIC TABLE", "unclassified heading: Formula of X", "Q8: incomplete MCQ option set folded into stem", "Q13: incomplete MCQ option set folded into stem"], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student was asked to find the mass of salt dissolved in $ 1 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sea water. She was given the following instructions. Step A Weigh an empty evaporating basin Step B Transfer 50 $ cm^{3} $ of sea water into the basin Step C Heat the sea water in the basin until all the water has evaporated Step D Allow the basin and residue to cool Step E Weigh the basin and residue of salt", "marks": 9, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "During the experiment, the student used several pieces of apparatus. Some of them are shown in the table. Complete the table.", "label": "a", "marks": 6, "partId": "q01-6fa6e7ba-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State, with a reason, one safety precaution that the student should take when doing this experiment. Precaution Reason", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000031"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagrams show the reactions of some metals with cold water and with dilute hydrochloric acid. Metals in cold water Metals in dilute hydrochloric acid", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Answer the following questions, using only the metals that appear in the diagrams.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6fa6e7ba-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name two metals that react with cold water.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6fa6e7ba-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name one metal that reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid but not with cold water.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6fa6e7ba-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Arrange the five metals in order of reactivity.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6fa6e7ba-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Some magnesium powder is added to dilute sulfuric acid in a test tube. A colourless solution is formed and a gas is given off. When more magnesium is added, the reaction continues for a while and then stops, leaving some magnesium powder in the test tube. When a flame is placed at the mouth of the test tube, the gas burns with a squeaky pop.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the gas produced.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6fa6e7ba-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why the reaction stops.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6fa6e7ba-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the name of the colourless solution.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6fa6e7ba-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "How could you separate the magnesium powder from the colourless solution?", "label": "b-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6fa6e7ba-pb-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In some fireworks, magnesium powder reacts quickly with oxygen in the air. During this reaction heat energy is produced.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6fa6e7ba-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What name is given to reactions in which heat energy is produced?", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6fa6e7ba-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the compound formed when magnesium reacts with oxygen.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6fa6e7ba-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789046926441.png", "width": 1375, "format": "png", "height": 414, "sha256": "2b70fa9952b766c064b4d3e3b822cb3910c4b522f3fa9e6a98d8b42fde30c432", "assetId": "img:2b70fa9952b766c064b4d3e3b822cb3910c4b522f3fa9e6a98d8b42fde30c432", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000038", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789046926441.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789046926446.png", "width": 1366, "format": "png", "height": 290, "sha256": "e5daae69940757ef88e1d6f67f91f4ceedc15e3d866ed198c954738cf9a63297", "assetId": "img:e5daae69940757ef88e1d6f67f91f4ceedc15e3d866ed198c954738cf9a63297", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000040", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789046926446.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "When solutions are mixed together, precipitates sometimes form.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Barium carbonate is an insoluble compound. It is formed as a precipitate when solutions of the soluble compounds barium chloride and sodium carbonate are mixed. When solutions of the soluble compounds potassium chloride and sodium sulfate are mixed, no precipitate is formed. Complete the table to show the results of mixing solutions of some compounds.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-6fa6e7ba-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When solutions of lead(II) nitrate and potassium bromide are mixed, a precipitate of lead(II) bromide and a solution of potassium nitrate are produced. $\\mathrm {P b} \\left(\\mathrm {N O} _ {3}\\right) _ {2} (\\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots) + 2 \\mathrm {K B r} (\\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {P b B r} _ {2} (\\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots) + 2 \\mathrm {K N O} _ {3} (\\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots)$ The equation for the reaction is Complete the equation by inserting the state symbols.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In order to prepare a pure, dry sample of lead(II) bromide, a student took the mixture produced in part (b). He then filtered the mixture washed the solid residue with distilled water left the solid in a warm place for several hours", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-6fa6e7ba-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Why did the student filter the mixture?", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-6fa6e7ba-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Why did he wash the solid residue?", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-6fa6e7ba-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Why is it better to use distilled water rather than tap water to wash the solid residue?", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-6fa6e7ba-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Why did he leave the solid in a warm place?", "label": "c-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-6fa6e7ba-pc-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000060"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram shows the positions of some elements in the Periodic Table. 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 0", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the following sentence. The elements in the Periodic Table are arranged in order of.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-6fa6e7ba-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name an element shown in the diagram that is:", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name two elements in the diagram that react together to form an ionic compound. (1) and", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-6fa6e7ba-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a dot and cross diagram for the ions in the compound formed in (c)(i). Show only the outer electrons. Include the charge on each ion.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-6fa6e7ba-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Chlorine reacts quickly with hot iron to form iron(III) chloride. Bromine reacts less quickly with hot iron to form iron(III) bromide. Suggest how fluorine reacts with hot iron and name the compound formed.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-6fa6e7ba-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When chlorine gas is bubbled through an aqueous solution of sodium bromide, a displacement reaction takes place. The ionic equation for the reaction is: $\\mathrm {C l} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) + 2 \\mathrm {B r} ^ {-} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {C l} ^ {-} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {B r} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q})$ State the colour change that you would observe in the solution during this reaction. Colour at start Colour at end", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-6fa6e7ba-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789046926501.png", "width": 102, "format": "png", "height": 97, "sha256": "50dca270d9fa0430542916f7eb4b01dbe6978e73f3356c07330ee7a3a882dacb", "assetId": "img:50dca270d9fa0430542916f7eb4b01dbe6978e73f3356c07330ee7a3a882dacb", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000078", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789046926501.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000079"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The apparatus in the diagram is used to collect the oxygen produced by the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide, $ \\mathrm{H_{2}O_{2}} $", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-6fa6e7ba-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe a test to show that the gas collected in the syringe is oxygen.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Manganese(IV) oxide is a catalyst for this reaction. State and explain the effect of a catalyst on the rate of this reaction.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-6fa6e7ba-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The graph shows the results from an experiment using a 0.50 mol/dm $ ^{3} $ solution of hydrogen peroxide at $ 2 5 \\, \\mathrm{^ {\\circ} C} $", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-6fa6e7ba-pd", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789046926527.png", "width": 1273, "format": "png", "height": 1672, "sha256": "ee940229b354ac230a0ad3fa4285ae5e39fb1c55e2bf4dee04b25ccd90cacfec", "assetId": "img:ee940229b354ac230a0ad3fa4285ae5e39fb1c55e2bf4dee04b25ccd90cacfec", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000101", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789046926527.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "On the same axes, sketch the curve you would expect with the same volume of a 0.25 mol/dm $ ^{3} $ solution of hydrogen peroxide at $ 2 5 \\mathrm{~} ^ {\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . Label this curve A.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-6fa6e7ba-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "On the same axes, sketch the curve you would expect with the same volume of a 0.50 mol/dm $ ^{3} $ solution of hydrogen peroxide at $ 3 5 \\mathrm{~} ^ {\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . Label this curve B.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-6fa6e7ba-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789046926517.png", "width": 1130, "format": "png", "height": 676, "sha256": "ad3baf965831f6599fb06dcda51518e0213f1b01058dff1f3ae244860f9a6ce3", "assetId": "img:ad3baf965831f6599fb06dcda51518e0213f1b01058dff1f3ae244860f9a6ce3", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000095", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789046926517.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789046926527.png", "width": 1273, "format": "png", "height": 1672, "sha256": "ee940229b354ac230a0ad3fa4285ae5e39fb1c55e2bf4dee04b25ccd90cacfec", "assetId": "img:ee940229b354ac230a0ad3fa4285ae5e39fb1c55e2bf4dee04b25ccd90cacfec", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000101", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789046926527.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q05-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The element carbon has three common isotopes. These are carbon-12, carbon-13 and carbon-14.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the table to show the number of protons and neutrons in each isotope of carbon.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-6fa6e7ba-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain, in terms of electrons, why the three isotopes have the same chemical properties.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what is meant by the term relative atomic mass, $ A_{r} $", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-6fa6e7ba-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A sample of carbon contained 98.90% carbon-12 and 1.10% carbon-13. Use this information to calculate the relative atomic mass of carbon in the sample. Give your answer to two decimal places. Relative atomic mass", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-6fa6e7ba-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000106"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Decane is a hydrocarbon found in crude oil. The diagram shows the structure of a decane molecule.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why decane is described as a hydrocarbon.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6fa6e7ba-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the molecular formula for decane.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6fa6e7ba-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Decane and ethene, $ C_{2} H_{4} $ are produced during the cracking of eicosane, $ C_{2 0} H_{4 2} $ Ethene is used to make poly(ethene).", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_2_1789046926569.png", "width": 796, "format": "png", "height": 702, "sha256": "1aaf7f3dc2a0ac778b0c9c7cfc47b81211a7d555cddfabcdefeedb6f648173e7", "assetId": "img:1aaf7f3dc2a0ac778b0c9c7cfc47b81211a7d555cddfabcdefeedb6f648173e7", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000120", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789046926569.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the name given to this type of polymerisation?", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6fa6e7ba-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the diagram to state two changes that occur during the formation of poly(ethene).", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6fa6e7ba-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why cracking is an important process in the oil industry.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6fa6e7ba-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789046926562.png", "width": 735, "format": "png", "height": 228, "sha256": "236cd643e6b6b0bf302cff004f8eeddf1c048cf9202d3d9ce99cfac3e930657d", "assetId": "img:236cd643e6b6b0bf302cff004f8eeddf1c048cf9202d3d9ce99cfac3e930657d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000116", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789046926562.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789046926569.png", "width": 796, "format": "png", "height": 702, "sha256": "1aaf7f3dc2a0ac778b0c9c7cfc47b81211a7d555cddfabcdefeedb6f648173e7", "assetId": "img:1aaf7f3dc2a0ac778b0c9c7cfc47b81211a7d555cddfabcdefeedb6f648173e7", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000120", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789046926569.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q07-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "When ammonia gas and hydrogen chloride gas mix, they react together to form a white solid called ammonium chloride. The equation for the reaction is: $\\mathrm {N H} _ {3} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {H C l} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N H} _ {4} \\mathrm {C l} (\\mathrm {s})$ After five minutes, a white ring of solid ammonium chloride formed. A cotton wool pad was soaked in ammonia solution and another was soaked in hydrogen chloride solution. The two pads were then put into opposite ends of a dry glass tube at the same time.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "What name is given to the movement of the two gases?", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-6fa6e7ba-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify which gas is moving faster and give a reason for your choice.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-6fa6e7ba-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The experiment was repeated at a higher temperature. State and explain how this change would affect the time taken for the white ring to form.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Gas particles move at a speed of several hundred metres per second at room temperature. Suggest one reason why it took five minutes for the white ring to form.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-6fa6e7ba-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789046926583.png", "width": 1113, "format": "png", "height": 377, "sha256": "bd044510d88d5c9bc1c1579311f99d6885edf72d591c929f272ef49b5835d88c", "assetId": "img:bd044510d88d5c9bc1c1579311f99d6885edf72d591c929f272ef49b5835d88c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000129", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789046926583.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q08-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "When water is added to a mixture of sand and cement, a reaction takes place between silicon dioxide in the sand and calcium oxide in the cement. The reaction produces a salt called calcium silicate. The equation for the reaction is: $\\mathrm {S i O} _ {2} + \\mathrm {C a O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C a S i O} _ {3}$", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why silicon dioxide reacts with calcium oxide.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-6fa6e7ba-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Part of the structure of silicon dioxide is shown in the diagram.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789046926641.png", "width": 464, "format": "png", "height": 350, "sha256": "1c1eb1b7cdd702b03653c5a0abfba05d8b48404e441b3c669fb44e47d09412f9", "assetId": "img:1c1eb1b7cdd702b03653c5a0abfba05d8b48404e441b3c669fb44e47d09412f9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000142", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789046926641.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What does particle A represent? Give a reason for your answer.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-6fa6e7ba-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain, in terms of its bonding and structure, why silicon dioxide has a very high melting point.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-6fa6e7ba-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 9, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789046926641.png", "width": 464, "format": "png", "height": 350, "sha256": "1c1eb1b7cdd702b03653c5a0abfba05d8b48404e441b3c669fb44e47d09412f9", "assetId": "img:1c1eb1b7cdd702b03653c5a0abfba05d8b48404e441b3c669fb44e47d09412f9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000142", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789046926641.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q09-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "When sodium is burned in air, one of the products is a pale yellow solid, X.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "A sample of solid X was found to contain 1.15 g of sodium and 0.80 g of oxygen.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-6fa6e7ba-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Show, by calculation, that the empirical formula of X is NaO.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-6fa6e7ba-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The relative formula mass of X is 78. Deduce the formula of X.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-6fa6e7ba-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Solid X reacts with water to form sodium hydroxide, NaOH, and hydrogen peroxide, $ \\mathrm{H_{2}O_{2}} $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation to represent the reaction between X and water.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-6fa6e7ba-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The solution formed in the reaction between X and water turns red litmus blue. Identify the ion that causes this change.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-6fa6e7ba-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The displayed formula for hydrogen peroxide is H—O—O—H. Complete the dot and cross diagram to show the arrangement of the outer shell (valence) electrons in a molecule of hydrogen peroxide. $\\mathrm {H} _ {\\mathrm {X}} ^ {\\circ} \\mathrm {O} \\mathrm {O} _ {\\mathrm {X}} ^ {\\bullet} \\mathrm {H}$", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-6fa6e7ba-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 10, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q10-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student carried out a series of tests on a solid, M, in order to identify the ions that could be present. The table shows her results.", "marks": 10, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the ion responsible for", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-6fa6e7ba-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "the lilac colour in the flame test", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-6fa6e7ba-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "the green precipitate when sodium hydroxide solution was added", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-6fa6e7ba-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "the yellow precipitate when silver nitrate solution was added", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-6fa6e7ba-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe how the student should carry out a flame test on solid M.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Why was dilute nitric acid added to the solution of solid M before using silver nitrate solution?", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-6fa6e7ba-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Why should dilute hydrochloric acid not be used in place of dilute nitric acid in this test?", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-6fa6e7ba-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The tests for negative ions that the student carried out involved precipitation. Suggest one negative ion that cannot be identified by a precipitation reaction.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-6fa6e7ba-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 11, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q11-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000159"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Lead can be extracted from lead(II) sulfide, PbS, in two stages. Stage 1: Lead(II) sulfide is heated in air. It reacts with oxygen to produce lead(II) oxide and sulfur dioxide. Stage 2: The lead(II) oxide is then heated in a blast furnace with coke.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for the reaction in Stage 1.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-6fa6e7ba-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the reaction that occurs when lead(II) oxide is heated with coke in a blast furnace is: $2 \\mathrm {P b O} + \\mathrm {C} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {P b} + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2}$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State, with a reason, whether PbO is oxidised or reduced in this reaction.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-6fa6e7ba-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the minimum mass, in tonnes, of coke needed to react with 44.6 tonnes of lead(II) oxide. $ [ 1 \\mathrm{t o n n e}=1 0^{6} \\mathrm{g} ] $", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-6fa6e7ba-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["Mass of coke needed"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The molten lead obtained from the blast furnace contains 0.1% silver dissolved as an impurity. The silver is removed by: adding zinc to the mixture of molten lead and silver at 530 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ and removing the mixture of molten zinc and silver that forms on top of the molten lead heating the mixture of molten zinc and silver until the zinc boils off as a gas, leaving almost pure, solid silver behind Use the information above to answer the following questions.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-6fa6e7ba-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What can you deduce about the relative solubility of silver in zinc and in lead?", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-6fa6e7ba-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What can you deduce about the melting point of the mixture of zinc and silver?", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-6fa6e7ba-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What can you deduce about the boiling point of zinc compared to that of silver? Explain your answer.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-6fa6e7ba-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why so much trouble is taken to remove such a small amount of silver from the lead.", "label": "c-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-6fa6e7ba-pc-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 12, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q12-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) Crystals of hydrated zinc sulfate, $ \\mathrm{Z n S O_{4}. x H_{2} O} $ , contain water of crystallisation. He weighed the empty tube A. He placed a sample of hydrated zinc sulfate crystals in tube A and reweighed it. He heated the tube, allowed it to cool and weighed it again. He repeated this process until two consecutive masses were the same. This is known as ''heating to constant mass''. When hydrated zinc sulfate crystals are heated gently, they decompose according to the following equation: $\\mathrm {Z n S O} _ {4}. \\mathrm {x H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {Z n S O} _ {4} + \\mathrm {x H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ The following masses were recorded: $\\mathrm {M a s s o f t u b e A} = 1 0. 1 2 \\mathrm {g}$ $\\mathrm {M a s s o f t u b e A} + \\mathrm {Z n S O} _ {4}. \\mathrm {x H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} = 1 8. 7 3 \\mathrm {g}$ Mass of tube A and $ \\mathrm{Z n S O_{4}} $ after heating to constant mass = 14.95 g (i) Calculate the mass of $ \\mathrm{Z n S O}_{4} $ formed after heating to constant mass. (ii) Calculate the mass of water collected after heating to constant mass. (iii) The relative formula mass of $ \\mathrm{Z n S O_{4}} $ is 161 The relative formula mass of water is 18 Use this information, and your answers to (a)(i) and (a)(ii), to calculate the value of x in the formula $ \\mathrm{Z n S O_{4}. x H_{2}O} $ Show your working. A student used the apparatus shown to remove and collect the water of crystallisation from the crystals in order to find the value of x.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Why is it necessary to heat the crystals to constant mass?", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-6fa6e7ba-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe how the student could use a chemical test to show that the liquid collected was water.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-6fa6e7ba-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 13, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789046926734.png", "width": 1382, "format": "png", "height": 729, "sha256": "3a56eb89b19a53f6038270e17c67ac4bff3781245a221221804d5e2571fa6e0a", "assetId": "img:3a56eb89b19a53f6038270e17c67ac4bff3781245a221221804d5e2571fa6e0a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000195", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789046926734.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q13-6fa6e7ba", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 120, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 9, "2": 12, "3": 8, "4": 11, "6": 8, "8": 6, "9": 8, "11": 10, "12": 11, "13": 8}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": null, "session": "Summer 2012", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "7703c573-740f-56aa-aa74-8cb10f0bf2a7"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 6, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-7703c573-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "Ignore extra apparatus on top of tripod eg beaker Minimum is 2 vertical or diagonal lines with something on top"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "measuring cylinder"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Or 2D diagram"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "Ignore D"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(top pan) balance/scale(s)/weighing machine"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A or E"}], "answerText": "1 mark for each correct answer", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(b)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-7703c573-q1b", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "It"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "leave basin(to cool)before removing"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "crucible tongs/plastic gloves"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 use(beaker)tongs/hot hand/(rigger/oven)glove(s)(to remove/lift the basin)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "to avoid burning hand"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 basin will/may be hot"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 tie hair back/tuck in tie"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 might catch fire(in Bunsen burner)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "the reason must match the precaution"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to wearing lab.coats/protective clothing"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(2.9x2)=5.8(g)"}], "answerText": "M1 wear(safety) glasses/spectacles/goggles/eye protection\nM2 salt/solution/water may spit out(when evaporating the salty water)/may get in your eye\nIGNORE references to hazards eg toxic/irritant", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(c)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-7703c573-q2ci", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "MgO"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "magnesium oxide"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE incorrect formula"}], "answerText": "exothermic", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-7703c573-q3a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "sulphate for sulfate insoluble barium sulphate / BaSO4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "incorrect name of ppt."}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 no precipitate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "no (visible) change solution (formed)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 precipitate of calcium sulfate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "sulphate for sulfate insoluble calcium sulfate / CaSO4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "incorrect name of ppt."}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE colours"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "penalise incorrect extra observations (e.g. effervescence) ONCE only"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "For M1 and M3 only: if only precipitate appears twice (with no names), penalise missing names once only"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "if only names correct (with no precipitates), penalise omission of precipitate once only"}], "answerText": "M1 precipitate of barium sulfate", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(b)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-7703c573-q3b", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "obtain the lead(II) bromide/the residue/the solid OR remove the liquid/solution/potassium nitrate/water"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "separate the solid and liquid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "to wash away/remove the (remaining) potassium nitrate/lead(II) nitrate/potassium bromide/solution IGNORE clean"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "wash away/remove (remaining soluble) impurities to make it pure"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "make the mixture pure"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "distilled water is pure/does not contain(dissolved) impurities/ions/substances/compounds/other chemicals (that would contaminate the lead(II) bromide)/residue/solid IGNORE elements IGNORE references to distilled water being cleaner(ORA)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "reverse argument for tap water"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "any suggestion that the water/impurities react"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iv)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "to evaporate the water/to dry (the solid/crystals)/increase rate of evaporation(of water) IGNORE liquid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "to avoid decomposition(if heated strongly)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "to evaporate the potassium nitrate/solution any reference to crystallisation"}], "answerText": "aq aq s aq", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-7703c573-q4a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "proton number/number of protons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "mass number/RAM"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "sodium/potassium"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Na/K"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "fluorine/chlorine/bromine"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "F/Cl/Br/F2/Cl2/Br2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "fluoride/chloride/bromide"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "sodium OR potassium"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "AND"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "fluorine OR chlorine OR bromine OR hydrogen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Answers can be in either order"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE incorrect symbols/formulae if names are correct"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Marks do not have to be CQ on(c)(i), and all marks can be scored here for correct diagrams of the ions in a hydrogen halide"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 Na or K with 8 ele