'{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "June 2017", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": "Thursday 18 May 2017 – Morning", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH0/1CR", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "421db8f1-5e17-57c1-86ab-e1eddcb60bf7"}, "warnings": ["unclassified heading: volume of sodium hydroxide =", "Q15: incomplete MCQ option set folded into stem"], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Bromine is an element in Group 7 of the Periodic Table.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these is the formula for a molecule of bromine? A 2Br B $ \\mathrm{B r^{2}} $ Br2 C D $ \\mathrm{B r}_{2} $", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A small amount of liquid bromine is placed in a gas jar containing air. The jar is then sealed. After two minutes, a brown gas is seen just above the surface of the liquid. After two hours, the whole gas jar is full of the brown gas. after two minutes after two hours", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255031.png", "width": 424, "format": "png", "height": 594, "sha256": "79120438822d7ca63979fe31898a7d21c2f21ea98b396360a32785dd71f4d107", "assetId": "img:79120438822d7ca63979fe31898a7d21c2f21ea98b396360a32785dd71f4d107", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000032", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255031.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789049255036.png", "width": 370, "format": "png", "height": 514, "sha256": "5044ccbc979857efcaaa38349fa24f6b820012aaafc5c1ef6825b7019fb0fa5b", "assetId": "img:5044ccbc979857efcaaa38349fa24f6b820012aaafc5c1ef6825b7019fb0fa5b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000033", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789049255036.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789049255042.png", "width": 364, "format": "png", "height": 516, "sha256": "87552e3fa24ea7f129d4122b6753be01261e6ed263d421e07e81abd774de81dd", "assetId": "img:87552e3fa24ea7f129d4122b6753be01261e6ed263d421e07e81abd774de81dd", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000035", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789049255042.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these is the process that causes the brown gas to fill the gas jar? A condensation B diffusion evaporation sublimation", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-421db8f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain, using the particle theory, the observations seen in the gas jar.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-421db8f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "This apparatus is used to demonstrate the movement of ammonia gas and hydrogen chloride gas. The gases are given off by the solutions at each end of the tube. When the gases meet, they form a white solid. Which letter shows the position where the white solid forms? A B C D", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-421db8f1-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255060.png", "width": 1327, "format": "png", "height": 541, "sha256": "d9a1b44f8934fa80c1af1236c3c73b489178b2a92a13649761cc2a8cc30dc848", "assetId": "img:d9a1b44f8934fa80c1af1236c3c73b489178b2a92a13649761cc2a8cc30dc848", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000044", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255060.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255031.png", "width": 424, "format": "png", "height": 594, "sha256": "79120438822d7ca63979fe31898a7d21c2f21ea98b396360a32785dd71f4d107", "assetId": "img:79120438822d7ca63979fe31898a7d21c2f21ea98b396360a32785dd71f4d107", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000032", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255031.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789049255036.png", "width": 370, "format": "png", "height": 514, "sha256": "5044ccbc979857efcaaa38349fa24f6b820012aaafc5c1ef6825b7019fb0fa5b", "assetId": "img:5044ccbc979857efcaaa38349fa24f6b820012aaafc5c1ef6825b7019fb0fa5b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000033", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789049255036.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789049255042.png", "width": 364, "format": "png", "height": 516, "sha256": "87552e3fa24ea7f129d4122b6753be01261e6ed263d421e07e81abd774de81dd", "assetId": "img:87552e3fa24ea7f129d4122b6753be01261e6ed263d421e07e81abd774de81dd", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000035", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789049255042.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255060.png", "width": 1327, "format": "png", "height": 541, "sha256": "d9a1b44f8934fa80c1af1236c3c73b489178b2a92a13649761cc2a8cc30dc848", "assetId": "img:d9a1b44f8934fa80c1af1236c3c73b489178b2a92a13649761cc2a8cc30dc848", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000044", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255060.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Iron is a metal that has many uses. One problem with iron is that it can rust when exposed to air.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name the two substances that must be present in air for iron to rust.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "These are four methods of preventing iron from rusting painting oiling coating in plastic galvanising The table lists three items made from iron. Complete the table by giving the best method of preventing each item from rusting. You may use each method once, more than once or not at all.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these metals is used to galvanise iron? A aluminium B lead C tin D zinc", "label": "c", "marks": 1, "partId": "q02-421db8f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000061"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) The box shows some methods that can be used to separate mixtures. The table lists some separations. Complete the table by giving the best method for each separation. You may use each method once, more than once or not at all.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "When a mixture of ethanol and water is separated, two colourless liquids, A and B, are obtained. Liquid A is ethanol containing a small amount of water. Liquid B is pure water.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe a chemical test to show that liquid A contains water.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-421db8f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe a physical test to show that liquid B is pure water.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-421db8f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q03-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000070", "e000074"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The Periodic Table consists of elements arranged in order of increasing atomic number.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "What is meant by the term atomic number? A the number of neutrons in an atom B the number of protons in an atom C the number of protons plus the number of electrons in an atom D the number of protons plus the number of neutrons in an atom", "label": "a", "marks": 1, "partId": "q04-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Elements in the same group of the Periodic Table have similar chemical properties. This is because their atoms contain the same number of A protons B neutrons C electrons in the outer shell D protons and neutrons", "label": "a-ii", "marks": 1, "partId": "q04-421db8f1-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The elements in the periods of the Periodic Table show a change in properties across the period from left to right. The table gives information about some elements in Period 2. giant or molecular for structure high or low for melting point Complete the table by writing one of these words in each blank space. acidic or basic for acid-base character of the oxide", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000095"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student uses this apparatus to investigate the rate of reaction between an excess of magnesium and two different dilute acids, X and Y. This is the method given to the student. place 0.5g of magnesium ribbon into the conical flask use a measuring cylinder to add $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of dilute acid X and then replace the bung record the total volume of gas collected every 20 seconds for two minutes repeat the method using $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of dilute acid Y instead of dilute acid X in each experiment, keep the temperature the same and the magnesium in excess", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The student decides to use a burette, rather than a measuring cylinder, to add the acid to the conical flask.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Using a burette would be an improvement only if another change is made to the method. State the other change needed to the method.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-421db8f1-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the advantage of using a burette.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-421db8f1-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the student''s results. Draw a curve of best fit for each set of points. Plot the results for each acid on the grid. Label each curve as acid X or acid Y.", "label": "b", "marks": 4, "partId": "q05-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255126.png", "width": 1246, "format": "png", "height": 1280, "sha256": "3f01e3933cfc01822d3d161da6b778de58951d0d01a66ae67d22d285cfedf16b", "assetId": "img:3f01e3933cfc01822d3d161da6b778de58951d0d01a66ae67d22d285cfedf16b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000119", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255126.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain how the curves show which acid has the greater concentration.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-421db8f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the graph to find the total volume of gas collected at 70s for acid X. Show on the graph how you obtained your answer.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-421db8f1-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["volume of gas"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The average (mean) rate of the reaction for acid Y can be calculated using the expression $\\mathrm {a v e r a g e r a t e} = \\frac {\\mathrm {t o t a l v o l u m e o f g a s c o l l e c t e d}}{\\mathrm {t i m e t o c o l l e c t t h e g a s}}$ Calculate the average rate, in $ cm^{3}/s $ , for the first 30s for acid Y.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-421db8f1-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255117.png", "width": 1217, "format": "png", "height": 587, "sha256": "b20a73d57cd523252027d597d8ce2f86d8578d7b034598a128077c94254628aa", "assetId": "img:b20a73d57cd523252027d597d8ce2f86d8578d7b034598a128077c94254628aa", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000102", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255117.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255126.png", "width": 1246, "format": "png", "height": 1280, "sha256": "3f01e3933cfc01822d3d161da6b778de58951d0d01a66ae67d22d285cfedf16b", "assetId": "img:3f01e3933cfc01822d3d161da6b778de58951d0d01a66ae67d22d285cfedf16b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000119", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255126.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000114"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Copper and iron are metals. They have the typical properties of metals, including a high melting point and good electrical conductivity.", "marks": 15, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a labelled diagram to show the arrangement of the particles involved in the bonding within a metal.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why metals have good electrical conductivity.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-421db8f1-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why some metals have high melting points.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-421db8f1-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Copper and iron both form compounds containing ions with a 2+ charge. Sodium hydroxide is used to distinguish between solutions containing $ \\mathrm{C u}^{2+} $ and $ \\mathrm{F e}^{2+} $ ions. What would be observed when aqueous sodium hydroxide is added separately to each solution?", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Copper can be found naturally as a pure metal. Iron is found only in compounds and has to be extracted by chemical reactions.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-421db8f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why copper can be found as a pure metal.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-421db8f1-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The most common method of extracting iron is by the reduction of iron(III) oxide in a blast furnace. The diagram shows a blast furnace. Write a chemical equation to show how carbon monoxide forms in the blast furnace. (1)", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-421db8f1-pc-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255149.png", "width": 1234, "format": "png", "height": 875, "sha256": "ecc404dd4856a70c92b3567f878634b4483dadb79673b2df17486a2954d7bf67", "assetId": "img:ecc404dd4856a70c92b3567f878634b4483dadb79673b2df17486a2954d7bf67", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000140", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255149.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the reaction between iron(III) oxide and carbon monoxide is $\\mathrm {F e} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3} + 3 \\mathrm {C O} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {F e} + 3 \\mathrm {C O} _ {2}$ Explain which element is reduced in this reaction.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-421db8f1-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe how molten slag forms in the blast furnace.", "label": "c-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-421db8f1-pc-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255149.png", "width": 1234, "format": "png", "height": 875, "sha256": "ecc404dd4856a70c92b3567f878634b4483dadb79673b2df17486a2954d7bf67", "assetId": "img:ecc404dd4856a70c92b3567f878634b4483dadb79673b2df17486a2954d7bf67", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000140", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255149.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram shows how a student attempts to prepare some hydrogen gas.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State why no hydrogen is produced.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these pairs of chemicals could be used to safely prepare hydrogen? A copper and dilute sulfuric acid B gold and dilute hydrochloric acid C potassium and dilute hydrochloric acid D zinc and dilute sulfuric acid", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-421db8f1-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In an experiment, the student then successfully prepares some hydrogen and measures the volume of hydrogen collected. She does the same experiment four times. The table shows her results.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which experiment produces an anomalous result.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-421db8f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the average (mean) volume of hydrogen collected.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-421db8f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["average volume"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why it is important for the student to repeat the experiment.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-421db8f1-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255170.png", "width": 1125, "format": "png", "height": 564, "sha256": "4b784851921598ab8fb6fc8bd8477dbbdc5b9beacd96fc4cc0c3879d35f5ea53", "assetId": "img:4b784851921598ab8fb6fc8bd8477dbbdc5b9beacd96fc4cc0c3879d35f5ea53", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000148", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255170.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000158"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "When iodine reacts with an excess of chlorine at $ - 8 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ , a compound forms. The percentage composition by mass of this compound is $\\mathrm {I} = 5 4. 4 \\% \\quad \\mathrm {C I} = 4 5. 6 \\%$", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Show, by calculation, that the empirical formula of this compound is $ \\mathrm{I C I}_{3} $", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The molecular formula of this compound is $ \\mathrm{I}_{2} \\mathrm{C l}_{6} $ $ \\mathrm{I}_{2} \\mathrm{C l}_{6} $ is a yellow solid at room temperature and forms a liquid when heated. A dynamic equilibrium exists in this liquid, represented by this equation. $\\mathrm {I} _ {2} \\mathrm {C l} _ {6} (\\mathrm {I}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {I C l} _ {2} ^ {+} (\\mathrm {I}) + \\mathrm {I C l} _ {4} ^ {-} (\\mathrm {I})$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When a reaction is in dynamic equilibrium, the forward and backward reactions occur at the same time. State two other features of a reaction that is in dynamic equilibrium.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-421db8f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why liquid $ \\mathrm{I}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{6} $ conducts electricity, but solid $ \\mathrm{I}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{6} $ does not.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-421db8f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q08-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Bromine, chlorine and iodine are halogens in Group 7 of the Periodic Table. The three halogens have similar chemical properties but different reactivities.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "A teacher uses this apparatus to demonstrate the difference in reactivity between these halogens. The teacher does each experiment in a fume cupboard. The table shows the observation for each halogen.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255213.png", "width": 1268, "format": "png", "height": 402, "sha256": "d90c45d8010748ebff8fd7972aaf23d0f9554c48554ddffc7bb083ea61f33349", "assetId": "img:d90c45d8010748ebff8fd7972aaf23d0f9554c48554ddffc7bb083ea61f33349", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000177", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255213.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why the teacher uses a fume cupboard.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-421db8f1-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the information in the table to explain the order of reactivity of the three halogens.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-421db8f1-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student states that the order of reactivity cannot be found from this experiment because bromine is a liquid, chlorine is a gas and iodine is a solid at room temperature. Evaluate the student''s statement.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-421db8f1-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Bromine, chlorine and iodine all react with hydrogen. In each reaction, a hydrogen halide (HX) is formed, where X represents the halogen.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for the reaction between hydrogen and bromine.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-421db8f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a dot and cross diagram to represent a molecule of hydrogen chloride, HCl. Show only the outer shell electrons.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-421db8f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the solution formed when hydrogen chloride dissolves in water.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-421db8f1-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 9, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255213.png", "width": 1268, "format": "png", "height": 402, "sha256": "d90c45d8010748ebff8fd7972aaf23d0f9554c48554ddffc7bb083ea61f33349", "assetId": "img:d90c45d8010748ebff8fd7972aaf23d0f9554c48554ddffc7bb083ea61f33349", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000177", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255213.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q09-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000180"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student uses this apparatus to find the mass of magnesium oxide that forms when a strip of magnesium ribbon is burned in air. This is the student''s method. weigh an empty crucible and lid place a coiled strip of freshly cleaned magnesium ribbon in the crucible weigh the crucible, lid and ribbon heat the crucible strongly for several minutes, lifting the lid and quickly replacing it several times leave the crucible, lid and contents to cool and then reweigh", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Describe the appearance of the freshly cleaned magnesium ribbon and the appearance of the magnesium oxide that forms. magnesium ribbon magnesium oxide", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why the student lifts the lid and quickly replaces it several times during the experiment. lifts lid replaces lid", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-421db8f1-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the mass of oxygen required to completely react with 0.6 g of magnesium to form magnesium oxide. The equation for the reaction is $2 \\mathrm {M g} + \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {M g O}$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The mass of magnesium oxide that forms is less than expected. This could be because some of the magnesium reacts with nitrogen to form magnesium nitride, $ \\mathrm{M g_{3} N_{2}} $ Write a chemical equation for the reaction between magnesium and nitrogen to form magnesium nitride.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-421db8f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 10, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255227.png", "width": 593, "format": "png", "height": 660, "sha256": "47415470d6c50f8ccdb469c786bed037a82a5724d64e428246963b9142019ddf", "assetId": "img:47415470d6c50f8ccdb469c786bed037a82a5724d64e428246963b9142019ddf", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000190", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255227.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q10-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The alkanes are a homologous series of hydrocarbons. The cycloalkanes are another homologous series of hydrocarbons, where the carbon atoms are arranged in a ring structure. Cyclopropane is the simplest cycloalkane. It can be represented as The tables give the names and molecular formulae of the first three members of each series.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "One feature of a homologous series is that each member has the same general formula.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the general formula for the homologous series of alkanes? A $ C_{n} H_{n} $ B $ C_{n} H_{2 n-2} $ C $ C_{n} H_{2 n} $ D $ C_{n} H_{2 n+2} $", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-421db8f1-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the general formula for the homologous series of cycloalkanes? Use the information from the cycloalkane table to help you answer the question. A $ C_{n} H_{n} $ B $ C_{n} H_{2 n-2} $ C $ C_{n} H_{2 n} $ D $ C_{n} H_{2 n+2} $", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-421db8f1-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State two other features of a homologous series of compounds.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-421db8f1-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Alkanes and cycloalkanes are saturated hydrocarbons.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what is meant by the term saturated.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-421db8f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw the displayed formula for ethane, $ \\mathrm{C_{2} H_{6}} $", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-421db8f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The displayed formula for cyclopropane is Draw a displayed formula for cyclobutane, $ \\mathrm{C_{4} H_{8}} $", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-421db8f1-pb-iii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255263.png", "width": 235, "format": "png", "height": 264, "sha256": "ea44cd99ef6e95636364d913088640a3ce57ab0671fbc77db45a04b8cc072fe0", "assetId": "img:ea44cd99ef6e95636364d913088640a3ce57ab0671fbc77db45a04b8cc072fe0", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000231", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255263.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The reaction between cyclopropane and bromine is similar to the reaction between methane and bromine. One product of the reaction between cyclopropane and bromine is hydrogen bromide, HBr.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-421db8f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the condition required for this reaction to occur.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-421db8f1-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the displayed formula for the other product of this reaction.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-421db8f1-pc-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255271.png", "width": 186, "format": "png", "height": 208, "sha256": "f12e6ab4b7581176050e94fe426e4d8c8f99aadbbf3c9b92881d689d91400832", "assetId": "img:f12e6ab4b7581176050e94fe426e4d8c8f99aadbbf3c9b92881d689d91400832", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000237", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255271.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 11, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255255.png", "width": 292, "format": "png", "height": 187, "sha256": "a929ad9e874e12b674f62fb9d9c50c191706f4fde5c695fb386f053c216da710", "assetId": "img:a929ad9e874e12b674f62fb9d9c50c191706f4fde5c695fb386f053c216da710", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000213", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255255.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255263.png", "width": 235, "format": "png", "height": 264, "sha256": "ea44cd99ef6e95636364d913088640a3ce57ab0671fbc77db45a04b8cc072fe0", "assetId": "img:ea44cd99ef6e95636364d913088640a3ce57ab0671fbc77db45a04b8cc072fe0", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000231", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255263.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255271.png", "width": 186, "format": "png", "height": 208, "sha256": "f12e6ab4b7581176050e94fe426e4d8c8f99aadbbf3c9b92881d689d91400832", "assetId": "img:f12e6ab4b7581176050e94fe426e4d8c8f99aadbbf3c9b92881d689d91400832", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000237", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255271.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q11-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000215", "e000216"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student is given a mixture of two solid ionic compounds, A and B. Each solid contains one cation and one anion. Compound A is insoluble in water. Compound B is soluble in water.", "marks": 9, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Describe how the student could separate the mixture to obtain solid A and a solution of B.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the results of two tests the student does on a solution of B.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the information in the table to identify the cation and the anion present in compound B. cation anion", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-421db8f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the gas given off in test 1.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-421db8f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student adds dilute hydrochloric acid to solid A. A gas that turns limewater milky is given off.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-421db8f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which anion is present in solid A.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-421db8f1-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a test to show that solid A contains calcium ions.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-421db8f1-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 12, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q12-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000243"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This apparatus can be used to find the volume of dilute sulfuric acid needed to neutralise 25.0 $ cm^{3} $ of aqueous sodium hydroxide, NaOH.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Describe how to use the apparatus to find the volume of dilute sulfuric acid needed to neutralise the sodium hydroxide. Assume that the apparatus is clean and does not need rinsing.", "label": "a", "marks": 4, "partId": "q13-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The volume of dilute sulfuric acid needed can also be found by measuring the highest temperature reached when the two solutions are mixed. In a series of experiments, different volumes of dilute sulfuric acid are added separately to $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ samples of aqueous sodium hydroxide. The graph shows the highest temperatures in each experiment. In each reaction, the starting temperatures of the two solutions are the same. $^{\\circ} C$ Volume of acid added in $ \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ Use the graph to answer these questions.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255305.png", "width": 1075, "format": "png", "height": 772, "sha256": "469548f7c9de6554d8b26f761f31309e85b6cc101c425c23225d285725546539", "assetId": "img:469548f7c9de6554d8b26f761f31309e85b6cc101c425c23225d285725546539", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000261", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255305.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the starting temperature of each experiment. starting temperature = ... $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-421db8f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Find the volume of acid required to exactly neutralise the sodium hydroxide.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-421db8f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["volume of acid"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Find the two different volumes of acid that produce an increase in temperature of $ 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-421db8f1-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 13, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255295.png", "width": 830, "format": "png", "height": 920, "sha256": "e62de2e91edfab08ffbf3331c2cdbc9442baae3f4bf151b525da49af666bec07", "assetId": "img:e62de2e91edfab08ffbf3331c2cdbc9442baae3f4bf151b525da49af666bec07", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000252", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255295.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789049255305.png", "width": 1075, "format": "png", "height": 772, "sha256": "469548f7c9de6554d8b26f761f31309e85b6cc101c425c23225d285725546539", "assetId": "img:469548f7c9de6554d8b26f761f31309e85b6cc101c425c23225d285725546539", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000261", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789049255305.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q13-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Lithium is an element in Group 1 of the Periodic Table. Lithium is stored in oil to prevent it from coming into contact with air and water.", "marks": 10, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "When a piece of lithium is removed from the oil, dried and cut, the exposed surface A bubbles and fizzes B changes from shiny to dull C bursts into flame D does not change", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When lithium is added to cold water in a beaker, hydrogen forms.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the test for hydrogen.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-421db8f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A few drops of phenolphthalein indicator are added to the solution in the beaker. Explain why the phenolphthalein turns pink.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-421db8f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows some information about lithium oxide and lithium carbonate. Complete the table by giving the two missing formulae.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-421db8f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Caesium is another element in Group 1 of the Periodic Table.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-421db8f1-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table below lists some statements about the reaction of caesium with cold water compared to the reaction of lithium with cold water. Place ticks ( $ \\checkmark $ ) in the boxes to show the two correct statements.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-421db8f1-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for the reaction of caesium with water.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-421db8f1-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 14, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q14-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000281", "e000285"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Hydrobromic acid can be neutralised by adding sodium hydroxide solution. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {H B r} + \\mathrm {N a O H} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N a B r} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ A solution of hydrobromic acid has a concentration of $ 0.200\\mathrm{mol / dm}^{3}. $", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the amount, in moles, of HBr in $ 2 0. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of the hydrobromic acid solution.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-421db8f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the volume of $ 0.1 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ sodium hydroxide solution needed to exactly neutralise the hydrobromic acid. Give the unit.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-421db8f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In another neutralisation reaction, a student uses $ 3 0. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of $ 0. 2 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ aqueous sodium hydroxide solution. Calculate the mass of sodium hydroxide contained in this solution. Every effort has been made to contact copyright holders to obtain their permission for the use of copyright material. Pearson Education Ltd. will, if notified, be happy to rectify any errors or omissions and include any such rectifications in future editions.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-421db8f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["mass of sodium hydroxide"]}], "number": 15, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q15-421db8f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 120, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 5, "2": 6, "4": 5, "5": 12, "6": 15, "7": 6, "8": 6, "10": 7, "12": 9, "13": 7, "14": 10, "15": 6}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "Summer 2017", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "0dbef751-1b24-59ec-a8be-977299282bc1"}, "entries": [{"mcq": true, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-0dbef751-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B(diffusion)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is B"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not correct because condensation is the change of state from a gas to liquid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not correct because evaporation is change of state from a liquid to gas"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because the change of state from sublimation is solid to gas"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW particles evaporate"}], "answerText": "D(Br2)\nThe only correct answer is D\nA is not correct because Br is the symbol for bromine\nB is not correct because the 2 is a superscript not a subscript\nC is not correct because the 2 is not a subscript", "confidence": 0.85, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": "D", "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-0dbef751-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT O2"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE O"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE air"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT moisture"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT H2O"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If both name and formula given, mark name only"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Item Method"}], "answerText": "M1 oxygen\nM2 water (vapour)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": true, "qwc": false, "label": "1", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-0dbef751-q1", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "(c)\nD(zinc)\nThe only correct answer is D\nA is not correct because zinc is the only metal used to galvanise iron\nB is not correct because zinc is the only metal used to galvanise iron\nC is not correct because zinc is the only metal used to galvanise iron", "confidence": 0.85, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": "D", "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)", "marks": 4, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-0dbef751-q3a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Method"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "filtration"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "fractional distillation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "simple distillation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "to obtain ethanol from a mixture of ethanol and water"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "fractional distillation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(add to)anhydrous/white copper(II)sulfateM2turns blueORM1add to cobalt(II) chloride paper/cobalt chloride paperM2turns pink"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTturns copper(II)sulfate from white to blue for 2 marksACCEPTblue cobalt(II) chlorideALLOWanhydrous cobalt(II) chlorideACCEPTturns cobalt(II) chloride from blue to pink for 2 marksM2DEP onM1or near misse.g. just copper(II)sulfateIGNOREany reference to testing with indicators"}], "answerText": "Separation\nto obtain sand from a mixture of sand and water\nto separate crude oil into its components\nto obtain pure water from salt water", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(b)(ii)", "marks": 2, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-0dbef751-q3bii", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT boil it/heat until it boils"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 100℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "it boils at 100℃"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW its boiling point is 100℃ for 1 mark"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW heat it and it boils at 100℃ for 2 marks"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 measure/determine/test its freezing point"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT freeze it/cool until it freezes it freezes at 0℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 0℃"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW its freezing point is 0℃ for 1 mark"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW cool it and it freezes at 0℃ for 2 marks"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 measure/determine/test its density"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW its density is 1g/cm3for 1 mark"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 1g/cm3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP on M1 throughout"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "M1 measure/determine/test its boiling point", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-0dbef751-q4ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is BA is not correct because atomic number is not the number of neutrons in an atomC is not correct because atomic number is not the number of protons plus the number of electrons in an atomD is not correct because atomic number is not the number of protons plus the number of neutrons in an atom"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C(electrons in the outer shell)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is CA is not correct because the number of protons does not determine chemical propertiesB is not correct because the number of neutrons does not determine chemical propertiesD is not correct because the number of protons and neutrons does not determine chemical properties"}], "answerText": "B(the number of protons in an atom)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(c)", "marks": 2, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-0dbef751-q5c", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M1 DEP M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Mark CSQ on candidate''s labelling of their curves"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If no labelling assume steeper curve is acid Y"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 vertical line drawn to touch curve at t=70s"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ORhorizontal line drawn to touch curve at t=70s"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2value read correctly from candidate''s graph to nearest gridline"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Expected value in range16-17(cm3)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(e)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M117(cm3)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(17÷30)=0.57(cm3/s)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTvalue read correctly from candidate''s graph to nearest gridline"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTany number of sig figs e.g.0.6,0.567,0.56 recurring"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "MarkM2CSQ onM1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "M1 Y(has the greater concentration)\nM2(because) the curve(for acid Y)has a steeper slope/greater gradient(showing that the reaction is faster)\nOR(because) it produces the larger volume of gas/more gas in the same time", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)(i)", "marks": 3, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-0dbef751-q6ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M1 minimum of 5 circles in regular pattern in at least two rows"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE different numbers of ions and electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 circle(s) labelled cation(s)/metal ion(s)/positive ion(s)/atom(s)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "+ drawn in circle is sufficient"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE particle"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 spread of electrons in between circles AND labelled"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "e or e- is sufficient"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 delocalised electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT sea of electrons"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE free electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 are able to flow (in an electric field)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT are able to move / are mobile"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to carrying a charge/current"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP on mention of electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any mention of ions/atoms/nuclei/protons moving scores 0/2"}], "answerText": "○=cation/metal ion\n•=delocalised electron", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)(iii)", "marks": 2, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-0dbef751-q6aiii", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT strong forces (of attraction) between nuclei of atoms and (delocalised) electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 large amount of (thermal/heat) energy needed to overcome the forces"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT strong metallic bonding / metallic bondsNot just strong bondsNot just heatNot just more energy"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP on M1 or near miss, e.g. strong bondsAny mention of ionic bonds or covalent bonds or intermolecular forces being broken or overcome scores 0/2"}], "answerText": "M1 strong (electrostatic) forces (of attraction) between cations/metal ions and (delocalised) electrons", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(b)", "marks": 2, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-0dbef751-q6b", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE shades"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(Fe2+) green precipitate"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE names of precipitates or formulae"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT any other colours"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Both colours correct but no mention of precipitates score 1/2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "it is unreactive/it is not very reactive/it is low in the reactivity series"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT it is not as reactive as iron/it is below iron in the reactivity series"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE inert"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "CO2+C→2CO/2C+O2→2CO"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT multiples and halves"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1iron/Fe"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(because) it loses oxygen/because oxygen has been removed"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW it loses O"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE gains electrons"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW the iron(III)ion/Fe3+(is reduced because it) has gained electrons for 1 mark"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2DEP on M1"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW Iron(III)oxide/Fe2O3/Fe3+(is reduced because it) has lost oxygen for 1 mark"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iv)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1calcium carbonate decomposes/forms/changes into calcium oxide"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT limestone for calcium carbonate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2calcium oxide reacts with silicon dioxide/silica"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT correct chemical equations"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE sand"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW calcium carbonate reacts with silicon dioxide for 1 mark"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE reacts with impurities"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "M1(Cu2+) blue precipitate", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-0dbef751-q7ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT silver is below hydrogen in the reactivity series"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D(zinc and sulfuric acid)"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE silver is unreactive/silver has a low reactivity/silver is inert"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is D"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not correct because copper does not react with dilute sulfuric acid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not correct because gold does not react with dilute hydrochloric acidC is not correct because the reaction between potassium and dilute hydrochloric acid is explosive and therefore not safe"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Experiment 3 because the volume collected is much lower than/very different to the other three"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any answer that suggests the result/value in experiment 3 is much lower/much different to the other threee.g.it is much lower than the other three or there is a large difference between it and the other three"}], "answerText": "silver does not react with(dilute sulfuric) acid", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "8(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 8, "entryId": "ms-0dbef751-q8a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTany number of sig figs except1,butallow use of0.4in calculation of ratio inM2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2Divide by the smaller number to obtain1:3ratioOR0.428:1.28=1:3"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWanswers toM1given asfractionsonlyif it is clear that division bysmaller has taken place to obtain a ratio"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1rate of forward reaction=rate of backward reaction"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNOREforwa