 number of the group that contains the noble gases.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the group whose atoms form ions with a charge of +1. (1) A 1 B 2 C 6 D 7", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-2f82da9d-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the group whose atoms form ions with a charge of $ - 1 $ (1) A 1 B 2 C 6 D 7", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-2f82da9d-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram shows the arrangement of particles in the three states of matter. Each circle represents a particle. X Y Z", "marks": 4, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Use the letters X, Y and Z to give the starting and finishing states of matter for each of the changes in the table. The first one has been done for you.", "label": "a", "marks": 3, "partId": "q02-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these changes takes place when solid iodine is heated to form iodine gas? A crystallisation B evaporation melting D sublimation", "label": "b", "marks": 1, "partId": "q02-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021696.png", "width": 435, "format": "png", "height": 462, "sha256": "30e7ba305db61fa31cbf21cd3566ba44ef5815fe4bea6cccfc559e766a42323c", "assetId": "img:30e7ba305db61fa31cbf21cd3566ba44ef5815fe4bea6cccfc559e766a42323c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000037", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021696.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789044021702.png", "width": 436, "format": "png", "height": 430, "sha256": "ee01841b382437a8b11d365b9bcacd126569a6dc392b2fa2acf23c67effce2ba", "assetId": "img:ee01841b382437a8b11d365b9bcacd126569a6dc392b2fa2acf23c67effce2ba", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000039", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789044021702.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789044021709.png", "width": 441, "format": "png", "height": 444, "sha256": "940da7bc9360fedba0b7fd354d588555d8ef8a76a0ba6985e2ce9ad9fd840d82", "assetId": "img:940da7bc9360fedba0b7fd354d588555d8ef8a76a0ba6985e2ce9ad9fd840d82", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000041", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789044021709.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.75, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000046"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student places a few purple crystals at the bottom of a beaker containing some cold water. The crystals start to dissolve. ( a ) State how the appearance of the crystals and the water change as the crystals dissolve. (2) crystals...", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which process occurs as the crystals dissolve to form a solution? A condensation B crystallisation diffusion D melting", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student repeats the experiment using hot water instead of cold water.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State how the change in the appearance of the water differs when hot water is used instead of cold water.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-2f82da9d-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain, in terms of particles, why the change differs when hot water is used instead of cold water.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-2f82da9d-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021738.png", "width": 918, "format": "png", "height": 674, "sha256": "9f5009b3d7577dc0c3fb5839e74c33177765f9f85f3a59aca71c420b7ba422d9", "assetId": "img:9f5009b3d7577dc0c3fb5839e74c33177765f9f85f3a59aca71c420b7ba422d9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000056", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021738.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The maximum mass of a solid that dissolves in 100g of water at a given temperature is called its solubility. The table gives the solubility of potassium nitrate at six different temperatures.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Plot the points on the grid and draw a curve of best fit.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021795.png", "width": 1167, "format": "png", "height": 1826, "sha256": "3c0f5b2f6468ecd27506b7de52a2c4a6df95d7e2d5e81a09dd54a9c1e3e40a99", "assetId": "img:3c0f5b2f6468ecd27506b7de52a2c4a6df95d7e2d5e81a09dd54a9c1e3e40a99", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000072", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021795.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Extend your curve to find the solubility of potassium nitrate at $ 1 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $ solubility = g per 100 g of water", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use your graph to find the maximum mass of potassium nitrate that could dissolve in 50g of water at $ 3 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021795.png", "width": 1167, "format": "png", "height": 1826, "sha256": "3c0f5b2f6468ecd27506b7de52a2c4a6df95d7e2d5e81a09dd54a9c1e3e40a99", "assetId": "img:3c0f5b2f6468ecd27506b7de52a2c4a6df95d7e2d5e81a09dd54a9c1e3e40a99", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000072", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021795.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000070"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Crude oil is a liquid that contains a mixture of many hydrocarbons. The diagram shows a fractionating column used in the distillation of crude oil. The six fractions obtained are shown. One use for each of four of the fractions is also shown.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Describe what is done to the crude oil before it enters the fractionating column.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State how the temperature changes from the top of the column to the bottom.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a use for gasoline and a use for bitumen. gasoline. bitumen.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the fraction that contains the largest molecules.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-2f82da9d-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the physical property that allows the different fractions to be collected at different heights in the column.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-2f82da9d-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021827.png", "width": 920, "format": "png", "height": 602, "sha256": "c8c6660da4c96cef44f45beb363769c3c8e859f59aba8a869630301c00e29f63", "assetId": "img:c8c6660da4c96cef44f45beb363769c3c8e859f59aba8a869630301c00e29f63", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000080", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021827.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about elements in Groups 1 and 7 of the Periodic Table.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows two ways in which potassium can be converted into potassium chloride. Give the names of gas X, colourless solution Y and acid Z. gas X. colourless solution Y. acid Z.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021839.png", "width": 1579, "format": "png", "height": 463, "sha256": "c4d8c6f6247f1119141625f25c9f45cde3b475bf00cb73b2019f284281269443", "assetId": "img:c4d8c6f6247f1119141625f25c9f45cde3b475bf00cb73b2019f284281269443", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000091", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021839.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When sodium is burned in iodine gas, sodium iodide is formed.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for the reaction between sodium and iodine.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-2f82da9d-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a test to show that an aqueous solution of sodium iodide contains iodide ions. test for iodide ions. observation", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-2f82da9d-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021839.png", "width": 1579, "format": "png", "height": 463, "sha256": "c4d8c6f6247f1119141625f25c9f45cde3b475bf00cb73b2019f284281269443", "assetId": "img:c4d8c6f6247f1119141625f25c9f45cde3b475bf00cb73b2019f284281269443", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000091", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021839.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Copper pyrites is an ore of copper that contains copper, iron and sulfur.", "marks": 9, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The percentage composition by mass of copper pyrites is Cu 34.60% Fe 30.52% S 34.88% Show, by calculation, that the empirical formula of copper pyrites is $ \\mathrm{C u F e S_{2}} $", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Copper is obtained from copper pyrites in a two-stage process. Stage 1 Copper pyrites is heated in air. $2 \\mathrm {C u F e S} _ {2} + 3 \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {C u S} + 2 \\mathrm {F e O} + 2 \\mathrm {S O} _ {2}$ Stage 2 The copper(II) sulfide is separated and then heated in air. It reacts with oxygen to form copper and sulfur dioxide.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why the sulfur in the reaction in stage 1 is described as being oxidised.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-2f82da9d-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for the reaction that occurs in stage 2.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-2f82da9d-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Sulfur dioxide dissolves in water to form an acidic solution.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the ion that causes this solution to be acidic.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-2f82da9d-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State how litmus paper can be used to show that the solution is acidic.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-2f82da9d-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give two observations that are made when a piece of magnesium ribbon is added to the acidic solution.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-2f82da9d-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "In an experiment, a student adds a piece of zinc to some dilute hydrochloric acid in a test tube. The student measures the temperature before adding the zinc. After adding the zinc, he stirs the mixture and measures the highest temperature reached. The diagram shows his results.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Use the readings to complete the table, giving all values to the nearest $ 0. 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $", "label": "a", "marks": 2, "partId": "q08-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student wants to find out if there is a relationship between the reactivity of a metal and the temperature rise. He repeats the experiment four times, using a different metal each time. The table shows his results.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State three factors that the student should keep constant in each experiment.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-2f82da9d-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Using information from the table, state the relationship between the reactivity of a metal and the temperature rise.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-2f82da9d-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why there is no temperature rise when gold is added to the acid.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-2f82da9d-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021863.png", "width": 585, "format": "png", "height": 580, "sha256": "b4eb378d47f4287d2fbef685a8c82edf8c95ff25d3e052a1646de55749335b9b", "assetId": "img:b4eb378d47f4287d2fbef685a8c82edf8c95ff25d3e052a1646de55749335b9b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000118", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021863.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789044021871.png", "width": 168, "format": "png", "height": 670, "sha256": "28febed26d5e1324152e53d0ec88062278fe0f5f70a5f08adb54e8058b4244a9", "assetId": "img:28febed26d5e1324152e53d0ec88062278fe0f5f70a5f08adb54e8058b4244a9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000122", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789044021871.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789044021880.png", "width": 167, "format": "png", "height": 672, "sha256": "d2f4b7c2a2f29e7517f762afe744bb881d97e45de24be387d91fb7744013da61", "assetId": "img:d2f4b7c2a2f29e7517f762afe744bb881d97e45de24be387d91fb7744013da61", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000123", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789044021880.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q08-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000126", "e000130"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The ions present in ionic compounds can be identified using simple tests. some cations (positive ions) can be identified using a flame test some anions (negative ions) can be identified by observing reactions in solutions of the compounds Table 1 shows the flame test colours for four cations. Table 1 Table 2 shows the results of three tests used to identify anions in solution. Use the information in the tables to answer these questions.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "In the tests, compound X gives a red flame and produces effervescence when hydrochloric acid is added. Suggest two possible identities for compound X.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In the tests, compound Y gives a blue flame and produces a yellow colour when methyl orange is added. A student concludes that compound Y is tantalum hydroxide. Give two reasons why this conclusion may not be correct.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which additional test from Table 2 would show that the only anion in compound Y is the hydroxide ion?", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-2f82da9d-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "An aqueous solution contains either carbonate ions or hydrogencarbonate ions. Using only information from the tables, explain how you could decide if the solution contains carbonate or hydrogencarbonate ions.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 9, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q09-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000139", "e000142"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student uses this apparatus to investigate the heat energy released when nitric acid is added to potassium hydroxide solution. She uses this method. put $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of potassium hydroxide solution into the polystyrene cup measure the temperature of the potassium hydroxide solution add 5.00 $ cm^{3} $ of nitric acid from the burette stir the mixture and measure the highest temperature reached add further 5.00 $ cm^{3} $ samples of nitric acid, stir and measure the highest temperature reached after each addition", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name the piece of apparatus that should be used to measure the $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of potassium hydroxide solution.", "label": "a", "marks": 1, "partId": "q10-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the student''s results.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The result for $ 2 0. 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of acid is anomalous. Suggest two possible mistakes, other than misreading the thermometer, that the student might have made to produce the anomalous result.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-2f82da9d-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest a true value for the temperature when $ 2 0. 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of acid is added.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-2f82da9d-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In another experiment, the student records these results. Calculate the heat energy released using the equation $Q = m \\times 4. 1 8 \\times \\Delta T$ $ Q=\\mathrm{t h e a t e n e r g y r e l e a s e d i n J} $ $ m=\\mathrm{m a s s o f t h e m i t u r e i n g} $ $ \\Delta T=\\mathrm{c h a n g e i n t e m e r t a r e i n} \\mathrm{^{\\circ} C} $ [assume mass of $ 1. 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of the mixture is $ 1. 0 0 \\mathrm{g} $]", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["heat energy released"]}], "number": 10, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021905.png", "width": 804, "format": "png", "height": 720, "sha256": "4d230425b31783d9f31aa3ad6a3c697e9ae81988296ff6aafd8f32ade6c21686", "assetId": "img:4d230425b31783d9f31aa3ad6a3c697e9ae81988296ff6aafd8f32ade6c21686", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000155", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021905.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q10-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000165", "e000170"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about titanium and its compounds.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Titanium is a metal. The diagram shows the arrangement of the particles in titanium.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021927.png", "width": 1246, "format": "png", "height": 339, "sha256": "f09b1eccb479af5615ce1a1a7aec1457e670862118ae817a7ad5c398009714d8", "assetId": "img:f09b1eccb479af5615ce1a1a7aec1457e670862118ae817a7ad5c398009714d8", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000182", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021927.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why metals such as titanium are good conductors of electricity.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-2f82da9d-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why metals such as titanium are malleable.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-2f82da9d-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Titanium(IV) chloride, $ \\mathrm{T i C l_{4}} $ , and titanium(IV) oxide, $ \\mathrm{T i O_{2}} $ , are both covalent compounds. $ \\mathrm{T i C l_{4}} $ is a liquid at room temperature. $ \\mathrm{T i O_{2}} $ is a solid with a high melting point. Explain these properties in terms of the structures of the two compounds.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A mixture of titanium(IV) oxide and carbon reacts with chlorine to form titanium(IV) chloride and carbon dioxide. Write a chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Titanium(IV) chloride reacts with magnesium to form titanium and magnesium chloride, $ \\mathrm{M g C l_{2}} $ Write a chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-2f82da9d-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 11, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021927.png", "width": 1246, "format": "png", "height": 339, "sha256": "f09b1eccb479af5615ce1a1a7aec1457e670862118ae817a7ad5c398009714d8", "assetId": "img:f09b1eccb479af5615ce1a1a7aec1457e670862118ae817a7ad5c398009714d8", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000182", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021927.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q11-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A mixture of carbon monoxide, carbon dioxide and hydrogen is known in industry as synthesis gas. Synthesis gas is converted to methanol, $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} O H} $ , by passing it over a heated solid catalyst. The equations for the two reactions are $\\begin{array}{l l l l} \\mathrm {R e a c t i o n 1} & \\mathrm {C O (g)} + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {C H} _ {3} \\mathrm {O H} (\\mathrm {g}) & \\Delta H = - 9 1 \\mathrm {k J / m o l} \\\\ \\mathrm {R e a c t i o n 2} & \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) + 3 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {C H} _ {3} \\mathrm {O H} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {g}) & \\Delta H = - 4 9 \\mathrm {k J / m o l} \\end{array}$", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Assume that both reactions reach a position of equilibrium.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "For reaction 1, predict whether using a high or a low temperature would produce the higher yield of methanol. Give a reason for your choice. prediction reason ...", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-2f82da9d-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "For reaction 2, predict whether using a high or a low pressure would produce the higher yield of methanol. Give a reason for your choice. prediction reason ...", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-2f82da9d-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The catalyst increases the rate of both the forward reaction and the backward reaction. Suggest why the catalyst has no effect on the position of equilibrium.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Reaction 1 can be represented by a reaction profile diagram.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021991.png", "width": 842, "format": "png", "height": 721, "sha256": "b6c6f59c32e4d6aa088d3e539db1d75be0349b264128e3968a9d138d0c002ffc", "assetId": "img:b6c6f59c32e4d6aa088d3e539db1d75be0349b264128e3968a9d138d0c002ffc", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000203", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021991.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the profile by showing the products of the reaction and the enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ , for the reaction.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-2f82da9d-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw an arrow on the profile to represent the activation energy for the forward reaction. Label this arrow E.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-2f82da9d-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the effect, if any, of the catalyst on the enthalpy change for the reaction.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-2f82da9d-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 12, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044021991.png", "width": 842, "format": "png", "height": 721, "sha256": "b6c6f59c32e4d6aa088d3e539db1d75be0349b264128e3968a9d138d0c002ffc", "assetId": "img:b6c6f59c32e4d6aa088d3e539db1d75be0349b264128e3968a9d138d0c002ffc", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000203", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044021991.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q12-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Calcium carbonate decomposes when heated. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {C a C O} _ {3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C a O} + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2}$", "marks": 10, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the maximum mass of CaO that could be obtained when 20 tonnes of $ \\mathrm{C a C O_{3}} $ is decomposed. Give the unit. $[ M _ {\\mathrm {r}} \\mathrm {o f C a O} = 5 6; \\quad M _ {\\mathrm {r}} \\mathrm {o f C a C O} _ {3} = 1 0 0; \\quad 1 \\mathrm {t o n n e} = 1 0 ^ {6} \\mathrm {g} ]$ $\\mathrm {m a s s o f C a O} =$", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Slaked lime, $ \\mathrm{C a ( O H )_{2}} $ forms when water is added to calcium oxide. Give the chemical name of slaked lime.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Slaked lime is often added to soil to raise the pH of the soil. A chemist neutralises $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of $ 0. 5 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ hydrochloric acid with slaked lime. $\\mathrm {C a} (\\mathrm {O H}) _ {2} + 2 \\mathrm {H C l} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C a C l} _ {2} + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the amount, in moles, of HCl that is neutralised. $\\mathrm {a m o u n t o f H C I} =$", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-2f82da9d-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the minimum mass, in grams, of $ \\mathrm{C a ( O H )}_{2} $ required to neutralise the HCl. $[ M _ {\\mathrm {r}} \\mathrm {o f C a (O H)} _ {2} = 7 4 ]$ $\\mathrm {m i n i u m m a s s o f C a (O H) _ {2}} =$", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-2f82da9d-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A clear solution of slaked lime is made by dissolving $ \\mathrm{C a ( O H )_{2}} $ in an excess of water. This solution is left exposed to air. The solution slowly goes milky as a faint white precipitate forms. Explain why a faint white precipitate forms.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-2f82da9d-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 13, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q13-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The table shows the displayed formulae of six hydrocarbons, Q, R, S, T, U and V.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which two hydrocarbons will instantly decolourise bromine water? A R and V B Q and U C S and T D Q and T", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which two hydrocarbons have the general formula $ C_{n} H_{2 n+2} $? A R and V B Q and U C S and T D Q and T", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which hydrocarbon is an isomer of U? A Q B R C T D V", "label": "c", "marks": 1, "partId": "q14-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which two hydrocarbons have the empirical formula $ \\mathrm{C H_{2}}? $ A R and V B Q and S C Rand S D T and U", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-2f82da9d-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The substitution reaction between hydrocarbon T and bromine is similar to the reaction between methane and bromine.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-2f82da9d-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State a condition, other than temperature, that is required for this reaction to take place.", "label": "e-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-2f82da9d-pe-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest a displayed formula for a possible organic product of the reaction between hydrocarbon T and bromine.", "label": "e-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-2f82da9d-pe-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 14, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q14-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000227"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The flow diagram shows the main stages in an industrial process to manufacture ammonia.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of this industrial process.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify gases A and B. gas B.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the purpose of the condenser.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the catalyst that is used in the reactor.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-2f82da9d-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest two reasons why the unreacted gases are recycled.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-2f82da9d-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The reaction to make ammonia is reversible and can reach a position of equilibrium. The graph shows the percentage yield of ammonia at equilibrium, and at different temperatures and pressures. Percentage yield of ammonia at equilibrium (%)", "label": "f", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-2f82da9d-pf", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044022058.png", "width": 974, "format": "png", "height": 911, "sha256": "795d07b579122f9aa4ed0992b680327b5cff29cf345b23ac2b0f26c5ccf44a67", "assetId": "img:795d07b579122f9aa4ed0992b680327b5cff29cf345b23ac2b0f26c5ccf44a67", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000263", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044022058.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the conditions of temperature and pressure that would produce the largest percentage yield of ammonia.", "label": "f-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-2f82da9d-pf-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Find the percentage yield of ammonia at equilibrium, at a pressure of 200 atmospheres and a temperature of $ 4 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $", "label": "f-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-2f82da9d-pf-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why, in the industrial process, the percentage yield of ammonia at 200 atmospheres and $ 4 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ is only 15%.", "label": "f-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-2f82da9d-pf-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 15, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044022046.png", "width": 1250, "format": "png", "height": 632, "sha256": "8a4984b02b6d693c49331bc7be3cf1b7cb9bbaf007bd9786294734c2d9b01bf4", "assetId": "img:8a4984b02b6d693c49331bc7be3cf1b7cb9bbaf007bd9786294734c2d9b01bf4", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000254", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044022046.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044022058.png", "width": 974, "format": "png", "height": 911, "sha256": "795d07b579122f9aa4ed0992b680327b5cff29cf345b23ac2b0f26c5ccf44a67", "assetId": "img:795d07b579122f9aa4ed0992b680327b5cff29cf345b23ac2b0f26c5ccf44a67", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000263", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044022058.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q15-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The mineral rozenite contains crystals of hydrated iron(II) sulfate, $ \\mathrm{F e S O_{4}. x H_{2} O} $ She uses this apparatus to remove and collect the water of crystallisation from a sample of iron(II) sulfate crystals. She uses this method. weigh empty tube A to find its mass place a sample of hydrated iron(II) sulfate crystals into tube A and reweigh heat tube A allow tube A to cool and reweigh repeat the process until the mass no longer changes Heating until the mass no longer changes is known as heating to constant mass. When iron(II) sulfate crystals are heated gently, they decompose according to this equation. $\\mathrm {F e S O} _ {4}. x \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {F e S O} _ {4} + x \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ These are the student''s results. A student wants to find the value of x.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State why it is necessary to heat the crystals to constant mass.", "label": "a", "marks": 1, "partId": "q16-2f82da9d-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the mass of $ \\mathrm{F e S O_{4}} $ formed after heating to constant mass. $\\mathrm {m a s s o f F e S O} _ {4} \\mathrm {f o r m e d} =$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q16-2f82da9d-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the mass of water collected in tube B after heating to constant mass.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q16-2f82da9d-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the value for x in the formula $ \\mathrm{F e S O_{4}. x H_{2} O} $ Give your answer to the nearest whole number. $ [ M_{r} $ of $ \\mathrm{F e S O_{4}}=1 5 2; M_{r} $ of $ \\mathrm{H_{2} O}=1 8] $", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q16-2f82da9d-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When the student adds the water from tube B to anhydrous copper(II) sulfate, she observes that the mixture gets hot and that there is a colour change from white to blue. Explain these observations.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q16-2f82da9d-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 16, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044022080.png", "width": 1278, "format": "png", "height": 485, "sha256": "2220c761ced6bdaab7919b4d43a0aee410f98274c5dce96c8de8468979f80f6f", "assetId": "img:2220c761ced6bdaab7919b4d43a0aee410f98274c5dce96c8de8468979f80f6f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000270", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044022080.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q16-2f82da9d", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000281"]}], "paperTotal": 120, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 5, "2": 4, "3": 6, "4": 7, "5": 7, "6": 7, "7": 9, "8": 7, "9": 8, "10": 7, "13": 10, "14": 6, "16": 8}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "January 2018", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH0_1C_1801_MS", "canonicalDocumentId": "e801c627-c540-560b-a755-863cbd1f231e"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-e801c627-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "N"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT 8"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A(1)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(e)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D(7)"}], "answerText": "Si", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 3, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-e801c627-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Starting state"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Finishing state"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 mark for each correct row"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ice to water"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "solid iodine to iodine vapour"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Z"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "X"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "molten iron to solid iron"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Y"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Z"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ethene to (poly)ethene"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "X"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Z"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D(sublimation)"}], "answerText": "Change", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3", "marks": 2, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-e801c627-q3", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M1(crystals)-get smaller"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT disappear"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE dissolve"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE reference to(incorrect) colours/loses colour"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE mass decreases"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW pink"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE goes cloudy"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW(water)turns to colour of crystals"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT other incorrect observat