G", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "June 2018", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": "Thursday 17 May 2018 – Morning", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH0/1C", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "e6282607-c214-5df2-87e6-5622a573e9b3"}, "warnings": ["unclassified heading: Diagram 1", "unclassified heading: Experiment 2", "Q14: incomplete MCQ option set folded into stem", "Q15: incomplete MCQ option set folded into stem"], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Chromatography can be used to separate the substances in a mixture.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Diagram 1 shows the apparatus used to separate the different dyes in a food colouring. The box lists some terms used in chromatography. Use the terms from the box to label diagram 1.", "label": "a", "marks": 3, "partId": "q01-e6282607-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789054674008.png", "width": 1702, "format": "png", "height": 631, "sha256": "a911a39639d939adbb9d4aa3fc79719dfdf2de5e66609da551eb5f2f41c2c0e1", "assetId": "img:a911a39639d939adbb9d4aa3fc79719dfdf2de5e66609da551eb5f2f41c2c0e1", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000024", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789054674008.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Diagram 2 shows a chromatogram produced using four different food colourings, P, Q, R and S. $\\begin{array}{c c c c c} \\hline \\mathrm {P} & \\mathrm {Q} & \\mathrm {R} & \\mathrm {S} \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}$ finish Diagram 2", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-e6282607-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789054674018.png", "width": 336, "format": "png", "height": 464, "sha256": "7f512c7a81e808d9d4991cb338c48535d7fd35d0dd087c8bf24d336d290f5283", "assetId": "img:7f512c7a81e808d9d4991cb338c48535d7fd35d0dd087c8bf24d336d290f5283", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000030", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789054674018.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which food colouring contains only one dye? A P B Q C R D S", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-e6282607-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which food colourings have one dye in common? A P, Q and R B P, R and S C Q, R and S D P, Q, R and S", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-e6282607-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which food colouring contains the largest number of dyes.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-e6282607-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789054674008.png", "width": 1702, "format": "png", "height": 631, "sha256": "a911a39639d939adbb9d4aa3fc79719dfdf2de5e66609da551eb5f2f41c2c0e1", "assetId": "img:a911a39639d939adbb9d4aa3fc79719dfdf2de5e66609da551eb5f2f41c2c0e1", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000024", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789054674008.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789054674018.png", "width": 336, "format": "png", "height": 464, "sha256": "7f512c7a81e808d9d4991cb338c48535d7fd35d0dd087c8bf24d336d290f5283", "assetId": "img:7f512c7a81e808d9d4991cb338c48535d7fd35d0dd087c8bf24d336d290f5283", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000030", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789054674018.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q01-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram shows the apparatus used to prepare carbon dioxide in the laboratory.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "What is the name of the piece of apparatus containing the dilute hydrochloric acid? A burette B pipette C tap funnel D thistle funnel", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-e6282607-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-e6282607-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_2_1789054674057.png", "width": 1107, "format": "png", "height": 57, "sha256": "70b9554b4d1dacf78e9d3feffeb5ccb6f3d2a031d87e94536b9f7de2696126dd", "assetId": "img:70b9554b4d1dacf78e9d3feffeb5ccb6f3d2a031d87e94536b9f7de2696126dd", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000053", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789054674057.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these is a true statement about carbon dioxide? A it turns red litmus blue B it turns limewater milky C it relights a glowing spill D it burns with a squeaky pop", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-e6282607-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows how carbon dioxide is collected by downward delivery in air.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-e6282607-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why carbon dioxide can be collected by downward delivery in air.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-e6282607-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give another method of collecting carbon dioxide.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-e6282607-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When carbon dioxide dissolves in water, a weakly acidic solution forms. Suggest a pH value for this solution.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-e6282607-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Carbon dioxide also forms when copper(II) carbonate is decomposed by heating. The equation for this reaction is $\\mathrm {C u C O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C u O} (\\mathrm {s}) + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ State the change in colour of the solid when copper(II) carbonate decomposes. from", "label": "f", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-e6282607-pf", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest two properties of carbon dioxide that make it suitable for use in fire extinguishers.", "label": "g", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-e6282607-pg", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789054674049.png", "width": 1263, "format": "png", "height": 712, "sha256": "2dbdd173124f0beb870adde25efbb275715c0dbd2e97c9a4ab45f40915a3134f", "assetId": "img:2dbdd173124f0beb870adde25efbb275715c0dbd2e97c9a4ab45f40915a3134f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000046", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789054674049.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789054674057.png", "width": 1107, "format": "png", "height": 57, "sha256": "70b9554b4d1dacf78e9d3feffeb5ccb6f3d2a031d87e94536b9f7de2696126dd", "assetId": "img:70b9554b4d1dacf78e9d3feffeb5ccb6f3d2a031d87e94536b9f7de2696126dd", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000053", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789054674057.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A teacher investigates the reaction between sodium and water. The teacher fills a trough with water. She adds a few drops of litmus solution to the water, and then adds a piece of sodium.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The sodium floats on the water. It reacts with the water and produces bubbles of hydrogen gas.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-e6282607-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State two other observations that are made during the reaction.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-e6282607-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Balance the equation for the reaction between sodium and water. Include the state symbols.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-e6282607-pa-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_2_1789054674086.png", "width": 1364, "format": "png", "height": 62, "sha256": "d85a246dddb3baf7269cd14aa3a47a557577446c6126f46d04bd0cd2c23d3b30", "assetId": "img:d85a246dddb3baf7269cd14aa3a47a557577446c6126f46d04bd0cd2c23d3b30", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000077", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789054674086.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Lithium and potassium react in a similar way to sodium when added to water.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-e6282607-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why they have a similar reaction in terms of the electronic configurations of their atoms.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-e6282607-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Place the elements lithium, potassium and sodium in order of reactivity. most reactive least reactive", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-e6282607-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789054674077.png", "width": 1150, "format": "png", "height": 213, "sha256": "35b4febfb998f5017b19ddc8d381eb75c6fa3a40db2df3069dfdd11f054580b3", "assetId": "img:35b4febfb998f5017b19ddc8d381eb75c6fa3a40db2df3069dfdd11f054580b3", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000073", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789054674077.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789054674086.png", "width": 1364, "format": "png", "height": 62, "sha256": "d85a246dddb3baf7269cd14aa3a47a557577446c6126f46d04bd0cd2c23d3b30", "assetId": "img:d85a246dddb3baf7269cd14aa3a47a557577446c6126f46d04bd0cd2c23d3b30", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000077", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789054674086.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Use the Periodic Table on page 2 to help you answer this question.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which word correctly describes substances found in the Periodic Table? A alloys B compounds C elements D mixtures", "label": "a", "marks": 1, "partId": "q04-e6282607-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The substances in the Periodic Table are arranged in order of increasing A atomic number B mass number C nucleon number D relative atomic mass", "label": "b", "marks": 1, "partId": "q04-e6282607-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table lists properties of some of the gases in Group 0 of the Periodic Table. Complete the table by giving the symbol for neon an estimate for the boiling point of argon the reaction of xenon with metals", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-e6282607-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The photograph shows an electric light bulb. The tungsten filament becomes very hot when the light bulb is switched on. Suggest why argon is a more suitable gas than air to use in the light bulb.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-e6282607-pd", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789054674110.png", "width": 1007, "format": "png", "height": 601, "sha256": "10fd12e703fec9943d0130df3efc9fd1d1e74a007d12545f0404b20b75906aef", "assetId": "img:10fd12e703fec9943d0130df3efc9fd1d1e74a007d12545f0404b20b75906aef", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000104", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789054674110.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789054674110.png", "width": 1007, "format": "png", "height": 601, "sha256": "10fd12e703fec9943d0130df3efc9fd1d1e74a007d12545f0404b20b75906aef", "assetId": "img:10fd12e703fec9943d0130df3efc9fd1d1e74a007d12545f0404b20b75906aef", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000104", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789054674110.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000098"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student tries to make a pure, dry sample of hydrated cobalt(II) chloride crystals. He uses dilute hydrochloric acid and solid cobalt(II) oxide. This is the student''s method. Step 1 pour about $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of dilute hydrochloric acid into a beaker Step 2 warm the acid using a Bunsen burner Step 3 add a small amount of cobalt(II) oxide and stir the mixture with a glass rod Step 4 add further small amounts of cobalt(II) oxide until it stops reacting Step 5 filter the final mixture and collect the filtrate in an evaporating basin Step 6 leave the filtrate until all of the water has evaporated His sample of cobalt(II) oxide contains a small amount of a solid impurity that dissolves in water, but does not react with the acid.", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State why it is not necessary to have a precise measurement of the volume of hydrochloric acid in step 1.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-e6282607-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why the acid is warmed in step 2.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-e6282607-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why a glass rod, rather than a metal spatula, is used to stir the mixture in step 3. (1)", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-e6282607-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State how the student will know when the cobalt(II) oxide stops reacting in step 4.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-e6282607-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why the method used in step 6 will not produce a pure sample of hydrated cobalt(II) chloride crystals.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-e6282607-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe how the student could produce a pure, dry sample of crystals from the filtrate in step 5.", "label": "f", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-e6282607-pf", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the formula and colour of three different types of cobalt(II) chloride. When water is added very slowly to solid $ \\mathrm{C o C l_{2}} $ , the colour of $ \\mathrm{C o C l_{2}} $ changes from blue to purple and then to pink.", "label": "g", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-e6282607-pg", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for the change from the purple solid to the pink solid.", "label": "g-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-e6282607-pg-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these words describes the change taking place when the pink solid is heated to form the blue solid? A crystallisation B dehydration C hydration D redox", "label": "g-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-e6282607-pg-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000122"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Tests are done on a sample of a solid, X. Solid X contains the ammonium ion, $ \\mathrm{N H_{4}^{+}} $ , one other cation and one anion. The table lists details of the tests done on solid X and the observations made for each test.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the gas given off in test 1.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-e6282607-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the formula of the other cation present in solid X.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-e6282607-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what test 3A and test 3B tell you about solid X. test 3A test 3B", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-e6282607-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the anion in solid X.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-e6282607-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000134"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Antimony, Sb, is an element in Group 5 of the Periodic Table. The mineral, stibnite, contains antimony sulfide, $ \\mathrm{S b_{2} S_{3}} $ Antimony can be obtained from stibnite in a two-stage process. Stage 1 stibnite is roasted in air $\\mathrm {S b} _ {2} \\mathrm {S} _ {3} + 5 \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {S b} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {4} + 3 \\mathrm {S O} _ {2}$ Stage 2 the oxide produced is heated with carbon to form antimony and carbon dioxide (a) (i) State why the sulfur in stage 1 is said to be oxidised. (ii) Complete the equation for the reaction in stage 2. $\\mathrm {S b} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {4} + \\dots \\dots \\dots \\mathrm {C} \\rightarrow \\dots \\dots \\dots \\mathrm {S b} + \\dots \\dots \\dots \\mathrm {C O} _ {2}$", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Bismuth is another element in Group 5 of the Periodic Table. Bismuth forms an oxide, $ \\mathrm{B i_{2} O_{3}} $ , which has a giant ionic structure.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-e6282607-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the formula of the bismuth ion in bismuth oxide.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-e6282607-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why bismuth oxide has a high melting point.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-e6282607-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Bismuth oxide reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to form bismuth chloride. Write a chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-e6282607-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student investigates the rate of reaction between zinc and hydrochloric acid, using an excess of zinc powder. She uses this apparatus. The student measures the volume of gas in the syringe every minute for ten minutes. The table shows her results.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Plot the student''s results on the grid.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-e6282607-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a curve of best fit. Volume of gas in $ cm^{3} $ Time in minutes", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-e6282607-pa-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_2_1789054674188.png", "width": 1246, "format": "png", "height": 790, "sha256": "79cde7c8eda2fbcf7a4b1d0acda9c80aacce6aa8345bb5464d84b2aef33cb2e5", "assetId": "img:79cde7c8eda2fbcf7a4b1d0acda9c80aacce6aa8345bb5464d84b2aef33cb2e5", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000165", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789054674188.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The result at two minutes is anomalous.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-e6282607-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest a mistake that the student could have made to produce this anomalous result.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-e6282607-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use your graph to estimate the volume of gas that was given off at two minutes. Show clearly on your graph how you obtain your answer.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-e6282607-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["volume of gas"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why the last four readings for the volume of gas are the same.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-e6282607-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State how the graph shows that the rate of reaction decreases during the first seven minutes.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-e6282607-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain, in terms of the particle collision theory, why the rate of reaction decreases during the first seven minutes.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-e6282607-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789054674181.png", "width": 1409, "format": "png", "height": 438, "sha256": "53340e7a12775a860698c7463d8b7bf0456594a7dec6d2a93bfa20aeaea0aa4f", "assetId": "img:53340e7a12775a860698c7463d8b7bf0456594a7dec6d2a93bfa20aeaea0aa4f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000159", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789054674181.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789054674188.png", "width": 1246, "format": "png", "height": 790, "sha256": "79cde7c8eda2fbcf7a4b1d0acda9c80aacce6aa8345bb5464d84b2aef33cb2e5", "assetId": "img:79cde7c8eda2fbcf7a4b1d0acda9c80aacce6aa8345bb5464d84b2aef33cb2e5", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000165", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789054674188.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q08-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000161"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This apparatus is used to test whether magnesium, solid magnesium chloride and an aqueous solution of magnesium chloride conduct electricity. The table shows the results. Explain these results, with reference to the type of particles in each substance.", "marks": 6, "parts": [], "number": 9, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789054674203.png", "width": 841, "format": "png", "height": 464, "sha256": "659bfb8210fea7c5f5b67958e6945c842c60f8d7df4f9d0e697a49eab832af20", "assetId": "img:659bfb8210fea7c5f5b67958e6945c842c60f8d7df4f9d0e697a49eab832af20", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000176", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789054674203.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q09-e6282607", "answerPrompts": ["(6)"], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000178"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Bromine is a red-brown liquid at room temperature. Liquid bromine forms a brown gas when warmed.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Explain what happens to the bromine molecules when liquid bromine is warmed to form a gas.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-e6282607-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Bromine reacts with water to form a mixture of hydrobromic acid, HBr, and hypobromous acid, HBrO. Write a chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-e6282607-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Hydrobromic acid reacts with magnesium carbonate to form a solution containing magnesium bromide. $\\mathrm {M g C O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {H B r} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {M g B r} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {I}) + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ Crystals of hydrated magnesium bromide, $ \\mathrm{M g B r}_{2}. 6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} $ , can be obtained from this solution. $\\mathrm {M g B r} _ {2} + 6 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {M g B r} _ {2}. 6 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-e6282607-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "An excess of hydrobromic acid is reacted with 0.125 mol of magnesium carbonate. Show, by calculation, that the maximum theoretical mass of hydrated magnesium bromide that can be made is 36.5 g. $ [ M_{r} $ of $ \\mathrm{M g B r_{2}. 6 H_{2} O}=2 9 2 ] $", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-e6282607-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In an experiment using 0.125 mol of magnesium carbonate, with an excess of hydrobromic acid, the mass of hydrated magnesium bromide obtained is 26.4 g. Suggest two reasons why the actual mass obtained is less than the maximum theoretical mass.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-e6282607-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 10, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q10-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Malachite is an ore of copper containing copper(II) carbonate and several other compounds that are insoluble in water. You are supplied with several pieces of malachite, these chemicals and items of apparatus. Chemicals: dilute sulfuric acid magnesium powder Apparatus: beakers filter funnel and paper pestle and mortar Describe how you would use the chemicals and the apparatus to obtain a sample of copper from the malachite.", "marks": 6, "parts": [], "number": 11, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q11-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Crude oil is a mixture of hydrocarbons. Fractional distillation of crude oil and cracking of hydrocarbon fractions are two of the processes used in an oil refinery.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which property of hydrocarbons is used to separate crude oil into fractions? A boiling point B chemical reactivity C density D melting point", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-e6282607-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "These are the main fractions obtained from crude oil. bitumen diesel gasoline fuel oil kerosene refinery gases", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-e6282607-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give one use for the refinery gases.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-e6282607-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give one use for kerosene.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-e6282607-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State which fraction is the most viscous.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-e6282607-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Catalytic cracking is used to break down long-chain alkanes into shorter-chain alkanes and alkenes.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-e6282607-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the catalyst used in industrial cracking.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-e6282607-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the temperature used in industrial cracking.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-e6282607-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Tetradecane $ \\mathrm{(C_{1 4} H_{3 0})} $ can be cracked to make ethene $ \\mathrm{(C_{2} H_{4})} $ and only one other hydrocarbon. Write a chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-e6282607-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw the displayed formula of ethene.", "label": "c-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-e6282607-pc-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the polymer formed from ethene.", "label": "c-v", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-e6282607-pc-v", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why this polymer is difficult to dispose of.", "label": "c-vi", "marks": null, "partId": "q12-e6282607-pc-vi", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 12, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q12-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student investigates the reaction between zinc and dilute sulfuric acid. She uses this method. put $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of dilute sulfuric acid into a polystyrene cup measure the initial temperature of the acid add 2.0g of zinc to the acid and stir the mixture measure the temperature of the mixture after one minute The student does the experiment three times. For each experiment, she uses the same size pieces of zinc but different concentrations of sulfuric acid. The diagram shows the temperatures for each experiment. Experiment 1 1. 0 mol/dm $ ^{3} $ $ \\mathrm{H_{2} S O_{4}} $ temperature after one minute Experiment 3", "marks": 10, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Record the temperature readings in the table and calculate the temperature increase for each experiment. Give all values to the nearest 0.5 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-e6282607-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why the temperature increase changes as the concentration of the sulfuric acid increases.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-e6282607-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student does another experiment at the same initial temperature as experiment 3. She uses the same size pieces of zinc but uses $ 2 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of dilute sulfuric acid. The acid is in excess in both reactions.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-e6282607-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain the effect, if any, of this change on the initial rate of reaction when compared to experiment 3.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-e6282607-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain the effect, if any, of this change on the temperature increase when compared to experiment 3.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q13-e6282607-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 13, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789054674289.png", "width": 268, "format": "png", "height": 414, "sha256": "fe256dc18b7f23f27c046d49ff31afd12af68ee80362a581654a1dc276dd3547", "assetId": "img:fe256dc18b7f23f27c046d49ff31afd12af68ee80362a581654a1dc276dd3547", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000241", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789054674289.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789054674294.png", "width": 322, "format": "png", "height": 413, "sha256": "1986f8c1a11b6b1c8e436318471721d27f9e4124115ee9437540bd2e3af3d1e1", "assetId": "img:1986f8c1a11b6b1c8e436318471721d27f9e4124115ee9437540bd2e3af3d1e1", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000242", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789054674294.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q13-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000245"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Iron deficiency anaemia occurs when the body does not have enough iron(II) ions. Iron deficiency can be overcome by taking iron tablets. weigh an iron tablet dissolve the tablet in an excess of dilute sulfuric acid titrate the solution with potassium permanganate solution, $ \\mathrm{K M n O}_{4} $ The table shows her results. A chemist wants to find out the percentage of iron(II) ion $ \\mathrm{(F e^{2+})} $ in an iron tablet. She uses this method.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the amount, in moles, of $ \\mathrm{K M n O_{4}} $ in $ 1 7. 4 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of $ 0. 0 2 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ potassium permanganate solution. $\\mathrm {a m o u n t o f K M n O} _ {4} =$", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-e6282607-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In the titration, 1 mol of $ \\mathrm{K M n O_{4}} $ reacts with 5 mol of $ \\mathrm{F e}^{2+}. $ Calculate the amount, in moles, of $ \\mathrm{F e}^{2+} $ in the iron tablet. $\\mathrm {a m o u n t o f F e} ^ {2 +} =$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-e6282607-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the mass, in grams, of $ \\mathrm{F e}^{2+} $ in the iron tablet. $ [ A_{\\mathrm{r}} $ of $ \\mathrm{F e}^{2+}=5 6. 0 ] $ $\\mathrm {m a s s o f F e} ^ {2 +} =$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-e6282607-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the percentage by mass of $ \\mathrm{F e}^{2+} $ in the iron tablet. $\\mathrm {F e} ^ {2 +}$", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q14-e6282607-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 14, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q14-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000258"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A teacher uses this apparatus to demonstrate the electrolysis of molten zinc chloride. crystals of a shiny, grey solid form at one of the electrodes a pale green substance forms at the other electrode the lamp goes out after the teacher stops heating the zinc chloride A student records these observations.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State what is meant by the term electrolysis.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-e6282607-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why graphite is more suitable to use for the electrodes than magnesium in this electrolysis.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-e6282607-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these is a correct statement for this electrolysis? A the pale green substance is chloride B both products are elements C the pale green substance forms at the negative electrode D the shiny grey solid is zinc chloride", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-e6282607-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student writes this ionic half-equation for the reaction that forms the pale green substance. $2 \\mathrm {C l} ^ {-} + 2 \\mathrm {e} ^ {-} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {C l}$ Identify the two mistakes in her ionic half-equation.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-e6282607-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The lamp goes out after the teacher stops heating the zinc chloride, because electrons are no longer flowing through the wires. Explain why electrons are no longer flowing through the wires. TOTAL MARKS FOR PAPER = 120 MARKS Every effort has been made to contact copyright holders to obtain their permission for the use of copyright material. Pearson Education Ltd. will, if notified, be happy to rectify any errors or omissions and include any such rectifications in future editions.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q15-e6282607-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 15, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789054674329.png", "width": 1155, "format": "png", "height": 799, "sha256": "4609deab96e31e1bfe7742f2ed551c2a608a2ce2d705f319e53c962d12126883", "assetId": "img:4609deab96e31e1bfe7742f2ed551c2a608a2ce2d705f319e53c962d12126883", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000270", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789054674329.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q15-e6282607", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 120, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"2": 11, "3": 6, "4": 7, "5": 12, "6": 5, "7": 7, "8": 11, "9": 6, "10": 8, "11": 6, "13": 10, "14": 5, "15": 8}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "Summer 2018", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "696bac95-194a-5fda-88ce-46414134f1c1"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 3, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Four correct scores3Two correct scores2One correct scores1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C(R)The only correct answer is C because food colouring Rproduces one spot so contains only one dyeA is not correct because food colouring P produces four spots so does not contain only one dyeB is not correct because food colouring Q produces three spots so does not contain only one dyeD is not correct because food colouring S produces two spots so does not contain only one dye"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C(Q,R and S)The only correct answer is C because food colourings Q,R and S have one dye in common as they all produce one spot which has travelled the same distanceA is not correct because P,Q and R do not all produce one spot which has travelled the same distanceB is not correct because P,R and S do not all produce one spot which has travelled the same distanceD is not correct because P,Q,R and S do not all produce one spot which has travelled the same distance"}], "answerText": "如图所示", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(b)(iii)", "marks": 2, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q1biii", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW“four dyes”"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWblobs/dots/marks/points for spots"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2DEP onM1"}], "answerText": "M1P\nM2largest number of/four spots(in the chromatogram)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": true, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "CaCO3+2HCl→CaCl2+CO2+H2O"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1H2O"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 correct balancing"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT multiples"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP on M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Use of lower case letters, incorrect subscript/superscript, penalise M1, but can score M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B(it turns limewater milky)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is B because carbon dioxide turns limewater milky"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not correct because carbon dioxide does not turn red litmus blue"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not correct because carbon dioxide does not relight a glowing spill"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because carbon dioxide does not burn with a squeaky pop"}], "answerText": "C(tap funnel)\nThe only correct answer is C because the apparatus containing the dilute hydrochloric acid is called a tap funnel\nA is not correct because the apparatus containing the dilute hydrochloric acid is not called a burette\nB is not correct because the apparatus containing the dilute hydrochloric acid is not called a pipette\nD is not correct because the apparatus containing the dilute hydrochloric acid is not called a thistle funnel", "confidence": 0.85, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": "C", "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(d)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q2di", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE heavier than air"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE more dense than oxygen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(gas) syringe / over water"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT description of collecting over water"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(e)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "any value between 4(.0)和6.9"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(f)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(from) green"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(to) black"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT shades of green e.g. dark"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Award(1) for both colours correct but in wrong order"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(g)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "any two from:"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1does not support combustion"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2more dense than air"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3can be compressed (into a fire extinguisher cylinder)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4does not conduct electricity"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWdoes not burn / not flammable"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWmore dense than oxygen"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE heavier than air"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to reactivity / cost / not harmful"}], "answerText": "it is more dense than air", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)(i)", "marks": 2, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q3ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW dissolves"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE floats fizzing/bubbles/effervescence"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to flames/sparks/heat produced/explodes"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Na(s)+2H2O(l)→2NaOH(aq)+(1)H2(g)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW multiples and fractions"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(both) contain one electron in the outer(most)/valence shell"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW same number of electrons in the outer(most) shell"}], "answerText": "Any two from:\nM1 sodium gets smaller/disappears\nM2 sodium moves/darts around\nM3 white trail\nM4 melts/forms a ball\nM5 litmus/solution/liquid turns blue\nM1 correct balancing\nM2 correct state symbols\n(most reactive) potassium/K\n(least reactive) lithium/Li", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": true, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q4a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A(atomic number)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is A because the substances found in the Periodic Table (elements) are arranged in order of increasing atomic number"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not correct because the substances found in the Periodic Table (elements) are not arranged in order of increasing mass number"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not correct because the substances found in the Periodic Table (elements) are not arranged in order of increasing nucleon number"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because the substances found in the Periodic Table (elements) are not arranged in order of increasing relative atomic mass"}], "answerText": "C(elements)\nThe only correct answer is C because the substances found in the Periodic Table are elements\nA is not correct because the substances found in the Periodic Table are not alloys\nB is not correct because the substances found in the Periodic Table are not compounds\nD is not correct because the substances found in the Periodic Table are not mixtures", "confidence": 0.85, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": "C", "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(c)", "marks": 3, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q4c", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "REJECTNE/ne/nEIGNORE units"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Gas"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Symbol"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Boiling point"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Reaction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "helium"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "neon"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Ne"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "argon"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "40 to100"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "krypton"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "xenon"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "no reaction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 argon does not react with tungsten/filamentM2(because) argon has full outer shell of electrons/does not(easily) gain or lose or share electronsOR tungsten/filament reacts with oxygen"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW metalALLOW argon is inert/unreactiveALLOW metalALLOW tungsten combusts in oxygen/is oxidised in oxygen"}], "answerText": "", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q5ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Assume“it” refers to the acid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT(because) cobalt(II) oxide is added until no more of it can react"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW(because) cobalt(II) oxide is added until no more of it can dissolve"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "to increase the rate of reaction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTto make reaction faster"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNOREreferences to dissolving the cobalt(II) oxide"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNOREreferences to increases (kinetic) energy / particles move more/faster"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "glass does not react with acid/solution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "metal may/does react with acid/solution"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNOREglass is unreactive"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWso no other/unwanted metal ions form"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWglass is not a good (thermal) conductor andso less likely to burn yourself (or reverse argument for metal)"}], "answerText": "(because) all of the acid/HCl is reacted/used up\nOR\n(because) the cobalt(II) oxide is in excess", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(d)", "marks": 1, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q5d", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW cobalt(II)oxide/it for solidALLOW dissolving for disappearingIGNORE references to fizzing/effervescence/gas given off"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(e)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "the(soluble)impurity will also be present with the(cobalt chloride)crystals"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW the(soluble)impurity remains/won''t be removed by filtration/in Step 5"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(f)"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE any initial steps that try to remove impuritiese.g. filter/washM1heat/boil(the filtrate/evaporating basin)M2until reach crystallisation point/ until solution is concentrated/saturated/ until crystals formon the end of a glass rodM3leave the solution(to cool)andfilter(to remove the crystals)M4wash the crystals(with a smallamount of deionised water)M5dry the crystals on filter/tissuepaper/in a(warm)oven"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW evaporateALLOW until most/some of the water has evaporatedIf solution is heated to evaporate all waterat this stage see METHOD2below.If M2is scored but the saturated solution is thenleft to evaporate the remaining waterthenM3cannot be awarded,butM4&M5can be awardedIGNOREjust\"dry it\"ALLOWleave(the crystals)to dryREJECThot oven or any method of direct heating(eg Bunsen burner)"}], "answerText": "solid stops disappearing/solid settles/left over", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(f)", "marks": 1, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q5f", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW evaporate"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE just “dry it”"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW leave to dry"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT hot oven or any method of direct heating (eg Bunsen burner)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M5 DEP on M4 in METHOD 2 only"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(g)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "CoCl2.2H2O+4H2O→CoCl2.6H2O"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B(dehydration)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is B because when the pink solid CoCl2.6H2O is heated to from the blue solid CoCl2 it is losing water which is dehydration"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not correct because when the pink solid CoCl2.6H2O is heated to from the blue solid CoCl2 it is losing water which is not crystallisation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not correct because when the pink solid CoCl2.6H2O is heated to from the blue solid CoCl2 it is losing water which is not hydration"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because when the pink solid CoCl2.6H2O is heated to from the blue solid CoCl2 it is losing water which is not a redox reaction"}], "answerText": "METHOD 2\nIf the filtrate is heated to evaporate all water:\nM1 heat/boil (the filtrate / evaporating basin)\nM4 wash the crystals (with a small amount of deionised water)\nM5 dry the crystals on filter/tissue paper / in a (warm) oven", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "rowspan continuation row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q6a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "If name and formula given, both must be correct"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "K+"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(test 3A) no carbonate(ion) presentM2(test 3B) no halide(ion) present"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTCO32-ALLOWhydrogencarbonate/HCO3-ACCEPTno chloride,bromide or iodide(ion) present(all three halides must be mentioned)ALLOWone halide if result is given e.g.no chloride ions present because a white precipitate would form"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "sulfate/SO42-"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If name and formula given both must be correct"}], "answerText": "ammonia/NH3", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q7ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT oxidation number has increased/changed from-2to+4ALLOW gainedO/Ohas been addedIGNORE references to electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Sb2O4+2C→2Sb+2CO2"}], "answerText": "(it has) gained oxygen/oxygen has been added(to it)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7(b)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q7bi", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 strong electrostatic forces/attractions between the oppositely-charged ions"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT strong ionic bonding/bonds/many ionic bonds"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE giant ionic structure/lattice"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 large amount of (thermal/heat) energy required to overcome these forces/attractions"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT large amount of (thermal/heat) energy required to break the bonds"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE more energy required"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP on M1 or near miss e.g. “strong bonds”"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If reference to intermolecular forces/metallic/covalent bonding, then score 0 out of 2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Bi2O3+6HCl→2BiCl3+3H2O"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 H2O as only product not containing Bi"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 equation fully correct i.e. formula of BiCl3and balanced"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT multiples and halves"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP on M1"}], "answerText": "Bi3+", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "8(a)(i)", "marks": 2, "number": 8, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q8ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE plotting of(0,0).Deduct one mark for each point plotted incorrectly."}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "suitable curve drawn, avoiding the anomalous point"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW curve drawn± half a square through other points"}], "answerText": "M1 and M2 all points plotted correctly(± half a square)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "8(b)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 8, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q8bi", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW misread the syringe/syringe not read at eye level"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 value read correctly(±1cm3) from candidate''s graph"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 vertical line drawn at 2 min intersecting curve"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW a cross on the curve at 2 mins"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR horizontal line drawn from vertical axis intersecting curve at 2 min"}], "answerText": "measured volume of gas later (than 2 minutes)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "8(c)", "marks": 2, "number": 8, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q8c", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW references to no more gas given off"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 because all the acid has reacted/the acid has been used up"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE the reactants have been used up"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE the zinc has reacted"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE the zinc is in excess"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT all of the zinc has reacted/the zinc has been used up"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "the gradient/slope of the curve decreases"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT the curve becomes less steep"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW the curve levels off"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 fewer particles(of acid/zinc to react)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 fewer(successful)collisions(between particles)per second"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW concentration of acid decreases"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT less frequent(successful)collisions"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to less chance of collision"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to wrong type of particles eg molecules"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any reference to particles losing energy/moving more slowly scores0 out of2."}], "answerText": "M1 the reaction has finished", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "9", "marks": 6, "number": 9, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q9", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE any references to carrying charge throughout the question"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW sea of electrons"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE free electrons"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW are mobile"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP on mention of electrons in M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any mention of moving ions/atoms/nuclei/protons loses M1&M2"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE refs to electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4 DEP on M3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT refs to electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M6 DEP on M5"}], "answerText": "(magnesium):\nM1 delocalised electrons\nM2 are able to flow/move (through the structure)\n(solid MgCl2):\nM3(positive and negative) ions\nM4 are in fixed positions/can only vibrate/cannot move\n(aqueous MgCl2):\nM5(positive and negative) ions\nM6 can move/flow(to electrodes of opposite charge)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "10(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 10, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q10a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT molecules/particles gain energyACCEPT the(mean/average)speed/velocity of the moleculesincreasesACCEPT molecules move faster"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 molecules/particles/they escape(from the liquid)ORintermolecular forces are brokenANDthe molecules/particlesmove further apart"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNOREevaporate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "$Br_{2}+H_{2}O\\rightarrow HBr+HBrO$"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW reactantsin either orderALLOW productsin either order"}], "answerText": "M1 the(mean/average)energy of the molecules/particlesincreases", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "10(c)(i)", "marks": 3, "number": 10, "entryId": "ms-696bac95-q10ci", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M3 DEP on valid working inM2"}], "answerText": "M1 n[MgBr2.6H2O]=0.125(mol)\nM2 mass of MgBr2.6H2O=0.125×292\nM3=36.5(g)\nOR\nM1 mass of MgCO3=0.125×84\nOR 10.5(g)\nM2 84(g) of MgCO3 give 292(g) of MgBr2.6H2O\nOR mass of MgBr2.6H2O=(292÷84)×10.5(g)\nM3 mass of MgBr2.6H2O=36.5(g)\nOR\nM1 mass of MgBr2=0.125×184\nOR 23(g)\nM2 mass of 6H2O=0.125×6×18\nOR 13.5(g)\nM3 23+13.5=36.5(g)\nOR\n36.5÷292=0.125 scores(3)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [{"ecf": false, "text": "M1 n[MgBr2.6H2O]=0.125(mol)\nM2 mass of MgBr2.6H2O=0.125×2