, "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "11(a): bare integer cell \"4\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "11(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "11(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "11(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (iii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (iv)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "January 2020", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": "Thursday 9 January 2020", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH1/1CR", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "1ed33ec4-b402-59d2-ac10-e808f3b34257"}, "warnings": [], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram shows the particles in the atom of an element. particle Y particle Z", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Particle Y is a proton. What is particle Z? A an electron B a molecule C a neutron D a nucleus", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-1ed33ec4-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these has the smallest mass? A an electron B a neutron C a nucleus D a proton", "label": "b", "marks": 1, "partId": "q01-1ed33ec4-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the mass number of this atom? A 4 B 5 C 9 D 13", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-1ed33ec4-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the atomic number of this atom? A 4 B 5 C 9 D 13", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-1ed33ec4-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the element that contains this atom.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-1ed33ec4-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what is formed when this atom loses its outer shell electrons.", "label": "e-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-1ed33ec4-pe-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667427.png", "width": 480, "format": "png", "height": 496, "sha256": "a5a7a8de0ea49913f4b5c2e2b66d8d28e4c377fccfb8995cc9f3ec798b4333cf", "assetId": "img:a5a7a8de0ea49913f4b5c2e2b66d8d28e4c377fccfb8995cc9f3ec798b4333cf", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000024", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667427.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-1ed33ec4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about states of matter.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows how the particles of a substance are arranged in two different states. solid liquid gas", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-1ed33ec4-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667461.png", "width": 416, "format": "png", "height": 417, "sha256": "cf2a873b82c35c0227fbfa1d35f9f3587c59b8c73cb6ad2fb6c26988327e68c9", "assetId": "img:cf2a873b82c35c0227fbfa1d35f9f3587c59b8c73cb6ad2fb6c26988327e68c9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000053", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667461.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789044667466.png", "width": 417, "format": "png", "height": 417, "sha256": "618ab3ea31c8c4e28739d85c6566819962ddfd92f6f97d056d9d8bed21c70879", "assetId": "img:618ab3ea31c8c4e28739d85c6566819962ddfd92f6f97d056d9d8bed21c70879", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000055", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789044667466.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789044667474.png", "width": 415, "format": "png", "height": 405, "sha256": "1db7a94ea5bf501cfaaa0573bbe52d040ffada2bba0ac1c0412d8cf397422a1b", "assetId": "img:1db7a94ea5bf501cfaaa0573bbe52d040ffada2bba0ac1c0412d8cf397422a1b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000057", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789044667474.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the diagram to show how particles are arranged in the liquid state.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-1ed33ec4-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the state of matter in which the particles have the most energy.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-1ed33ec4-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The state symbols (s), (l), (g) and (aq) are often used in chemistry. The table shows some physical changes. Complete the table by giving the state symbol before and after each change.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-1ed33ec4-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why hot water evaporates more quickly than cold water.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-1ed33ec4-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667461.png", "width": 416, "format": "png", "height": 417, "sha256": "cf2a873b82c35c0227fbfa1d35f9f3587c59b8c73cb6ad2fb6c26988327e68c9", "assetId": "img:cf2a873b82c35c0227fbfa1d35f9f3587c59b8c73cb6ad2fb6c26988327e68c9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000053", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667461.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789044667466.png", "width": 417, "format": "png", "height": 417, "sha256": "618ab3ea31c8c4e28739d85c6566819962ddfd92f6f97d056d9d8bed21c70879", "assetId": "img:618ab3ea31c8c4e28739d85c6566819962ddfd92f6f97d056d9d8bed21c70879", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000055", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789044667466.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789044667474.png", "width": 415, "format": "png", "height": 405, "sha256": "1db7a94ea5bf501cfaaa0573bbe52d040ffada2bba0ac1c0412d8cf397422a1b", "assetId": "img:1db7a94ea5bf501cfaaa0573bbe52d040ffada2bba0ac1c0412d8cf397422a1b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000057", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789044667474.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-1ed33ec4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000064"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The Group 7 elements are called halogens. Halogens form compounds called halides. Three of the halogens are represented by the formulae $ X_{2}, Y_{2} $ and $ Z_{2} $ Solutions of these halogens are added separately to solutions of sodium halides, NaX, NaY and NaZ. The table shows whether or not a reaction occurs.", "marks": 9, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Use the information in the table to deduce the order of reactivity of the halogens $ X_{2}, Y_{2} $ and $ Z_{2} $ most reactive least reactive", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-1ed33ec4-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "An aqueous solution of halogen $ \\mathrm{Y}_{2} $ is orange. This solution is decolourised when it reacts with an alkene. Deduce the identity of halogen $ Y_{2} $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-1ed33ec4-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows some physical properties of the halogens. Complete the table by predicting a boiling point for chlorine, the state of fluorine at room temperature and the colour of astatine.", "label": "c", "marks": 3, "partId": "q03-1ed33ec4-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Why do the halogens have similar chemical properties? A they are non-metals B they are molecules C they have the same number of outer shell electrons D they are in the same period of the Periodic Table", "label": "c-ii", "marks": 1, "partId": "q03-1ed33ec4-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A teacher uses this apparatus to demonstrate the reaction between chlorine gas and iron wool. The teacher does the reaction in a fume cupboard.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-1ed33ec4-pd", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667527.png", "width": 1242, "format": "png", "height": 446, "sha256": "bd9bb6ca9cdc7d12774329467c1fe5a23c5b54717d245ce9d7ae38de2538df40", "assetId": "img:bd9bb6ca9cdc7d12774329467c1fe5a23c5b54717d245ce9d7ae38de2538df40", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000090", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667527.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why the teacher does the reaction in a fume cupboard.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-1ed33ec4-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The product of the reaction between iron and chlorine is iron(III) chloride. The ions in iron(III) chloride are $ \\mathrm{F e}^{3+} $ and $ \\mathrm{C l}^{-} $ Use this information to give the chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-1ed33ec4-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667527.png", "width": 1242, "format": "png", "height": 446, "sha256": "bd9bb6ca9cdc7d12774329467c1fe5a23c5b54717d245ce9d7ae38de2538df40", "assetId": "img:bd9bb6ca9cdc7d12774329467c1fe5a23c5b54717d245ce9d7ae38de2538df40", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000090", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667527.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-1ed33ec4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000072", "e000082"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about ammonium chloride.", "marks": 9, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Give the formula of the ammonium ion.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-1ed33ec4-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe a test to show that ammonium chloride contains ammonium ions.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-1ed33ec4-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation shows the thermal decomposition of ammonium chloride. $\\mathrm {N H} _ {4} \\mathrm {C l} (\\mathrm {s}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {N H} _ {3} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {H C l} (\\mathrm {g})$ State what the $ \\rightleftharpoons $ symbol indicates about this reaction.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-1ed33ec4-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the formation of ammonium chloride in a glass tube. cotton wool soaked in concentrated hydrochloric acid ammonium chloride cotton wool soaked in concentrated ammonia solution", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-1ed33ec4-pd", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667547.png", "width": 269, "format": "png", "height": 220, "sha256": "51337292b8bf6b26b798b8760893de37f40b7d49460a589da45b0922c72c161e", "assetId": "img:51337292b8bf6b26b798b8760893de37f40b7d49460a589da45b0922c72c161e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000105", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667547.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789044667555.png", "width": 276, "format": "png", "height": 227, "sha256": "e9deb0906bf2d05c7d8e4771423092654cf606b99c8e816449e240acca949a25", "assetId": "img:e9deb0906bf2d05c7d8e4771423092654cf606b99c8e816449e240acca949a25", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000108", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789044667555.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain how the mean speed of ammonia molecules compares with the mean speed of hydrogen chloride molecules.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-1ed33ec4-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Gas particles travel very quickly. Give two reasons why it takes several minutes for the ammonium chloride to form.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-1ed33ec4-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667547.png", "width": 269, "format": "png", "height": 220, "sha256": "51337292b8bf6b26b798b8760893de37f40b7d49460a589da45b0922c72c161e", "assetId": "img:51337292b8bf6b26b798b8760893de37f40b7d49460a589da45b0922c72c161e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000105", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667547.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789044667555.png", "width": 276, "format": "png", "height": 227, "sha256": "e9deb0906bf2d05c7d8e4771423092654cf606b99c8e816449e240acca949a25", "assetId": "img:e9deb0906bf2d05c7d8e4771423092654cf606b99c8e816449e240acca949a25", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000108", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789044667555.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-1ed33ec4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A teacher uses the reaction between phosphorus and oxygen to calculate the percentage of oxygen in air. She uses this apparatus and excess phosphorus. The volume of gas in the tube decreases as the phosphorus reacts with oxygen. The teacher measures the volume of gas in the tube at one-minute intervals. The table shows the teacher''s results.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State how the results show that all the oxygen has reacted.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-1ed33ec4-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give one change to this experiment that would make the results more accurate.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-1ed33ec4-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the results to calculate the percentage of oxygen in air. Give your answer to one decimal place.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-1ed33ec4-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667564.png", "width": 960, "format": "png", "height": 668, "sha256": "506af35ab6f440b1452f254b729652c29a2325f75e269c63edcd8408e865347d", "assetId": "img:506af35ab6f440b1452f254b729652c29a2325f75e269c63edcd8408e865347d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000115", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667564.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q05-1ed33ec4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000117"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The reaction between magnesium and copper(II) sulfate solution is exothermic. This apparatus is used to measure the temperature increase when excess magnesium is added to $ 1 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of copper(II) sulfate solution.", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State why a reaction occurs when magnesium is added to copper(II) sulfate solution.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-1ed33ec4-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the word equation for this reaction. magnesium + copper(II) sulfate $ \\rightarrow $", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-1ed33ec4-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The temperature at the start of the reaction is $ 2 0. 2^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ The maximum temperature recorded is $ 5 6. 3^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-1ed33ec4-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the heat energy change, in joules, for the reaction. [mass of $ 1. 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of solution $ = 1. 0 0 \\mathrm{g} $ ] [c for the solution $ = 4. 2 \\mathrm{J / g / ^ {\\circ} C} $ ]", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-1ed33ec4-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["heat energy change"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why it is better to use a polystyrene cup rather than a glass beaker in this experiment.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-1ed33ec4-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The reaction between zinc and copper(II) sulfate solution is also exothermic.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-1ed33ec4-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A mass of 0.500g of zinc is reacted with an excess of copper(II) sulfate solution. The heat energy change is 1.67 kJ. Calculate the molar enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ , in kJ/mol. Include a sign in your answer. Give your answer to three significant figures. $\\Delta H =$", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-1ed33ec4-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The ionic equation for the reaction between zinc and copper(II) sulfate is $\\mathrm {Z n} (\\mathrm {s}) + \\mathrm {C u} ^ {2 +} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {Z n} ^ {2 +} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {C u} (\\mathrm {s})$ Explain why this is a redox reaction.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-1ed33ec4-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667596.png", "width": 1040, "format": "png", "height": 558, "sha256": "aa7432de3787fea42d71918583df33d00b744353311fb542fd5a885c3672d2af", "assetId": "img:aa7432de3787fea42d71918583df33d00b744353311fb542fd5a885c3672d2af", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000123", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667596.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-1ed33ec4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student investigates the reaction between sodium hydroxide solution and hydrochloric acid. He uses this method. Step 1 add $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of dilute hydrochloric acid to a conical flask Step 2 add a $ 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ portion of sodium hydroxide solution to the conical flask Step 3 test the pH of the mixture using both universal indicator paper and a pH meter The student repeats step 2 and step 3 until a total of $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution has been added.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State the piece of apparatus that should be used to measure $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of hydrochloric acid.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-1ed33ec4-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the type of reaction that occurs between hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-1ed33ec4-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Graph 1 shows how the pH of the mixture changes as the sodium hydroxide solution is added. Graph 1", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-1ed33ec4-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667646.png", "width": 1103, "format": "png", "height": 1481, "sha256": "87db4fe0d4e18e234ad03e4adc90a733690fe46e996240c77bd829e4280434cc", "assetId": "img:87db4fe0d4e18e234ad03e4adc90a733690fe46e996240c77bd829e4280434cc", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000153", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667646.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Determine the pH after 40 $ cm^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution has been added.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-1ed33ec4-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest the colour of the universal indicator paper when these volumes of sodium hydroxide solution have been added.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-1ed33ec4-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the formula of the ion that causes sodium hydroxide to be alkaline.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-1ed33ec4-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Another student investigates how the temperature changes when the sodium hydroxide solution is added to the hydrochloric acid. The hydrochloric acid and the sodium hydroxide solution are at the same temperature at the start of the investigation. The student records the temperature of the mixture after adding each $ 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ portion of sodium hydroxide solution. Graph 2 shows her results. Graph 2 Explain the shape of graph 2.", "label": "c", "marks": 3, "partId": "q07-1ed33ec4-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667678.png", "width": 1275, "format": "png", "height": 741, "sha256": "f09fc5c3653a62bc8b1984072b692d8a4ee0f800d42a5d16e93b0b5cbfe3de63", "assetId": "img:f09fc5c3653a62bc8b1984072b692d8a4ee0f800d42a5d16e93b0b5cbfe3de63", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000162", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667678.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667646.png", "width": 1103, "format": "png", "height": 1481, "sha256": "87db4fe0d4e18e234ad03e4adc90a733690fe46e996240c77bd829e4280434cc", "assetId": "img:87db4fe0d4e18e234ad03e4adc90a733690fe46e996240c77bd829e4280434cc", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000153", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667646.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667678.png", "width": 1275, "format": "png", "height": 741, "sha256": "f09fc5c3653a62bc8b1984072b692d8a4ee0f800d42a5d16e93b0b5cbfe3de63", "assetId": "img:f09fc5c3653a62bc8b1984072b692d8a4ee0f800d42a5d16e93b0b5cbfe3de63", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000162", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667678.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q07-1ed33ec4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) The diagram shows the arrangement of electrons in an atom of calcium and in an atom of chlorine. Describe, in terms of electrons, what happens when calcium reacts with chlorine to form the ionic compound calcium chloride, $ \\mathrm{C a C l}_{2} $", "marks": 14, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Describe tests to show that an aqueous solution of calcium chloride contains calcium ions and chloride ions. calcium ions. chloride ions", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-1ed33ec4-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Solid calcium chloride does not conduct electricity. Aqueous solutions of calcium chloride do conduct electricity. A student uses this method to investigate how the conductivity of a solution changes when calcium chloride is dissolved in pure water. Step1 add $ 1 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of pure water to a beaker Step 2 add one spatula of solid calcium chloride to the beaker Step 4 measure the conductivity of the solution Step 3 stir the solution Step 5 repeat until nine spatulas of solid calcium chloride have been added The table shows the student''s results.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-1ed33ec4-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Plot the results on the grid and draw two straight lines of best fit. Ignore the anomalous result. Conductivity of solution in arbitrary units Number of spatulas of calcium chloride", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-1ed33ec4-pc-i", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667788.png", "width": 945, "format": "png", "height": 853, "sha256": "43b9c7c553ad67413c78963cf1e9dfd33ec22e9c6c17cda71c8d47530b3c202a", "assetId": "img:43b9c7c553ad67413c78963cf1e9dfd33ec22e9c6c17cda71c8d47530b3c202a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000184", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667788.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the trend shown on the graph for the first six spatulas of calcium chloride.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-1ed33ec4-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest an error the student could have made to cause the anomalous result.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-1ed33ec4-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe another way to make solid calcium chloride conduct electricity.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-1ed33ec4-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667697.png", "width": 661, "format": "png", "height": 616, "sha256": "6eb80f4d72e18e2ea2e1d15c8eba6b5ff91629f537207635ea92eae676a683b1", "assetId": "img:6eb80f4d72e18e2ea2e1d15c8eba6b5ff91629f537207635ea92eae676a683b1", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000167", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667697.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789044667721.png", "width": 545, "format": "png", "height": 547, "sha256": "2481bc121f830b60a4e149109239f39c848b7803c0a5a731e2ce599c7b655714", "assetId": "img:2481bc121f830b60a4e149109239f39c848b7803c0a5a731e2ce599c7b655714", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000168", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789044667721.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667788.png", "width": 945, "format": "png", "height": 853, "sha256": "43b9c7c553ad67413c78963cf1e9dfd33ec22e9c6c17cda71c8d47530b3c202a", "assetId": "img:43b9c7c553ad67413c78963cf1e9dfd33ec22e9c6c17cda71c8d47530b3c202a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000184", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667788.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q08-1ed33ec4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000181"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about alkenes and polymers.", "marks": 13, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Ethene $ \\left( \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{4} \\right) $ can be represented by different types of formula. Complete the table by giving the missing information. ii) Ethene is a member of the homologous series of alkenes. All members of the same homologous series have the same general formula. Give two other characteristics of a homologous series.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-1ed33ec4-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Ethene is used to make poly(ethene).", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-1ed33ec4-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the type of polymerisation used to form poly(ethene).", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-1ed33ec4-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the equation for the polymerisation of ethene.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-1ed33ec4-pb-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667800.png", "width": 906, "format": "png", "height": 311, "sha256": "4ccb45272ea20e1f704b59d2e0a45a6b7f506fbc8240b7c4a0b70296bc946a81", "assetId": "img:4ccb45272ea20e1f704b59d2e0a45a6b7f506fbc8240b7c4a0b70296bc946a81", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000199", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667800.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Poly(ethene) is used to make plastic bags. Corn starch from plants can also be used to make polymers for plastic bags. The table gives some information about poly(ethene) and polymers made from corn starch. Use the information in the table and your knowledge to discuss the advantages and disadvantages of using poly(ethene) to make plastic bags.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-1ed33ec4-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the repeat unit of another polymer. Draw the displayed formula of the monomer used to make this polymer.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-1ed33ec4-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667832.png", "width": 223, "format": "png", "height": 297, "sha256": "12c1d38a1ab41bbb2afd3f8b42a6568338c3324f26b51b8856009843f16a8969", "assetId": "img:12c1d38a1ab41bbb2afd3f8b42a6568338c3324f26b51b8856009843f16a8969", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000206", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667832.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 9, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667800.png", "width": 906, "format": "png", "height": 311, "sha256": "4ccb45272ea20e1f704b59d2e0a45a6b7f506fbc8240b7c4a0b70296bc946a81", "assetId": "img:4ccb45272ea20e1f704b59d2e0a45a6b7f506fbc8240b7c4a0b70296bc946a81", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000199", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667800.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667832.png", "width": 223, "format": "png", "height": 297, "sha256": "12c1d38a1ab41bbb2afd3f8b42a6568338c3324f26b51b8856009843f16a8969", "assetId": "img:12c1d38a1ab41bbb2afd3f8b42a6568338c3324f26b51b8856009843f16a8969", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000206", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667832.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q09-1ed33ec4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000192", "e000203"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about carbon and its compounds.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a dot-and-cross diagram to show the outer shell electrons in a molecule of carbon dioxide, $ \\mathrm{C O_{2}} $", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-1ed33ec4-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The atoms in carbon dioxide are held together by covalent bonds. Describe the forces of attraction in a covalent bond.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-1ed33ec4-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows three different structures of carbon. diamond graphite C $ _{60} $ fullerene", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-1ed33ec4-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667844.png", "width": 336, "format": "png", "height": 297, "sha256": "35f6688663749427d0e7114edafd7e676335c7976dfb96a4a4918ca80d1a396f", "assetId": "img:35f6688663749427d0e7114edafd7e676335c7976dfb96a4a4918ca80d1a396f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000213", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667844.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789044667864.png", "width": 286, "format": "png", "height": 329, "sha256": "b4b480a36d409f822e73d5f598908b8b9e4557d8c4630b6f2a252a2d824ef7fe", "assetId": "img:b4b480a36d409f822e73d5f598908b8b9e4557d8c4630b6f2a252a2d824ef7fe", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000215", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789044667864.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789044667869.png", "width": 297, "format": "png", "height": 311, "sha256": "cf53208618d8b5c91f9cf4356f3b2b5a30a33b1d4854587946fbebfe3aefa837", "assetId": "img:cf53208618d8b5c91f9cf4356f3b2b5a30a33b1d4854587946fbebfe3aefa837", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000217", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789044667869.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why graphite conducts electricity.", "label": "b-i", "marks": 2, "partId": "q10-1ed33ec4-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why diamond has a much higher melting point than $ C_{60} $ fullerene. Refer to structure and bonding in your answer.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-1ed33ec4-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 10, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667844.png", "width": 336, "format": "png", "height": 297, "sha256": "35f6688663749427d0e7114edafd7e676335c7976dfb96a4a4918ca80d1a396f", "assetId": "img:35f6688663749427d0e7114edafd7e676335c7976dfb96a4a4918ca80d1a396f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000213", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667844.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789044667864.png", "width": 286, "format": "png", "height": 329, "sha256": "b4b480a36d409f822e73d5f598908b8b9e4557d8c4630b6f2a252a2d824ef7fe", "assetId": "img:b4b480a36d409f822e73d5f598908b8b9e4557d8c4630b6f2a252a2d824ef7fe", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000215", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789044667864.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789044667869.png", "width": 297, "format": "png", "height": 311, "sha256": "cf53208618d8b5c91f9cf4356f3b2b5a30a33b1d4854587946fbebfe3aefa837", "assetId": "img:cf53208618d8b5c91f9cf4356f3b2b5a30a33b1d4854587946fbebfe3aefa837", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000217", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789044667869.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q10-1ed33ec4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about the reduction of metal oxides.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Solid oxides of copper can be reduced by reacting them with methane gas. Complete the equation for the reaction between copper(II) oxide and methane. Include state symbols. $\\dots \\mathrm {C u O} (\\dots \\dots \\dots) + \\dots \\mathrm {C H} _ {4} (\\dots \\dots \\dots) \\rightarrow \\dots \\mathrm {C u} (\\dots \\dots \\dots) + \\dots \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\dots \\dots \\dots) + \\dots \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\dots \\dots \\dots)$", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-1ed33ec4-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A teacher uses this apparatus to demonstrate the reaction between a different oxide of copper and methane.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-1ed33ec4-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667884.png", "width": 1405, "format": "png", "height": 597, "sha256": "3e7c3b71a8bfee0fa6b3aed8591ed5999d7d1553bf344ae9398a109eed1e7b86", "assetId": "img:3e7c3b71a8bfee0fa6b3aed8591ed5999d7d1553bf344ae9398a109eed1e7b86", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000229", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667884.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The teacher heats the oxide of copper until the reaction is complete. The table shows the teacher''s results. Use the teacher''s results to show that the empirical formula of this oxide of copper is $ \\mathrm{C u_{2} O} $", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-1ed33ec4-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The teacher wears safety glasses and a lab coat during the demonstration. Give one other safety precaution that she should take.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-1ed33ec4-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Iron forms when iron(III) oxide is heated with carbon. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {F e} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3} + 3 \\mathrm {C} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {F e} + 3 \\mathrm {C O}$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-1ed33ec4-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State how the equation shows that iron(III) oxide is reduced.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-1ed33ec4-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why carbon monoxide should not be released into the atmosphere.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-1ed33ec4-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the maximum mass, in tonnes, of iron that can be produced when 30.0 tonnes of iron(III) oxide are reacted with an excess of carbon. $[ 1 \\mathrm {t o n n e} = 1. 0 \\times 1 0 ^ {6} \\mathrm {g} ]$", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-1ed33ec4-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A mixture of 25 000 mol of iron(III) oxide and 840 000 g of carbon is heated. Use this equation to show that the iron(III) oxide is in excess. $\\mathrm {F e} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3} + 3 \\mathrm {C} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {F e} + 3 \\mathrm {C O}$ $\\text{Total for Question 11 = 15 marks}$", "label": "c-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-1ed33ec4-pc-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 11, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789044667884.png", "width": 1405, "format": "png", "height": 597, "sha256": "3e7c3b71a8bfee0fa6b3aed8591ed5999d7d1553bf344ae9398a109eed1e7b86", "assetId": "img:3e7c3b71a8bfee0fa6b3aed8591ed5999d7d1553bf344ae9398a109eed1e7b86", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000229", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789044667884.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q11-1ed33ec4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000231"]}], "paperTotal": 110, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 6, "2": 7, "3": 9, "4": 9, "6": 12, "8": 14, "9": 13, "10": 11}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "January 2020", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_1CR_msc_20200305", "canonicalDocumentId": "2969c0ec-34a1-58ab-aaa0-e9bbe16da6f2"}, "entries": [{"mcq": true, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-2969c0ec-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A Electron"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is A because electrons have a relative mass of 1/1836 compared to a proton or a neutron"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not correct because a neutron has a relative mass of 1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C in not correct because the nucleus contains 4 protons and 5 neutrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because a proton has a relative mass of 1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C 9"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is C because the mass number is the sum of the protons and neutrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not correct because the atomic number is 4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not correct because 5 is the number of neutrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because 13 is the total number of protons, neutrons and electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A 4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is A because the atomic number is equal to the number of protons which is 4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not correct because 5 is the number of neutrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not correct because 9 is the total number of particles in the nucleus"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because 13 is the total number of protons, neutrons and electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(e)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "beryllium/Be"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(positive) ion"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW ecf from the element given in(e)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any positive beryllium ion(or other ecf ion)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT any negative ion"}], "answerText": "C Neutron\nThe only correct answer is C because the nucleus contains protons and neutrons. Protons are identified as the white dots\nA is not correct because electrons occur in the shells\nB is not correct because a molecule is not a particle found in the nucleus\nD is not correct because the nucleus contains protons and neutrons", "confidence": 0.85, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": "C", "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-2969c0ec-q2ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW particles filling the whole box"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE the size of the particles"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT a regular arrangement"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Gas"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW gaseous"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(water evaporates)I to g"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(crystals of iodine sublime)s to g"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3(ice melts)s to I"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW words for M1,M2 and M3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(particles/molecules have)more energy"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2to overcome/break the forces(between water molecules)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW water has more energy"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW(particles/molecules have)move faster"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE vibrate more"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW to overcome/break the bonds(between water molecules)ORto break away from one another OR so escape more easily"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to collisions or activation energy"}], "answerText": "Particles should be close together and should fill from the bottom of the box, some particles should touch", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-2969c0ec-q3a", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW ZYXALLOW lower case lettersIGNORE size of numberIGNORE any names given"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "bromine"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW bromine waterOR bromine solution/Br/Br2REJECT bromide"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(Fluorine) gas/vapour(chlorine) range between-150℃ to10℃ inclusive(Astatine) dark grey/black"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT blue-black"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C-the halogens have the same number of outer shell electrons.The only correct answer is C because the halogens are in group 7 and have similar reactions because they have the same number of electrons in their outer shells.A is not correct because the fact halogens are non-metals does not make them react in a similar way.B is not correct because the fact halogens are molecules does not make them react in a similar way.D is not correct because elements in the same period have different numbers of outer shell electrons and react differently."}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Chlorine is toxic/poisonous"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE harmful/dangerous/irritantIGNORE any reference to products"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1FeCl3M22Fe+3Cl2→2FeCl3M2 rest of the equation balanced"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW correct charges on the ionsREJECT incorrect capitalsREJECT large or super-script 3ALLOW multiples or fractions for M2M2 dep on M1"}], "answerText": "Most Z2Y2Least X2", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-2969c0ec-q4a", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW NH4+1and NH41+"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1add sodium hydroxide solution(and warm)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(test the gas with damp)red litmus"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3turns blue"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2expose the gas to concentrated hydrochloric acid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3white smoke produced"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW(test the gas with damp)universal indicator"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If universal indicator is used allow blue purple for M3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3dep on litmus or universal indicator in M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If sodium hydroxide solution is not added max=1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(the reaction is) reversible"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT reaction that goes both ways/both forwards and backwards reactions occur"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to equilibrium"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(molecules/particles of)ammonia move/diffuse faster"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2because the ammonium chloride forms near(er)to the HCl OR because the ammonia has travelled further(in the same time)"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to the masses/sizes of the particles"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any two from:"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(gas particles)move in random directions/don’t travel in straight lines OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(gas particles)collide with air/other particles"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3(gas particles)collide with the walls/sides(of the tube) OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW air/other particles slow them down"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE any references to rate of reaction/collisions"}], "answerText": "NH4+", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-2969c0ec-q5a", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW there is a constant volume in the tube"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW the volume of gas stops decreasing"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW no change after4/5 minutes"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to time"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT the volume of gas stops increasing"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(use a glass tube/ scale with) smaller divisions."}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW use a glass tube/ scale with0.1cm3divisions"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW use a smaller scale"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to repeating the experiment"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to temperature"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 Volume of oxygen=11.5(cm3)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(11.5÷48.5)x100"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M323.7%"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct answer to1 dp with or without working scores3"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW ecf fromM1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 must be to1dp"}], "answerText": "Results are the same at the end", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-2969c0ec-q6ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW magnesium can displace copper"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW magnesium is higher than copper in the reactivity series"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT magnesium is more reactive than copper(II) or Cu2+ or copper sulfate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "magnesium sulfate + copper"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Both are required for the mark. Either order."}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT copper(II)"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE any chemical formulae given"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 Temperature rise = 36.1(℃)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct answer with or without working scores 2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 15162J"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW ecf from M1"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW 2 or more significant figures"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE negative sign"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An explanation that links any two of the following points"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 polystyrene is an insulator"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW polystyrene is not a(good) conductor of heat"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW polystyrene is a poor conductor of heat"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(so) reduces heat loss(to the surroundings)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW prevents heat loss"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW keeps heat in"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 temperature rise/change/reading will be closer to true value OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW temperature rise/change/reading will be more accurate/valid"}], "answerText": "magnesium is more reactive than copper", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7", "marks": 1, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-2969c0ec-q7", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Measuring cylinder / burette / (volumetric) pipette"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Neutralisation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT exothermic"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE base or alkali"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "12.4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "15cm3-red/orange"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "30cm3-blue/purple"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OH-/hydroxide(ion)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT OH"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 the reaction is exothermic(therefore the temperature rises)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW the reaction gives out heat(energy) or thermal energy"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE energy alone"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(after25cm3of sodium hydroxide)the reaction is complete OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW(after25cm3of sodium hydroxide)neutralisation happens"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3so adding more sodium hydroxide/liquid/solution cools the mixture down"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW so no more heat(energy) or thermal energy is given out OWTTE"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE energy alone"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "8(a)", "marks": 3, "number": 8, "entryId": "ms-2969c0ec-q8a", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to redox"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 chlorine gains electrons"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "Allow 1 mark from M1 and M2 for electron transfer from chlorine to calcium"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 two atoms of chlorine each gain one electron OR"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If chlorine molecules are gaining electrons do not award M3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 calcium loses 2 electrons and chlorine gains 1 electron"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any reference to sharing electrons or covalent or metallic bonding scores 0"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(test for Ca2+ ions)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 flame test(allow description of a flame test)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 orange-red flame colour"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW brick-red IGNORE orange / red alone M2 dep on M1"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW M1 add sodium hydroxide"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW M2 (slight) white precipitate (reject precipitate dissolves in excess sodium hydroxide)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(test for Cl- ions)"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE reference to nitric acid REJECT hydrochloric acid or sulfuric acid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4 white precipitate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4 dep on silver nitrate in M3"}], "answerText": "M1 calcium loses electrons", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "9", "marks": 2, "number": 9, "entryId": "ms-2969c0ec-q9", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M1(Empirical formula)CH2"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW sub and super script numbers for M1 and M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(General formula)CnH2n"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW letters other than n"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW capital letters"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any two from:"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 each member differs from the next by a CH2 group OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(each member has) same functional group"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3(each member has) similar/same chemical properties / similar/same (chemical) reactions"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4 trend in physical properties (between successive members)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT react in similar/same way"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT named physical property, e.g. boiling point"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT similar/same physical properties"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(i)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "addition"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW additional"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT condensation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 Single bond between the two carbons, 4 hydrogens joined by single bonds"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 trailing bonds through the brackets and the n to the right"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW n in any position outside the bracket to the right of the structure."}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW capital N"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any 5 points from:"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If only advantages or disadvantages given, max 3 marks"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 Poly(ethene) is cheaper than polymers from corn starch"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE durable"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 Poly(ethene) is stronger than polymers from corn starch"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 Poly(ethene) frees up land to grow food crops"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "10(a)(i)", "marks": 2, "number": 10, "entryId": "ms-2969c0ec-q10ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M2 rest of molecule correct"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 dep on M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 shared pair(s) of electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 attracted to (two) nuclei"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT nucleus. Must be plural for M2.M2 dep on mention of electrons in M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 Graphite has delocalised electrons"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE sea of electronsIGNORE free electronsIGNORE number of electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 (delocalised electron(s)) can move or flow (throughout the structure)"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to carrying a charge or currentIGNORE references to layersM2 dep on mentioning electrons in M1Any mention of ions scores 0"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 (diamond) giant covalent"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW macromolecularALLOW giant structure if M2 is scoredIGNORE tetrahedral structureREJECT molecules of diamond"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 (in melting diamond) covalent bonds are broken"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW description of covalent bonds"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3(C60)(simple) molecular structure"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW molecules of C60"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4(in melting C60) intermolecular forces (of attraction) are overcome"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M5 more energy is needed to break covalent bonds(in diamond) than intermolecular forces(in C60)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW strong covalent bonds and weak intermolecular forces(or attraction)"}], "answerText": "M1 four electrons between the carbon and each oxygen", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "11(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 11, "entryId": "ms-2969c0ec-q11a", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW multiples and fractions"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 Mass copper 3.18g and mass oxygen 0.40gM2 Moles copper=3.18/63.5OR0.0500molesM3 Moles oxygen=0.40/16OR0.025molesM4 Ratio of moles Cu:O is2:1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 and M3 allow ecf from M1M4 is dep on M2 and M3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any one from:M1 Use a safety screenM2 Position the class some distance from the apparatusM3 Do the experiment in a fume cupboardM4 Set fire to the (excess) methane gas straight away"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW tie hair backALLOW wear heat-proof gloves"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(Iron(III)oxide)loses oxygen"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW iron loses oxygenIGNORE any reference to electrons."}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Carbon monoxide is poisonous/toxic OR carbon monoxide reduces the ability of the blood to carry oxygen"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin in the blood"}], "answerText": "4 CuO(s)+CH4(g)→4 Cu(s)+CO2(g)+2H2O(l/g)M1 correct balancingM2 correct state symbols", "confid