ical elements. Use the Periodic Table to help you answer this question.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the element with atomic number 5", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-0f21a459-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the symbol of a metallic element in Period 3", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-0f21a459-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the element whose atoms contain 14 protons.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-0f21a459-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the element whose atoms have the electronic configuration 2.5", "label": "a-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-0f21a459-pa-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of the compound formed between oxygen and the element with atomic number 13", "label": "a-v", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-0f21a459-pa-v", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The position of an element in the Periodic Table can be used to predict its properties.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-0f21a459-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which group contains elements that are all unreactive? A Group 2 B Group 5 C Group 6", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-0f21a459-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these is the least reactive element in Group 1? A caesium D Group0 B lithium C potassium D sodium", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-0f21a459-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-0f21a459", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) The boxes list changes that may happen in a laboratory and the names of some changes. Draw one straight line from each change to its correct name.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "A student has two solids, X and Y. One of these solids is a pure substance and the other is a mixture. Describe how the student could identify which solid is pure and which is a mixture by measuring a physical property of each solid.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-0f21a459-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391411.png", "width": 1664, "format": "png", "height": 2235, "sha256": "f28c15f531b63307894534e160b3d7db52b8a6103ceb5f2a3bc1ae9fec49d244", "assetId": "img:f28c15f531b63307894534e160b3d7db52b8a6103ceb5f2a3bc1ae9fec49d244", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000044", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391411.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-0f21a459", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about metals.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Metals can be arranged in a reactivity series based on their reactions with water and their reactions with dilute hydrochloric acid. The table shows how four metals, P, Q, R and S, react with water and with dilute hydrochloric acid.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-0f21a459-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify which of the metals P, Q, R or S could be gold.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-0f21a459-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why the reaction between metal R and dilute hydrochloric acid was not done.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-0f21a459-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the information in the table to place the metals in order of reactivity from most reactive to least reactive.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-0f21a459-pa-iii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391432.png", "width": 616, "format": "png", "height": 666, "sha256": "cfae09304f0f758d8ba48883c99c1e1d9a27328577493583d0939ac63b5e0db7", "assetId": "img:cfae09304f0f758d8ba48883c99c1e1d9a27328577493583d0939ac63b5e0db7", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000056", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391432.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Zinc is used to coat iron gates to prevent the iron from rusting.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-0f21a459-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the name of this method of preventing iron from rusting.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-0f21a459-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State another method of preventing iron from rusting.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-0f21a459-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A mixture of zinc powder and copper(II) oxide is heated. The chemical equation for the reaction that takes place is", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-0f21a459-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State how the reaction shows that zinc is more reactive than copper. $\\mathrm {Z n} + \\mathrm {C u O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {Z n O} + \\mathrm {C u}$", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-0f21a459-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which substance is the oxidising agent.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-0f21a459-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391432.png", "width": 616, "format": "png", "height": 666, "sha256": "cfae09304f0f758d8ba48883c99c1e1d9a27328577493583d0939ac63b5e0db7", "assetId": "img:cfae09304f0f758d8ba48883c99c1e1d9a27328577493583d0939ac63b5e0db7", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000056", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391432.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-0f21a459", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000052"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Sodium hydroxide dissolves in water, forming a strongly alkaline solution. Ammonia dissolves in water, forming a slightly less alkaline solution. (a) (i) Identify the ion that makes the sodium hydroxide solution alkaline. (ii) What is a possible pH of ammonia solution? (1) A 3 B 6 C 11 D 14", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "When ammonia solution reacts with sulfuric acid, a neutralisation reaction occurs and ammonium sulfate forms.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-0f21a459-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "How does the sulfuric acid act in this reaction? A as a neutron donor B as a neutron acceptor C as a proton donor D as a proton acceptor", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-0f21a459-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows a beaker containing some ammonia solution and a few drops of phenolphthalein indicator. Dilute sulfuric acid is added to the beaker until it is in excess. What are the colours of the phenolphthalein indicator before and after adding excess sulfuric acid?", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-0f21a459-pb-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391456.png", "width": 801, "format": "png", "height": 334, "sha256": "2551cb65b7afb61c0fd70c4b3ee2bf1b96825c4f5d927d8549f48764fe6a761d", "assetId": "img:2551cb65b7afb61c0fd70c4b3ee2bf1b96825c4f5d927d8549f48764fe6a761d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000078", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391456.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Ammonium sulfate is used by gardeners as a fertiliser because it contains nitrogen.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-0f21a459-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why the chemical formula of ammonium sulfate is $ \\mathrm{(N H_{4})_{2} S O_{4}} $ Refer to the charges on the ions in your answer.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-0f21a459-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the relative formula mass of ammonium sulfate, $ \\mathrm{(N H_{4})_{2} S O_{4}} $", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-0f21a459-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the mass, in grams, of nitrogen in 1.0kg of ammonium sulfate.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-0f21a459-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391456.png", "width": 801, "format": "png", "height": 334, "sha256": "2551cb65b7afb61c0fd70c4b3ee2bf1b96825c4f5d927d8549f48764fe6a761d", "assetId": "img:2551cb65b7afb61c0fd70c4b3ee2bf1b96825c4f5d927d8549f48764fe6a761d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000078", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391456.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q04-0f21a459", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000081"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) Chlorine, bromine and iodine are elements in the Periodic Table. Explain how the position of these elements in the Periodic Table depends on their electronic configurations.", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Chlorine reacts with methane to form $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C I} $ and HCl", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-0f21a459-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the condition necessary for this reaction.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-0f21a459-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the equation for this reaction.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-0f21a459-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The bonds in a molecule of $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C l} $ are covalent. Explain, in terms of electrostatic attractions, what is meant by a covalent bond.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-0f21a459-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a dot-and-cross diagram for a molecule of $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C l} $ Show only the outer electrons of the atoms.", "label": "b-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-0f21a459-pb-iv", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391484.png", "width": 376, "format": "png", "height": 386, "sha256": "d6086f6315ae1239edcf905cb5b16ddd6cf0cb5ffa50a55471d196cf790e50b5", "assetId": "img:d6086f6315ae1239edcf905cb5b16ddd6cf0cb5ffa50a55471d196cf790e50b5", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000095", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391484.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "$ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C l} $ has a simple molecular structure. Explain why $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C l} $ has a low boiling point.", "label": "b-v", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-0f21a459-pb-v", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Graphite is another substance that contains covalent bonds. The diagram shows the structure of graphite. Most covalent substances do not conduct electricity. Explain why graphite is able to conduct electricity.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-0f21a459-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391498.png", "width": 482, "format": "png", "height": 328, "sha256": "7b40e11d3d8b8c5f5a0fd47daca9a0a3543cd53b2cee3cc5b477ff4e003a4368", "assetId": "img:7b40e11d3d8b8c5f5a0fd47daca9a0a3543cd53b2cee3cc5b477ff4e003a4368", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000100", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391498.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391484.png", "width": 376, "format": "png", "height": 386, "sha256": "d6086f6315ae1239edcf905cb5b16ddd6cf0cb5ffa50a55471d196cf790e50b5", "assetId": "img:d6086f6315ae1239edcf905cb5b16ddd6cf0cb5ffa50a55471d196cf790e50b5", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000095", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391484.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391498.png", "width": 482, "format": "png", "height": 328, "sha256": "7b40e11d3d8b8c5f5a0fd47daca9a0a3543cd53b2cee3cc5b477ff4e003a4368", "assetId": "img:7b40e11d3d8b8c5f5a0fd47daca9a0a3543cd53b2cee3cc5b477ff4e003a4368", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000100", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391498.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-0f21a459", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The table shows the molecular formulae of six organic compounds, A, B, C, D, E and F.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which homologous series compound B belongs to.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-0f21a459-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the letter of the compound that has the same empirical formula as its molecular formula. ii) Compound F exists as two isomers. Explain what is meant by the term isomers. Include the structures of the two isomers of compound F in your answer.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-0f21a459-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe how compound D can be obtained from crude oil using the industrial process of fractional distillation.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-0f21a459-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Compound C can be used to make a polymer.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-0f21a459-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the type of polymer formed from compound C.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-0f21a459-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the polymer formed from compound C.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-0f21a459-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw the structure of this polymer. Include the displayed formula of the repeat unit.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-0f21a459-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q06-0f21a459", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000106"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Dilute hydrochloric acid reacts with a solution of sodium thiosulfate $ \\mathrm{(N a_{2} S_{2} O_{3})} $ to form a precipitate. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {S} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \\mathrm {H C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {N a C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {S} (\\mathrm {s}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {I}) + \\mathrm {S O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State the name of the precipitate that forms.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-0f21a459-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The reaction is often used to investigate rates of reaction. The diagram shows the apparatus a student uses to investigate the effect of temperature on the rate of the reaction. This is the student''s method. pour $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of cold sodium thiosulfate solution into a conical flask and heat it to $ 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ draw a cross (X) on a piece of paper and place it under the flask add $ 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of dilute hydrochloric acid to the flask look at the cross from above and record the time taken until the cross cannot be seen The student repeats the experiment four times, using sodium thiosulfate solution at a different temperature each time. He keeps the volumes of sodium thiosulfate solution and hydrochloric acid constant in each experiment. Give two other factors that the student should keep constant.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-0f21a459-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391530.png", "width": 246, "format": "png", "height": 122, "sha256": "b7c401fa58d3d0e295b6814cda85587b1acbc912e9e9c20cf696044b4670e566", "assetId": "img:b7c401fa58d3d0e295b6814cda85587b1acbc912e9e9c20cf696044b4670e566", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000123", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391530.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789055391544.png", "width": 1062, "format": "png", "height": 478, "sha256": "786380d4e9fbb49d41f433737c8e692119742216fb692ecb44889453e307aeba", "assetId": "img:786380d4e9fbb49d41f433737c8e692119742216fb692ecb44889453e307aeba", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000124", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789055391544.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the student''s results. The highest temperature the student uses is $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ because he thinks the results might not be as accurate at temperatures higher than $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $ Suggest a reason why the results might not be as accurate at temperatures higher than $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-0f21a459-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student wants to compare the rates of the reaction at the different temperatures. He uses this formula to obtain a value for each rate of reaction $\\mathrm {r a t e} = \\frac {1}{\\mathrm {t i m e i n s}}$ The table shows the value of the rate of reaction at each temperature. Plot the values of temperature and rate of reaction on the grid. Draw a curve of best fit through the points.", "label": "d", "marks": 2, "partId": "q07-0f21a459-pd", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391581.png", "width": 1612, "format": "png", "height": 1129, "sha256": "bdca6b1db352b44572dc1c4a654f2893a60d76bb6f90456159d22e77c37845d2", "assetId": "img:bdca6b1db352b44572dc1c4a654f2893a60d76bb6f90456159d22e77c37845d2", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000145", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391581.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the graph to determine a value for the rate of the reaction at $ 4 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ Show on the graph how you obtained your answer. $\\mathrm {r a t e o f r e a c t i o n} =$", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-0f21a459-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the time that it would take for the cross not to be seen at $ 4 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $", "label": "e-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-0f21a459-pe-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe the relationship between rate of reaction and temperature shown by the graph.", "label": "e-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-0f21a459-pe-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain, in terms of particle collision theory, the effect that increasing the temperature has on the rate of a reaction.", "label": "f", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-0f21a459-pf", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391530.png", "width": 246, "format": "png", "height": 122, "sha256": "b7c401fa58d3d0e295b6814cda85587b1acbc912e9e9c20cf696044b4670e566", "assetId": "img:b7c401fa58d3d0e295b6814cda85587b1acbc912e9e9c20cf696044b4670e566", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000123", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391530.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789055391544.png", "width": 1062, "format": "png", "height": 478, "sha256": "786380d4e9fbb49d41f433737c8e692119742216fb692ecb44889453e307aeba", "assetId": "img:786380d4e9fbb49d41f433737c8e692119742216fb692ecb44889453e307aeba", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000124", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789055391544.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391581.png", "width": 1612, "format": "png", "height": 1129, "sha256": "bdca6b1db352b44572dc1c4a654f2893a60d76bb6f90456159d22e77c37845d2", "assetId": "img:bdca6b1db352b44572dc1c4a654f2893a60d76bb6f90456159d22e77c37845d2", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000145", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391581.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q07-0f21a459", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000134", "e000141"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student uses this apparatus to investigate the heat energy change when a salt dissolves in water to form a solution. This is the student''s method. add $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of distilled water to a polystyrene cup record the initial temperature of the water add a known mass of solid anhydrous copper(II) sulfate to the polystyrene cup and stir the solution with the thermometer until all the solid has dissolved record the maximum temperature of the copper(II) sulfate solution", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name the piece of apparatus the student should use to add the distilled water to the polystyrene cup.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-0f21a459-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student stirs the solution to help the solid dissolve more quickly. Suggest another reason why the student stirs the solution.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-0f21a459-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the colour of the copper(II) sulfate solution.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-0f21a459-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the temperatures in one experiment. maximum temperature Complete the table, giving all values to the nearest 0.1 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-0f21a459-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391676.png", "width": 266, "format": "png", "height": 608, "sha256": "2c665457bc81eac16d45ed6c4a5989cf57aad59bd1661bc85090575cfb27c024", "assetId": "img:2c665457bc81eac16d45ed6c4a5989cf57aad59bd1661bc85090575cfb27c024", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000163", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391676.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789055391682.png", "width": 344, "format": "png", "height": 544, "sha256": "96425aa84421477d67443bde6316621d32da5579bb27142963c8705328922967", "assetId": "img:96425aa84421477d67443bde6316621d32da5579bb27142963c8705328922967", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000164", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789055391682.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In a second experiment, when a student dissolves the anhydrous copper(II) sulfate in $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of distilled water, the increase in temperature is $ 3. 3^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-0f21a459-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Show that the heat energy change (Q) in this second experiment is approximately 700 J. [for water, $ c=4. 2 \\mathrm{J} / \\mathrm{g} /^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ ] [mass of $ 1. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of water=1.0g]", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-0f21a459-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In this experiment the student uses 1.70g of the anhydrous copper(II) sulfate Calculate the molar enthalpy change $ (\\Delta H) $ in kJ/mol. Include a sign in your answer. $ [M_{r} $ of $ \\mathrm{CuSO_{4}}=159.5] $ $ \\Delta H= $ kJ/mol", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-0f21a459-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Another student does a similar experiment but uses hydrated copper(II) sulfate instead of anhydrous copper(II) sulfate. The table shows his results. Explain what the results show about the type of energy change that occurs when hydrated copper(II) sulfate dissolves.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-0f21a459-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391614.png", "width": 617, "format": "png", "height": 362, "sha256": "602a716309299a4f2969369bab79c081a9ee368a3f4886681e9220869f402ba8", "assetId": "img:602a716309299a4f2969369bab79c081a9ee368a3f4886681e9220869f402ba8", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000153", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391614.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391676.png", "width": 266, "format": "png", "height": 608, "sha256": "2c665457bc81eac16d45ed6c4a5989cf57aad59bd1661bc85090575cfb27c024", "assetId": "img:2c665457bc81eac16d45ed6c4a5989cf57aad59bd1661bc85090575cfb27c024", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000163", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391676.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789055391682.png", "width": 344, "format": "png", "height": 544, "sha256": "96425aa84421477d67443bde6316621d32da5579bb27142963c8705328922967", "assetId": "img:96425aa84421477d67443bde6316621d32da5579bb27142963c8705328922967", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000164", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789055391682.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q08-0f21a459", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000167", "e000174"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Sodium hydrogencarbonate $ \\mathrm{(N a H C O_{3})} $ is also known as baking soda. Baking soda can be used to make cakes increase in size in an oven. This is the equation for the reaction that takes place when baking soda is heated. $2 \\mathrm {N a H C O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {C O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {g})$", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "What type of reaction is this? A combustion B decomposition oxidation D reduction", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-0f21a459-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why the reaction makes the cakes increase in size.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-0f21a459-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student uses this apparatus to investigate the reaction that takes place when sodium hydrogencarbonate is heated. This is the student''s method. weigh a crucible and record the mass add some sodium hydrogencarbonate to the crucible, reweigh it and record the mass heat the crucible and contents for five minutes, then allow to cool before weighing and recording the mass heat the crucible and contents again for a further three minutes, then allow to cool before weighing and recording the mass", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-0f21a459-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391705.png", "width": 840, "format": "png", "height": 535, "sha256": "97ccff4c0fe3ed875fbea58523ca4a4c7df4cb54aab1d9af0d4a796df397dd90", "assetId": "img:97ccff4c0fe3ed875fbea58523ca4a4c7df4cb54aab1d9af0d4a796df397dd90", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000186", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391705.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why the crucible and contents are heated for a further three minutes.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-0f21a459-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student considered using a lid on the crucible in the experiment. Suggest an advantage and a disadvantage of using a lid on the crucible. advantage disadvantage", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-0f21a459-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows some of the student''s results.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-0f21a459-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the mass of sodium hydrogencarbonate that the student uses.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-0f21a459-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Using this equation, calculate the maximum mass of sodium carbonate $ \\mathrm{(N a_{2} C O_{3})} $ that could form in the student''s reaction. $2 \\mathrm {N a H C O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {C O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {g})$ [ $ M_{\\mathrm{r}} $ of $ \\mathrm{N a H C O_{3}}=8 4 $ $ M_{\\mathrm{r}} $ of $ \\mathrm{N a_{2} C O_{3}}=1 0 6 $", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-0f21a459-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In a second experiment, the student uses a larger mass of sodium hydrogencarbonate. She calculates that she should obtain 4.8 g of sodium carbonate. She actually obtains 4.2 g of sodium carbonate.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-0f21a459-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the percentage yield from the student''s experiment.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-0f21a459-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["percentage yield"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Other than spillages, suggest a possible reason why the student''s actual yield is less than expected.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-0f21a459-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 9, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789055391705.png", "width": 840, "format": "png", "height": 535, "sha256": "97ccff4c0fe3ed875fbea58523ca4a4c7df4cb54aab1d9af0d4a796df397dd90", "assetId": "img:97ccff4c0fe3ed875fbea58523ca4a4c7df4cb54aab1d9af0d4a796df397dd90", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000186", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789055391705.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q09-0f21a459", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000197"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The table gives information about some lead compounds.", "marks": 13, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "When a sample of red lead oxide is heated, it changes into a yellow solid and a gas forms that relights a glowing splint. Complete the word equation for this reaction. red lead oxide $ \\rightarrow $", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-0f21a459-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A sample of one of the oxides of lead contains 86.6% lead and 13.4% oxygen by mass. Show by calculation that the sample is lead(IV) oxide, $ \\mathrm{P b O_{2}} $ $ [ A_{r} $ of $ \\mathrm{P b}=2 0 7 $ $ A_{r} $ of $ \\mathrm{O}=1 6] $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-0f21a459-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Red lead oxide reacts with warm dilute nitric acid.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-0f21a459-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the chemical equation for the reaction. $\\mathrm {P b} _ {3} \\mathrm {O} _ {4} (\\dots \\dots$", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-0f21a459-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student is given a sample of solid red lead oxide and some dilute nitric acid. Describe how the student could obtain a pure dry sample of lead(II) nitrate crystals.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-0f21a459-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 10, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q10-0f21a459", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000209"]}], "paperTotal": 110, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 7, "2": 6, "3": 8, "5": 12, "9": 12, "10": 13}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "November 2020", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_1C_2011_MS", "canonicalDocumentId": "d1b85e6b-1de4-5d58-8a20-99909f028cb6"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1", "marks": null, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-d1b85e6b-q1", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Boron/B"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Na/Mg/Al"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 cler"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Silicon/Si"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 cler"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iv)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Nitrogen/N"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW N2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 cler"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(v)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "aluminium oxide"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW Al2O3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 cler"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D Group 0 is correct because Group 0 contains elements that are all unreactive"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 comp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not correct because Group 2 does not contain elements that are all unreactive"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not correct because Group 5 does not contain elements that are all unreactive"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not correct because Group 6 does not contain elements that are all unreactive"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B lithium is correct because lithium is the least reactive element in Group 1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 comp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not correct because caesium is not the least reactive element in Group 1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not correct because potassium is not the least reactive element in Group 1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because sodium is not the least reactive element in Group 1"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": null, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-d1b85e6b-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "1 mark for each correct line form boxes on left If more than one line from a box on left column do not award mark for that box"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "a description includingM1 measure the melting pointM2 if fixed/sharp melting point the substance is pureM3 if melts over range of temperatures the substance is a mixture"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW measure boiling point for M1 and substitute b.p.for m.p in M2 and boils for melts in M3ALLOW max 2 if reference to freezing point as opposed to melting point"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3 grad"}], "answerText": "ice turns into water solid carbon dioxide turns directly into a gas a solute is stirred into a solvent", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3", "marks": null, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-d1b85e6b-q3", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Q"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "might be explosive/dangerous/unsafe"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 grad"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "R S P Q"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 cler"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "galvanising/galvanisation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 grad"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any one from paint/oil/grease/sacrificial protection OWTTE"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE barrier methodIf answer to(i) missing or incorrect credit galvanising in(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 grad"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "zinc displaces copper"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW zinc replaces copper/zinc takes oxygen from copper"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 grad"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 copper(II) oxide/CuO"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW because copper(II) oxide/CuO/it gives oxygen to zinc/is reduced"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2 expert"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 because copper(II) oxide/CuO/it loses oxygen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP M1 or near misse.g. Cu as it gives oxygen to zinc scores 1 mark for M2"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)\nIGNORE references to electrons", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)(i)", "marks": null, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-d1b85e6b-q4ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW HO-REJECT OH"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C11is correct because11is a possiblepHforammonia solutionA is not correct because3is not a possiblepHforammonia solutionB is not correct because6is not a possiblepHforammonia solutionD is not correct because14is not a possiblepHforammonia solution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1comp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C a proton donor is correct becausean acid actsas a proton donorA is not correct becausean acid does not actas a neutron donorB is not correct becausean acid does not actas a neutron acceptorD is not correct becausean acid does not actas a proton acceptor"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1comp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is correct becausephenolphthalein is pink in alkaliand colourless in acidA is not correct becausephenolphthalein is not orange in alkali and red in acidB is not correct becausephenolphthalein is not yellow in alkali and red in acidD is not correct becausephenolphthalein is not colourless in alkali and pink in acid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1comp"}], "answerText": "hydroxide/OH-", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)", "marks": null, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-d1b85e6b-q5a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Ultraviolet radiation"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW UV radiationALLOW ultraviolet light/UV light/ultraviolet rays/UV rays"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1cler"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "$\\mathrm{Cl_{2}+CH_{4}\\rightarrow CH_{3}Cl+HCl}$"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW multiples"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1Grad"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 attraction between shared pair of electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Exp"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW M1 attraction of(two) nucleiM2 for shared/bonded pair of electrons(between them)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iv)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 the four shared pairs of electrons between carbon and the other four atoms"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Grad"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP M1ALLOW any combination of dots and crosses"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(v)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 weak forces of attraction between molecules/weak intermolecular forces"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW weak bonds between molecules/weak intermolecular bonds"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 little (heat) energy needed to overcome them"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE less energy0 marks if implication is that covalent bonds are weak/broken"}], "answerText": "Any two from\nM1 all in Group 7/same group\nM2 because all have 7/same number of electrons in outer shell\nM3 the number of shells determines the Period they are in\nM2 and nuclei of the two/both atoms(in the bond)ORM1 bonding/shared pair of electronsM2 attracted to (both) nuclei of atoms(in the bond)\nM2 rest of molecule correct including the three lone pairs of electrons around chlorine atom", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)(i)", "marks": null, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-d1b85e6b-q6ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M2 because fits general formulaCnH2n+2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 not dep on M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWC3H8"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1cler"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(compounds ofFwith same molecular formula/C4H10)but different structural/displayed formulae"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 structural/displayed formula ofbutane"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 structural/displayed formulae ofmethylpropane"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "a description including the following points"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "4Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1heat/vapourise crude oil"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWboil"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2pass into(fractionating)column/tower"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3fractions/compounds/molecules/hydrocarbons separate because of different boiling points"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWidea oftemperature gradient"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4compoundDcollectedattopofcolumn/inrefinerygasfraction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Allmarks could be scored froma suitablylabelleddiagram"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "MAX3ifdescriptionoflabprocess"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Ifco