tail": "orphan row before first entry: (c)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: 15 marks", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "January 2021", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": "Monday 11 January 2021", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH1/1C", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "14c22354-4dd0-5d8e-b89d-92ae3530f74e"}, "warnings": [], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about states of matter.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Use the words solid, liquid or gas to give the initial and final state of matter for each of the changes listed in the table. The first one has been done for you.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-14c22354-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Particles in a solid are closely packed, arranged in a regular pattern and vibrate about fixed positions. Describe the arrangement and movement of the particles in a gas.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-14c22354-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-14c22354", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000026"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The table gives the melting and boiling points of four pure substances, W, X, Y and Z.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which substance is a gas at 100 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $? A W B X C Y D Z", "label": "a", "marks": 1, "partId": "q02-14c22354-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which substance is a liquid for the largest range of temperature? A W B X C Y D Z", "label": "a-ii", "marks": 1, "partId": "q02-14c22354-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which substance is a liquid at 1000 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ and a gas at 2000 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $? A W B X C Y D Z", "label": "a-iii", "marks": 1, "partId": "q02-14c22354-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Substance Y does not conduct electricity when solid but does conduct electricity when molten. Give the type of bonding in substance Y.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-14c22354-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest how the melting point of a pure substance changes when an impurity is added.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-14c22354-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-14c22354", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000031"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Lead nitrate and potassium iodide react to form the insoluble solid lead iodide. Crystals of lead nitrate and potassium iodide are placed at opposite ends of a container of water. Solid lead iodide forms after several minutes. The diagram shows the container at the start and after several minutes. After several minutes", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name the two processes that occur before the solid lead iodide forms.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-14c22354-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why solid lead iodide takes less time to form when the reaction is repeated using water at a higher temperature.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-14c22354-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The formula for lead nitrate is $ \\mathrm{P b ( N O_{3} )_{2}} $ (i) Give the number of different elements in lead nitrate.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-14c22354-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the charge on the lead ion in $ \\mathrm{P b ( N O_{3} )_{2}} $", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-14c22354-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the chemical equation for the reaction between lead nitrate and potassium iodide. $\\mathrm {P b} \\left(\\mathrm {N O} _ {3}\\right) _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\mathrm {K I} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {P b I} _ {2} (\\mathrm {s}) + \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\mathrm {K N O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q})$", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-14c22354-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490347.png", "width": 691, "format": "png", "height": 350, "sha256": "21f145cd4b7afe7d60e7d610d136e038a95b237e0c4dd9a47e8c2f49d05f95fc", "assetId": "img:21f145cd4b7afe7d60e7d610d136e038a95b237e0c4dd9a47e8c2f49d05f95fc", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000058", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490347.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789045490382.png", "width": 539, "format": "png", "height": 221, "sha256": "4158abf39f5ee7385af15d420a873da78eb934ee5da866cee976675c10976752", "assetId": "img:4158abf39f5ee7385af15d420a873da78eb934ee5da866cee976675c10976752", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000059", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789045490382.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-14c22354", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about rusting.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "When iron rusts, it reacts with oxygen in the air. A student uses the rusting of iron to find the percentage of oxygen in a sample of air. The diagram shows the apparatus. These are the student''s results. volume of air in conical flask and connecting tube $ = 2 6 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ volume of air in gas syringe at start = 100 $ cm^{3} $ volume of air in gas syringe at end = 25 $ cm^{3} $ Calculate the percentage of oxygen in the sample of air using the student''s results.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-14c22354-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490395.png", "width": 1464, "format": "png", "height": 557, "sha256": "ba2a6e3dc296e1f1a3a068ef4968473458c43157e621006dceb7ad63b6ba5164", "assetId": "img:ba2a6e3dc296e1f1a3a068ef4968473458c43157e621006dceb7ad63b6ba5164", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000072", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490395.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Cars are painted to prevent the iron in car bodies from rusting. Explain how painting prevents the iron in car bodies from rusting.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-14c22354-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Some car manufacturers use paint containing tiny particles of zinc. Explain how particles of zinc prevent iron in car bodies from rusting even when this paint is scratched.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-14c22354-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490395.png", "width": 1464, "format": "png", "height": 557, "sha256": "ba2a6e3dc296e1f1a3a068ef4968473458c43157e621006dceb7ad63b6ba5164", "assetId": "img:ba2a6e3dc296e1f1a3a068ef4968473458c43157e621006dceb7ad63b6ba5164", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000072", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490395.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-14c22354", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about the separation of mixtures.", "marks": 10, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The box gives some methods used to separate mixtures. Complete the table by giving the correct method from the box for each separation. Each method can be used once, more than once or not at all.", "label": "a", "marks": 4, "partId": "q05-14c22354-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student uses chromatography to analyse the composition of purple ink. The diagram shows the student''s chromatogram at the end of the experiment.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-14c22354-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490410.png", "width": 724, "format": "png", "height": 832, "sha256": "eb901ac8c1c372f7fa9dbc178b03ffbc371b1d8427a80163cac85193cb1be6e9", "assetId": "img:eb901ac8c1c372f7fa9dbc178b03ffbc371b1d8427a80163cac85193cb1be6e9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000092", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490410.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which dyes are contained in the purple ink.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-14c22354-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which dye is least soluble in the solvent.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-14c22354-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A different chromatography experiment is set up. A spot of food colouring is placed on the start line. A food dye in the colouring has an $ R_{f} $ value of 0.72 The distance between the start line and the solvent front is 120mm. Calculate the distance the food dye moves from the start line.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-14c22354-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490410.png", "width": 724, "format": "png", "height": 832, "sha256": "eb901ac8c1c372f7fa9dbc178b03ffbc371b1d8427a80163cac85193cb1be6e9", "assetId": "img:eb901ac8c1c372f7fa9dbc178b03ffbc371b1d8427a80163cac85193cb1be6e9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000092", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490410.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-14c22354", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000085", "e000089"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about salts.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "When solutions of salts are mixed together, precipitates sometimes form. The insoluble salt barium carbonate forms as a precipitate when solutions of the soluble salts ammonium carbonate and barium chloride react together. When solutions of the soluble salts potassium chloride and magnesium sulfate are mixed, no precipitate forms. Complete the table to show the results of mixing solutions of some soluble salts.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-14c22354-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student has four unlabelled beakers, each containing a colourless solution of a different salt. The four solutions are potassium chloride potassium carbonate potassium iodide sodium chloride Describe a method to identify each solution. Do not refer to safety in your answer.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-14c22354-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q06-14c22354", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000107"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about lithium oxide.", "marks": 13, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the electron configurations of an atom of lithium and an atom of oxygen. oxygen Describe the changes in electronic configuration when lithium and oxygen react to form lithium oxide, $ \\mathrm{L i_{2} O} $", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-14c22354-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490447.png", "width": 341, "format": "png", "height": 416, "sha256": "b8678925b5934b93cbddf1e8f8ebd22724b61ba8a83b400ddf1d601b2e489771", "assetId": "img:b8678925b5934b93cbddf1e8f8ebd22724b61ba8a83b400ddf1d601b2e489771", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000119", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490447.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789045490450.png", "width": 340, "format": "png", "height": 351, "sha256": "48e5b4db5c4dff8ee8e9128ebbd8aadcbb8ee04f462d8509ce0d69c1776626e2", "assetId": "img:48e5b4db5c4dff8ee8e9128ebbd8aadcbb8ee04f462d8509ce0d69c1776626e2", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000120", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789045490450.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Lithium oxide reacts with water to form lithium hydroxide as the only product. A scientist uses this apparatus to measure the temperature change of the reaction. This is the scientist''s method. pour 100g of water into a polystyrene cup record the temperature of the water add the lithium oxide and stir the mixture record the maximum temperature reached The diagram shows the thermometer readings before and after adding the lithium oxide.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-14c22354-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490458.png", "width": 530, "format": "png", "height": 359, "sha256": "0875664ea6973c5343d3986c26c3c53f8cef59366dba419344febc441f733d9b", "assetId": "img:0875664ea6973c5343d3986c26c3c53f8cef59366dba419344febc441f733d9b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000124", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490458.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789045490490.png", "width": 240, "format": "png", "height": 913, "sha256": "026b59c726ed249bb0e07512522076f6b1081ba54752bb3bb2fa36079f9bd4c4", "assetId": "img:026b59c726ed249bb0e07512522076f6b1081ba54752bb3bb2fa36079f9bd4c4", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000131", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789045490490.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789045490494.png", "width": 241, "format": "png", "height": 906, "sha256": "df3594a6fd8f360a14fc0fe541aac29f99c24fec557890d80983817ef74ca8d7", "assetId": "img:df3594a6fd8f360a14fc0fe541aac29f99c24fec557890d80983817ef74ca8d7", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000132", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789045490494.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the table, giving all values to the nearest 0.1 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-14c22354-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the heat energy change in the reaction. Give your answer to two significant figures. $[ \\mathrm {c} = 4. 2 \\mathrm {J} / \\mathrm {g} / ^ {\\circ} \\mathrm {C} ]$", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-14c22354-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["heat energy change"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In another experiment the scientist obtains these results. Calculate the molar enthalpy change $ (\\Delta H) $ in kJ/mol. Include a sign in your answer.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-14c22354-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why the scientist does the experiment in a polystyrene cup.", "label": "b-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-14c22354-pb-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490447.png", "width": 341, "format": "png", "height": 416, "sha256": "b8678925b5934b93cbddf1e8f8ebd22724b61ba8a83b400ddf1d601b2e489771", "assetId": "img:b8678925b5934b93cbddf1e8f8ebd22724b61ba8a83b400ddf1d601b2e489771", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000119", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490447.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789045490450.png", "width": 340, "format": "png", "height": 351, "sha256": "48e5b4db5c4dff8ee8e9128ebbd8aadcbb8ee04f462d8509ce0d69c1776626e2", "assetId": "img:48e5b4db5c4dff8ee8e9128ebbd8aadcbb8ee04f462d8509ce0d69c1776626e2", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000120", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789045490450.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490458.png", "width": 530, "format": "png", "height": 359, "sha256": "0875664ea6973c5343d3986c26c3c53f8cef59366dba419344febc441f733d9b", "assetId": "img:0875664ea6973c5343d3986c26c3c53f8cef59366dba419344febc441f733d9b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000124", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490458.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789045490490.png", "width": 240, "format": "png", "height": 913, "sha256": "026b59c726ed249bb0e07512522076f6b1081ba54752bb3bb2fa36079f9bd4c4", "assetId": "img:026b59c726ed249bb0e07512522076f6b1081ba54752bb3bb2fa36079f9bd4c4", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000131", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789045490490.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789045490494.png", "width": 241, "format": "png", "height": 906, "sha256": "df3594a6fd8f360a14fc0fe541aac29f99c24fec557890d80983817ef74ca8d7", "assetId": "img:df3594a6fd8f360a14fc0fe541aac29f99c24fec557890d80983817ef74ca8d7", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000132", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789045490494.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-14c22354", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000134", "e000140"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about the halogens.", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The table gives some information about the halogens. Complete the table by predicting the physical state of astatine at room temperature and the colour of astatine.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-14c22354-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Bromine has two isotopes with mass numbers 79 and 81", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-14c22354-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The relative percentages of each isotope in a sample of bromine are $\\mathrm {bromine} - 7 9 = 5 1. 0 \\%$ bromine-81=49.0% Calculate the relative atomic mass of this sample of bromine. Give your answer to one decimal place.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-14c22354-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["relative atomic mass"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why both isotopes of bromine have the same chemical properties.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-14c22354-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student investigates the reactivity of some halogens. She uses these solutions of halogens and their halides. bromine, chlorine and iodine sodium bromide, sodium chloride and sodium iodide She adds each halogen solution to each halide solution. The table shows her results.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-14c22354-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain how these results show the order of reactivity of bromine, chlorine and iodine.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-14c22354-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why the student does not need to add bromine solution to sodium bromide solution.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-14c22354-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The ionic equation for the reaction between bromine and sodium iodide is $\\mathrm {B r} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \\mathrm {I} ^ {-} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {I} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \\mathrm {B r} ^ {-} (\\mathrm {a q})$ Explain why this is a redox reaction.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-14c22354-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q08-14c22354", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000148", "e000163"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Propane is a hydrocarbon with the formula $ \\mathrm{C_{3} H_{8}} $ (a) State why propane is a hydrocarbon.", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name the poisonous gas that forms when propane is burned in a limited supply of air.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-14c22354-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why this gas is poisonous to humans.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-14c22354-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram represents a molecule of propane. Describe the forces of attraction between the atoms in a molecule of propane.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-14c22354-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490546.png", "width": 1053, "format": "png", "height": 525, "sha256": "378acbf98454ec94dbc04086abf52f3826896477c3d09e69a5abfcfce0182a69", "assetId": "img:378acbf98454ec94dbc04086abf52f3826896477c3d09e69a5abfcfce0182a69", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000174", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490546.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Propane can be produced by cracking. An equation for cracking is $\\mathrm {C} _ {1 3} \\mathrm {H} _ {2 8} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C} _ {3} \\mathrm {H} _ {8} + 2 \\mathrm {C} _ {3} \\mathrm {H} _ {6} + 2 \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4}$ Explain why cracking is an important process in the oil industry.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-14c22354-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Propane reacts with bromine in the presence of ultraviolet radiation.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-14c22354-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the equation for this reaction. $\\mathrm {C} _ {3} \\mathrm {H} _ {8} + \\mathrm {B r} _ {2} \\rightarrow$", "label": "e-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-14c22354-pe-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of this type of reaction.", "label": "e-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-14c22354-pe-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 9, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490546.png", "width": 1053, "format": "png", "height": 525, "sha256": "378acbf98454ec94dbc04086abf52f3826896477c3d09e69a5abfcfce0182a69", "assetId": "img:378acbf98454ec94dbc04086abf52f3826896477c3d09e69a5abfcfce0182a69", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000174", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490546.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q09-14c22354", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about alkanes.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The graph shows the boiling points of several unbranched alkanes. $\\mathrm {i n} ^ {\\circ} \\mathrm {C}$", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-14c22354-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490580.png", "width": 1056, "format": "png", "height": 1671, "sha256": "281694ec75a6ab203d2cf9fb73e7417f290e1c03c2276d7c34aac303bb79a8bb", "assetId": "img:281694ec75a6ab203d2cf9fb73e7417f290e1c03c2276d7c34aac303bb79a8bb", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000189", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490580.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a curve of best fit.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-14c22354-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the graph to find the boiling point of the alkane with 7 carbon atoms in its molecule. Show on the graph how you obtain your answer.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-14c22354-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain the trend shown by the graph.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-14c22354-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram represents two isomers with the formula $ \\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{1 2} $ H Explain why these compounds are isomers.", "label": "b", "marks": 2, "partId": "q10-14c22354-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490606.png", "width": 418, "format": "png", "height": 223, "sha256": "caf3a43f6fc44f8a7355bd44fa2d0273c95f77d4ec5dd37899adf06dc8a06e85", "assetId": "img:caf3a43f6fc44f8a7355bd44fa2d0273c95f77d4ec5dd37899adf06dc8a06e85", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000195", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490606.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789045490903.png", "width": 353, "format": "png", "height": 354, "sha256": "ff729b24d6a2619a7bcfaeb141698e4c00674131aaa09e5ef34e9e544207daa7", "assetId": "img:ff729b24d6a2619a7bcfaeb141698e4c00674131aaa09e5ef34e9e544207daa7", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000196", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789045490903.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "An alkane contains 82.8% carbon and 17.2% hydrogen by mass. Show by calculation that the empirical formula of this alkane is $ C_{2} H_{5} $", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-14c22354-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Deduce the molecular formula of this alkane.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-14c22354-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the complete combustion of one mole of an alkane can be represented by $\\mathrm {a l k a n e} + \\mathrm {Z O} _ {2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {X C O} _ {2} + \\mathrm {Y H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ Complete combustion of one mole of the alkane produces 308 g of carbon dioxide and 144 g of water. X, Y and Z are the numbers used to balance the equation. Calculate the values of X, Y and Z. [M $ _{r} $ of $ CO_{2}=4 4 $ , M $ _{r} $ of $ H_{2}O=1 8 $ ]", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-14c22354-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 10, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490580.png", "width": 1056, "format": "png", "height": 1671, "sha256": "281694ec75a6ab203d2cf9fb73e7417f290e1c03c2276d7c34aac303bb79a8bb", "assetId": "img:281694ec75a6ab203d2cf9fb73e7417f290e1c03c2276d7c34aac303bb79a8bb", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000189", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490580.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490606.png", "width": 418, "format": "png", "height": 223, "sha256": "caf3a43f6fc44f8a7355bd44fa2d0273c95f77d4ec5dd37899adf06dc8a06e85", "assetId": "img:caf3a43f6fc44f8a7355bd44fa2d0273c95f77d4ec5dd37899adf06dc8a06e85", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000195", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490606.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789045490903.png", "width": 353, "format": "png", "height": 354, "sha256": "ff729b24d6a2619a7bcfaeb141698e4c00674131aaa09e5ef34e9e544207daa7", "assetId": "img:ff729b24d6a2619a7bcfaeb141698e4c00674131aaa09e5ef34e9e544207daa7", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000196", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789045490903.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q10-14c22354", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about reactions that form gases.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Hydrogen peroxide decomposes to form water and oxygen. The equation for the reaction is $2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} + \\mathrm {O} _ {2}$ 25. 0 $ cm^{3} $ of hydrogen peroxide solution are poured into a conical flask and 1.00 g of solid manganese(IV) oxide is added. Bubbles of oxygen gas are formed.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-14c22354-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the test for oxygen gas.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-14c22354-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe a method to show that solid manganese(IV) oxide is a catalyst in this reaction and not a reactant.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-14c22354-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student uses this apparatus to investigate the rate of the reaction between zinc and an excess of dilute hydrochloric acid. This is the student''s method. pour $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of dilute hydrochloric acid into a conical flask add about 1.2g of zinc lumps record the volume of hydrogen gas collected every 30s until no more hydrogen is collected The graph shows the student''s results.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-14c22354-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490951.png", "width": 774, "format": "png", "height": 285, "sha256": "11fa2fdc836473277991fbee125b5fd23f4c08c23544e4bbe98b181e3103d25c", "assetId": "img:11fa2fdc836473277991fbee125b5fd23f4c08c23544e4bbe98b181e3103d25c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000218", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490951.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789045490955.png", "width": 1269, "format": "png", "height": 1101, "sha256": "ef490f4be2de3259cd0fbf17287df359d2365f46e61932216fa995be8a6b1030", "assetId": "img:ef490f4be2de3259cd0fbf17287df359d2365f46e61932216fa995be8a6b1030", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000224", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789045490955.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the mean (average) rate of reaction, in $ cm^{3}/s $ , in the first 120 s. $\\mathrm {c m} ^ {3} / \\mathrm {s}$", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-14c22354-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the reaction between zinc and hydrochloric acid is $\\mathrm {Z n} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {H C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {Z n C l} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ Use this equation and the particle collision theory to explain why the rate of reaction is greatest at the start of the reaction.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-14c22354-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student repeats the experiment at a higher temperature but keeps all other conditions the same. On the grid, draw the curve you would expect to see in this experiment. Question continues on next page", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-14c22354-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why the rate of reaction is greater if the same mass of zinc powder is used instead of zinc lumps. All other conditions are kept the same.", "label": "b-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-14c22354-pb-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In another experiment, the student adds 0.55 g of zinc to a solution containing $ 2. 5 0 \\times1 0^{-2} $ moles of hydrochloric acid. Use the equation to show that hydrochloric acid is in excess. $\\mathrm {Z n} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {H C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {Z n C l} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ [ $ A_{\\mathrm{r}} $ of Zn=65] $(\\mathrm {T o t a l f o r Q u e s t i o n 1 1 = 1 5 m a r k s})$ $\\mathrm {T O T A L F O R P A P E R} = 1 1 0 \\mathrm {M A R K S}$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-14c22354-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 11, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045490951.png", "width": 774, "format": "png", "height": 285, "sha256": "11fa2fdc836473277991fbee125b5fd23f4c08c23544e4bbe98b181e3103d25c", "assetId": "img:11fa2fdc836473277991fbee125b5fd23f4c08c23544e4bbe98b181e3103d25c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000218", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045490951.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789045490955.png", "width": 1269, "format": "png", "height": 1101, "sha256": "ef490f4be2de3259cd0fbf17287df359d2365f46e61932216fa995be8a6b1030", "assetId": "img:ef490f4be2de3259cd0fbf17287df359d2365f46e61932216fa995be8a6b1030", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000224", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789045490955.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q11-14c22354", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 110, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 6, "2": 5, "3": 7, "4": 7, "5": 10, "7": 13, "8": 12, "9": 12}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "January 2021", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_1C_2101_MS", "canonicalDocumentId": "571678af-1b7e-58f9-be5b-93038ef1581c"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 3, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-571678af-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Award 1 mark for each correct rowALLOW gas to solid for sublimation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "End"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "liquid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "gas"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "liquid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "gas"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A description that refers to any three of the following pointsM1 irregular/random arrangement(of particles)M2 large gaps between them/far apart/widely spacedM3 random movement/move freelyM4 move(very) quickly"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW spread outIGNORE references to kinetic energy"}], "answerText": "Start\nsolid\nsolid\ngas\nliquid\n6 marks", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-571678af-q2ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "A is the correct answer because 100℃ is above the boiling point of W"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not the correct answer because X is a solid at 100℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not the correct answer because Y is a solid at 100℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not the correct answer because Z is a solid at 100℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is the correct answer because X is a liquid for 1840℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not the correct answer because W is a liquid for 67℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not the correct answer because Y is a liquid for 1150℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not the correct answer because Z is a liquid for 330℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is the correct answer because Y is a liquid at 1000℃ and a gas at 2000℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not the correct answer because W is a gas at 1000℃ and at 2000℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not the correct answer because X is a liquid at 1000℃ and 2000℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not the correct answer because Z is a gas at 1000℃ and at 2000℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ionic"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW electrovalent"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "the (impure) substance will melt over a range of temperatures"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW the (impure) substance will have a lower melting point"}], "answerText": "A\n5 marks", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)(i)", "marks": 2, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-571678af-q3ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Answers can be in either order"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An explanation that links any two of the following pointsM1 crystals dissolve fasterM2(potassium iodide/lead nitrate/water) particles move faster/(lead/iodide) ions move faster/rate of diffusion increasesM3 therefore(lead and iodide) ions/particles meet/collide after a shorter period of time/sooner"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW(potassium iodide/lead nitrate/water) particles have more energyALLOWmolecules in place of particles if referring to waterIGNOREreferences to more collisions or more energetic collisions"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3/three2+/+2"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWPb2+"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Pb(NO3)2(aq)+2KI(aq)→PbI2(s)+2KNO3(aq)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ALOWmultiples and fractions"}], "answerText": "M1 dissolvingM2 diffusion\n7 marks", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)", "marks": 3, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-571678af-q4a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Correct answer of 20.5% with or without working scores 3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(volume of oxygen=)100-25OR75(cm3)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWecf from M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M275÷365×100"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWecf from M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M320.5(%)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW2or more significant figures"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECTincorrect roundingUse of265 instead of365gives an answer of28.3and scores2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Alternative method"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(volume of air left=)265+25OR290(cm3)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2290÷365×100OR79.5(%)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 paint provides a barrier"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWpaint forms a coating(on the iron)/paint is non-permeable"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2which prevents oxygen/water getting to/reacting with the iron"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWair"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1zinc is more reactive/higher in the reactivity series(than iron)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWzinc is a sacrificial metal"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2zinc will oxidise/react/corrode instead of/before iron"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNOREreferences to zinc rusting"}], "answerText": "Example calculation\n7 marks", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)", "marks": 4, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-571678af-q5a", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW filteringALLOW distillationREJECT simple distillation or distillation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1A and B"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 dep on M1 correct or missing"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 because the spot is closest to the start line/travelled the least distance(from the start line)/has the lowestRfvalue"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 dep on M1 correct or missing"}, {"kind": "example-calculation", "text": "Example calculation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct answer of86 or86.4(mm) with or without working scores2"}], "answerText": "Method\nfiltration\nsimple distillationor fractional distillation\nfractional distillation\ncrystallisation\nM2 because they are the same height/moved the same distance up the paper/have the sameRfvalues as the spots in the purple ink\nM1D\n10 marks", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)", "marks": 3, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-571678af-q6a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "precipitate of barium sulfate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "if barium sulfate and calcium carbonate correct but without including‘precipitate of’ scores 1 out of 2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "no precipitate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "no precipitate"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW correct formulae"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "precipitate of calcium carbonate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "precipitate of calcium sulfate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A description that refers to any six of the following pointsM1 do a flame testM2 sodium chloride produces a yellow flameM3 add acidM4 potassium carbonate effervesces / bubblesM5 add dilute nitric acidM6 add silver nitrate (solution)M7 potassium chloride gives a white precipitateM8 potassium iodide gives a yellow precipitate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any description of a flame testACCEPT yellow-orange or orangeIGNORE any flame colour given for the potassium compoundsALLOW any named acidACCEPT carbon dioxide/gas given off which turns limewater cloudy for M4M4 is dep on M3M7 and M8 are dep on M6ALLOW addition of chlorine/bromine solution as an alternative to M6M7 no colour change with potassium chloride"}], "answerText": "precipitate of barium carbonate", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7(a)", "marks": 3, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-571678af-q7a", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW lithium loses one electron/gives one electron to oxygen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 oxygen gains two electrons"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW oxygen becomes 2,8"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 lithium(ion) has an electron configuration of 2 and oxide(ion) is 2,8"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "All 3 marks can be scored from diagrams showing the electron configurations of the ions"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "0 marks if reference to sharing electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(temperature after)=27.7℃"}