": "q01-20626212-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name a compound.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-20626212-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name a mixture.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-20626212-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name an element that is a gas at room temperature.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-20626212-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name an element that forms a basic oxide.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-20626212-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name two elements that are in the same group of the Periodic Table.", "label": "f", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-20626212-pf", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-20626212", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000024"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student does a chromatography experiment using ink 1, ink 2, and three known dyes A, B and C. The student uses water as the solvent. The diagram shows the student''s chromatogram.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Deduce what conclusions can be made about the composition of ink 1.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-20626212-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give one conclusion that can be made about ink 2.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-20626212-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest how the student could change the experiment to find the composition of ink 2.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-20626212-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the $ R_{f} $ value of dye C, giving your answer to 2 significant figures.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-20626212-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045253718.png", "width": 1144, "format": "png", "height": 1011, "sha256": "e6d6551a128f8e08961d9ea2c59247075738481471ba697f2e30dca902f9bdab", "assetId": "img:e6d6551a128f8e08961d9ea2c59247075738481471ba697f2e30dca902f9bdab", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000036", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045253718.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-20626212", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Crude oil is a mixture of organic compounds. Most of these compounds are members of the same homologous series.", "marks": 13, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State the name of this homologous series.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-20626212-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "An industrial process is used to separate crude oil into fractions.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-20626212-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The process depends on a difference in a property of the fractions. What is this property? A boiling point B density melting point D solubility", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-20626212-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The boxes give some uses of fractions and some names of fractions. Draw one straight line from each use to its correct fraction.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-20626212-pb-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045253739.png", "width": 1413, "format": "png", "height": 1225, "sha256": "17cb1be069de30648a6b6f37b0a1378275d1186dd5f7b0acd3fa7fd6dc692a96", "assetId": "img:17cb1be069de30648a6b6f37b0a1378275d1186dd5f7b0acd3fa7fd6dc692a96", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000052", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045253739.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Fuels obtained from the fractions may contain impurities. Explain how the combustion of a common impurity in fuels may cause an environmental problem.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-20626212-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Some of the fractions contain long-chain molecules which are not very useful.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-20626212-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of the process used to convert long-chain molecules into more useful shorter-chain molecules.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-20626212-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the catalyst and temperature used in the industrial process to convert long-chain molecules into shorter-chain molecules. catalyst temperature", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-20626212-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When $ C_{1 3} H_{2 8} $ is used in this process, three different molecules are formed. Complete the equation for this reaction. $\\mathrm {C} _ {1 3} \\mathrm {H} _ {2 8} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C} _ {8} \\mathrm {H} _ {1 8} +$", "label": "d-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-20626212-pd-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045253739.png", "width": 1413, "format": "png", "height": 1225, "sha256": "17cb1be069de30648a6b6f37b0a1378275d1186dd5f7b0acd3fa7fd6dc692a96", "assetId": "img:17cb1be069de30648a6b6f37b0a1378275d1186dd5f7b0acd3fa7fd6dc692a96", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000052", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045253739.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-20626212", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "When iron is left in damp air, rust forms on its surface.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State the chemical name for rust.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-20626212-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain how a barrier method prevents rusting.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-20626212-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student uses this apparatus to find the approximate percentage by volume of oxygen in air. This is the student''s method. place a graduated glass tube in a beaker of water place some damp iron wool and a rubber bung in the top of the tube record the reading of the water level in the tube leave the apparatus for a few days record the reading of the water level again The diagram shows the readings at the start and at the end of the experiment. start end", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-20626212-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045253785.png", "width": 1059, "format": "png", "height": 810, "sha256": "fe2c5dbd61a40406b54101547f87fe389bdca4f7ef8984f1cdc03bb7545e2491", "assetId": "img:fe2c5dbd61a40406b54101547f87fe389bdca4f7ef8984f1cdc03bb7545e2491", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000068", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045253785.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045253806.png", "width": 178, "format": "png", "height": 604, "sha256": "c91d3529166d0e50589ae8a0691914378a17a9797ecc3144098c592974c8e14f", "assetId": "img:c91d3529166d0e50589ae8a0691914378a17a9797ecc3144098c592974c8e14f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000076", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045253806.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789045253828.png", "width": 178, "format": "png", "height": 600, "sha256": "96672c1647eda46623795efeb19c0406a92b79207655769aa95b752c66844521", "assetId": "img:96672c1647eda46623795efeb19c0406a92b79207655769aa95b752c66844521", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000078", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789045253828.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the readings to complete the table, giving all values to the nearest $ 0. 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3}. $", "label": "b-i", "marks": 2, "partId": "q04-20626212-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student uses these results to calculate the percentage by volume of oxygen in air. Suggest why her calculated value is lower than the expected value.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": 1, "partId": "q04-20626212-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student repeats the experiment using the same apparatus. These are her results for the second experiment. volume of air in tube at start = 80.0 $ cm^{3} $ reading at start=20.0 reading at end=35.5 Use the results to calculate the percentage by volume of oxygen in air.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-20626212-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045253785.png", "width": 1059, "format": "png", "height": 810, "sha256": "fe2c5dbd61a40406b54101547f87fe389bdca4f7ef8984f1cdc03bb7545e2491", "assetId": "img:fe2c5dbd61a40406b54101547f87fe389bdca4f7ef8984f1cdc03bb7545e2491", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000068", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045253785.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045253806.png", "width": 178, "format": "png", "height": 604, "sha256": "c91d3529166d0e50589ae8a0691914378a17a9797ecc3144098c592974c8e14f", "assetId": "img:c91d3529166d0e50589ae8a0691914378a17a9797ecc3144098c592974c8e14f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000076", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045253806.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789045253828.png", "width": 178, "format": "png", "height": 600, "sha256": "96672c1647eda46623795efeb19c0406a92b79207655769aa95b752c66844521", "assetId": "img:96672c1647eda46623795efeb19c0406a92b79207655769aa95b752c66844521", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000078", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789045253828.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q04-20626212", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000081"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student uses this apparatus to investigate the effect of heat on different solid metal carbonates. This is the student''s method. use a spatula to put some metal carbonate in the boiling tube fit the delivery tube into position pour some limewater into the test tube start a timer and immediately begin to heat the metal carbonate record the time when a change first occurs in the limewater The student repeats the method using different metal carbonates. When a metal carbonate is heated a reaction sometimes occurs. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {m e t a l c a r b o n a t e} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {m e t a l o x i d e} + \\mathrm {c a r b o n d i o x i d e}$", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State the name given to this type of reaction.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-20626212-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State two variables that the student should control in this investigation.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-20626212-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why bubbles appear in the limewater immediately after heating has started but before there is any change to the metal carbonate.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-20626212-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain the purpose of limewater in this investigation.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-20626212-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows some of the results for the student''s investigation.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-20626212-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the colour change that occurs for copper(II) carbonate.", "label": "e-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-20626212-pe-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a chemical equation for this reaction of copper(II) carbonate.", "label": "e-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-20626212-pe-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "There is a relationship between the position of a metal in the reactivity series and how easily the metal carbonate reacts when heated. Use the student''s results and your own knowledge to deduce this relationship.", "label": "f", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-20626212-pf", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State how you should extend the investigation to see if your deduction is correct.", "label": "f-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-20626212-pf-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045253922.png", "width": 1411, "format": "png", "height": 564, "sha256": "9fd548a04ebd6783c98eb4bbc15bcbd274123717dfc2abb54db8474bb49725f6", "assetId": "img:9fd548a04ebd6783c98eb4bbc15bcbd274123717dfc2abb54db8474bb49725f6", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000094", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045253922.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-20626212", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000110"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Zinc reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to form hydrogen.", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Give a chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-20626212-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a test for hydrogen gas.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-20626212-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student investigates the reaction between pieces of zinc and dilute hydrochloric acid. In each experiment, he uses the same mass of zinc but a different volume of the acid. He collects the hydrogen and measures its volume in each experiment. The graph shows the student''s results. Volume of hydrogen in $ \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-20626212-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045253951.png", "width": 1068, "format": "png", "height": 1474, "sha256": "c33c1d72a1a9ca6bcfc1b1830a6bda2c851df7850b289c481d80e97e493992d3", "assetId": "img:c33c1d72a1a9ca6bcfc1b1830a6bda2c851df7850b289c481d80e97e493992d3", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000123", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045253951.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the graph to find the minimum volume of acid needed to react with all of the zinc.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-20626212-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student repeats the investigation, using hydrochloric acid of double the original concentration. Determine the volume of hydrogen that would be collected using $ 1 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of this acid. Show your working on the graph.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-20626212-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain how increasing the concentration of the hydrochloric acid affects the rate of reaction.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-20626212-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The rate of reaction could also be affected by changing the temperature of the hydrochloric acid, or by using a catalyst. Explain one other way in which the rate of reaction between zinc and hydrochloric acid can be affected.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-20626212-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045253951.png", "width": 1068, "format": "png", "height": 1474, "sha256": "c33c1d72a1a9ca6bcfc1b1830a6bda2c851df7850b289c481d80e97e493992d3", "assetId": "img:c33c1d72a1a9ca6bcfc1b1830a6bda2c851df7850b289c481d80e97e493992d3", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000123", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045253951.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-20626212", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The formation of ions and covalent bonds involves electrons. The table gives the electronic configurations of atoms of hydrogen, lithium and chlorine.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Describe the different roles of electrons in the formation of ions in lithium chloride covalent bonds in hydrogen chloride", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-20626212-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why lithium chloride has a higher melting point than hydrogen chloride. Refer to structure and bonding in your answer.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-20626212-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-20626212", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000135"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) (i) Organic compounds can exist as isomers. Explain what is meant by the term isomers. (ii) Organic compound Q reacts with bromine, without the presence of ultraviolet radiation, to form the compound $ \\mathrm{C_{4} H_{8} B r_{2}} $ Draw the displayed formulae of two isomers of Q.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "An acrylic polymer can be formed from molecules with this structure.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-20626212-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045254028.png", "width": 325, "format": "png", "height": 199, "sha256": "28f531e280ae379effca03e91b0062a8835ff8723db781313363261a3584a034", "assetId": "img:28f531e280ae379effca03e91b0062a8835ff8723db781313363261a3584a034", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000145", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045254028.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student describes the molecule as an unsaturated hydrocarbon Explain whether this is a correct description.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-20626212-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the type of polymerisation that occurs in the formation of the polymer.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-20626212-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the equation for the polymerisation reaction.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-20626212-pb-iii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045254036.png", "width": 553, "format": "png", "height": 198, "sha256": "db72538ff6a42b5649f136773e862cbbc3822d2778106bd2655764b29661a5e8", "assetId": "img:db72538ff6a42b5649f136773e862cbbc3822d2778106bd2655764b29661a5e8", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000149", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045254036.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Octane is a compound in petrol. The equation for the complete combustion of octane is $\\mathrm {C} _ {8} \\mathrm {H} _ {1 8} + 1 2. 5 \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow 8 \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} + 9 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-20626212-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The fuel tank of a car contains $ 5 0. 0 \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ of octane. Calculate the mass, in kg, of carbon dioxide formed if all the octane in the fuel tank undergoes complete combustion. [mass of 1 dm $ ^{3} $ of octane=700 g]", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-20626212-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State an environmental problem caused by carbon dioxide.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-20626212-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045254028.png", "width": 325, "format": "png", "height": 199, "sha256": "28f531e280ae379effca03e91b0062a8835ff8723db781313363261a3584a034", "assetId": "img:28f531e280ae379effca03e91b0062a8835ff8723db781313363261a3584a034", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000145", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045254028.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045254036.png", "width": 553, "format": "png", "height": 198, "sha256": "db72538ff6a42b5649f136773e862cbbc3822d2778106bd2655764b29661a5e8", "assetId": "img:db72538ff6a42b5649f136773e862cbbc3822d2778106bd2655764b29661a5e8", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000149", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045254036.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q08-20626212", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Lithium, sodium and potassium are the first three elements in Group 1 of the Periodic Table. (a) Suggest why these three elements are all stored in paraffin oil.", "marks": 13, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Caesium, Cs, is below potassium in Group 1.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-20626212-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a similarity and a difference between the reactions of potassium with water and caesium with water. similarity. difference.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-20626212-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the chemical equation for the reaction between caesium and water.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-20626212-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student investigates the temperature change in the reaction between dilute acids and solutions of Group 1 hydroxides. He uses this apparatus. This is the student''s method. measure the temperature of $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of hydrochloric acid pour the acid into a polystyrene cup add $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution to the acid measure the maximum temperature of the mixture", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-20626212-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045254074.png", "width": 732, "format": "png", "height": 526, "sha256": "b6494f3e667d5fae42b12a05c0c8080d984dd2fe078024f08d4488df776b3ba8", "assetId": "img:b6494f3e667d5fae42b12a05c0c8080d984dd2fe078024f08d4488df776b3ba8", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000166", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045254074.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest what could be added to the apparatus to improve the experiment.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-20626212-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain a change to the method that would improve the accuracy of the experiment.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-20626212-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "These are the student''s results. $\\begin{array}{l} \\mathrm {t e m p e r a t u r e o f h y d r o c h l o r i c a c i d} = 1 9. 9 ^ {\\circ} \\mathrm {C} \\\\ \\mathrm {m a x i m u m t e m p e r a t u r e o f m i x t u r e} = 2 6. 5 ^ {\\circ} \\mathrm {C} \\\\ \\end{array}$", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-20626212-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the energy change, Q, in joules for this reaction. [mass of $ 1. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of mixture $ = 1. 0 \\mathrm{g} $] [for the mixture, c=4.2 J/g/ $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $]", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-20626212-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In the student''s reaction between hydrochloric acid and sodium hydroxide, 0.050mol of water forms. Calculate the molar enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ , in kJ/mol for this reaction. $\\Delta H =$", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-20626212-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 9, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789045254074.png", "width": 732, "format": "png", "height": 526, "sha256": "b6494f3e667d5fae42b12a05c0c8080d984dd2fe078024f08d4488df776b3ba8", "assetId": "img:b6494f3e667d5fae42b12a05c0c8080d984dd2fe078024f08d4488df776b3ba8", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000166", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789045254074.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q09-20626212", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about salts.", "marks": 15, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Soluble salts can be prepared by the reaction between a metal oxide and an acid. The equation for this type of reaction is $\\mathrm {m e t a l o x i d e} + \\mathrm {a c i d} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {s a l t} + \\mathrm {w a t e r}$", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-20626212-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the name given to this type of reaction.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-20626212-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State, in terms of protons, what happens in this reaction.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-20626212-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student is given $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of dilute sulfuric acid and a bottle of solid copper(II) carbonate. Describe the method that the student should use to prepare a saturated solution of copper(II) sulfate. In your answer, refer to the pieces of apparatus that the student should use.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-20626212-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student produces dry crystals of hydrated copper(II) sulfate from the saturated solution. He calculates that 6.40 g of dry crystals should be formed. The mass of dry crystals he actually obtains is 1.80 g less than he calculated. Calculate the student''s percentage yield. Give your answer to one decimal place.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-20626212-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Gypsum is hydrated calcium sulfate. A sample of gypsum contains 79% of calcium sulfate by mass. Calculate the value of x in $ \\mathrm{C a S O_{4}. x H_{2}O} $ [ $ M_{\\mathrm{r}} $ of $ \\mathrm{C a S O_{4}}=1 3 6 $ $ M_{\\mathrm{r}} $ of $ \\mathrm{H_{2}O}=1 8 ] $", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-20626212-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe a test for calcium ions in the sample of gypsum.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-20626212-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 10, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q10-20626212", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 110, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 6, "2": 7, "3": 13, "5": 12, "6": 12, "7": 8, "9": 13, "10": 15}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "January 2021", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_1CR_2101_MS", "canonicalDocumentId": "7411fed8-4143-54d4-ba83-d72bca490e08"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-7411fed8-q1", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "copper"}, {"kind": "note", 