se, "text": "Explain which two boxes represent a mixture.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-a49dc9f1-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The list gives the names of some methods used in the separation of mixtures: chromatography Use names from the list to choose a suitable method for each separation. crystallisation distillation Each name may be used once, more than once or not at all. filtration", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-a49dc9f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Separating water from sodium chloride solution.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-a49dc9f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Separating the blue dye from a mixture of blue and red dyes.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-a49dc9f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Separating potassium nitrate from potassium nitrate solution.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-a49dc9f1-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057261891.png", "width": 248, "format": "png", "height": 255, "sha256": "aad1b505917f157103157a2d3a4b0c3af2667b19086fedeb3237920b6d235f6d", "assetId": "img:aad1b505917f157103157a2d3a4b0c3af2667b19086fedeb3237920b6d235f6d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000033", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057261891.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789057261900.png", "width": 251, "format": "png", "height": 248, "sha256": "0d6d49123985f6c15aebf1e8ecb28f18b70166a04a548a89bab40b623f062691", "assetId": "img:0d6d49123985f6c15aebf1e8ecb28f18b70166a04a548a89bab40b623f062691", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000035", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789057261900.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789057261907.png", "width": 248, "format": "png", "height": 249, "sha256": "199c9a5e6b6d235caa350ad26cc868d976ae265ab4bb4407e56d9b74ae723265", "assetId": "img:199c9a5e6b6d235caa350ad26cc868d976ae265ab4bb4407e56d9b74ae723265", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000037", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789057261907.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_4_1789057261912.png", "width": 248, "format": "png", "height": 245, "sha256": "2a54f1358110819fae75bed98956214c200f4c0d6f6aadc176d60e81cbb5ad2c", "assetId": "img:2a54f1358110819fae75bed98956214c200f4c0d6f6aadc176d60e81cbb5ad2c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000039", "sourceName": "assets/crop_4_1789057261912.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_5_1789057261919.png", "width": 247, "format": "png", "height": 243, "sha256": "f694d1bc11dedfc6c99fecac508bc8462ce458e417c52fe06a651c4364cdbd82", "assetId": "img:f694d1bc11dedfc6c99fecac508bc8462ce458e417c52fe06a651c4364cdbd82", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000040", "sourceName": "assets/crop_5_1789057261919.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_6_1789057261926.png", "width": 237, "format": "png", "height": 224, "sha256": "1041e867ffffb1f8944cae1d39a218834a9482056a2d9d6ca8a4602b73376898", "assetId": "img:1041e867ffffb1f8944cae1d39a218834a9482056a2d9d6ca8a4602b73376898", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000042", "sourceName": "assets/crop_6_1789057261926.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-a49dc9f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Ammonium chloride decomposes in a reversible reaction. The equation for this reaction is $\\mathrm {N H} _ {4} \\mathrm {C l} (\\mathrm {s}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {N H} _ {3} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {H C l} (\\mathrm {g})$", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State how the equation shows that the reaction is reversible.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-a49dc9f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Some ammonium chloride is heated gently in a test tube. The diagram shows the test tube after it has been heated gently for a short time.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-a49dc9f1-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057261931.png", "width": 873, "format": "png", "height": 588, "sha256": "449bd290622701ea09d59146c1cfae14e2953f966ddfce7702aa9e61ad9deb74", "assetId": "img:449bd290622701ea09d59146c1cfae14e2953f966ddfce7702aa9e61ad9deb74", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000064", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057261931.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify solid X and the two gases formed in region Y of the test tube. Solid X. Gases in region Y.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-a49dc9f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which change of state occurs in the test tube during heating? A condensing B evaporating C melting D subliming", "label": "b-ii", "marks": 1, "partId": "q03-a49dc9f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "An experiment involving ammonium chloride can be used to show the process of diffusion. The diagram shows the apparatus at the start of the experiment. cotton wool soaked in concentrated ammonia solution cotton wool soaked in concentrated hydrochloric acid At the end of the experiment, a white solid forms in the test tube. Explain which position, A, B or C, shows where the white solid forms.", "label": "c", "marks": 3, "partId": "q03-a49dc9f1-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057261936.png", "width": 977, "format": "png", "height": 210, "sha256": "5bf37c62fb5b4854a8751c5ae02c105a719ea082e971601e4df622591cf2994c", "assetId": "img:5bf37c62fb5b4854a8751c5ae02c105a719ea082e971601e4df622591cf2994c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000078", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057261936.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057261931.png", "width": 873, "format": "png", "height": 588, "sha256": "449bd290622701ea09d59146c1cfae14e2953f966ddfce7702aa9e61ad9deb74", "assetId": "img:449bd290622701ea09d59146c1cfae14e2953f966ddfce7702aa9e61ad9deb74", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000064", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057261931.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057261936.png", "width": 977, "format": "png", "height": 210, "sha256": "5bf37c62fb5b4854a8751c5ae02c105a719ea082e971601e4df622591cf2994c", "assetId": "img:5bf37c62fb5b4854a8751c5ae02c105a719ea082e971601e4df622591cf2994c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000078", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057261936.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-a49dc9f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The percentage by volume of oxygen in air can be found by using the rusting of iron. An excess of wet iron filings is used. At the start of each experiment, the reading on the syringe is recorded and the apparatus is then left for a week so that the reaction is complete. The reading on the syringe is then recorded again. A student sets up this apparatus to measure the volume of oxygen in a sample of air.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the readings in one experiment. the syringe reading at the end of this experiment Complete the table to show: the volume of oxygen used in the experiment.", "label": "a", "marks": 2, "partId": "q04-a49dc9f1-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_2_1789057261955.png", "width": 808, "format": "png", "height": 464, "sha256": "75f0211fa189660c41581a0b6e3dceb2be9d9a14cfb130b6abf2e48c5b7a5af6", "assetId": "img:75f0211fa189660c41581a0b6e3dceb2be9d9a14cfb130b6abf2e48c5b7a5af6", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000090", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789057261955.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the results recorded by a different student in her experiment. Calculate the percentage of oxygen in air using these results.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-a49dc9f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows some possible causes of anomalous results in this experiment. Use terms from the box to complete the table, showing possible causes and their effects on the volume of oxygen used in this experiment. decreased increased no effect Each term may be used once, more than once, or not at all.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-a49dc9f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057261941.png", "width": 1326, "format": "png", "height": 458, "sha256": "5dc7a68275253cda0ed38c114f4568915efebfdea7bef3fb9de36b4ef25ff1db", "assetId": "img:5dc7a68275253cda0ed38c114f4568915efebfdea7bef3fb9de36b4ef25ff1db", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000085", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057261941.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789057261955.png", "width": 808, "format": "png", "height": 464, "sha256": "75f0211fa189660c41581a0b6e3dceb2be9d9a14cfb130b6abf2e48c5b7a5af6", "assetId": "img:75f0211fa189660c41581a0b6e3dceb2be9d9a14cfb130b6abf2e48c5b7a5af6", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000090", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789057261955.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-a49dc9f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000095", "e000097", "e000104"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A metal is added to copper(II) sulfate solution. $\\mathrm {m e t a l} + \\mathrm {c o p p e r} (\\mathrm {I I}) \\mathrm {s u l f a t e} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {m e t a l} \\mathrm {s u l f a t e} + \\mathrm {c o p p e r}$ Displacement reactions are exothermic. The more reactive the metal added, the greater the temperature rise. pour some copper(II) sulfate solution into a boiling tube and record its temperature using a thermometer add some metal to the tube and stir with the thermometer record the maximum temperature of the contents of the tube. He repeats the method using the same amount, in moles, of different metals. A student uses the following method in an experiment to compare the reactivities of different metals:", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "To make the experiment valid, he starts with the copper(II) sulfate solution and the added metal at the same temperature. State two other variables that must be controlled if the experiment is to be valid. 2.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-a49dc9f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Another student uses the same method three times for each of the metals E,F,G and H. The table shows her results for these metals.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-a49dc9f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student calculates the mean temperature increase for metals E and F. She does not include anomalous values in her calculations. Calculate the mean temperature increase for metals G and H, ignoring any anomalous values. Write your answers in the table.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-a49dc9f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which metal is the most reactive.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-a49dc9f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which metal is less reactive than copper.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-a49dc9f1-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-a49dc9f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000119"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about the elements in Group 1 of the Periodic Table and their reactions with water.", "marks": 9, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State why sodium and potassium are in Group 1 of the Periodic Table.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-a49dc9f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A reaction occurs when a small piece of sodium is added to a large volume of water in a trough.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-a49dc9f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give two observations that you would make during this reaction.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-a49dc9f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "After the reaction has finished, a few drops of universal indicator are added to the solution in the trough. Explain the final colour of the universal indicator.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-a49dc9f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the most likely pH value of the solution in the trough after the reaction is complete? A 2 B 5 C 8 D 12", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-a49dc9f1-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of a Group 1 metal that is less reactive than sodium.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-a49dc9f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A small piece of potassium is added to a large volume of water in a trough. Give one observation that is made when potassium is added to water that is not made when sodium is added to water.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-a49dc9f1-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the equation for the reaction of rubidium with water. State symbols are not required. Rb+ $ \\mathrm{H_{2}O}\\rightarrow $ RbOH+ $ \\mathrm{H_{2}} $", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-a49dc9f1-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-a49dc9f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about hydrocarbons.", "marks": 13, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the formulae of some hydrocarbons.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-a49dc9f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the letters that represent hydrocarbons with the general formula $ C_{n}H_{2 n} $", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-a49dc9f1-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why B is the only hydrocarbon shown as a displayed formula.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-a49dc9f1-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which two letters represent isomers.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-a49dc9f1-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "How many of the hydrocarbons are members of the homologous series of alkanes?", "label": "a-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-a49dc9f1-pa-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Many hydrocarbons are used as fuels. There are problems associated with this use.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-a49dc9f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain how the combustion of a hydrocarbon can lead to the formation of a poisonous gas.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-a49dc9f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why this gas is poisonous to humans.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-a49dc9f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Fuels used in cars often have sulfur compounds removed. Explain how the combustion of these fuels in car engines still leads to the formation of acid rain.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-a49dc9f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-a49dc9f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000144"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The copper(II) carbonate in the mineral, malachite, reacts with hydrochloric acid according to this equation. $\\mathrm {C u C O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {H C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C u C l} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ Some students investigate the effect of changing the concentration of acid on the rate of this reaction. The diagram shows the apparatus they use. This is the method they use: set the balance to zero add an excess of malachite lumps to the conical flask and replace the cotton wool start a timer and record the balance reading after one minute. The experiment is repeated using different concentrations of hydrochloric acid. The mass and number of malachite lumps are kept the same in each experiment.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the results obtained in one series of experiments. State why the balance readings have negative values.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-a49dc9f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The graph shows the results of this series of experiments. loss in mass / g The circled point indicates an anomalous result.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-a49dc9f1-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057261968.png", "width": 1068, "format": "png", "height": 909, "sha256": "981cb3efc51379cfb9c1a0667d82f3471c33163cd84243adf3116ab859c683b3", "assetId": "img:981cb3efc51379cfb9c1a0667d82f3471c33163cd84243adf3116ab859c683b3", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000169", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057261968.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest one mistake the students could have made to produce this result.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-a49dc9f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the relationship shown by the graph.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-a49dc9f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why an increase in the concentration of the acid causes an increase in the rate of the reaction. You should use the particle collision theory in your answer.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-a49dc9f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057261963.png", "width": 1077, "format": "png", "height": 471, "sha256": "76affca7271389be93fdd95eea344db53e44c6482d309f6b6fe09b4a7b69a4a2", "assetId": "img:76affca7271389be93fdd95eea344db53e44c6482d309f6b6fe09b4a7b69a4a2", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000158", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057261963.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057261968.png", "width": 1068, "format": "png", "height": 909, "sha256": "981cb3efc51379cfb9c1a0667d82f3471c33163cd84243adf3116ab859c683b3", "assetId": "img:981cb3efc51379cfb9c1a0667d82f3471c33163cd84243adf3116ab859c683b3", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000169", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057261968.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q08-a49dc9f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000165"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The flow chart shows how ethene can be obtained industrially from crude oil.", "marks": 19, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Step 1 involves the use of a tall column. Describe how the diesel fraction is obtained from the crude oil in step 1.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-a49dc9f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In step 2, saturated compounds such as dodecane are obtained from the mixture of hydrocarbons in the diesel fraction. Explain why dodecane is described as a saturated hydrocarbon.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-a49dc9f1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these formulae is that of an alkane? $\\square \\mathrm {A} \\mathrm {C} _ {7} \\mathrm {H} _ {1 2}$ $\\square \\mathrm {B} \\mathrm {C} _ {9} \\mathrm {H} _ {1 8}$ $\\square \\mathrm {C} \\mathrm {C} _ {1 1} \\mathrm {H} _ {2 4}$ $\\mathrm {C} _ {1 3} \\mathrm {H} _ {3 0}$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-a49dc9f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In step 3, cracking is used to convert alkanes into alkenes. Complete the equation to show the reaction in which one molecule of dodecane is converted into two molecules of ethene and one molecule of another hydrocarbon. $\\mathrm {C} _ {1 2} \\mathrm {H} _ {2 6} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4} +$", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-a49dc9f1-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Alkanes and alkenes both react with halogens, but in different ways. The equations for two examples of these different reactions are shown. equation 1 $\\begin{array}{l} \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {6} + \\mathrm {C l} _ {2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {5} \\mathrm {C l} + \\mathrm {c o m p o u n d} X \\\\ \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4} + \\mathrm {B r} _ {2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4} \\mathrm {B r} _ {2} \\\\ \\end{array}$ equation 2", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-a49dc9f1-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the condition needed for the reaction in equation 1 to occur.", "label": "e-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-a49dc9f1-pe-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Deduce the formula of compound X.", "label": "e-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-a49dc9f1-pe-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a dot-and-cross diagram to represent a molecule of $ \\mathrm{C_{2} H_{5} Cl} $ Show only the outer electrons of each atom.", "label": "e-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-a49dc9f1-pe-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Equation 2 shows an example of an addition reaction. State the type of reaction shown by equation 1.", "label": "e-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-a49dc9f1-pe-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Alkenes can be distinguished from alkanes using bromine water.", "label": "f", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-a49dc9f1-pf", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What colour change occurs in the reaction between propene and bromine water? A colourless to orange B colourless to green C green to colourless D orange to colourless", "label": "f-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-a49dc9f1-pf-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A compound formed in the reaction between propene and bromine water has the percentage composition by mass C = 25.9% H = 5.0% Br = 57.6% and O = 11.5% Calculate the empirical formula of this compound.", "label": "f-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-a49dc9f1-pf-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["empirical formula"]}], "number": 9, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057261987.png", "width": 1481, "format": "png", "height": 128, "sha256": "3aaf81577cbd3215602e20e919c37fd2f88a71234056f0604f3d8e6ee71ec46e", "assetId": "img:3aaf81577cbd3215602e20e919c37fd2f88a71234056f0604f3d8e6ee71ec46e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000177", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057261987.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q09-a49dc9f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "When aqueous solutions of potassium hydroxide and nitric acid are mixed together, an exothermic reaction occurs. The diagram shows the apparatus used in an experiment to measure the temperature increase. This is the student''s method; use the larger measuring cylinder to add $ 2 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of aqueous potassium hydroxide to the polystyrene cup record the steady temperature use the smaller measuring cylinder to add $ 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of dilute nitric acid to the cup, stir the mixture with the thermometer record the highest temperature of the mixture continue adding further $ 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ portions of dilute nitric acid to the cup, stirring and recording the temperature, until a total volume of $ 3 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ has been added.", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "A teacher advises the student to use a $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ burette instead of the $ 1 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ measuring cylinder. Suggest two reasons why it would be better to use a burette instead of a measuring cylinder to add the acid in this experiment.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-a49dc9f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the thermometer readings at the start and at the end of one experiment. Complete the table to show: the thermometer reading at the start of the experiment the temperature rise in the experiment.", "label": "b", "marks": 2, "partId": "q10-a49dc9f1-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057262012.png", "width": 199, "format": "png", "height": 725, "sha256": "98dbea0950b09d9ff04537c7ce6775ae510185a7c15364497d6744e9e17aeeca", "assetId": "img:98dbea0950b09d9ff04537c7ce6775ae510185a7c15364497d6744e9e17aeeca", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000221", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057262012.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789057262023.png", "width": 211, "format": "png", "height": 723, "sha256": "414803b115e331c5d146cf53a9bad1492588b9ae13865cbf2fc4544e8c6d8d79", "assetId": "img:414803b115e331c5d146cf53a9bad1492588b9ae13865cbf2fc4544e8c6d8d79", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000222", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789057262023.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Another student uses the same method, adding the dilute nitric acid from a burette. The table shows his results. This is the student''s graph. The point where the lines cross represents complete neutralisation.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-a49dc9f1-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057262030.png", "width": 1026, "format": "png", "height": 887, "sha256": "9a8361d1ae3f09ebe33ef0f6eec85bf9f626237b5d3ca25b25c4ed8ccf0cea5d", "assetId": "img:9a8361d1ae3f09ebe33ef0f6eec85bf9f626237b5d3ca25b25c4ed8ccf0cea5d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000232", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057262030.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the maximum temperature reached during the experiment.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-a49dc9f1-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["maximum temperature"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the volume of dilute nitric acid that exactly neutralises the $ 2 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of aqueous potassium hydroxide. $\\mathrm {v o l u m e} =$", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-a49dc9f1-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Another student records these results. volume of aqueous potassium hydroxide = 20.0 $ cm^{3} $ starting temperature of aqueous potassium hydroxide = 18.5 $ ^{\\circ} C $ maximum temperature of mixture = 30.0 $ ^{\\circ} C $ volume of dilute nitric acid = 20.0 $ cm^{3} $ Calculate the heat energy released in this experiment. $\\mathrm {c} = 4. 2 \\mathrm {J} / \\mathrm {g} / ^ {\\circ} \\mathrm {C}$ mass of 1 $ cm^{3} $ of mixture = 1 g", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-a49dc9f1-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In another experiment, the heat energy released is 1600 J when 0.040 mol of potassium hydroxide is neutralised. Calculate the value of $ \\Delta H $ , in kJ/mol, for the neutralisation of potassium hydroxide. $\\Delta H =$", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q10-a49dc9f1-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 10, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057261991.png", "width": 267, "format": "png", "height": 541, "sha256": "6a8a236c249157ea1b7fe35bf2c45f69fe7a463d922a9e39e868ed5b187bc20c", "assetId": "img:6a8a236c249157ea1b7fe35bf2c45f69fe7a463d922a9e39e868ed5b187bc20c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000210", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057261991.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789057261996.png", "width": 737, "format": "png", "height": 509, "sha256": "cfa58b4d12edab683ffe963631d305349a0a61f4d0dc5a4b9876b69cbe6fea92", "assetId": "img:cfa58b4d12edab683ffe963631d305349a0a61f4d0dc5a4b9876b69cbe6fea92", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000211", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789057261996.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057262012.png", "width": 199, "format": "png", "height": 725, "sha256": "98dbea0950b09d9ff04537c7ce6775ae510185a7c15364497d6744e9e17aeeca", "assetId": "img:98dbea0950b09d9ff04537c7ce6775ae510185a7c15364497d6744e9e17aeeca", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000221", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057262012.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789057262023.png", "width": 211, "format": "png", "height": 723, "sha256": "414803b115e331c5d146cf53a9bad1492588b9ae13865cbf2fc4544e8c6d8d79", "assetId": "img:414803b115e331c5d146cf53a9bad1492588b9ae13865cbf2fc4544e8c6d8d79", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000222", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789057262023.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057262030.png", "width": 1026, "format": "png", "height": 887, "sha256": "9a8361d1ae3f09ebe33ef0f6eec85bf9f626237b5d3ca25b25c4ed8ccf0cea5d", "assetId": "img:9a8361d1ae3f09ebe33ef0f6eec85bf9f626237b5d3ca25b25c4ed8ccf0cea5d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000232", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057262030.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q10-a49dc9f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000227", "e000230"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Many different salts can be prepared from acids.", "marks": 17, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the reactants used in two salt preparations. Complete the table to show the name of the salt formed and the other product(s) in each case.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-a49dc9f1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student uses the reaction between aluminium hydroxide and dilute sulfuric acid to prepare a pure, dry sample of aluminium sulfate crystals. The equation for the reaction used to prepare this salt is $2 \\mathrm {A l} (\\mathrm {O H}) _ {3} + 3 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {A l} _ {2} \\left(\\mathrm {S O} _ {4}\\right) _ {3} + 6 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ The diagram shows the steps in the student''s method. dilute sulfuric acid step 1 aluminium hydroxide step 2 step 3 step 4 step 5", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-a49dc9f1-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057262041.png", "width": 195, "format": "png", "height": 414, "sha256": "b48219b669b055046466059f81af5fb22df6c22df89f220e0cc762cf6a7700cb", "assetId": "img:b48219b669b055046466059f81af5fb22df6c22df89f220e0cc762cf6a7700cb", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000260", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057262041.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789057262048.png", "width": 222, "format": "png", "height": 392, "sha256": "ef54155c2c82416fca6af2129fefdc89f0e0b1d098ac8ee9715f204e1250d7a3", "assetId": "img:ef54155c2c82416fca6af2129fefdc89f0e0b1d098ac8ee9715f204e1250d7a3", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000263", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789057262048.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789057262053.png", "width": 252, "format": "png", "height": 452, "sha256": "de19eae2b196993fb1d28b4d1f608123beb85fe5c68dd65f1f8d80cd1b613a78", "assetId": "img:de19eae2b196993fb1d28b4d1f608123beb85fe5c68dd65f1f8d80cd1b613a78", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000265", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789057262053.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_4_1789057262061.png", "width": 200, "format": "png", "height": 254, "sha256": "65180cc963a6ca7f53fe9ce4e80f2eab9d1538657026f94a45016c2571a289fc", "assetId": "img:65180cc963a6ca7f53fe9ce4e80f2eab9d1538657026f94a45016c2571a289fc", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000267", "sourceName": "assets/crop_4_1789057262061.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_5_1789057262072.png", "width": 131, "format": "png", "height": 80, "sha256": "2b127f4aadfba467e785793a20708f293c26d4a04a3311b5dd28d48ec17c2983", "assetId": "img:2b127f4aadfba467e785793a20708f293c26d4a04a3311b5dd28d48ec17c2983", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000269", "sourceName": "assets/crop_5_1789057262072.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State two ways to make sure that all the acid is reacted in step 2.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-a49dc9f1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the purpose of filtration in step 3.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-a49dc9f1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In step 5, the basin is left to cool to room temperature to allow crystals of aluminium sulfate to form. State one method of drying these crystals.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-a49dc9f1-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student records this information about the reagents she uses in her preparation. mass of aluminium hydroxide = 3.9 g amount of sulfuric acid = 0.090 mol Determine which reagent is in excess, making use of this information and the equation in part (b).", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-a49dc9f1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["reagent used in excess"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Another student prepares 0.25 mol of aluminium sulfate. The formula of aluminium sulfate is $ \\mathrm{A l}_{2} \\left( \\mathrm{S O}_{4} \\right)_{3} $ Calculate the mass of aluminium sulfate prepared.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-a49dc9f1-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for another reaction used to prepare a sample of a salt is $\\mathrm {P b O} + 2 \\mathrm {H N O} _ {3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {P b} \\left(\\mathrm {N O} _ {3}\\right) _ {2} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ In one experiment, the amount of lead(II) oxide used was 0.75 mol and the amount of nitric acid used was 1.5 mol. At the end of the experiment, the mass of lead(II) nitrate obtained was 209 g. Calculate the percentage yield of lead(II) nitrate in this experiment. [ $ M_{\\mathrm{r}} $ of lead(II) nitrate=331]", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q11-a49dc9f1-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 11, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789057262041.png", "width": 195, "format": "png", "height": 414, "sha256": "b48219b669b055046466059f81af5fb22df6c22df89f220e0cc762cf6a7700cb", "assetId": "img:b48219b669b055046466059f81af5fb22df6c22df89f220e0cc762cf6a7700cb", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000260", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789057262041.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789057262048.png", "width": 222, "format": "png", "height": 392, "sha256": "ef54155c2c82416fca6af2129fefdc89f0e0b1d098ac8ee9715f204e1250d7a3", "assetId": "img:ef54155c2c82416fca6af2129fefdc89f0e0b1d098ac8ee9715f204e1250d7a3", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000263", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789057262048.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789057262053.png", "width": 252, "format": "png", "height": 452, "sha256": "de19eae2b196993fb1d28b4d1f608123beb85fe5c68dd65f1f8d80cd1b613a78", "assetId": "img:de19eae2b196993fb1d28b4d1f608123beb85fe5c68dd65f1f8d80cd1b613a78", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000265", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789057262053.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_4_1789057262061.png", "width": 200, "format": "png", "height": 254, "sha256": "65180cc963a6ca7f53fe9ce4e80f2eab9d1538657026f94a45016c2571a289fc", "assetId": "img:65180cc963a6ca7f53fe9ce4e80f2eab9d1538657026f94a45016c2571a289fc", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000267", "sourceName": "assets/crop_4_1789057262061.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_5_1789057262072.png", "width": 131, "format": "png", "height": 80, "sha256": "2b127f4aadfba467e785793a20708f293c26d4a04a3311b5dd28d48ec17c2983", "assetId": "img:2b127f4aadfba467e785793a20708f293c26d4a04a3311b5dd28d48ec17c2983", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000269", "sourceName": "assets/crop_5_1789057262072.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q11-a49dc9f1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000254"]}], "paperTotal": 110, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 5, "2": 7, "3": 7, "4": 8, "5": 8, "6": 9, "7": 13, "8": 5, "9": 19, "10": 12, "11": 17}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": null, "session": null, "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "fd84b468-ed62-5e97-be75-fb00c118c720"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-fd84b468-q1a", "guidance": [], "answerText": "Nucleus", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(b)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-fd84b468-q1b", "guidance": [], "answerText": "Proton", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(c)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-fd84b468-q1c", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "accept equal numbers of positive and negative particles/charges"}], "answerText": "Equal numbers of protons and electrons", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(d)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-fd84b468-q1d", "guidance": [], "answerText": "5", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(e)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-fd84b468-q1e", "guidance": [], "answerText": "Lithium", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)(i)", "marks": 2, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-fd84b468-q2ai", "guidance": [{"kin