ce": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "8(d)(i): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "June 2011", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": "Wednesday 15 June 2011 – Morning", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH0/2C", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "6398c196-1b3d-58d5-a2eb-ef60f560d52c"}, "warnings": ["unclassified heading: Mass of sodium azide required =", "Q5: incomplete MCQ option set folded into stem"], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A small piece of potassium is added to water. The list below shows some statements. Only four of these statements describe what happens when potassium reacts with water. Place a cross ( $ \\times $ ) in the box next to each of the four correct statements. potassium oxide solution is formed fizzing occurs potassium sinks to the bottom of the water potassium moves around potassium melts bubbles of oxygen gas are produced a lilac flame is seen potassium reacts to form an acidic solution", "marks": 4, "parts": [], "number": 1, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-6398c196", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Choose the name of a substance from the box to answer parts (a) to (e). Each name may be used once, more than once or not at all. ammonia chlorine haematite iron sodium hydroxide Give the name of", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "a solid that conducts electricity.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6398c196-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "a metal ore.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6398c196-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "a substance formed in the Haber process.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6398c196-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "a substance used to make soap.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6398c196-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "a substance used to make fertilisers.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-6398c196-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-6398c196", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The photograph shows the planet Venus. Although Venus is similar in size to the Earth, it is very different in other ways. The temperature at the surface of Venus is about 470 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The atmospheric pressure is 90 times that of the Earth. The clouds in the atmosphere of Venus are made up of droplets of sulfuric acid. The table lists some properties of metals that could be used to make a space probe to land on Venus. The probe needs to be launched with enough energy to escape the Earth''s gravity. To make this easier, the mass of the probe needs to be as low as possible. The probe also needs to withstand the conditions on the surface of Venus. Use the information in the table to answer parts (a) to (c).", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which metal in the table could be used to make a probe with the lowest density?", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-6398c196-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Why would this metal be unsuitable for making a probe to land on Venus?", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-6398c196-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Very small amounts of lead can be used in electrical circuits. Why would lead not be suitable for use in the electrical circuits of a probe to land on Venus?", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-6398c196-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Choose a metal from the table that would be the most suitable for making a probe to land on Venus. Give two reasons for your choice. Metal Reasons", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-6398c196-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064334358.png", "width": 737, "format": "png", "height": 748, "sha256": "119eb630a0f619112e064e744629dd11e860f74d1fe69ab6ec07931a39d435c1", "assetId": "img:119eb630a0f619112e064e744629dd11e860f74d1fe69ab6ec07931a39d435c1", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000043", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064334358.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-6398c196", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000048"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Here are some statements about the compound ethene. ethene has the displayed formula ethene is a gas at room temperature ethene burns with a smoky flame ethene is unsaturated ethene is insoluble in water ethene can be prepared from ethanol ethene is used to make the polymer poly(ethene)", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State why ethene is described as unsaturated.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-6398c196-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe a chemical test to show that ethene is an alkene. Test Result", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-6398c196-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the following equation that represents the preparation of ethene from ethanol. $\\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {5} \\mathrm {O H} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4} + \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-6398c196-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the name given to this type of reaction?", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-6398c196-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the equation to show the formation of poly(ethene) from ethene.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-6398c196-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064334385.png", "width": 640, "format": "png", "height": 186, "sha256": "1416b7207a59c577e7bc67856eb2eaff9671afc615306f7d78f8e0f73eff99f9", "assetId": "img:1416b7207a59c577e7bc67856eb2eaff9671afc615306f7d78f8e0f73eff99f9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000076", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064334385.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064334377.png", "width": 226, "format": "png", "height": 174, "sha256": "ebd57c12e59ee06c20deb573269c72530f494061d804890edebbe1747998f07a", "assetId": "img:ebd57c12e59ee06c20deb573269c72530f494061d804890edebbe1747998f07a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000061", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064334377.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064334385.png", "width": 640, "format": "png", "height": 186, "sha256": "1416b7207a59c577e7bc67856eb2eaff9671afc615306f7d78f8e0f73eff99f9", "assetId": "img:1416b7207a59c577e7bc67856eb2eaff9671afc615306f7d78f8e0f73eff99f9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000076", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064334385.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q04-6398c196", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "When soap is shaken with water, a lather forms. A lather is a collection of small bubbles that form on the surface of the water. Very little soap is needed to form a lather with pure water. Water that needs a much larger quantity of soap to form a lather is called hard water. Water becomes hard when certain compounds are dissolved in it. This is the method she used. Equal amounts of five different compounds were dissolved in equal volumes of pure water in separate test tubes. Soap solution was added to each test tube, one drop at a time. One drop of soap solution has a volume of $ 0. 0 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3}. $ The test tubes were shaken after each addition of soap solution. Soap solution was added drop by drop until a lather formed on shaking. The volume of soap solution needed to form a lather was recorded. The experiment was repeated three times with each compound. Pure water was also tested in the same way. Her results are shown in the table: A student carried out an experiment to find out which compounds make water hard.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name two compounds that made the water hard.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-6398c196-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Why did the student carry out the experiment three times with each compound?", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-6398c196-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Circle the anomalous result in the table.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-6398c196-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What should the student have done after she identified this anomalous result?", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-6398c196-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Place a cross ( $ \\times $ ) in one box next to the name of the apparatus that the student should use to add the soap solution. beaker burette measuring cylinder pipette", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-6398c196-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the average (mean) volume of soap solution needed to form a lather with the magnesium chloride solution. Give your answer to two decimal places. Average (mean) = ... $ cm^{3} $", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-6398c196-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-6398c196", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000090"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Diamond and graphite are two naturally-occurring forms of carbon. The diagrams below show the arrangement of the carbon atoms in diamond and in graphite. The black dots ( $ \\cdot $ ) represent carbon atoms.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name the type of structure in diamond and explain, in terms of its bonding, why diamond has a high melting point.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-6398c196-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain, in terms of its structure, why graphite can act as a lubricant.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-6398c196-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The structure of graphite has one feature in common with that of metals. This feature allows graphite to conduct electricity. Suggest what this feature is and why it allows graphite to conduct electricity.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-6398c196-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In 1985, a new form of carbon was discovered. It was called buckminsterfullerene after the architect Buckminster Fuller, who designed buildings with complex geometric shapes. Buckminsterfullerene $ \\left( \\mathrm{C}_{60} \\right) $ has a simple molecular structure containing 60 carbon atoms per molecule. It looks a little bit like a football. Suggest why buckminsterfullerene has a much lower melting point than diamond.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-6398c196-pd", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064334420.png", "width": 517, "format": "png", "height": 520, "sha256": "3bf687020daedfd6d7be1b63b148c50e6d262718c429382d5bc878cdb6ee8a02", "assetId": "img:3bf687020daedfd6d7be1b63b148c50e6d262718c429382d5bc878cdb6ee8a02", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000112", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064334420.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064334420.png", "width": 517, "format": "png", "height": 520, "sha256": "3bf687020daedfd6d7be1b63b148c50e6d262718c429382d5bc878cdb6ee8a02", "assetId": "img:3bf687020daedfd6d7be1b63b148c50e6d262718c429382d5bc878cdb6ee8a02", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000112", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064334420.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q06-6398c196", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000105"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Sodium azide $ \\mathrm{(N a N_{3})} $ is a stable compound at room temperature but decomposes when heated to $ 3 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . The equation for the decomposition is: $2 \\mathrm {N a N} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {N a} (\\mathrm {I}) + 3 \\mathrm {N} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ Sodium azide is used to produce nitrogen gas to inflate car airbags. If a car is involved in a collision, the sodium azide decomposes. The nitrogen gas is produced very rapidly and the airbag inflates almost immediately.", "marks": 9, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "A fully-inflated airbag has a total volume of 108 dm $ ^{3} $ . Calculate the amount of nitrogen, in moles, in a fully-inflated airbag. [You should assume that the volume of one mole of nitrogen inside the airbag is $ 2 4 \\mathrm{~ d m}^{3} $]", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6398c196-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["Amount of nitrogen"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use your answer to (a)(i) to calculate the mass, in grams, of sodium azide required to produce $ 1 0 8 \\mathrm{~ d m}^{3} $ of nitrogen.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6398c196-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The airbag also contains potassium nitrate. This reacts with sodium formed in the decomposition of sodium azide. The equation for the reaction is: $1 0 \\mathrm {N a} (\\mathrm {I}) + 2 \\mathrm {K N O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {K} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {s}) + 5 \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {s}) + \\mathrm {N} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6398c196-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest one reason why the makers of the airbag might want this reaction to occur.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6398c196-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The airbag also contains silicon dioxide $ \\mathrm{(S i O_{2})} $ which reacts with the oxides produced in the reaction above. This forms a glassy solid which seals all the products into the airbag. The glassy solid contains potassium silicate $ \\left(\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{S i O}_{3}\\right) $ Construct an equation for the formation of potassium silicate from potassium oxide. Include state symbols.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6398c196-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Another use of sodium azide is to make lead(II) azide, which can be used as a detonator for explosives. Lead(II) azide has the formula of $ \\mathrm{P b} \\left( \\mathrm{N}_{3} \\right)_{2} $ Lead(II) azide can be made by the following reaction: $\\mathrm {P b} \\left(\\mathrm {N O} _ {3}\\right) _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \\mathrm {N a N} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {P b} \\left(\\mathrm {N} _ {3}\\right) _ {2} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {N a N O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q})$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6398c196-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What name is given to this type of reaction?", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6398c196-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What method would you use to remove the lead(II) azide from the final reaction mixture?", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-6398c196-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064334432.png", "width": 1135, "format": "png", "height": 672, "sha256": "6a39ada2ece0e4bc8dfc460371369524e5726ff7e7259e6b1e364b35bf542e8f", "assetId": "img:6a39ada2ece0e4bc8dfc460371369524e5726ff7e7259e6b1e364b35bf542e8f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000117", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064334432.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-6398c196", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The following verse is about water $ \\mathrm{(H_{2}O)} $ and dilute sulfuric acid $ \\mathrm{(H_{2}SO_{4})}. $ Johnny was a chemist''s son But Johnny is no more What Johnny thought was $ \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} $ $\\mathrm {W a s H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4}$", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Johnny looked at a beaker containing sulfuric acid and thought that it was water. He then drank the liquid. Suggest why it is possible to mistake sulfuric acid for water.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-6398c196-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Anhydrous copper(II) sulfate changes from white to blue when added to dilute sulfuric acid. Suggest why.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-6398c196-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Sulfuric acid is manufactured by the contact process. One stage of this process involves the reaction of sulfur dioxide with oxygen. $2 \\mathrm {S O} _ {2} + \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm {S O} _ {3}$ State the conditions used in this stage of the process. Pressure (in atmospheres) Temperature (in $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $) Catalyst", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-6398c196-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "$ 1 0. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of a concentrated solution of sulfuric acid was carefully diluted with water. More water was then added until the final volume of the solution was $ 1. 0 0 \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ $ (1 0 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3}). $ In an experiment, a student found that $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of the diluted sulfuric acid reacted with $ 3 0. 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution. The concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution was 0.200 mol/dm $ ^{3}. $ The equation for the reaction is: $2 \\mathrm {N a O H} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-6398c196-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the amount, in moles, of sodium hydroxide in $ 3 0. 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of a solution of concentration 0.200 mol/dm $ ^{3} $ . Amount of sodium hydroxide = mol", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-6398c196-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Using your answer to (d)(i), calculate the amount, in moles, of sulfuric acid in $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of the diluted acid. Amount of sulfuric acid in 25.0 $ \\mathrm{c m^{3}} $ = ... mol", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-6398c196-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Using your answer to (d)(ii), calculate the concentration, in mol/dm $ ^{3} $ , of the diluted sulfuric acid. Concentration of the diluted sulfuric acid = ... mol/dm $ ^{3} $", "label": "d-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-6398c196-pd-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Using your answer to (d) (iii), calculate the concentration, in mol/dm $ ^{3} $ , of the original, concentrated sulfuric acid. Concentration of the original, concentrated acid = ... mol/dm $ ^{3} $", "label": "d-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-6398c196-pd-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q08-6398c196", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 60, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 4, "2": 5, "3": 6, "5": 8, "7": 9, "8": 11}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": null, "session": "June 2011", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "09f487b0-e064-5bce-aed9-e576a44fb6da"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-09f487b0-q1", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "• potassium moves around (box 4)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "• potassium melts (box 5)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "• a lilac flame is seen (box 7)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "[If more than four boxes are ticked, deduct a mark for each incorrect answer above four]"}], "answerText": "• Fizzing occurs (box 2)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-09f487b0-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "haematite"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ammonia"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "sodium hydroxide"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(e)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ammonia"}], "answerText": "iron", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-09f487b0-q3ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Mg"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(a)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "It would react with the sulfuric acid/the clouds/the atmosphere/it will fizz"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "It is too reactive/very reactive/the most reactiveDissolve in the(sulfuric) acid/eq"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "“reactive” by itself"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "it has low melting point/would melt/temperature on Venus is higher than the melting point of lead(ora)IGNORE heavy/dense"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Lead would be a liquidAnswer using data from table e.g.328℃ is lower than temp on Venus"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "TitaniumAny two from: it has a low density/is lightweightit has a high melting point/wouldn’t melt/temperature on Venus is lower than the melting point of titanium(ora)does not react with sulfuric acid/the clouds/the atmosphere/it will not fizz"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "TiThe probe would be lightRemains solidReason marks can be scored for copper(density mark would need to be compared to lead)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Light on its own/light in weight on its own"}], "answerText": "Magnesium", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-09f487b0-q4ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Can undergo addition reactions / does not contain the maximum number of hydrogen (atoms)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(add) bromine (water) / Br2"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to any other solvent"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "decolourised / turns (from orange/brown to) colourless"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE starting colour"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE clear"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE discolour"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2ndmark dependent on 1stmark having been awarded, but for near miss on reagent, e.g. bromine in presence of uv, observation mark can be awarded"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "Ignore references to any products, correctly named or otherwise"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "KMnO4 / potassium (per) manganate (VII) with either an acid or an alkali"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(purple to) colourless (if acid used)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(purple to) green (if alkali used)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1"}], "answerText": "Contains a (carbon to carbon) double bond / contains C=C / multiple bond\nIGNORE references to ‘free’ bond / spare bond", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-09f487b0-q5a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "MgCl2/ CaCl2/ FeCl2/ ferrous chloride/ iron chloride"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Iron(III) chloride/ ferric chloride"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "To check the reliability/ repeatability(of the results/method)"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to obtaining a mean/average"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to identifying anomalous results"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "To get concordant/ consistent/precise results"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "To increase the accuracy(of the results)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "To increase the validity/to make it a fair test"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "To increase the accuracy of the method"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "4.30 circled"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Repeat the experiment"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE refs to validity/fair test"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Discard/ignore this result"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Work out average only using other 2 results"}], "answerText": "Any two from magnesium chloride, calcium chloride, iron(II) chloride\nIf more than 2 given, deduct 1 mark for each incorrect answer\nIf name and formula given, both must be correct", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(d)", "marks": 1, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-09f487b0-q5d", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Minor spelling mistakes, e.g. burrete / burete"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Biuret"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(e)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "$\\frac{(1.60+1.70+1.65)}{3}$"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "=1.65"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(2ndmark consequential on 1st and can be awarded if small slip in data)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct answer on its own scores 2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Award 1 mark for a correct average of the data for any other row except iron(II) chloride or water"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Award 1 mark for use of 1.90 and 1.95 to give 1.93"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any answer not to 2 d.p.(loses 2ndmark only)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1"}], "answerText": "Burette", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-09f487b0-q6a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Macromolecular"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Max 2 for mentioning of ionic or metallic bonding orIntermolecular forcesBonds loosened"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Layers slide / slip / move over each otherIGNORE particles in layers such as atoms, butREJECT if ions / molecules / electrons for first mark only"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Sheets / planes slide"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Rows slide"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Weak (intermolecular forces of) attraction between layers / weak van der Waals (forces of attraction) between layers"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any indication that the forces are those of attraction, e.g. forces overcome / forces are broken / forces hold the layers together"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any reference to bonds between layers / molecules"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to bonds within the layers"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Delocalised electrons(which) move / mobile / flowIGNORE references to “carrying” charge / current"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Refs to atoms / ions / molecules scores 0/2"}], "answerText": "Giant (structure / lattice / atomic / molecular)\nCovalent\nIdea that (covalent) bonds are broken(Covalent bonds) are strong / many bonds (are broken) / lots of {energy/heat} required\nNB No penalty for referring to graphite", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(d)", "marks": 2, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-09f487b0-q6d", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Smaller molecules/simpler structure than diamond"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any indication that the forces are those of attraction,e.g.forces overcome/forces broken/forces hold the molecules together"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "First and third marking points can be awarded for correct comparisons between the two structures,e.g.buckminsterfullerene is simple molecular whereas diamond is giant covalent scores the first mark;weak intermolecular forces of attraction in buckminsterfullerene are broken as opposed to the covalent bonds in diamond(scores the 3rd mark,as well as the 2nd)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "MAX1 for any mention of covalent bonds are broken in Buckminster fullerene"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any reference to bonds between molecules"}], "answerText": "Any two from:\nNot a giant structure\nIGNORE simple molecular\nWeak intermolecular (forces of ) attraction/weak (forces of) attraction between molecules/weak van der Waals (forces of attraction) between molecules\nNo covalent bonds break (when melting)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "8(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 8, "entryId": "ms-09f487b0-q8a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "it looks the same"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(Sulfuric acid / it) contains water"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Aqueous"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(Copper sulfate) becomes hydrated"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Contains hydrogen and oxygen / the elements of water"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Pressure: >1 but ≤5(atm)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Temperature:350 to 550(℃)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Catalyst:vanadium(V)oxide"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any range within this range, including1-2atm"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any range within this range"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Vanadium pentoxide/V2O5/vanadium oxide/vanadium(5)oxide"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Values in alternative units"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Values in alternative units"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "other oxidation states"}], "answerText": "It (like water) is a colourless (liquid)\nIgnore it is clear / transparent\nIgnore references to smell", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "8(d)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 8, "entryId": "ms-09f487b0-q8di", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Correct answer with no working scores2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "=0.006(00)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "6 for 1 mark only(i.e. not dividing by 1000)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "0.003(00)OR(d)(i)÷2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "0.003(00)x40OR(d)(ii)x40=0.12(0)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct answer with no working scores2Award 1 mark for dividing d(ii) by 25 and correctly evaluating"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "0.12(0)x100=12(.0)OR(d)(iii)x100"}], "answerText": "30(.00)x0.2(00)1000", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [{"ecf": false, "text": "30(.00)x0.2", "marks": 0, "reject": false, "ordinal": 1, "rawText": "30(.00)x0.2", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "1000", "marks": null, "reject": false, "ordinal": 2, "rawText": "1000", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}], "icTable": null, "warnings": ["1: bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1: bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1: bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "2(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "2(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "2(a): bare integer cell 