qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH0/2C", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "c4075b63-51fb-5be3-8ee0-4f8b67fd76e8"}, "warnings": ["unclassified heading: Amount of carbonate reacted = mol", "unclassified heading: Relative formula mass = ."], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The melting points of three related compounds are $\\begin{array}{l l} \\mathrm {c a p r i c a c i d} & 3 2 ^ {\\circ} \\mathrm {C} \\\\ \\mathrm {f o r m i c a c i d} & 8 ^ {\\circ} \\mathrm {C} \\\\ \\mathrm {p a l m i t i c a c i d} & 6 3 ^ {\\circ} \\mathrm {C} \\end{array}$ The boiling point of all these compounds is above 100 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $", "marks": 4, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Use the grid to draw a bar chart of the melting points.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-c4075b63-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063921902.png", "width": 1311, "format": "png", "height": 1082, "sha256": "ea7006e12e9c511abae983ac437ca886e15cbb6703ad7003faa6282206e79c18", "assetId": "img:ea7006e12e9c511abae983ac437ca886e15cbb6703ad7003faa6282206e79c18", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000024", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063921902.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Room temperature has been marked on the grid. Use your bar chart to give the physical state of each acid at room temperature.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-c4075b63-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063921902.png", "width": 1311, "format": "png", "height": 1082, "sha256": "ea7006e12e9c511abae983ac437ca886e15cbb6703ad7003faa6282206e79c18", "assetId": "img:ea7006e12e9c511abae983ac437ca886e15cbb6703ad7003faa6282206e79c18", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000024", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063921902.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-c4075b63", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student produces this chromatogram for four dyes, A, B, C and D.", "marks": 4, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Put a cross ( $ \\times $ ) in a box to indicate your answer.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-c4075b63-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which one of the dyes contains three colours? A B C D", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-c4075b63-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which one of the dyes contains one colour only? A B C D", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-c4075b63-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Each dye is made from one or more of the colours blue, red and yellow. The student thinks that the result for one dye is incorrect. Suggest which result is incorrect. Explain your answer. The incorrect result is because ...", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-c4075b63-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063921911.png", "width": 627, "format": "png", "height": 684, "sha256": "d514056e0470bbeccdf96b6fbd95d3fd7199992a834d446b71012f52ed8edd25", "assetId": "img:d514056e0470bbeccdf96b6fbd95d3fd7199992a834d446b71012f52ed8edd25", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000029", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063921911.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-c4075b63", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The photograph shows an aeroplane that has a rocket motor.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "One of the tanks on the aeroplane contains liquid oxygen.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-c4075b63-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the diagram to show the arrangement of the particles in a liquid. One particle has been drawn for you.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-c4075b63-pa-i", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_2_1789063921933.png", "width": 494, "format": "png", "height": 312, "sha256": "757486cb5d8ec6c61dba7972e51aae82d7a421bfb2c558d8cb3e9869fd56ee0a", "assetId": "img:757486cb5d8ec6c61dba7972e51aae82d7a421bfb2c558d8cb3e9869fd56ee0a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000050", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789063921933.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Much more oxygen can be stored in the tank when the oxygen is a liquid rather than a gas. Give a reason for this in terms of the arrangement of the particles.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-c4075b63-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Magnesium burns in oxygen to form magnesium oxide.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-c4075b63-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063921942.png", "width": 730, "format": "png", "height": 642, "sha256": "685197f625f86aca7d0cff042359d65dee25e2de6e26f4924d1759cbb63d3482", "assetId": "img:685197f625f86aca7d0cff042359d65dee25e2de6e26f4924d1759cbb63d3482", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000054", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063921942.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State two observations that can be made when magnesium burns in oxygen.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-c4075b63-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the formula of magnesium oxide.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-c4075b63-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A small amount of magnesium oxide is dissolved in water. When universal indicator is added to this solution, the indicator turns blue.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-c4075b63-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What does the observation with the indicator show about magnesium oxide?", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-c4075b63-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the ion that is responsible for the universal indicator turning blue.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-c4075b63-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063921928.png", "width": 821, "format": "png", "height": 546, "sha256": "86d795cb27cc747ba4d709c8b1d3be9e6c99fc28861171e594a5a99e40fd8c65", "assetId": "img:86d795cb27cc747ba4d709c8b1d3be9e6c99fc28861171e594a5a99e40fd8c65", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000047", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063921928.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789063921933.png", "width": 494, "format": "png", "height": 312, "sha256": "757486cb5d8ec6c61dba7972e51aae82d7a421bfb2c558d8cb3e9869fd56ee0a", "assetId": "img:757486cb5d8ec6c61dba7972e51aae82d7a421bfb2c558d8cb3e9869fd56ee0a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000050", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789063921933.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063921942.png", "width": 730, "format": "png", "height": 642, "sha256": "685197f625f86aca7d0cff042359d65dee25e2de6e26f4924d1759cbb63d3482", "assetId": "img:685197f625f86aca7d0cff042359d65dee25e2de6e26f4924d1759cbb63d3482", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000054", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063921942.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q03-c4075b63", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "An excess of dilute hydrochloric acid was added to a lump of calcium carbonate in a beaker. The mass of the beaker and contents was recorded every 30 seconds. The graph shows the results. Mass of beaker and contents in grams $\\mathrm {C a C O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {H C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C a C l} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {I}) + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ The equation for the reaction is", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State two observations that can be made when dilute hydrochloric acid is added to calcium carbonate.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-c4075b63-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the test for carbon dioxide gas.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-c4075b63-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe the relationship between the mass of the beaker and contents, and the time. (1)", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-c4075b63-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "After how many minutes did the reaction stop?", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-c4075b63-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why the reaction eventually stopped.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-c4075b63-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the compounds, other than water, present in the solution in the beaker (i) after two minutes", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-c4075b63-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "after five minutes", "label": "e-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-c4075b63-pe-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The experiment was repeated using the same mass of calcium carbonate, but as a powder instead of a single lump. On the graph, sketch the curve you would expect to obtain from this second experiment.", "label": "f", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-c4075b63-pf", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063921955.png", "width": 1240, "format": "png", "height": 1091, "sha256": "c3db561f2dd5372c25efb45ba9a9d81c9b860ea68b6e42d3b175ea888d1910bd", "assetId": "img:c3db561f2dd5372c25efb45ba9a9d81c9b860ea68b6e42d3b175ea888d1910bd", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000063", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063921955.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-c4075b63", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Soluble salts can be made by reacting an acid with a metal hydroxide, a metal oxide, or a metal carbonate. Insoluble salts can be made by using a precipitation reaction.", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the table to show which acid or metal compound is used to make each salt listed. For each metal compound, state whether it would be used as a solid or in aqueous solution.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-c4075b63-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "An acid is a source of hydrogen ions, $ \\mathrm{H}^{+} $ Write an equation to show the ions formed when sulfuric acid is dissolved in water.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-c4075b63-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Lead(II) chloride is an insoluble salt that can be prepared by reacting lead(II) nitrate with sodium chloride. Describe how you would prepare a pure, dry sample of lead(II) chloride starting from solid lead(II) nitrate and solid sodium chloride.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-c4075b63-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-c4075b63", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000080"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This is a recipe for making plum wine. Dissolve 1.5 kg of sugar in 5 dm $ ^{3} $ of warm water. Add yeast and 8 kg of plums. Pour the mixture into a flask. Leave the flask for several weeks until the reaction has stopped. Remove the solid yeast and pour the clear liquid into bottles.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Sugar contains sucrose, $ C_{1 2} H_{2 2} O_{1 1} $ When yeast is added, water reacts with sucrose to form glucose, $ \\mathrm{C_{6} H_{1 2} O_{6}} $ Write a chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-c4075b63-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The glucose is then converted into ethanol by the yeast $\\mathrm {C} _ {6} \\mathrm {H} _ {1 2} \\mathrm {O} _ {6} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {5} \\mathrm {O H} (\\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-c4075b63-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "How would you know when the reaction has stopped?", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-c4075b63-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "How could the solid yeast be removed from the mixture?", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-c4075b63-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Ethanol can be converted into chloroethene, $ \\mathrm{C H_{2}=C H C l} $ , in three stages. Stage 1 Ethanol is dehydrated to form ethene, $ \\mathrm{C H_{2}=C H_{2}} $ Stage 2 Ethene is converted into 1,2-dichloroethane, $ \\mathrm{C H_{2} C I C H_{2} C I} $ Stage 3 1,2-dichloroethane is converted into chloroethene and hydrogen chloride", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-c4075b63-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Why is the reaction in Stage 1 described as dehydration?", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-c4075b63-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the catalyst used in the reaction in Stage 1.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-c4075b63-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest the name or formula of the substance used to react with ethene in Stage 2.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-c4075b63-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for the reaction in Stage 3.", "label": "c-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-c4075b63-pc-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Chloroethene can be used to make the polymer poly(chloroethene), also known as PVC. The displayed formula for part of the PVC molecule is", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-c4075b63-pd", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063922077.png", "width": 431, "format": "png", "height": 227, "sha256": "15fcded920b53937f7b48252a5feee10610a9bdd90ea8e3f77ee1148c8f07899", "assetId": "img:15fcded920b53937f7b48252a5feee10610a9bdd90ea8e3f77ee1148c8f07899", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000110", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063922077.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a displayed formula for a chloroethene molecule.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-c4075b63-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe, in terms of structure and bonding, what happens when chloroethene molecules are converted into poly(chloroethene).", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-c4075b63-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063922005.png", "width": 1206, "format": "png", "height": 88, "sha256": "29bb8c11854ced739b375ada750a060302d5ecb49264e0324e382353dc4fb9ab", "assetId": "img:29bb8c11854ced739b375ada750a060302d5ecb49264e0324e382353dc4fb9ab", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000087", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063922005.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789063922010.png", "width": 1003, "format": "png", "height": 955, "sha256": "40591ff4da63d8c50f14e83d0e2494fff0efb521068110d69c2cab90a7041169", "assetId": "img:40591ff4da63d8c50f14e83d0e2494fff0efb521068110d69c2cab90a7041169", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000093", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789063922010.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063922077.png", "width": 431, "format": "png", "height": 227, "sha256": "15fcded920b53937f7b48252a5feee10610a9bdd90ea8e3f77ee1148c8f07899", "assetId": "img:15fcded920b53937f7b48252a5feee10610a9bdd90ea8e3f77ee1148c8f07899", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000110", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063922077.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-c4075b63", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student set up this apparatus to measure the volume of carbon dioxide given off when a sample of a carbonate of a Group 2 metal was reacted with dilute nitric acid. She weighed out some of the carbonate and put it in a conical flask. She then added an excess of dilute nitric acid. After adding the acid she placed the bung and delivery tube into the conical flask. She measured the total volume of gas collected at room temperature and pressure (rtp) in the measuring cylinder. Her results are shown in the table. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {X C O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {H N O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {X} \\left(\\mathrm {N O} _ {3}\\right) _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {l}) + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ where X is the symbol for the Group 2 metal.", "marks": 10, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the amount, in moles, of carbon dioxide gas collected. (Assume that one mole of gas has a volume of 24000 $ cm^{3} $ at rtp) Amount of carbon dioxide gas collected = mol", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-c4075b63-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Deduce the amount, in moles, of the carbonate that reacted.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-c4075b63-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Using the mass of the carbonate and your answer to (a)(ii), calculate the relative formula mass $ ( M_{r} ) $ of this carbonate. Give your answer to the nearest whole number.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-c4075b63-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate a value for the relative atomic mass of the Group 2 metal, X, and use the Periodic Table on page 2 to suggest its identity. Relative atomic mass of X = ... Identity of X=.", "label": "a-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-c4075b63-pa-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "After the student had completed the experiment she was told that the metal carbonate was calcium carbonate. She calculated that 0.888 g of calcium carbonate would produce $ 2 1 3 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of carbon dioxide. She was certain that she had measured the mass of the metal carbonate correctly. Suggest two reasons why the volume of gas she collected was less than $ 2 1 3 \\mathrm{c m}^{3}. $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-c4075b63-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063922099.png", "width": 1402, "format": "png", "height": 601, "sha256": "c7e52e91831687aa86e2709db31f9e43fffcee5ae49a699c1a88cae2a4cfa8b5", "assetId": "img:c7e52e91831687aa86e2709db31f9e43fffcee5ae49a699c1a88cae2a4cfa8b5", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000116", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063922099.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-c4075b63", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000121"]}], "paperTotal": 60, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 4, "2": 4, "4": 11, "5": 12, "6": 11, "7": 10}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": null, "session": "January 2013", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "UG034327", "canonicalDocumentId": "1561cbc6-a276-559d-b623-d41898df8475"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-1561cbc6-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "2 marks for all 3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 mark for any 2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "horizontal lines at correct heights"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "vertical lines ending at correct heights"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1- capric AND palmitic solid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2- formic liquid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "S"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "I"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "any other state symbols"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "bar drawn at height of 32\nbar drawn at height of 8\nbar drawn at height of 62-64", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-1561cbc6-q4a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "gas/carbon dioxide given off"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2- lump/calcium carbonate/solid disappears/getts smaller"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "dissolves forms a colourless solution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1-(bubble through) limewater/calcium hydroxide solution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2-(goes) milky/cloudy/chalky"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "white precipitate/suspension/solid(formed)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 dependent on M1 or near miss,e.g. Ca(OH)2(s)IGNORE references to lighted spill goes out"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "time increases,mass decreases"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "reverse statement mass decreases with time(they have a) negative correlation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "mass goes down with no reference to time"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to mass eventually stops decreasing"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3min18s to3min30s"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3.3 to3.5"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "lump/calcium carbonate/solid completely reacted"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "used up/has gone"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "has dissolved(both) reactants used up"}], "answerText": "M1- bubbles(of gas)/fizzing/effervescence", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(e)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-1561cbc6-q4ei", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "hydrogen chloride for hydrochloric acid correct formulae"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "hydrogen chloride for hydrochloric acid correct formula"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(f)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 - steeper curve to left of original starting at, or close to(100,0)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 - levels at98.4g"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "curves that''dip''below98.4bymore than1/2small square"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "calcium chloride AND hydrochloric acid\nIGNORE carbon dioxide / carbonic acid / calcium carbonate\ncalcium chloride AND hydrochloric acid\nIGNORE carbon dioxide / carbonic acid", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)", "marks": 5, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-1561cbc6-q5a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Acid used"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Metal compound"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "correct formulae"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "silver ethanoate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Solid or aqueous solution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "sulfuric(acid)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "solid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "silver nitrate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "nitric(acid)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "solid/aqueous/solution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "H2SO4→H++HSO4-/H2SO4→2H++SO42- M1- formula of both ions correct M2-balanced equation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "H3O+in place ofH+"}], "answerText": "Salt made\nName", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(c)", "marks": 1, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-1561cbc6-q5c", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "dissolve one in water"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "penalise M1 is any other reagents added"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2- mix/add(the two solutions)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "react"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3- filter"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "decant"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4- wash residue/solid/lead((II))chloride(with deionised/distilled water)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M5- dry on filter paper/in a(warm)oven/leave to dry/heat"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "other sensible methods of drying"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "strong heating"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "M1- dissolve both(lead(II)nitrate and sodium chloride)in water", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-1561cbc6-q6a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "lower case symbols and numbers not given as subscripts"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "no more bubbles/fizzing/effervescenceIGNORE when no more ethanol is formed/all the glucose has reacted/all the yeast has reacted/references to mass/references to temperaturefiltration/filteringIGNORE sieving"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "no more gas/carbon dioxide given offdecant"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "evaporation/distillation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(the elements of) water removed(ii)aluminium oxide/Al2O3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "H2O removed2 hydrogen (atoms)and 1 oxygen (atom)are removed(concentrated) sulfuric acid(concentrated) phosphoric acid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "dilute acidphosphorus/phosphorous"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "chlorine (gas)/Cl2If both name and formula given, both must be correct(iv)CH2ClCH2Cl→CH2(=)CHCl+HCl"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "correct name or f