ull, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "7(a): bare integer cell \"3\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "7(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "9(a)(i): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "9(a)(i): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "January 2015", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": "Friday 16 January 2015 – Morning", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH0/2C", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "53711967-dc78-5d9b-b1db-f655fd599e2b"}, "warnings": ["unclassified heading: most reactive", "Q9: incomplete MCQ option set folded into stem"], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram represents a particle of ammonia.", "marks": 3, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "This particle of ammonia is A an atom B an ion C a lattice D a molecule", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-53711967-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which type of bonding is present in this particle of ammonia? A covalent B hydrogen C ionic D metallic", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-53711967-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the formula of ammonia?", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-53711967-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063974792.png", "width": 275, "format": "png", "height": 192, "sha256": "1a5044df06f92883967566d517d764c6de713dc4fa9a32adc2191fca27c40fdf", "assetId": "img:1a5044df06f92883967566d517d764c6de713dc4fa9a32adc2191fca27c40fdf", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000022", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063974792.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-53711967", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The solubility of a solid in water is the maximum mass of the solid that can dissolve in 100 g of water at a given temperature. An aqueous solution containing this maximum mass can be described as a saturated solution. The graph shows the solubilities of three solids at different temperatures.", "marks": 4, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "What is the relationship between solubility and temperature for solid A?", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-53711967-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which solid is the most soluble at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}? $", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-53711967-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain what you would observe if a saturated solution of solid A were cooled from $ 9 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ to $ 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-53711967-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063974804.png", "width": 1713, "format": "png", "height": 1103, "sha256": "25b288a208ba348936fb3d766ddf96cd8a43a6e0b0d49b4960adc6c2592b6d32", "assetId": "img:25b288a208ba348936fb3d766ddf96cd8a43a6e0b0d49b4960adc6c2592b6d32", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000038", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063974804.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-53711967", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram shows how iron is produced in a blast furnace.", "marks": 10, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of raw material P and of product Q.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-53711967-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equations for some reactions in a blast furnace are $\\mathrm {C} + \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C O} _ {2}$ B C + $ \\mathrm{C O_{2}} $ $ \\rightarrow $ 2CO C $ \\mathrm{F e_{2}O_{3}}+3 \\mathrm{C O} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{F e}+3 \\mathrm{C O}_{2} $ D $ \\mathrm{C a C O}_{3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C a O}+ \\mathrm{C O}_{2} $ $\\mathrm {E} \\quad \\mathrm {C a O} + \\mathrm {S i O} _ {2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C a S i O} _ {3}$ The table shows some types of reaction that occur in a blast furnace. Complete the table by writing a letter, A, B, C, D, or E, to link each type of reaction to an appropriate reaction equation. Each letter may be used once, more than once or not at all. The first one has been done for you.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-53711967-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The rusting of iron objects is a major problem. Name the two substances needed for iron to rust.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-53711967-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The order of reactivity of three metals is zinc iron tin least reactive Iron objects can be prevented from rusting by coating them with zinc or tin. Some of these objects may be scratched when used, so the coating may come off. Use the order of reactivity of the metals to suggest why coating these objects with zinc is more effective than coating them with tin.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-53711967-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063974825.png", "width": 1388, "format": "png", "height": 491, "sha256": "09937f6d3ebe7eb1d6b01fa849f32b6f4f188f7a5ff833c16f70b3a25c31a855", "assetId": "img:09937f6d3ebe7eb1d6b01fa849f32b6f4f188f7a5ff833c16f70b3a25c31a855", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000044", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063974825.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-53711967", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000056"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) Wine can be made from grapes. The grapes are crushed to produce an aqueous solution containing glucose. Yeast is then added to this solution. The solution is kept at a constant temperature for a period of time. The glucose is converted into ethanol. (i) Name the process in which glucose is converted into ethanol. (ii) What is the purpose of the yeast?", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Grape vines can be attacked by a fungus that ruins the grapes. The fungus can be killed using Bordeaux mixture, a solid containing copper(II) sulfate and calcium hydroxide.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-53711967-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State a test to show that Bordeaux mixture contains calcium ions. test for calcium ions observation ...", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-53711967-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A sample of Bordeaux mixture is dissolved in water. Describe separate tests to show that this solution contains copper(II) ions and sulfate ions. test for copper(II) ions test for sulfate ions. observation", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-53711967-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Ethanol can be manufactured by passing a hot mixture of ethene and steam, at a high pressure, over a catalyst. State the pressure used and name the catalyst. pressure atm catalyst", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-53711967-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the conversion of ethanol into ethene can be written using displayed formulae. The table gives some average bond energies. Use information from the table to calculate the enthalpy change, in kJ/mol, for the conversion of ethanol into ethene. kJ/mol", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-53711967-pd", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063974851.png", "width": 908, "format": "png", "height": 202, "sha256": "2a1dd8133ce13fdf4ad766339451e7379ce52de88af5960902b902fd0082d52e", "assetId": "img:2a1dd8133ce13fdf4ad766339451e7379ce52de88af5960902b902fd0082d52e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000083", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063974851.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063974851.png", "width": 908, "format": "png", "height": 202, "sha256": "2a1dd8133ce13fdf4ad766339451e7379ce52de88af5960902b902fd0082d52e", "assetId": "img:2a1dd8133ce13fdf4ad766339451e7379ce52de88af5960902b902fd0082d52e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000083", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063974851.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q04-53711967", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000085"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student uses this apparatus to investigate the temperature change that occurs when potassium hydroxide is dissolved in water. She uses this method. pour $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of water into the polystyrene cup and measure the temperature of the water add 3g of potassium hydroxide and stir record the highest temperature of the solution", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "These diagrams show the thermometer readings before and after the student added the potassium hydroxide. Use the readings to complete the table.", "label": "a", "marks": 3, "partId": "q05-53711967-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_2_1789063974865.png", "width": 195, "format": "png", "height": 532, "sha256": "0b54e3a402f62934e5af921cd24e0a29f24e195e17626a125cab2a8323471d16", "assetId": "img:0b54e3a402f62934e5af921cd24e0a29f24e195e17626a125cab2a8323471d16", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000095", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789063974865.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789063974871.png", "width": 196, "format": "png", "height": 541, "sha256": "59f506231bb1005a3f223eb5dba988fd69985fa03e87045933618a9950249db5", "assetId": "img:59f506231bb1005a3f223eb5dba988fd69985fa03e87045933618a9950249db5", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000096", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789063974871.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student uses her results to calculate the enthalpy change for dissolving potassium hydroxide in water. She compares her value with a data book value. $\\text{Student''s value} = - 3 2 \\mathrm{k J / m o l}.$ $\\mathrm {D a t a b o o k v a l u e} = - 5 5 \\mathrm {k J / m o l}.$ There are no errors in the student''s method or in the calculation. Suggest two reasons why the student''s value differs from the data book value.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-53711967-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789063974860.png", "width": 692, "format": "png", "height": 463, "sha256": "da32e481c12ad6aa69be8f603094a07f95d0ae58f23dd86450317229d89e82b9", "assetId": "img:da32e481c12ad6aa69be8f603094a07f95d0ae58f23dd86450317229d89e82b9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000089", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789063974860.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789063974865.png", "width": 195, "format": "png", "height": 532, "sha256": "0b54e3a402f62934e5af921cd24e0a29f24e195e17626a125cab2a8323471d16", "assetId": "img:0b54e3a402f62934e5af921cd24e0a29f24e195e17626a125cab2a8323471d16", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000095", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789063974865.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789063974871.png", "width": 196, "format": "png", "height": 541, "sha256": "59f506231bb1005a3f223eb5dba988fd69985fa03e87045933618a9950249db5", "assetId": "img:59f506231bb1005a3f223eb5dba988fd69985fa03e87045933618a9950249db5", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000096", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789063974871.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-53711967", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000099"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Potassium sulfide is an ionic compound.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the table to show the arrangement of electrons in the ions formed when potassium and sulfur react to form potassium sulfide. Give the charge on each of the ions.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-53711967-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why potassium sulfide conducts electricity when molten.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-53711967-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why potassium sulfide has a high melting point.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-53711967-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-53711967", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000110"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Sulfuric acid can be manufactured from sulfur in a four-stage process. stage 1 sulfur is burned in air to form sulfur dioxide $S + O _ {2} \\rightarrow S O _ {2}$ stage 2 the sulfur dioxide is reacted with more oxygen to form sulfur trioxide $\\mathrm {S O} _ {2} + \\frac {1}{2} \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {S O} _ {3}$ stage 3 the sulfur trioxide is absorbed in concentrated sulfuric acid to make oleum $\\mathrm {S O} _ {3} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {7}$ stage 4 the oleum is carefully diluted with water to form sulfuric acid", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for the formation of sulfuric acid from oleum.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-53711967-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A mass of 80 tonnes of sulfur is reacted with oxygen in stage 1. Calculate the maximum mass, in tonnes, of sulfur trioxide that can be produced in stage 2. [1 tonne = 1.0 $ \\times10^{6} $ g]", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-53711967-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the minimum volume at rtp, in cubic decimetres $ \\mathrm{(d m^{3})} $ of oxygen required to completely react with 64 tonnes of sulfur dioxide.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-53711967-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q07-53711967", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000128"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student is supplied with aqueous solutions of these substances. bromine chlorine iodine potassium bromide potassium chloride potassium iodide Describe two experiments the student could perform, using some of the solutions, to show the order of reactivity of bromine, chlorine and iodine. Your answer should include the observations that the student would expect to make, and a chemical equation for one of the reactions.", "marks": 5, "parts": [], "number": 8, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q08-53711967", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Nitrogen dioxide $ \\left( \\mathrm{N O}_{2} \\right) $ is a brown gas. Dinitrogen tetraoxide $ \\left(\\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right) $ is a colourless gas. The two gases can exist together in dynamic equilibrium according to the equation $2 \\mathrm {N O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {N} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {g}) \\quad \\Delta H = - 5 8 \\mathrm {k J / m o l}$ A mixture of nitrogen dioxide gas and dinitrogen tetraoxide gas is allowed to reach equilibrium in a sealed container at $ 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ . This equilibrium mixture is brown in colour.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The sealed container is immersed in hot water at $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ As the temperature of the gas mixture increases,the pressure of the gas mixture also increases.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-53711967-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Predict the effect of the increase in temperature on the position of equilibrium.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-53711967-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Predict the effect of the increase in pressure on the position of equilibrium.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-53711967-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why it is difficult to predict which way the equilibrium will shift.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-53711967-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why the equilibrium mixture is a darker shade of brown at $ 6 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ than the equilibrium mixture at $ 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-53711967-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 9, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q09-53711967", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 60, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 3, "2": 4, "3": 10, "5": 5, "6": 7, "8": 5, "9": 5}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "January 2015", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "UG040458", "canonicalDocumentId": "ed36b1ae-5eef-5a3f-8063-a643f291d15b"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-ed36b1ae-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A(covalent)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "NH3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "H3N"}], "answerText": "D(a molecule)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-ed36b1ae-q3a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "slag/CaSiO3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Type of reaction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Letter"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "one that gives out heat"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "one that is a thermal decomposition"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "one that is a neutralisation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "E"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "one that forms a poisonous gas"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1-oxygenIGNOREO M2-water"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "airO2moisture/H2O"}], "answerText": "M1P-iron ore/haematiteignore iron(III) oxide/Fe2O3M2Q-calcium silicate", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(c)", "marks": 2, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-ed36b1ae-q4c", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "any pressure or range within this rangephosphoric(V) acid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "any other oxidation state"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(Σ bonds broken)348+412+360(=1120)M2(Σ bonds made)612+463(=1075)M3M1-M2/Σ bonds broken-Σ bonds madeM4(+)45(kJ/mol)Correct answer with no working scores4-45(kJ/mol) scores3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "32313186"}], "answerText": "M1(pressure)-60-70 atmM2(catalyst)-phosphoric acid/H3PO4ignore references to concentration", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)", "marks": 3, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-ed36b1ae-q5a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "27.1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "one trailing zero"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "more than one trailing zero"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "18.8"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(+)8.3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1heat(energy)/thermal energy lost(to the atmosphere)ignore just energy lostM2potassium hydroxide dissolves(very/too)slowly"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "water evaporates potassium hydroxide does not completely dissolve potassium hydroxide is impure less than3g of potassium hydroxide is used more than50cm3of water is used"}], "answerText": "M1温度after\nM2温度before\nM3温度change\nRecorded temperatures correct but in wrong order scores1 forM1和M2M3csq onM1和M2", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)", "marks": 3, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-ed36b1ae-q6a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Arrangement of electrons in atom"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Arrangement of electrons in ion"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Charge on ion"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "K(1)+/K+1S2-/S-2positive for potassium and negative for sulfide for 1 mark"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2.8.8"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(1)+/+1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2.8.8"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2-/-2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ions move/travel (to the electrodes)M1(electrostatic) forces (of attraction) between (oppositely charged) ionsM2 are (relatively) strongM3 large amount of energy required to overcome the forces / separate the ions from the latticeM2 dep on mention of forces (of attraction) or bondsMention of covalent bonds or intermolecular forces no M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ions are free to move / ions are mobileionic bonding / ionic bondsbreak the bonds"}], "answerText": "Element\nM1- both arrangements correctM2- charge on potassium ionM3- charge on sulfide ion", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-ed36b1ae-q7a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "multiples and fractions"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M132(of S)→80(of SO3)(tonnes or g)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2mass of SO3=80"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3=200(tonnes)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2csq on M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3csq on M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct answer with no working scores 3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1n(S)=(n(SO3))=80x106/32(mol)(=2500000(000(g))"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2mass of SO3=M1x80(=2000000(000(g))"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3=M2÷106/200(tonnes)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M164(g)(of SO2) reacts with 12(dm3)(ofO2)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(64 tonnes) reacts 12x106(dm3)OR1.2x107(dm3)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2csq on M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct answer with no working scores 2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1n(SO2)=64x106/64(mol)(=106mol)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2M1/2x24/1.2x107(dm3)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1mass of oxygen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "accept1.2x1010cm3"}], "answerText": "H2S2O7+H2O→2H2SO4", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "8", "marks": 5, "number": 8, "entryId": "ms-ed36b1ae-q8", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "yellow / brown"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "red"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2-(solution) turns orange"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "red-brown / orange"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "yellow"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3- add (aqueous) bromine to (aqueous) KI"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "correct ionic equations"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4-(solution) turns brown"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "accept Cl2+2KI→I2+2KCl if chlorine is added to potassium iodide"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M5- Cl2+2KBr→Br2+2KCl OR Br2+2KI→I2+2KBr"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "Ignore state symbols"}], "answerText": "M1- add (aqueous) chlorine to (aqueous) KBr", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "8", "marks": 5, "number": 8, "entryId": "ms-ed36b1ae-q8", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M2- no change"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "orange/ yellow/brown solution/colour produced only if it is clear that no reaction has occurred"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "red"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3- add (aqueous) iodine to (aqueous) KBr"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "yellow"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4- no change/ no change"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "brown/ red-brown/orange solution/colour produced only if it is clear that no reaction has occurred"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If this route is chosen then M5 cannot be scored"}], "answerText": "M1- add (aqueous) bromine to (aqueous) KCl", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "9(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 9, "entryId": "ms-ed36b1ae-q9ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "moves in the endothermic direction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "shifts to the side of the reactants"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "shifts to the right"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "impossible to know which shift is greater / impossible to know which change has the greater effect"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "moves in the exothermic direction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "shifts to the side of the products"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "shifts to the side with fewer (gas) moles/molecules"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "the (two) effects are opposing one another"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1- greater proportion of NO2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2-(increase of) temperature has a greater effect than"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(increase of) pressure"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "more NO2 present"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "equilibrium shifts to left"}], "answerText": "shifts to left", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}], "icTable": null, "warnings": ["1(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "orphan row before first entry: 2(a)(i)(ii)", "orphan row before first entry: (b)", "3(a): bare integer cell \"3\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "3(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "orphan row before first entry: (d)", "orphan row before first entry: 4(a)(i)(ii)", "orphan row before first entry: (b)(i)(ii)", "4(c): bare integer cell \"4\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "5(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "6(a): bare integer cell \"13\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "7(a): bare integer cell \"3\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "7(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "9(a)(i): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "9(a)(i): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)"], "paperTotal": null, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "questionTotals": {}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-ms-v1"}', '{"findings": [{"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 3, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "1"}, {"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 4, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "2"}, {"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 10, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "3"}, {"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 5, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "5"}, {"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 7, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "6"}, {"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 5, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "8"}, {"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 5, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "9"}], "msPaperTotal": null, "qpPaperTotal": 60, "mismatchCount": 0, "paperTotalConflict": false}', NULL, '2026-09-13 12:59:09.217717+00');
