: "ed2cb4143f6779144a2c2277aaad76c13c444dd4c43aeecaf3b86d3dc2a86479", "assetId": "img:ed2cb4143f6779144a2c2277aaad76c13c444dd4c43aeecaf3b86d3dc2a86479", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000052", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104588984.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The graph shows the temperature at which the maximum mass dissolved is the same for each solute. Determine this temperature.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-b5287cd4-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use your graph to calculate the solubility, in g per 100 g of water, of sodium nitrate at $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ [1.0 $ cm^{3} $ of water has a mass of 1.0 g]", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-b5287cd4-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["solubility of sodium nitrate ing per 100g of water"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "$ 2 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of a saturated solution of potassium nitrate is cooled from $ 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ to $ 2 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $ Use your graph to determine the mass, in grams, of potassium nitrate that crystallises. Show your working on the graph.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-b5287cd4-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789104588984.png", "width": 1491, "format": "png", "height": 1654, "sha256": "ed2cb4143f6779144a2c2277aaad76c13c444dd4c43aeecaf3b86d3dc2a86479", "assetId": "img:ed2cb4143f6779144a2c2277aaad76c13c444dd4c43aeecaf3b86d3dc2a86479", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000052", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104588984.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-b5287cd4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000049"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Methanol, $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} O H} $ is the first member of the homologous series of alcohols.", "marks": 10, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Give two characteristics of a homologous series.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b5287cd4-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Methanol is heated with potassium dichromate(VI) and one other reagent. The methanol is oxidised to methanoic acid, HCOOH", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b5287cd4-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the formula of the other reagent.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b5287cd4-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the colour change that occurs during the reaction. (2) from ... to ...", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b5287cd4-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw the displayed formula for methanol and for methanoic acid in the boxes.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": 2, "partId": "q05-b5287cd4-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Methanol reacts with methanoic acid to form an ester. Complete the equation for this reaction. $\\mathrm {C H} _ {3} \\mathrm {O H} + \\mathrm {H C O O H} \\rightarrow$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b5287cd4-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The structural formula of an ester is $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C O O C H_{2} C H_{2} C H_{2} C H_{3}} $ A butyl ethanoate B butyl methanoate", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b5287cd4-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the name of this ester? C ethyl butanoate D methyl butanoate", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b5287cd4-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Deduce the molecular formula of this ester.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b5287cd4-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-b5287cd4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000070"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A scientist reacts lead(II) nitrate solution with sodium bromide solution. This is the equation for the reaction. $\\mathrm {P b} \\left(\\mathrm {N O} _ {3}\\right) _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \\mathrm {N a B r} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {P b B r} _ {2} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {N a N O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q})$", "marks": 14, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Describe how the scientist could obtain a pure dry sample of lead(II) bromide $ \\mathrm{(P b B r_{2})} $ from the reaction mixture.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b5287cd4-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The scientist reacts an excess of lead(II) nitrate solution with $ 2 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium bromide solution of concentration $ 2. 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3}. $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b5287cd4-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Show that the amount of sodium bromide used is 0.050 mol.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b5287cd4-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Show that the maximum theoretical mass of lead(II) bromide is approximately 9 g. [for $ \\mathrm{P b B r}_{2} $ $ M_{\\mathrm{r}}=3 6 7 $]", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b5287cd4-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The scientist electrolyses molten lead(II) bromide using this apparatus.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b5287cd4-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789104589027.png", "width": 1149, "format": "png", "height": 579, "sha256": "bbbf4f0ae919aaa33bf63cea5b9a4ac580c3a396efd7d03ce0e1ede9417f071a", "assetId": "img:bbbf4f0ae919aaa33bf63cea5b9a4ac580c3a396efd7d03ce0e1ede9417f071a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000091", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104589027.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why lead(II) bromide needs to be molten rather than solid for electrolysis to occur.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b5287cd4-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The electrolyte is at a temperature of $ 4 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ Explain a suitable material for the electrodes.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b5287cd4-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the half-equation that occurs at the negative electrode.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b5287cd4-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Bromine forms at the positive electrode. This is the half-equation for the reaction at the positive electrode. $2 \\mathrm {B r} ^ {-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {B r} _ {2} + 2 \\mathrm {e} ^ {-}$", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b5287cd4-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what is observed at the positive electrode.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b5287cd4-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why the half-equation represents an oxidation reaction.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b5287cd4-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789104589027.png", "width": 1149, "format": "png", "height": 579, "sha256": "bbbf4f0ae919aaa33bf63cea5b9a4ac580c3a396efd7d03ce0e1ede9417f071a", "assetId": "img:bbbf4f0ae919aaa33bf63cea5b9a4ac580c3a396efd7d03ce0e1ede9417f071a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000091", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104589027.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-b5287cd4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about hydrogen chloride (HCI). Hydrogen chloride is a covalent compound.", "marks": 13, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State, in terms of electrostatic attraction, what is meant by a covalent bond.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b5287cd4-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When hydrogen chloride gas is dissolved in an organic solvent, the hydrogen chloride remains a covalent molecule. When hydrogen chloride gas is dissolved in water, ions are formed. Explain what happens when dry blue litmus paper is dipped into separate samples of each solution.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b5287cd4-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In the presence of ultraviolet radiation, hydrogen reacts with chlorine to form hydrogen chloride. This is the equation for the reaction. $\\mathrm {H} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {C I} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {H C I} (\\mathrm {g})$ The table shows the bond energies.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b5287cd4-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the enthalpy change $ (\\Delta H) $ , in kJ/mol, for the reaction Include a sign in your answer. $\\Delta H =$", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b5287cd4-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a reaction profile for the reaction. Label the reactants, the products, $ \\Delta H $ and the activation energy $ (E_{\\mathrm{a}}) $", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b5287cd4-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-b5287cd4", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000110"]}], "paperTotal": 70, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 7, "2": 5, "3": 11, "4": 10, "5": 10, "6": 14, "7": 13}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "Summer 2024", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": "P75822A", "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_2C_2406_MS", "canonicalDocumentId": "8fe25072-6546-5de3-808d-6f21d3a9e309"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-8fe25072-q1", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "sulfur"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW S"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "beryllium"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW Be"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "boron"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW B"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iv)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2,8,4/2.8.4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT diagram showing electron configuration"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An explanation that links the following three pointsM1 the outer shell is further from the nucleus in sodium/sodium has more shells/sodium has a larger atomic radius ORAALLOW a sodium atom is larger than a lithium atomALLOW Li 2,1 Na 2,8,1M2 there is less attraction to the nucleus for the outer electron/outer shell in sodium ORAALLOW there is more shielding in sodium ORAM3 so the (outer) electron is more easily lost ORAIGNORE electrons (plural) in M1 and M2 but do not allow electrons in M3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 7"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-8fe25072-q3a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M2(F) bitumen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "aircraft fuel"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A description that refers to three of the following points"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 crude oil is heated/vaporised"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE evaporated"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(the vapour) passes into/rises up the fractionating column/ chamber OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 the kerosene/the fraction is tapped off/removed at its boiling point range/condenses and removed"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW kerosene/the fraction is removed at the 3rd or 4th level"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 silica/alumina"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW silicon dioxide/SiO2/aluminium oxide/Al2O3/zeolites/aluminosilicates"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 any value or range between 600 and 700(℃) inclusive"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An explanation that links three of the following points"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 there is a surplus supply of/less demand for larger fractions/molecules/hydrocarbons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ORthere is not enough supply/greater demand for smaller fractions/molecules/hydrocarbons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 alkenes are produced which are needed to make polymers"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW plastics/to make ethanol"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 smaller fractions/alkanes/molecules/hydrocarbons are needed f