5 June 2016 – Afternoon", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH0/2C", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "9ef9b0c8-3a05-5e34-977c-7af899b976bc"}, "warnings": ["unclassified heading: (c) Aluminium is extracted from aluminium oxide using electrolysis.", "unclassified heading: mass of carbon dioxide =", "unclassified heading: TOTAL FOR PAPER = 60 MARKS"], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram shows the apparatus a student uses to separate a mixture of salt and sand. She adds the mixture to water in a beaker and then carries out the three stages shown. stage 1 stage 2 stage 3", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Give the names of the pieces of apparatus labelled X and Y.", "label": "a", "marks": 2, "partId": "q01-9ef9b0c8-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A liquid that dissolves substances is a A solute B solution C solvent D suspension", "label": "b", "marks": 1, "partId": "q01-9ef9b0c8-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The clear liquid that forms in stage 1 is a A solute B solution C solvent D suspension", "label": "b-ii", "marks": 1, "partId": "q01-9ef9b0c8-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "At which stage,1,2 or 3,is the sand collected?", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-9ef9b0c8-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "At which stage, 1, 2 or 3, is the salt collected?", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-9ef9b0c8-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What happens to the water in stage 3?", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-9ef9b0c8-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069471796.png", "width": 359, "format": "png", "height": 456, "sha256": "cb2a59920feda853672871593d3581becab8617b45aa0ea836fc2bcb835e64ee", "assetId": "img:cb2a59920feda853672871593d3581becab8617b45aa0ea836fc2bcb835e64ee", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000023", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069471796.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069471801.png", "width": 313, "format": "png", "height": 278, "sha256": "3d8edbf8d60cd6ab2bea0b92a3aed812b720ac2ebb35965056623439be1ef90e", "assetId": "img:3d8edbf8d60cd6ab2bea0b92a3aed812b720ac2ebb35965056623439be1ef90e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000025", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069471801.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789069471815.png", "width": 383, "format": "png", "height": 293, "sha256": "3251e19d18923237107b83dd45318d2dcdfbd9b9f5d46f60e8500ff5491ecdda", "assetId": "img:3251e19d18923237107b83dd45318d2dcdfbd9b9f5d46f60e8500ff5491ecdda", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000027", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789069471815.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-9ef9b0c8", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The apparatus in the diagram was set up to demonstrate the rusting of iron. after one week after two weeks", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "One week after the start of the experiment the volume of gas in the measuring cylinder has decreased. After two weeks there is no further decrease in volume of gas in the measuring cylinder. Explain these observations.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-9ef9b0c8-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Iron reacts with dilute sulfuric acid. The chemical equation for this reaction is $\\mathrm {F e} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {F e S O} _ {4} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2}$ Complete the word equation for the reaction. $\\mathrm {I r o n} + \\mathrm {s u l f u r i c a c i d} \\rightarrow$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-9ef9b0c8-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Aqueous sodium hydroxide can be used to distinguish between solutions containing iron(II) ions $ \\mathrm{(F e^{2+})} $ and iron(III) ions $ \\mathrm{(F e^{3+})}. $ State the observation made when aqueous sodium hydroxide is added separately to each solution. $\\mathrm {F e} ^ {2 +} (\\mathrm {a q}).$ $\\mathrm {F e} ^ {3 +} (\\mathrm {a q}).$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-9ef9b0c8-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069471836.png", "width": 594, "format": "png", "height": 691, "sha256": "e09a34f51c483b5380d9468b58f0b8a708dd1cc9d30e02c78eeb484f21151074", "assetId": "img:e09a34f51c483b5380d9468b58f0b8a708dd1cc9d30e02c78eeb484f21151074", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000048", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069471836.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069471852.png", "width": 380, "format": "png", "height": 584, "sha256": "ae7b2a6e2c1d283d46da2b0d9c02f2622b2944461862a6a0a1ba9b515ac49049", "assetId": "img:ae7b2a6e2c1d283d46da2b0d9c02f2622b2944461862a6a0a1ba9b515ac49049", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000049", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069471852.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789069471857.png", "width": 379, "format": "png", "height": 553, "sha256": "a2c27c560f0cb1764a754ec0a92a96e8a3c57d251d053616ad82c158178dc44a", "assetId": "img:a2c27c560f0cb1764a754ec0a92a96e8a3c57d251d053616ad82c158178dc44a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000051", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789069471857.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-9ef9b0c8", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram shows the elements in Period 3 of the Periodic Table.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Identify an element in Period 3 that forms a basic oxide.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-9ef9b0c8-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify an element in Period 3 that forms an acidic oxide.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-9ef9b0c8-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Magnesium and chlorine react together to form magnesium chloride, a compound with ionic bonding. The equation for the reaction is $M g + C l _ {2} \\rightarrow M g C l _ {2}$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-9ef9b0c8-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the dot and cross diagram to show the arrangement of the outer electrons in the magnesium and chloride ions formed. Show the charge on each ion.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-9ef9b0c8-pb-i", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069471876.png", "width": 1494, "format": "png", "height": 183, "sha256": "8102b9088dbdc8680bb1bb39a061d4b3535467992c064ec8238a3ddac351e765", "assetId": "img:8102b9088dbdc8680bb1bb39a061d4b3535467992c064ec8238a3ddac351e765", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000074", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069471876.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what is meant by the term ionic bonding.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-9ef9b0c8-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why magnesium chloride has a high melting point. Calculate the mass, in grams, of aluminium formed when a charge of 20 faradays is passed through aluminium oxide dissolved in molten cryolite. The ionic half-equation for the formation of aluminium is $\\mathrm {A l} ^ {3 +} + 3 \\mathrm {e} ^ {-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {A l}$", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-9ef9b0c8-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069471876.png", "width": 1494, "format": "png", "height": 183, "sha256": "8102b9088dbdc8680bb1bb39a061d4b3535467992c064ec8238a3ddac351e765", "assetId": "img:8102b9088dbdc8680bb1bb39a061d4b3535467992c064ec8238a3ddac351e765", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000074", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069471876.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q03-9ef9b0c8", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000066"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Crystals of copper(II) nitrate, $ \\mathrm{C u} \\left( \\mathrm{N O}_{3} \\right)_{2} $ , can be prepared by reacting solid copper(II) oxide, CuO, with dilute nitric acid.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-9ef9b0c8-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student is given a sample of copper(II) oxide containing small amounts of insoluble impurities. The passage is from her notebook and describes the method she uses to prepare some pure, dry crystals of copper(II) nitrate from her sample of copper(II) oxide. Stage 1: Place 50 $ cm^{3} $ of dilute nitric acid into a beaker and warm. Stage 2: Add the impure copper(II) oxide a little at a time and stir, until it is in excess. Stage 3: Filter the mixture. Stage 4: Heat the filtrate until the crystallisation point is reached. Stage 5: Allow the filtrate to cool. Stage 6: Filter off the crystals and dry with filter paper.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-9ef9b0c8-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Why is the acid warmed in stage 1?", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-9ef9b0c8-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "How will the student know when the copper(II) oxide is in excess in stage 2?", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-9ef9b0c8-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "How will the student know when the crystallisation point is reached in stage 4?", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-9ef9b0c8-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In which stage are the insoluble impurities removed?", "label": "b-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-9ef9b0c8-pb-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-9ef9b0c8", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "But-1-ene is a member of the homologous series of alkenes. The displayed formula of but-1-ene is The saturated compound cyclobutane is an isomer of but-1-ene. The displayed formula of cyclobutane is", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State what is meant by the term isomers.", "label": "a", "marks": 2, "partId": "q05-9ef9b0c8-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw the displayed formula of another isomer of but-1-ene.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": 1, "partId": "q05-9ef9b0c8-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe a test that would distinguish between but-1-ene and cyclobutane.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": 3, "partId": "q05-9ef9b0c8-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Using your knowledge of the reactions of ethene, complete the two chemical equations to show the formula of the organic product.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-9ef9b0c8-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The reaction between but-1-ene and steam. $\\mathrm {C H} _ {2} = \\mathrm {C H C H} _ {2} \\mathrm {C H} _ {3} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} \\rightarrow \\quad \\begin{array}{c c c c} | & | & | & | \\\\ - C & - C & - C & - C & - \\\\ | & | & | & | \\end{array}$", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-9ef9b0c8-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The polymerisation of but-1-ene. $n \\quad \\begin{array}{c c c} H & H \\\\ | & | \\\\ C = C \\\\ | & | \\\\ H & C _ {2} H _ {5} \\end{array} \\rightarrow$", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-9ef9b0c8-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069471907.png", "width": 292, "format": "png", "height": 218, "sha256": "58105d1319f7a2c82445df89bdd7988d602fc990149746c17b70790488fcde6c", "assetId": "img:58105d1319f7a2c82445df89bdd7988d602fc990149746c17b70790488fcde6c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000098", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069471907.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069471911.png", "width": 232, "format": "png", "height": 300, "sha256": "8d46b7c46210bf944fcdb26d6214ebe6aa277435ec0e98482c2aba6701bca65f", "assetId": "img:8d46b7c46210bf944fcdb26d6214ebe6aa277435ec0e98482c2aba6701bca65f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000101", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069471911.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q05-9ef9b0c8", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This apparatus can be used in a method to find the volume of sulfuric acid required to neutralise a solution of potassium hydroxide (KOH).", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "What name is given to this method?", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-9ef9b0c8-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which piece of apparatus should be used to measure the $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of KOH? A beaker B measuring cylinder C pipette D syringe", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-9ef9b0c8-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the colours that are seen if methyl orange is used as the indicator. colour before adding the acid ... colour after KOH is neutralised.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-9ef9b0c8-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student carries out the experiment. His burette readings are shown in the diagram. before after Use the diagram to complete the table. Give the readings to the nearest 0.05 $ \\mathrm{c m}^{3}. $", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-9ef9b0c8-pd", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069471946.png", "width": 150, "format": "png", "height": 456, "sha256": "1325cfb064a4fd8e8223c24b12b85b1f74d0bbcdd9424081220c25d09c3ec7ce", "assetId": "img:1325cfb064a4fd8e8223c24b12b85b1f74d0bbcdd9424081220c25d09c3ec7ce", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000125", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069471946.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069471950.png", "width": 153, "format": "png", "height": 463, "sha256": "e1f73d8cf6ebed52f4050ffd9f1e78d0fb19af4a7db0ec1f70696a4c7fa45dee", "assetId": "img:e1f73d8cf6ebed52f4050ffd9f1e78d0fb19af4a7db0ec1f70696a4c7fa45dee", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000127", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069471950.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A second student did the experiment four times, using a different solution of potassium hydroxide. The table shows her results. Concordant results are those within $ 0. 2 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of one another.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-9ef9b0c8-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Place ticks in the table to indicate which results are concordant with one another.", "label": "e-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-9ef9b0c8-pe-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use your ticked results to calculate the average (mean) volume of acid added.", "label": "e-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-9ef9b0c8-pe-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069471927.png", "width": 568, "format": "png", "height": 665, "sha256": "ca21635453f40372c820dea2855c93bc445a80425c3c10d5d7d9cb4dd4afebd4", "assetId": "img:ca21635453f40372c820dea2855c93bc445a80425c3c10d5d7d9cb4dd4afebd4", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000114", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069471927.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069471946.png", "width": 150, "format": "png", "height": 456, "sha256": "1325cfb064a4fd8e8223c24b12b85b1f74d0bbcdd9424081220c25d09c3ec7ce", "assetId": "img:1325cfb064a4fd8e8223c24b12b85b1f74d0bbcdd9424081220c25d09c3ec7ce", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000125", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069471946.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069471950.png", "width": 153, "format": "png", "height": 463, "sha256": "e1f73d8cf6ebed52f4050ffd9f1e78d0fb19af4a7db0ec1f70696a4c7fa45dee", "assetId": "img:e1f73d8cf6ebed52f4050ffd9f1e78d0fb19af4a7db0ec1f70696a4c7fa45dee", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000127", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069471950.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q06-9ef9b0c8", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000130", "e000132"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Crude oil is a complex mixture of organic compounds called hydrocarbons. It is separated into fractions using a fractionating tower.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which fraction has the lowest boiling point?", "label": "a", "marks": 1, "partId": "q07-9ef9b0c8-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which fraction is the most viscous?", "label": "b", "marks": 1, "partId": "q07-9ef9b0c8-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Some fractions containing long-chain hydrocarbons are cracked. The cracking of octadecane, $ ( C_{1 8} H_{3 8} ) $ , produces octane, $ ( C_{8} H_{1 8} ) $ , and one other product. Write a chemical equation for this cracking reaction.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-9ef9b0c8-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why it is important to crack long-chain hydrocarbon fractions.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-9ef9b0c8-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Octane is one of the hydrocarbons in the petrol used in cars. $C _ {8} H _ {1 8} + 1 2 \\frac {1}{2} O _ {2} \\rightarrow 8 C O _ {2} + 9 H _ {2} O$ The equation for the complete combustion of octane is The incomplete combustion of octane produces a poisonous gas that reduces the capacity of blood to carry oxygen. Write a chemical equation for this incomplete combustion of octane.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-9ef9b0c8-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069471958.png", "width": 900, "format": "png", "height": 938, "sha256": "1b18fb8e0eadb298d305bc258a869796a6356b6aa1c4afff9b13e86fbd8ba0b2", "assetId": "img:1b18fb8e0eadb298d305bc258a869796a6356b6aa1c4afff9b13e86fbd8ba0b2", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000137", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069471958.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q07-9ef9b0c8", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This is a recipe for making Irish soda bread. add 170g of wholemeal flour, 170g of plain flour, 10g of salt and 10.5g of bicarbonate of soda (sodium hydrogencarbonate, $ \\mathrm{N a H C O_{3}} $ ）to a bowl and stir pour in $ 2 9 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of buttermilk and stir quickly to form a soft dough form the dough into a round ball and slightly flatten it cut a cross in the top and bake for 30 minutes in an oven at $ 2 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ When sodium hydrogencarbonate is heated, it forms carbon dioxide gas. $2 \\mathrm {N a H C O} _ {3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {C O} _ {3} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2}$", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the mass, in grams, of carbon dioxide that would be produced by completely decomposing 10.5 g of sodium hydrogencarbonate. $ [ M_{r} $ of $ \\mathrm{N a H C O_{3}}=8 4 ] $", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-9ef9b0c8-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use your answer from part (a) to calculate the volume, in $ cm^{3} $ , at room temperature and pressure, of carbon dioxide that would be produced by completely decomposing 10.5 g of sodium hydrogencarbonate. Assume one mole of carbon dioxide has a volume of $ 2 4 0 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ at room temperature and pressure. $\\mathrm {c m} ^ {3}$ $(\\mathrm {T o t a l f o r Q u e s t i o n 8 = 4 m a r k s})$ Every effort has been made to contact copyright holders to obtain their permission for the use of copyright material. Pearson Education Ltd. will, if notified, be happy to rectify any errors or omissions and include any such rectifications in future editions.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-9ef9b0c8-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q08-9ef9b0c8", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 60, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 7, "2": 6, "4": 5}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "Summer 2016", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH0_2C_1606_MS", "canonicalDocumentId": "ae9e233f-1826-5a87-864e-a527fecbcaec"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-ae9e233f-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Accept stirrerReject metal"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C(solvent)B(solution)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "evaporated/went into the air"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "accept boilsaccept turns into vapour"}], "answerText": "M1(X)-(stirring/glass/plastic)rodM2(Y)-Bunsen(burner)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-ae9e233f-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text":