ource": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: dehydration", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: hydration", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: oxidation", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: reduction", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: 4 (e)(i)(ii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: Total", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "5(a)(i): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: 5(b)(i)(ii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M2=18900(J)/19000(J)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M1mass[CH3OH]=84.7-83.2OR1.5(g)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M2n[CH3OH]=1.5÷32OR0.046875(mol)ORM1÷32", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M3ΔH=18900÷M2OR403200(J/mol)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M4ΔH=-400(kJ/mol)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "5(c): bare integer cell \"15\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "June 2017", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": "Wednesday 14 June 2017 – Morning", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH0/2C", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "8d0a429e-bbff-5d24-aff9-17ce1f25d16f"}, "warnings": [], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Sodium sulfate is a compound with many uses.", "marks": 4, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The formula of the main compound used as the source of sodium sulfate is $ \\mathrm{N a_{2} S O_{4}. 1 0 H_{2} O} $ How many different elements are shown in this formula? A 2 B 3 C 4 D 5", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-8d0a429e-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Sodium sulfate can be made from sodium hydroxide and sulfuric acid. Balance the equation for the reaction between sodium hydroxide and sulfuric acid. (1) $ \\mathrm{N a O H}+ \\mathrm{H_{2} S O_{4}} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{N a_{2} S O_{4}}+ \\mathrm{H_{2} O} $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-8d0a429e-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Sodium hydroxide is manufactured by the electrolysis of brine in the diaphragm cell. Sulfuric acid is manufactured using the contact process. The table contains some statements about these two processes. Place ticks ( $ \\checkmark $ ) in the boxes to show the two correct statements.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-8d0a429e-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-8d0a429e", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000031"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram shows the positions of some elements in part of the Periodic Table. He", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "How many periods and groups are shown in this diagram?", "label": "a", "marks": 1, "partId": "q02-8d0a429e-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "How many elements shown in the diagram are noble gases? A 1 B 2 C 3 D 4", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8d0a429e-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the formula of the compound formed between magnesium and fluorine? (1) A MgF B $ \\mathrm{M g_{2} F} $ C $ \\mathrm{M g F_{2}} $ D $ \\mathrm{M g_{2} F_{2}} $", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8d0a429e-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the percentage composition by mass of a sample of silicon. Calculate the relative atomic mass of this sample of silicon. Give your answer to one decimal place.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8d0a429e-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["relative atomic mass"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A molecule of silicon tetrafluoride $ \\mathrm{(S i F_{4})} $ contains covalent bonds. Draw a dot and cross diagram to show the outer electrons in this molecule.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8d0a429e-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the boiling points of some compounds containing silicon. All of these compounds contain covalent bonds. $ \\mathrm{S i F}_{4} $ and $ \\mathrm{S i C l}_{4} $ have simple molecular structures. $ \\mathrm{S i O}_{2} $ has a giant covalent structure.", "label": "f", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8d0a429e-pf", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why the boiling point of $ \\mathrm{S i C l}_{4} $ is greater than the boiling point of $ \\mathrm{S i F}_{4} $", "label": "f-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8d0a429e-pf-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why the boiling point of $ \\mathrm{S i O_{2}} $ is very much greater than the boiling point of $ \\mathrm{S i C l_{4}} $", "label": "f-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8d0a429e-pf-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q02-8d0a429e", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000036", "e000039", "e000051", "e000056"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This apparatus can be used to investigate electrolysis.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name the particles that move through the connecting wires to form an electric current.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8d0a429e-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The electrodes are made of platinum, which is an inert metal. State what is meant by the term inert.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8d0a429e-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why the electrolytic cell containing $ \\mathrm{P b B r}_{2} $ needs to be heated before electrolysis can occur.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8d0a429e-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When NaCl(aq) is electrolysed, two gases form at the positive electrode and one gas forms at the negative electrode. The formulae of the species in $ \\mathrm{N a C l} ( \\mathrm{a q} ) $ are $ \\mathrm{N a}^{+}, \\mathrm{C l}^{-}, \\mathrm{H}^{+}, \\mathrm{O H}^{-} $ and $ \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}. $", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8d0a429e-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the gases formed at each electrode. positive electrode ... negative electrode.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8d0a429e-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give ionic half-equations to show the formation of each gas.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8d0a429e-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The ionic half-equation for one of the reactions in the cell containing copper(II) sulfate solution is $\\mathrm {C u} ^ {2 +} + 2 \\mathrm {e} ^ {-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C u}$ During the electrolysis, a charge of 0.040 faradays passes through this cell. Calculate the mass of copper metal formed.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8d0a429e-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069525090.png", "width": 1396, "format": "png", "height": 701, "sha256": "c1fee978eed70089ac85bf19986df00a94c7babcb26d0878c7e33f6dea12867d", "assetId": "img:c1fee978eed70089ac85bf19986df00a94c7babcb26d0878c7e33f6dea12867d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000062", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069525090.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-8d0a429e", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Compounds containing C=C double bonds are used to manufacture alcohols such as ethanol and addition polymers such as PVC. The table shows the formulae of some compounds containing C=C bonds.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which three of these compounds are hydrocarbons.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d0a429e-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which compound is shown as a displayed formula.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d0a429e-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Ethanol can be manufactured from compound A using reaction 1. Ethanol can also be manufactured from glucose using reaction 2. The equations for these reactions are reaction 1 $\\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {5} \\mathrm {O H}$ reaction 2 $C _ {6} H _ {1 2} O _ {6} \\rightarrow 2 C _ {2} H _ {5} O H + 2 C O _ {2}$ Give two advantages of using each reaction to manufacture ethanol. reaction 1. reaction 2.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d0a429e-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Compound C reacts with bromine to form a product with this formula.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d0a429e-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069525111.png", "width": 366, "format": "png", "height": 147, "sha256": "f7e573efecdc39738b3f9646b49446c621ff17ceb89864da3f17bbe18946b955", "assetId": "img:f7e573efecdc39738b3f9646b49446c621ff17ceb89864da3f17bbe18946b955", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000092", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069525111.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use this formula to determine the name of compound C.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d0a429e-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the colour of the product formed when compound C reacts with bromine.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d0a429e-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Compound X is an isomer of compound C.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d0a429e-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain what is meant by the term isomers.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d0a429e-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the conversion of compound X to an alcohol is $\\mathrm {C H} _ {2} = \\mathrm {C H} - \\mathrm {C H} _ {2} - \\mathrm {C H} _ {3} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C H} _ {3} - \\mathrm {C H} - \\mathrm {C H} _ {2} - \\mathrm {C H} _ {3}$ Place ticks ( $ \\checkmark $ ) in the boxes to show the two correct descriptions of this reaction.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d0a429e-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Compound D, chloroethene, can be used to manufacture the polymer PVC.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d0a429e-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the full name of PVC.", "label": "e-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d0a429e-pe-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write an equation, containing displayed formulae, to represent the reaction that occurs in the manufacture of PVC.", "label": "e-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d0a429e-pe-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069525111.png", "width": 366, "format": "png", "height": 147, "sha256": "f7e573efecdc39738b3f9646b49446c621ff17ceb89864da3f17bbe18946b955", "assetId": "img:f7e573efecdc39738b3f9646b49446c621ff17ceb89864da3f17bbe18946b955", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000092", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069525111.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q04-8d0a429e", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000079", "e000100"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student uses this apparatus to find the increase in temperature of water when methanol, $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} O H} $ , is burned.", "marks": 15, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "There are several reasons why the increase in temperature is less than expected.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d0a429e-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "One reason is the incomplete combustion of methanol to form only carbon monoxide and water. Write the chemical equation for this incomplete combustion.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d0a429e-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State another reason why the increase in temperature is less than expected.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d0a429e-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student records these results.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d0a429e-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the heat energy change (Q), in joules, in this experiment using the expression $Q = m \\times 4. 2 \\times \\Delta T$ where m is the mass of water in grams and $ \\Delta T $ represents the increase in temperature.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d0a429e-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The relative molecular mass of methanol is 32 Use this information and your value for Q to calculate the molar enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ , for the combustion of methanol. Give your answer in kJ/mol. $\\Delta H =$", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d0a429e-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student draws an energy level diagram for the complete combustion of methanol. $\\begin{array}{c c} \\mathrm {e n e r g y} & \\mathrm {c a r b o n d i o x i d e} \\\\ \\hline \\mathrm {m e t h a n o l} & \\mathrm {c a r b o n d i o x i d e} \\\\ \\end{array}$ Identify the two mistakes in his diagram.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d0a429e-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student is given this table of average (mean) bond energies. The equation for the complete combustion of methanol is $\\mathrm {H} - \\mathrm {C} - \\mathrm {O} - \\mathrm {H} + 1. 5 \\mathrm {O} = \\mathrm {O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {O} = \\mathrm {C} = \\mathrm {O} + 2 \\mathrm {H} - \\mathrm {O} - \\mathrm {H}$ Use this equation and the information in the table to calculate another value for the molar enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ , for the combustion of methanol. $\\Delta H =$ Every effort has been made to contact copyright holders to obtain their permission for the use of copyright material. Pearson Education Ltd. will, if notified, be happy to rectify any errors or omissions and include any such rectifications in future editions.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d0a429e-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069525138.png", "width": 407, "format": "png", "height": 370, "sha256": "cb408f8f1e4521c2486e31b4114bb448b7eb4b9874656ced42b3e244b41ce1b1", "assetId": "img:cb408f8f1e4521c2486e31b4114bb448b7eb4b9874656ced42b3e244b41ce1b1", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000106", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069525138.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069525144.png", "width": 445, "format": "png", "height": 262, "sha256": "fde660550c6ca32cd687ff7301afcd4f51109208f6a924f332a5502c66d8e55d", "assetId": "img:fde660550c6ca32cd687ff7301afcd4f51109208f6a924f332a5502c66d8e55d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000107", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069525144.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-8d0a429e", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000113", "e000125"]}], "paperTotal": 60, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 4, "3": 11, "5": 15}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "Summer 2017", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "331fb228-f36f-52dc-b6b4-ec65c3085c25"}, "entries": [{"mcq": true, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-331fb228-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2NaOH+(1)H2SO4→(1)Na2SO4+2H2O"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Accept fractions and multiples"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "brine is a solution of sodium chloride in water"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "√"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3 ticks with 2 correct scores 1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3 ticks with 1 correct scores 0"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "4 or 5 ticks scores 0"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "√"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "C(4)\nThe only correct answer is C\nA is not correct because there are 4 elements shown not 2\nB is not correct because there are 4 elements shown not 3\nD is not correct because there are 4 elements shown not 5\nthe temperature used in the contact process is greater than 1000℃\nan equation for the contact process isSO2+H2O→H2SO4\nthe reactions in the diaphragm cell are displacement reactions\nthe catalyst used in the contact process is vanadium(V) oxide", "confidence": 0.85, "markPoints": [{"ecf": false, "text": "C", "marks": 4, "reject": false, "ordinal": 1, "rawText": "C", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "The only correct answer is C\nA is not correct because there are 4 elements shown not 2\nB is not correct because there are 4 elements shown not 3\nD is not correct because there are 4 elements shown not 5", "marks": null, "reject": false, "ordinal": 2, "rawText": "The only correct answer is C\nA is not correct because there are 4 elements shown not 2\nB is not correct because there are 4 elements shown not 3\nD is not correct because there are 4 elements shown not 5", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}], "correctOption": "C", "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": true, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-331fb228-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B(2)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is B"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not correct because there are 2 noble gases shown not 1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not correct because there are 2 noble gases shown not 3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because there are 2 noble gases shown not 4"}], "answerText": "D(3 periods and 8 groups)\nThe only correct answer is D\nA is not correct because there are 3 periods and 8 groups shown not 2 periods and 4 groups\nB is not correct because there are 3 periods and 8 groups shown not 3 periods and 4 groups\nC is not correct because there are 3 periods and 8 groups shown not 2 periods and 8 groups", "confidence": 0.85, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": "D", "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-331fb228-q3a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT e- or e"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "not (chemically) reactive / does not react (with the electrolytes/with the products of electrolysis)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW unreactive"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW non-reactive"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to full outer shell of electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 PbBr2 needs to be molten/liquid/melted"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 so that the ions can flow/move/are mobile"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT solid PbBr2 does not conduct"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT the ions cannot flow/move/are not mobile in the solid"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to carry charge"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT references to electrons moving"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(positive) chlorine AND oxygen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(negative) hydrogen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT Cl2 for chlorine and O2 for oxygen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT names in any order"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT H2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If both name and formula given, mark name only"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 2Cl-→Cl2+2e-"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT 2Cl-→2e-→Cl2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 2H2O→4H+O2+4e-"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW 4OH-→2H2O+O2+4e-"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 2H2O+2e-→H2+2OH-"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW 2H++2e-→H2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT multiples/fractions in half-equations"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(e)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 n[Cu]=0.04(0)÷2OR0.02(0)(mol)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 mass[Cu]=1.3(g)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR M1x63.5 correctly evaluated"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT 1.27(g)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT 1.28(g) using 64 instead of 63.5"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct final answer with no working scores 2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "electrons", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)(i)", "marks": 2, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-331fb228-q4ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT formulae copied from table"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT C and H"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 B"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT converse argument about (all) the others"}], "answerText": "M1 A and B and C\nM2 (they/all) contain only carbon and hydrogen (atoms)\nACCEPT words with same meaning as only, eg solely, exclusively, just etc\nACCEPT particles/elements in place of atoms\nREJECT ions/molecules/compounds in place of atoms\nREJECT element/mixture in place of they/all\nREJECT H2\nM2 (because) it shows all the bonds (in the molecule)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(b)", "marks": 4, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-331fb228-q4b", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE more concentratedALLOW does not need further processingIGNORE no waste productsALLOW no greenhouse gas producedACCEPT can be used in countrieswith no oil reserves/with available land/with suitable climate to grow sugar caneALLOW 30 to 40℃ACCEPT thermal energyIGNORE references to batch and continuous processesIGNORE references to lower cost"}], "answerText": "(reaction 1):Any two from:M1(it produces) pure(r) ethanol/alcohol/productM2(it is a) fast(er)(reaction)M3(it has a) greater atom economyM4no carbon dioxide produced(so less pollution)(reaction 2):Any two from:M5(it) uses renewable/sustainable resources/does not use finite resourcesM6(it) uses atmospheric pressure/(it) does not need high pressure/(it) works at low pressuresM7(it) works at low/just above room temperature/(it) does not need much heat(energy)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)(i)", "marks": 2, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-331fb228-q5ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT multiples and fractions"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 all formulae correct"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 correctly balanced"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP on M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "thermal energy/heat(energy) lost to the surroundings/environment"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT lost to atmosphere/beaker/thermometer"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT evaporation of water/methanol"}], "answerText": "CH3OH+O2→CO+2H2O", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(b)(ii)", "marks": 4, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-331fb228-q5bii", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any number of sig fig except1,eg0.047"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT M2 from(b)(i)÷M2 from(b)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any number of sig fig"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any number of sig fig,eg403,403.2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Negative sign must be included"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(+)400/403/403.2etc scores3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Mark CSQ throughout"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct final answer with no working scores4"}], "answerText": "M1 mass[CH3OH]=84.7-83.2 OR 1.5(g)\nM2 18900÷1.5 OR 12600 OR 18900÷M1\nM3 ΔH=12600×32 OR 403200(J)\nM4 ΔH=-400(kJ/mol)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(b)(iii)", "marks": 2, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-331fb228-q5biii", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT product level should be below reactant levelACCEPT answers shown on diagramIGNORE references to activation energyIGNORE references to missing x-axis"}], "answerText": "M1 oxygen/other reactant missing from methanolM2 product level/ carbon dioxide and water above reactant level", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(c)", "marks": 4, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-331fb228-q5c", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE negative sign"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE sign"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Expected final answer is-535"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Positive sign not required"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If a clear statement is made that the reaction is exothermic, then sign can be negative"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct final answer with no working scores 4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "Route 1:\nM1 Σ(bonds broken)=(412×3)+360+463+(496×1.5)\nOR 2803(kJ/mol)\nM2 Σ(bonds made)=(743×2)+463×4)\nOR 3338(kJ/mol)\nRoute 2:\nM1 Σ(bonds broken)=(412×3)+360+(496×1.5)\nOR 2340(kJ/mol)\nM2 Σ(bonds made)=(743×2)+463×3)\nOR 2875(kJ/mol)\nM3 Correct calculation of difference between M1 and M2\nM4 If M2>M1 final answer must be negative\nIf M2<M1 final answer must be positive", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}], "icTable": null, "warnings": ["1(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1(a): bare integer cell \"4\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "2(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "orphan row before first entry: (c)", "orphan row before first entry: (d)", "orphan row before first entry: (e)", "orphan row before first entry: (ii)", "orphan row before first entry: M2 stronger in SiCl4/weaker in SiF4", "orphan row before first entry: M2 DEP on M1", "orphan row before first entry: ACCEPT attraction between SiCl4 molecules is greater(than that between SiF4 molecules)or reverse argument for2 marks", "orphan row before first entry: Award0/2if any reference to breaking covalent bonds", "orphan row before first entry: M1 SiO2has str