th": 644, "format": "png", "height": 634, "sha256": "0a2da468c14e8eb88fbb1b92fa6985c1562f1287df5ebe2816c93c79da4523eb", "assetId": "img:0a2da468c14e8eb88fbb1b92fa6985c1562f1287df5ebe2816c93c79da4523eb", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000026", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812030.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which period of the Periodic Table contains sulfur? A 2 B 3 C 4 D 6", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-3745aeee-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the charge on a sulfide ion? A 1+ B 2+ C 1- D 2-", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-3745aeee-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When a mixture of sulfur and zinc is heated to a high temperature a reaction occurs, forming the compound zinc sulfide, ZnS", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-3745aeee-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why the mixture of sulfur and zinc needs heating before a reaction occurs.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-3745aeee-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the relative formula mass $ ( M_{r} ) $ of zinc sulfide. [for Zn, $ A_{\\mathrm{r}}=6 5 $ for S, $ A_{\\mathrm{r}}=3 2 $", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-3745aeee-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812030.png", "width": 644, "format": "png", "height": 634, "sha256": "0a2da468c14e8eb88fbb1b92fa6985c1562f1287df5ebe2816c93c79da4523eb", "assetId": "img:0a2da468c14e8eb88fbb1b92fa6985c1562f1287df5ebe2816c93c79da4523eb", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000026", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812030.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-3745aeee", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) The diagram represents an atom of an element. (i) What is the name of the particle labelled W? (ii) What is the mass number of this atom? A 4 B 5 C 9 D 13", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "These are the symbols for the two isotopes of lithium.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-3745aeee-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In terms of sub-atomic particles, give one similarity and one difference between the two isotopes. similarity difference", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-3745aeee-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A sample of lithium contains 7.5% of $ _{3}^{6} $Li and 92.5% of $ _{3}^{7} $Li Calculate the relative atomic mass $ ( A_{r} ) $ of this sample of lithium.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-3745aeee-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812051.png", "width": 967, "format": "png", "height": 680, "sha256": "a8e411c866e4b299f44bc61345cca184a50ea5936feb95c5f1bd7d9ad562f5b9", "assetId": "img:a8e411c866e4b299f44bc61345cca184a50ea5936feb95c5f1bd7d9ad562f5b9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000043", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812051.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-3745aeee", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about magnesium metal and its compounds.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram represents the structure of magnesium. Complete the diagram by adding the labels.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3745aeee-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812082.png", "width": 1070, "format": "png", "height": 373, "sha256": "5df3948d6c31c98d8d24aca2ebe80646e9bfe0aa8b9791cbbaf27ea9109caa8e", "assetId": "img:5df3948d6c31c98d8d24aca2ebe80646e9bfe0aa8b9791cbbaf27ea9109caa8e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000064", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812082.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why magnesium is malleable.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3745aeee-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Magnesium burns in oxygen gas to form solid magnesium oxide.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3745aeee-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the chemical equation for the reaction by balancing the equation and adding the state symbols. $\\mathrm {M g (s)} + \\mathrm {O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {)} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {M g O} (\\mathrm {})$", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3745aeee-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why magnesium is oxidised in this reaction.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3745aeee-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student uses this method to make a solution of magnesium chloride. Step 1 measure $ 2 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of dilute hydrochloric acid into a beaker Step 2 add magnesium powder a little at a time Step 3 keep adding magnesium powder until it is in excess Step 4 remove the excess magnesium powder by filtration This is the equation for the reaction. $\\mathrm {M g} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {H C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {M g C l} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3745aeee-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why the student uses magnesium powder rather than magnesium ribbon.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3745aeee-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why the student adds an excess of magnesium in step 3.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3745aeee-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the minimum mass of magnesium needed to react with $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of $ 2. 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ hydrochloric acid. [for Mg, $ A_{\\mathrm{r}}=2 4 $]", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3745aeee-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812082.png", "width": 1070, "format": "png", "height": 373, "sha256": "5df3948d6c31c98d8d24aca2ebe80646e9bfe0aa8b9791cbbaf27ea9109caa8e", "assetId": "img:5df3948d6c31c98d8d24aca2ebe80646e9bfe0aa8b9791cbbaf27ea9109caa8e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000064", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812082.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q03-3745aeee", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about the manufacture of ethanol by the fermentation of glucose.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Fermentation needs to be done in the absence of air. Give two other conditions needed for fermentation.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-3745aeee-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why fermentation needs to be done in the absence of air.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-3745aeee-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the equation for fermentation. $\\mathrm {C} _ {6} \\mathrm {H} _ {1 2} \\mathrm {O} _ {6} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {5} \\mathrm {O H} + 2.$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-3745aeee-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A mixture containing 500 mol of glucose is fermented. A mass of 5750 g of ethanol is obtained. Calculate the percentage yield. [for ethanol, $ M_{r}=4 6 $]", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-3745aeee-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q04-3745aeee", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Sodium sulfate can be prepared by the reaction between sodium hydroxide solution and sodium hydrogensulfate $ \\mathrm{(N a H S O_{4})} $ solution. This is the equation for the reaction. $\\mathrm {N a O H} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {N a H S O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {I})$ Sodium hydrogensulfate solution is acidic. The student does a titration. A student adds $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution to a conical flask and adds two drops of indicator.", "marks": 14, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name a piece of apparatus that should be used to add $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution to the conical flask.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-3745aeee-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe the method the student should use to find the accurate volume of sodium hydrogensulfate solution needed to neutralise the $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-3745aeee-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student repeats the titration without the indicator and forms a solution of sodium sulfate. Describe how the student can obtain pure, dry crystals of hydrated sodium sulfate from the solution.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-3745aeee-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Crystals of hydrated sodium sulfate decompose when heated. This is the equation for the decomposition. $\\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4}. \\mathrm {x H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} + \\mathrm {x H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ A student uses this apparatus to find the value of x. hydrated sodium sulfate The student heats the crystals until the decomposition is complete. The table shows the student''s results. Use the results to calculate the value of x. [for $ \\mathrm{N a_{2} S O_{4}} $ $ M_{\\mathrm{r}}=1 4 2 $ for $ \\mathrm{H_{2}O}, M_{\\mathrm{r}}=1 8 $", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-3745aeee-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812142.png", "width": 887, "format": "png", "height": 445, "sha256": "b5ba351575a4a13617e69da349a6da2eb4fb7d427bfa9007659b91d3a327c463", "assetId": "img:b5ba351575a4a13617e69da349a6da2eb4fb7d427bfa9007659b91d3a327c463", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000106", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812142.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["value of x"]}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812142.png", "width": 887, "format": "png", "height": 445, "sha256": "b5ba351575a4a13617e69da349a6da2eb4fb7d427bfa9007659b91d3a327c463", "assetId": "img:b5ba351575a4a13617e69da349a6da2eb4fb7d427bfa9007659b91d3a327c463", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000106", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812142.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-3745aeee", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000109"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about electrolysis.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "This apparatus is used to collect the gases produced when an electric current passes through dilute sulfuric acid.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3745aeee-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812156.png", "width": 948, "format": "png", "height": 818, "sha256": "0d20fe6dfcb5e6a20c14c2920c76c2623e6e4ae04b8149716559ea0dbacff99f", "assetId": "img:0d20fe6dfcb5e6a20c14c2920c76c2623e6e4ae04b8149716559ea0dbacff99f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000117", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812156.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name a suitable material for the inert electrodes.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3745aeee-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a test to show that the gas produced at the positive electrode is oxygen.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3745aeee-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a test to show that the gas produced at the negative electrode is hydrogen.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3745aeee-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give an ionic half-equation for the formation of hydrogen at the negative electrode.", "label": "a-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3745aeee-pa-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The oxygen gas in the tube has a volume of $ 1 7. 8 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ at rtp. Calculate the mass, in grams, of oxygen gas in the tube. Give your answer to 3 significant figures. [for $ O_{2} $ at rtp,molar volume $ = 2 4 0 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $]", "label": "a-v", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3745aeee-pa-v", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "This apparatus is used to electrolyse a concentrated solution of sodium chloride.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3745aeee-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812174.png", "width": 975, "format": "png", "height": 632, "sha256": "c36e1918e5c1d5c8f7b650abfef755c4bb4983320ab556bc27f1c13ea158731f", "assetId": "img:c36e1918e5c1d5c8f7b650abfef755c4bb4983320ab556bc27f1c13ea158731f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000124", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812174.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why sodium chloride solution conducts electricity.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3745aeee-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The positive ions in the solution are $ \\mathrm{H^{+}} $ and $ \\mathrm{N a^{+}}. $ Give a reason why hydrogen forms at the negative electrode rather than sodium.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3745aeee-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A sample of the solution is taken from near the negative electrode. The sample is tested with universal indicator. Explain the final colour of the universal indicator.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3745aeee-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812156.png", "width": 948, "format": "png", "height": 818, "sha256": "0d20fe6dfcb5e6a20c14c2920c76c2623e6e4ae04b8149716559ea0dbacff99f", "assetId": "img:0d20fe6dfcb5e6a20c14c2920c76c2623e6e4ae04b8149716559ea0dbacff99f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000117", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812156.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812174.png", "width": 975, "format": "png", "height": 632, "sha256": "c36e1918e5c1d5c8f7b650abfef755c4bb4983320ab556bc27f1c13ea158731f", "assetId": "img:c36e1918e5c1d5c8f7b650abfef755c4bb4983320ab556bc27f1c13ea158731f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000124", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812174.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-3745aeee", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Gaseous ethanol is manufactured by reacting ethene gas with steam. This is the equation for the reaction. $\\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {5} \\mathrm {O H} (\\mathrm {g})$", "marks": 15, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The pressure on an equilibrium mixture of the three gases is decreased. All the other conditions are kept the same. Predict the effect of this change on the yield of ethanol at equilibrium, giving a reason for your answer.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3745aeee-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Predict the effect of adding a catalyst on the yield of ethanol at equilibrium, giving a reason for your answer.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3745aeee-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation shows the displayed formulae for the reactants and products. The table gives the bond energies.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3745aeee-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812195.png", "width": 905, "format": "png", "height": 200, "sha256": "726ec0d69bec0908985f277d61e281be0788d0e31bdf1877f1a56f92c4f87c35", "assetId": "img:726ec0d69bec0908985f277d61e281be0788d0e31bdf1877f1a56f92c4f87c35", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000138", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812195.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Using the data in the table and the equation, show that the enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ , is approximately $ -40\\mathrm{kJ}/\\mathrm{mol}. $", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3745aeee-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain, in terms of bonds broken and bonds made, why this reaction is exothermic.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3745aeee-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw an energy level diagram for the reaction between ethene and steam. Label the enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H. $ Energy", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3745aeee-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Ethanoic acid reacts with ethanol to form ester A and water. ester A", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3745aeee-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812205.png", "width": 859, "format": "png", "height": 560, "sha256": "b066552fae456b3d91c134c79ec9755014ee11db07fd4dc3555dbbc2fb7d5eef", "assetId": "img:b066552fae456b3d91c134c79ec9755014ee11db07fd4dc3555dbbc2fb7d5eef", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000147", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812205.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the equation by adding the displayed formula of ester A.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3745aeee-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of ester A.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3745aeee-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812195.png", "width": 905, "format": "png", "height": 200, "sha256": "726ec0d69bec0908985f277d61e281be0788d0e31bdf1877f1a56f92c4f87c35", "assetId": "img:726ec0d69bec0908985f277d61e281be0788d0e31bdf1877f1a56f92c4f87c35", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000138", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812195.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069812205.png", "width": 859, "format": "png", "height": 560, "sha256": "b066552fae456b3d91c134c79ec9755014ee11db07fd4dc3555dbbc2fb7d5eef", "assetId": "img:b066552fae456b3d91c134c79ec9755014ee11db07fd4dc3555dbbc2fb7d5eef", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000147", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069812205.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-3745aeee", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000140"]}], "paperTotal": 70, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 4, "2": 6, "5": 14, "6": 11, "7": 15}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "Summer 2024", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": "P73426A", "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_2CR_2406_MS", "canonicalDocumentId": "42d68e49-2818-5c3b-9237-89b9bb9bfbc1"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-42d68e49-q1", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "B(3)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not the correct answer because sulfur is not in period2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not the correct answer because sulfur is not in period4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not the correct answer because sulfur is not in period6"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D(2-)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not the correct answer because the charge on a sulfide ion is not1+"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not the correct answer because the charge on a sulfide ion is not2+"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not the correct answer because the charge on a sulfide ion is not1-"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "the reaction has a high activation energy"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW to give zinc and sulfur enough energy to react"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW speeds reaction up/increases rate of reaction/reacts quicker"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW the reaction is very slow at room temperature"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE unreactive/does not react"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW gains kinetic energy"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE any units given"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)\nTotal marks for question1=4", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2", "marks": 1, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-42d68e49-q2", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "C(neutron)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not the correct answer because electrons are not in the nucleus"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not the correct answer as ions are not in the nucleus"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not the correct answer as protons have a positive charge"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C(9)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not the correct answer because the proton number=4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not the correct answer as there are 5 neutrons"}, {"kind": "note",