RNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M3 manufacture of/making paints/pigments/dyes", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M4 manufacture of/making polymers/plastics/fibres", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M5 pickling of steel/cleaning metals", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M6 manufacture of/making explosives", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M7 manufacture of/making paper", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M8 in car batteries/battery acid", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "June 2018", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": "Wednesday 13 June 2018 – Morning", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH0/2C", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "8d262f6a-ad65-5742-8487-0ce90a11e77c"}, "warnings": ["unclassified heading: temperature", "unclassified heading: pressure", "Q7: incomplete MCQ option set folded into stem", "Q8: incomplete MCQ option set folded into stem"], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram shows a Bunsen burner.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The Bunsen burner uses methane as a fuel. Methane has the formula $ \\mathrm{C H_{4}} $ Give the names of the two elements in methane.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-8d262f6a-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When methane burns it reacts with a gas in the air. Give the name of this gas.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-8d262f6a-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the two substances that form when methane burns in plenty of air.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-8d262f6a-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the poisonous gas that forms when methane burns in a shortage of air.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-8d262f6a-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069548619.png", "width": 337, "format": "png", "height": 393, "sha256": "60b072730c36ed3b2a14f15db076d5a6c1d6ff7b88bd93be2b737f5eb54508d6", "assetId": "img:60b072730c36ed3b2a14f15db076d5a6c1d6ff7b88bd93be2b737f5eb54508d6", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000022", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069548619.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-8d262f6a", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student uses this apparatus to investigate the burning of four different liquid fuels, W, X, Y and Z. The table shows the student''s results.", "marks": 4, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the table by giving the increase in temperature for fuels X, Y and Z.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8d262f6a-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student uses the same mass of water and burns each fuel for the same period of time. Explain which fuel releases the most heat energy.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8d262f6a-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the name given to reactions that release heat energy? A decomposition B endothermic C exothermic D reduction", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8d262f6a-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069548637.png", "width": 880, "format": "png", "height": 478, "sha256": "fcb66feaedb0987e45f80d35d1aabc3b5fa93fec507ebe5bc7e91857eaae96c6", "assetId": "img:fcb66feaedb0987e45f80d35d1aabc3b5fa93fec507ebe5bc7e91857eaae96c6", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000031", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069548637.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069548644.png", "width": 782, "format": "png", "height": 306, "sha256": "6236fe65a29ee22ab5d350dc17a928163a42ce8ad81a481be03c7045a62acad9", "assetId": "img:6236fe65a29ee22ab5d350dc17a928163a42ce8ad81a481be03c7045a62acad9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000032", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069548644.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-8d262f6a", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000034"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student makes an alkali solution by dissolving a small volume of cleaning liquid in deionised water. He then titrates a sample of this solution with an acid until neutralisation is complete.", "marks": 4, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the burette readings for his titration. Use the readings to complete the table, giving all values to the nearest 0.05 $ cm^{3}. $", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8d262f6a-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069548659.png", "width": 168, "format": "png", "height": 715, "sha256": "69f49f47049c5f43ec44fa0aaba69cfaac5e94d044f4c9a5e3ea6cbb16107046", "assetId": "img:69f49f47049c5f43ec44fa0aaba69cfaac5e94d044f4c9a5e3ea6cbb16107046", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000046", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069548659.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069548682.png", "width": 189, "format": "png", "height": 716, "sha256": "c71aba0d275037fc7c518eef18116af2c3a2f7785f524eccd440f552020859eb", "assetId": "img:c71aba0d275037fc7c518eef18116af2c3a2f7785f524eccd440f552020859eb", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000047", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069548682.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Another student does a titration using a solution of a different cleaning liquid. The table shows her results. Concordant results are those that differ by $ 0. 2 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ or less.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8d262f6a-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Place ticks in the table to show which results are concordant.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8d262f6a-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the concordant results to calculate the average (mean) volume of acid added.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8d262f6a-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["average volume of acid"]}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069548659.png", "width": 168, "format": "png", "height": 715, "sha256": "69f49f47049c5f43ec44fa0aaba69cfaac5e94d044f4c9a5e3ea6cbb16107046", "assetId": "img:69f49f47049c5f43ec44fa0aaba69cfaac5e94d044f4c9a5e3ea6cbb16107046", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000046", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069548659.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069548682.png", "width": 189, "format": "png", "height": 716, "sha256": "c71aba0d275037fc7c518eef18116af2c3a2f7785f524eccd440f552020859eb", "assetId": "img:c71aba0d275037fc7c518eef18116af2c3a2f7785f524eccd440f552020859eb", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000047", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069548682.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-8d262f6a", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000049", "e000052"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Bromine, chlorine, fluorine and iodine are elements in Group 7 of the Periodic Table.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which element is the most reactive? A bromine B chlorine D iodine C fluorine", "label": "a", "marks": 1, "partId": "q04-8d262f6a-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which element is a solid at room temperature? A bromine B chlorine C fluorine D iodine", "label": "b", "marks": 1, "partId": "q04-8d262f6a-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which element has the darkest colour at room temperature? A bromine B chlorine C fluorine D iodine", "label": "c", "marks": 1, "partId": "q04-8d262f6a-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Bromine reacts with hydrogen to form hydrogen bromide. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {H} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {B r} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {H B r} (\\mathrm {g})$ The table shows some average bond energies. Use the values in the table to calculate the enthalpy change for the reaction between hydrogen and bromine.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8d262f6a-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-8d262f6a", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000081", "e000083"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Ethanol can be manufactured by fermentation or by the direct hydration of ethene.", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "In Brazil, the main source of sugar for fermentation is sugar cane. sugar cane is added to water sugar cane contains sucrose $ \\left( \\mathrm{C}_{1 2} \\mathrm{H}_{2 2} \\mathrm{O}_{1 1} \\right) $ that dissolves in the water during the fermentation process the sucrose is broken down into glucose $ \\left( \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{1 2} \\mathrm{O}_{6} \\right) $ this glucose is then converted into ethanol （ $ \\mathrm{C_{2} H_{5} O H} $ ）and carbon dioxide", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the substance that is added to the sucrose solution to allow fermentation to take place.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the equation for the conversion of sucrose into glucose. $\\mathrm {C} _ {1 2} \\mathrm {H} _ {2 2} \\mathrm {O} _ {1 1} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} \\rightarrow$", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for the conversion of glucose into ethanol and carbon dioxide.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Fermentation produces a solution that is a mixture of ethanol and water. Which of these is the most effective method of obtaining ethanol from this mixture? A crystallisation B filtration C fractional distillation D simple distillation", "label": "a-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pa-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In the direct hydration method, ethene reacts with steam. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {5} \\mathrm {O H}$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the catalyst used in this reaction.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the temperature and pressure used in this reaction.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "This apparatus is used to convert ethanol into ethene.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069548780.png", "width": 1291, "format": "png", "height": 602, "sha256": "bc12a646802ea8c5a6e099985285c1642c0073fea5628a46c69cb4997857cbb6", "assetId": "img:bc12a646802ea8c5a6e099985285c1642c0073fea5628a46c69cb4997857cbb6", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000109", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069548780.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the type of reaction taking place.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the function of the aluminium oxide in this reaction.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Ethene belongs to a homologous series of unsaturated hydrocarbons called alkenes.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what is meant by the term unsaturated.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the colour change that is observed when bromine water is shaken with ethene in a test tube. from", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8d262f6a-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069548780.png", "width": 1291, "format": "png", "height": 602, "sha256": "bc12a646802ea8c5a6e099985285c1642c0073fea5628a46c69cb4997857cbb6", "assetId": "img:bc12a646802ea8c5a6e099985285c1642c0073fea5628a46c69cb4997857cbb6", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000109", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069548780.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-8d262f6a", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Phosgene $ \\left( \\mathrm{C O C I}_{2} \\right) $ is used in industry to make polymers.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Phosgene is formed when carbon monoxide reacts with chlorine. $\\mathrm {C O} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {C I} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {C O C I} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\quad \\Delta H = - 1 0 8 \\mathrm {k J / m o l}$", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-8d262f6a-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The reaction mixture is kept at temperatures between 50 and $ 1 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ If a temperature above $ 2 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ is used, only a small amount of phosgene is formed. Suggest why only a small amount of phosgene is formed at temperatures above 200 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-8d262f6a-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Predict how the yield of phosgene will change if the reaction is carried out at a higher pressure. Give a reason for your answer. [assume the reaction reaches a position of equilibrium]", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-8d262f6a-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Phosgene reacts with water to form hydrochloric acid and carbon dioxide. Write a chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-8d262f6a-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the displayed formula of phosgene. Draw a dot and cross diagram to show the arrangement of all the outer electrons in a molecule of phosgene.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-8d262f6a-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069548828.png", "width": 114, "format": "png", "height": 200, "sha256": "e0420ceccce442835029fa35285b0516854d6c9999a51a2901eb9f5e59a9d9a8", "assetId": "img:e0420ceccce442835029fa35285b0516854d6c9999a51a2901eb9f5e59a9d9a8", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000127", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069548828.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069548828.png", "width": 114, "format": "png", "height": 200, "sha256": "e0420ceccce442835029fa35285b0516854d6c9999a51a2901eb9f5e59a9d9a8", "assetId": "img:e0420ceccce442835029fa35285b0516854d6c9999a51a2901eb9f5e59a9d9a8", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000127", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069548828.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q06-8d262f6a", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Magnesium carbonate decomposes when heated to form magnesium oxide and carbon dioxide. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {M g C O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {M g O} (\\mathrm {s}) + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ Step1 weigh a clean, dry crucible and record the mass Step 2 add some magnesium carbonate Step 3 reweigh the crucible and contents and record the new mass Step 4 heat the crucible and contents for five minutes Step 5 allow the crucible and contents to cool and then reweigh Step 6 repeat steps 4 and 5 until the mass of the crucible and contents does not change The student does the experiment four times. The table shows her results. A student uses this method to investigate the reaction.", "marks": 4, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State why the mass of the crucible and contents decreases during heating.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8d262f6a-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the reason for Step 6.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8d262f6a-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain in which experiment the student should have heated for a fourth period of five minutes.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8d262f6a-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-8d262f6a", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000140"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Acid indigestion is caused by having too much hydrochloric acid in the stomach. The equation for the reaction between magnesium hydroxide and hydrochloric acid is $\\mathrm {M g} (\\mathrm {O H}) _ {2} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {H C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {M g C l} _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {I})$ He uses 0.29g of magnesium hydroxide to neutralise the hydrochloric acid. A student investigates how much magnesium hydroxide is needed to neutralise $ 1 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of hydrochloric acid with a concentration of $ 0. 0 9 6 8 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3}. $", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the amount, in moles, of HCl in the hydrochloric acid. amount of HCl", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-8d262f6a-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the amount, in moles, of $ \\mathrm{M g(OH)}_{2} $ used by the student. $ [M_{r} $ of $ \\mathrm{M g(OH)}_{2}=5 8] $ $\\mathrm {M g} (\\mathrm {O H}) _ {2}$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-8d262f6a-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain whether the student used the right amount of magnesium hydroxide to neutralise the hydrochloric acid.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-8d262f6a-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q08-8d262f6a", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The flow chart shows the steps in the manufacture of sulfuric acid.", "marks": 10, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the reaction in step 2 is $\\mathrm {S O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\frac {1}{2} \\mathrm {O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {S O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {g}) \\quad \\Delta H = - 9 6 \\mathrm {k J / m o l}$", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-8d262f6a-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what the symbols $ \\rightleftharpoons $ and $ \\Delta H $ represent. $ \\Delta H. $", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-8d262f6a-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the catalyst used in step 2.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-8d262f6a-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the temperature and pressure used in the reaction in step 2. temperature pressure.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-8d262f6a-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Sulfur trioxide reacts with water to form sulfuric acid. This reaction is very exothermic. $\\mathrm {S O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {l}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\quad \\Delta H = - 2 2 8 \\mathrm {k J / m o l}$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-8d262f6a-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why the sulfur trioxide is not dissolved in water to form sulfuric acid in step 3.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-8d262f6a-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write chemical equations for the reactions that take place in step 3 and step 4.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-8d262f6a-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give two industrial uses for sulfuric acid. Every effort has been made to contact copyright holders to obtain their permission for the use of copyright material. Pearson Education Ltd. will, if notified, be happy to rectify any errors or omissions and include any such rectifications in future editions.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-8d262f6a-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 9, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069548873.png", "width": 1664, "format": "png", "height": 764, "sha256": "6b50d29f9a2a978e7219b9b43ee3c4be67070de60be2f78d9083d64bcce4b1be", "assetId": "img:6b50d29f9a2a978e7219b9b43ee3c4be67070de60be2f78d9083d64bcce4b1be", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000159", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069548873.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q09-8d262f6a", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 60, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 6, "2": 4, "3": 4, "4": 6, "5": 12, "7": 4, "8": 6, "9": 10}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "Summer 2018", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH0_2C_1806_MS", "canonicalDocumentId": "eeaf9c56-f211-5c7c-a9a8-e37822342420"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-eeaf9c56-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT C"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT H/H2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "oxygen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT O2IGNORE O"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 carbon dioxide"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT CO2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT H2O"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW steam"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT COIf name and formula given both must be correct"}], "answerText": "M1 carbon\nM2 hydrogen\nFor M1 and M2, if name and symbol/formula given both must be correct\nM2 water(vapour)\ncarbon monoxide", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-eeaf9c56-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "All three must be correct"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE trailing zeroes"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(+)10.3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(+)15.9"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(+)11.1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 fuel Y"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(because it produces the) largest temperature rise/increase"}, {"kind": "ecf", "text": "ECF on temperature changes recorded in table"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT(because it produces the) largest temperature change"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE reaches the highest final temperature"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C(exothermic)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is incorrect as decomposition is not the name given to reactions that release heat energy"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is incorrect as endothermic reactions take in heat energy"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is incorrect as reduction is not the name given to reactions that release heat energy"}], "answerText": "W\nX\nY\nZ", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)(i)", "marks": 2, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-eeaf9c56-q3ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(20.80)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Mark M2 CQ on M1If the answers are in the wrong order allow 1 mark"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3.15"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "17.65"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "All three columns must contain ticks"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Volume of acid added"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "26.25"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "25.10"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "25.75"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "25.30"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "25.20"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Concordant results"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "√"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "√"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "√"}], "answerText": "Burette reading after\nBurette reading before\nVolume of acid added\nM1 3.15M2 17.65", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(b)(ii)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-eeaf9c56-q3bii", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "CQ on any combination of ticked results rounded correctlyIf no results are ticked then the mark can only be awarded for 25.2(0)If only one column ticked then no mark can be awarded in(b)(ii)ALLOW any number of figures after the decimal point,but answer must be given to at least one decimal place"}], "answerText": "25.2(0)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [{"ecf": false, "text": "25.2", "marks": 0, "reject": false, "ordinal": 1, "rawText": "25.2", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-eeaf9c56-q4a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D(iodine)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is incorrect as bromine is a liquid at room temperature"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is incorrect as chlorine is a gas at room temperature"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is incorrect as fluorine is a gas at room temperature"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D(iodine)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is incorrect as bromine is brown and not as dark as iodine, which is dark grey"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is incorrect as chlorine is pale green"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is incorrect as fluorine is pale yellow"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 Σ(bonds broken) = 436+193"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR 629(kJ/mol)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 Σ(bonds made) = 2×366"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR 732(kJ/mol)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 ΔH=-103(kJ/mol)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1-M2 evaluated correctly with the correct sign"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE any signs in M1 and M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "negative sign required"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "-103 with or without working scores 3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(+)103 with or without working scores 2"}], "answerText": "C(fluorine)\nA is incorrect as bromine is not the most reactive element. It is not as reactive as fluorine\nB is incorrect as chlorine is not the most reactive element. It is not as reactive as fluorine\nD is incorrect as iodine is the least reactive element of the four", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-eeaf9c56-q5ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWzymaseIGNOREenzyme(s)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(C12H22O11+H2O\\rightarrow)2C6H12O6"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTmultiples andfractionsIGNOREstate symbolseven if incorrect"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C6H12O6\\rightarrow2C2H5OH+2CO2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTmultiples andfractionsIGNOREstate symbolseven if incorrectALLOWC2H6Ofor ethanol"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iv)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C(fractional distillation)A is incorrect as you couldnot obtain ethanol by crystallisationB is incorrect as you couldnot obtain ethanol by filtrationD is incorrect as simpledistillation is not the mosteffective way to obtainethanol"}], "answerText": "Yeast", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(b)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-eeaf9c56-q5bi", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT $ \\mathrm{H_{3}PO_{4}} $ If both name and formula given, mark name only"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT phosphorus acid"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE reference to concentration"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 300( $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any temperature, or range of temperatures, between 250 and 350 inclusive"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT temperatures in other units provided unit is given"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 60-70(atm)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any pressure or range of pressures between 60 and 70 inclusive"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT pressures in other units provided unit is given e.g. $ 6 \\times10^{6} $ Pa to $ 7 \\times10^{6} $ Pa"}], "answerText": "phosphoric acid", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)(i)", "marks": 2, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-eeaf9c56-q6ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW the reaction moves in the backwards direction OWTTE"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE changes in amounts of reactants and products e.g. less phosgene/more CO and Cl2 produced"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(because the forward)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "reaction is exothermic/releases heat(energy)/releases thermal energy"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT(because the) backward reaction is endothermic/takes in heat(energy)/takes in thermal energy"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to Le Chatelier''s principle, eg favours the reaction that tries to reduce the temperature/favours the backward reaction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 dep on M1 correct or missing"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(yield) increases/the amount of phosgene increases"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2as there are fewer moles/molecules(of gas) on the right"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTthere are fewer moles/molecules of product"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTreverse argument"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW particles"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECTatoms"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to Le Chatelier''s principle, eg favours the reaction with more moles(of gas)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2dep on M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text"