G", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M2 correct evaluation of the calculation shown inM1IGNORE signM3If Σ(bonds made)＞Σ(bonds broken)final answer must be negativeIf Σ(bonds made)＜Σ(bonds broken)final answer must be positive(and+sign given)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: -788 with no working scores3(+)788 scores2", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (b)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "5(c): bare integer cell \"10\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "6(a)(i): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "6(a)(i): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (b)(i)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (ii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "6(c): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "6(c): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "6(c): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "6(c): bare integer cell \"14\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (b)(i)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M2 because there are fewer moles/molecules (of gas) on the left", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (ii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "7(c)(i): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "7(c)(i): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "7(c)(i): bare integer cell \"8\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "8(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "8(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "8(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "8(a): bare integer cell \"7\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "June 2019", "subject": null, "duration": "Morning (Time: 1 hour 15 minutes)", "examDate": "Wednesday 12 June 2019", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH1/2C", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "16e341a9-4824-549a-be76-abca668eb91f"}, "warnings": ["unclassified heading: relative atomic mass", "Q3: incomplete MCQ option set folded into stem"], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram shows the particles in an atom of an element.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The box gives the names of some particles. electron ion molecule neutron proton Use words from the box to label the diagram.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-16e341a9-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the mass number of this atom.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-16e341a9-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the sentence about isotopes. Isotopes are atoms that have the same number of ... but have a different number of ...", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-16e341a9-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595475.png", "width": 1566, "format": "png", "height": 691, "sha256": "2806652f83e55abfef2a18896ad96ad49a5732439dd07e817e156ff6de63cfcc", "assetId": "img:2806652f83e55abfef2a18896ad96ad49a5732439dd07e817e156ff6de63cfcc", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000021", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595475.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-16e341a9", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The table gives some information about the halogens, chlorine, bromine and iodine.", "marks": 9, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the table.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-16e341a9-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Chlorine has two isotopes of mass numbers 35 and 37 The relative percentage of each isotope in a sample of chlorine is chlorine-35 77.78% chlorine-37 22.22% Calculate the relative atomic mass of this sample of chlorine. Give your answer to one decimal place.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-16e341a9-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student is given an aqueous solution of chlorine and an aqueous solution of potassium bromide. Explain how he can use these two solutions to compare the reactivity of chlorine with the reactivity of bromine.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-16e341a9-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595499.png", "width": 112, "format": "png", "height": 68, "sha256": "b54afd6eb07089822d235d0a5e944cddd1ecff9feff09ed52d090f07262c8c8a", "assetId": "img:b54afd6eb07089822d235d0a5e944cddd1ecff9feff09ed52d090f07262c8c8a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000038", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595499.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595499.png", "width": 112, "format": "png", "height": 68, "sha256": "b54afd6eb07089822d235d0a5e944cddd1ecff9feff09ed52d090f07262c8c8a", "assetId": "img:b54afd6eb07089822d235d0a5e944cddd1ecff9feff09ed52d090f07262c8c8a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000038", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595499.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-16e341a9", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000031"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Methanol, ethanol, propanol and butanol are alcohols. They are all liquids that evaporate easily when warmed. She uses this method. pour some methanol into an evaporating basin place the evaporating basin on top of a beaker containing hot water measure the time taken for the methanol to evaporate completely repeat the experiment with each of the other alcohols, using the same apparatus A student uses this apparatus to compare the time taken for the four liquids to evaporate.", "marks": 9, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State two variables the student should control to make sure her results are valid.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-16e341a9-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why it is not safe to heat the evaporating basin directly with a Bunsen flame.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-16e341a9-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the results of experiments done by four students, A, B, C and D.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-16e341a9-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the mean (average) time for butanol to evaporate.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-16e341a9-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["mean time"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain how the results show which alcohol evaporates most easily.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-16e341a9-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the relationship between the number of carbon atoms in the molecule and how easily the alcohol evaporates.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-16e341a9-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595509.png", "width": 690, "format": "png", "height": 699, "sha256": "d3b719a7ed87d8d04797ee494947e602ddfac08d11a2f414491113107152ac7c", "assetId": "img:d3b719a7ed87d8d04797ee494947e602ddfac08d11a2f414491113107152ac7c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000042", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595509.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-16e341a9", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000051"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about metals.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which statement describes metallic bonding? A electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions B electrostatic attraction between the nuclei of two atoms and a pair of electrons shared between them C electrostatic attraction between positively charged particles and delocalised electrons D electrostatic attraction between atoms", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-16e341a9-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Aluminium is malleable and can be easily shaped to make saucepans used for cooking food. State two other properties of aluminium that make it suitable for saucepans used for cooking food.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-16e341a9-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Magnalium is an alloy of aluminium and magnesium. The diagram shows how the atoms are arranged in this alloy.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-16e341a9-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595538.png", "width": 1155, "format": "png", "height": 505, "sha256": "74d06916edadf71ec0c424f8f494a44d1cde4e77ff0b1216a25ba7d9936e6057", "assetId": "img:74d06916edadf71ec0c424f8f494a44d1cde4e77ff0b1216a25ba7d9936e6057", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000067", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595538.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what is meant by the term alloy.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-16e341a9-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why magnalium is harder than aluminium.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-16e341a9-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595538.png", "width": 1155, "format": "png", "height": 505, "sha256": "74d06916edadf71ec0c424f8f494a44d1cde4e77ff0b1216a25ba7d9936e6057", "assetId": "img:74d06916edadf71ec0c424f8f494a44d1cde4e77ff0b1216a25ba7d9936e6057", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000067", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595538.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-16e341a9", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "During the Second World War, engineers developed a rocket-powered aircraft. combustion chamber The aircraft carried these two liquids hydrazine, $ \\mathrm{N_{2} H_{4}} $ hydrogen peroxide, $ \\mathrm{H_{2}O_{2}} $ When these two liquids mix in the combustion chamber, they evaporate and then react rapidly to form nitrogen gas, $ \\mathrm{N}_{2} $ , and steam, $ \\mathrm{H}_{2}\\mathrm{O} $ The reaction is exothermic. The equation for the reaction is The displayed formulae for the reactants and products are", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The tables give the bond energies for the bonds broken in the reactants and the bonds made in the products.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-16e341a9-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the data in the tables to calculate the total amount of energy required to break all of the bonds in the reactants.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-16e341a9-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the data in the tables to calculate the total amount of energy released when all of the bonds in the products are made.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-16e341a9-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ , in kJ/mol, for the reaction Include a sign in your answer. $ \\Delta H= $ kJ/mol", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-16e341a9-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain, in terms of bonds broken and bonds made, why this reaction is exothermic.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-16e341a9-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw an energy level diagram for the reaction between $ \\mathrm{N_{2} H_{4}} $ and $ \\mathrm{H_{2}O_{2}} $", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-16e341a9-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595586.png", "width": 112, "format": "png", "height": 68, "sha256": "b54afd6eb07089822d235d0a5e944cddd1ecff9feff09ed52d090f07262c8c8a", "assetId": "img:b54afd6eb07089822d235d0a5e944cddd1ecff9feff09ed52d090f07262c8c8a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000092", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595586.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595548.png", "width": 1138, "format": "png", "height": 677, "sha256": "36ec955a002f1a5fd1bb068e66af96cf655a29907c6c6a0b757a6edc675886b2", "assetId": "img:36ec955a002f1a5fd1bb068e66af96cf655a29907c6c6a0b757a6edc675886b2", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000073", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595548.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069595555.png", "width": 1247, "format": "png", "height": 58, "sha256": "168ee7dd7df8178170ae04422ceb921a2fb5389af4c21fecbb1121ebd42d9a09", "assetId": "img:168ee7dd7df8178170ae04422ceb921a2fb5389af4c21fecbb1121ebd42d9a09", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000080", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069595555.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789069595560.png", "width": 1357, "format": "png", "height": 352, "sha256": "867213e01bd67c057603905b871bc0dddb74b2502214f5543df367790505741e", "assetId": "img:867213e01bd67c057603905b871bc0dddb74b2502214f5543df367790505741e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000082", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789069595560.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595586.png", "width": 112, "format": "png", "height": 68, "sha256": "b54afd6eb07089822d235d0a5e944cddd1ecff9feff09ed52d090f07262c8c8a", "assetId": "img:b54afd6eb07089822d235d0a5e944cddd1ecff9feff09ed52d090f07262c8c8a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000092", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595586.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q05-16e341a9", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000084", "e000085"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Some cars in Brazil use ethanol, $ \\mathrm{C_{2} H_{5} O H} $ , as a fuel instead of petrol. The ethanol is made by the fermentation of glucose which is obtained from sugar cane. The sugar is extracted from the sugar cane and then dissolved in water to make a sugar solution.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name the substance that is added to the sugar solution that causes glucose to ferment.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-16e341a9-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which temperature is the most suitable for fermentation? $\\begin{array}{l} \\square A 0 ^ {\\circ} C \\\\ \\square B 1 0 ^ {\\circ} C \\\\ \\square C 3 0 ^ {\\circ} C \\\\ \\square D 8 0 ^ {\\circ} C \\\\ \\end{array}$", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-16e341a9-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why fermentation is done in the absence of air.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-16e341a9-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what is meant by the term fuel.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-16e341a9-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for the complete combustion of ethanol in air.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-16e341a9-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Ethanol is also manufactured by reacting steam with ethene, $ \\mathrm{C_{2} H_{4}} $ The equation for this reaction is $\\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {5} \\mathrm {O H} (\\mathrm {g})$ State the conditions of temperature and pressure used in this process.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-16e341a9-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When ethanol is heated with acidified potassium dichromate(VI), it is oxidised to ethanoic acid.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-16e341a9-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the colour change that occurs in the potassium dichromate(VI) during this reaction.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-16e341a9-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The structural formula of ethanoic acid is $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C O O H} $ Draw the displayed formula of ethanoic acid.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-16e341a9-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the equation for the reaction of ethanoic acid with sodium. $\\mathrm {C H} _ {3} \\mathrm {C O O H} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\mathrm {N a} (\\mathrm {s}) \\rightarrow$", "label": "d-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-16e341a9-pd-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q06-16e341a9", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Dinitrogen tetraoxide, $ \\mathrm{N_{2}O_{4}} $ , is a colourless gas. Nitrogen dioxide, $ \\mathrm{N O}_{2} $ is a brown gas. The two gases can exist together in dynamic equilibrium according to the equation $\\mathrm {N} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm {N O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Explain what is meant by the term dynamic equilibrium.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-16e341a9-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Some $ \\mathrm{N_{2}O_{4}} $ and some $ \\mathrm{NO_{2}} $ are put into a sealed gas syringe and allowed to form an equilibrium mixture. This equilibrium mixture is brown.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-16e341a9-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595634.png", "width": 794, "format": "png", "height": 361, "sha256": "4130ba7d4151de678bb90de3bff9e8b3958da83b5f46c6f81ea94a6bad170912", "assetId": "img:4130ba7d4151de678bb90de3bff9e8b3958da83b5f46c6f81ea94a6bad170912", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000118", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595634.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The pressure of the gas in the syringe is increased by pushing in the piston. The mixture is then allowed to reach a new equilibrium at the same temperature as before. Explain why the new equilibrium mixture contains less $ \\mathrm{N O_{2}} $ than the original equilibrium mixture.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-16e341a9-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student suggests that the new equilibrium mixture would be lighter in colour than the original equilibrium mixture, as there is now less $ \\mathrm{N O_{2}} $ present. Suggest why the new equilibrium mixture is actually darker than the original.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-16e341a9-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Carbon monoxide, CO, and oxides of nitrogen are produced in a car engine when petrol is burned. These oxides can be partly removed by using a catalytic converter fitted to the car''s exhaust system.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-16e341a9-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State how oxides of nitrogen are produced in the car engine.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-16e341a9-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a disadvantage of allowing oxides of nitrogen to escape into the atmosphere.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-16e341a9-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write a chemical equation for the reaction between nitrogen monoxide, NO, and carbon monoxide to form carbon dioxide and nitrogen.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-16e341a9-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595634.png", "width": 794, "format": "png", "height": 361, "sha256": "4130ba7d4151de678bb90de3bff9e8b3958da83b5f46c6f81ea94a6bad170912", "assetId": "img:4130ba7d4151de678bb90de3bff9e8b3958da83b5f46c6f81ea94a6bad170912", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000118", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595634.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-16e341a9", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The concentration of NaClO(aq) in a solution of bleach is found by reacting it with hydrochloric acid. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {N a C l O} (\\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \\mathrm {H C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N a C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {I}) + \\mathrm {C l} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ An excess of dilute hydrochloric acid is added to $ 4. 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of bleach solution. $ 6 0. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of chlorine gas is produced.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Explain a safety precaution that should be taken when doing this experiment.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-16e341a9-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the amount, in moles, of chlorine gas produced. Assume one mole of chlorine gas occupies $ 2 4 0 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3}. $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-16e341a9-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Determine the amount, in moles, of $ \\mathrm{N a C l O} $ in $ 4. 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of bleach.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-16e341a9-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the concentration, in mol/dm $ ^{3} $ , of the bleach solution.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-16e341a9-pb-iii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595674.png", "width": 112, "format": "png", "height": 66, "sha256": "5d7602816769c0faaba3c2d8f672ae2502093fbb25cecb40fcc2e43d226c6473", "assetId": "img:5d7602816769c0faaba3c2d8f672ae2502093fbb25cecb40fcc2e43d226c6473", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000139", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595674.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595701.png", "width": 1916, "format": "png", "height": 2509, "sha256": "8b3df05f23f60c84d5cf858089bdbe8da9cf56220702aa7ec5e1d46b682b13e0", "assetId": "img:8b3df05f23f60c84d5cf858089bdbe8da9cf56220702aa7ec5e1d46b682b13e0", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000141", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595701.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595674.png", "width": 112, "format": "png", "height": 66, "sha256": "5d7602816769c0faaba3c2d8f672ae2502093fbb25cecb40fcc2e43d226c6473", "assetId": "img:5d7602816769c0faaba3c2d8f672ae2502093fbb25cecb40fcc2e43d226c6473", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000139", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595674.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069595701.png", "width": 1916, "format": "png", "height": 2509, "sha256": "8b3df05f23f60c84d5cf858089bdbe8da9cf56220702aa7ec5e1d46b682b13e0", "assetId": "img:8b3df05f23f60c84d5cf858089bdbe8da9cf56220702aa7ec5e1d46b682b13e0", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000141", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069595701.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q08-16e341a9", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 70, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 6, "2": 9, "3": 9, "4": 7, "7": 8, "8": 7}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "Summer 2019", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_2C_msc_20190822", "canonicalDocumentId": "c3ace227-3560-52cd-8427-ff529ed96620"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 3, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-c3ace227-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "1 mark for each correct answer"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 protons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 neutrons"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "原子模型", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-c3ace227-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Physical state at room temperature"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Colour"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW black"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW any combination of grey and black eg grey-black"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "chlorine"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "gas"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "pale green"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "bromine"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "liquid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "red-brown"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "iodine"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "solid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(dark) grey"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 (35×77.78)+(37×22.22)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR 3544.44"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 3544.44÷100 OR 35.4444 OR M1÷100"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 35.4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(35×0.7778)+(37×0.2222)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR 35.4444/35.444/35.44 with no working scores2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "35.4 with no working scores3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 can be ECF from an incorrect M2"}], "answerText": "Name of halogen", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3", "marks": 2, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-c3ace227-q3", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M1 the volume of liquid/alcohol"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW amount of liquid/alcohol"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE mass"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE volume of water"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW temperature of surroundings"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to temperature of the alcohol"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW alcohols/the liquids can be easily ignited"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW any named alcohol from the table"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(64+63+60)÷3"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW 62.3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "62/62.3 with no working scores 2"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW 69/69.25/69.3 for 1 mark"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An explanation including the following two points:"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 methanol/CH3OH (evaporates most easily)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 because the time taken is the shortest"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT because has lowest (mean) time"}], "answerText": "(a)\nM2 the temperature of the water\nalcohols/the liquids are flammable/catch fire easily\nM2=62", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-c3ace227-q4a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any two from the following:M1 good conductor of heat/thermal energyM2 does not react with food/affect flavour of foodM3 resistant to corrosionM4 high melting pointM5 low density/lightweight/strong"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE non-toxicALLOW does not corrode/rustIGNORE unreactive/inertIGNORE references to recyclingIGNORE light"}], "answerText": "C(electrostatic attraction between positively charged particles and delocalised electrons) is correct as it describes metallic bondingA is incorrect since it describes ionic bonding not metallic bondingB is incorrect since it describes covalent bonding not metallic bondingD is incorrect since it describes interatomic or intermolecular forces not metallic bonding", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(c)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-c3ace227-q4ci", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTa mixture of(two ormore)metalsALLOWcombination for mixtureREJECTcompound or references tochemical bonding"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An explanation that links together the following threepoints:"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWlattice/layers/rows of atoms are disrupted/distortedALLOWlattice/layers/rows of atoms less regular"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1the regular arrangement of atoms isdistorted/disruptedOWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWmagnesium and aluminium atoms are of different sizes"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2because magnesium atoms are larger than aluminium atoms"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWlayers cannot(as easily)slide over one another"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3and therefore it is more difficult for the layers toslide over one another"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNOREreferences to strength ofmetallic bonds"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "a mixture of(two or more)elements,one ofwhich isa metal", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(c)", "marks": 3, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-c3ace227-q5c", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE horizontal axis drawn"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE enthalpy change shown"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE activation energy shown"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If only use words reactants(on left) and products(on right) award 1 mark from M2 and M3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "energy\nhydrazine+hydrogen peroxide\nnitrogen+water\nM1 right hand line below left hand line\nM2 correct names/formulae of both reactants\nM3 correct names/formulae of both products", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-c3ace227-q6ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE zymase"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C(30℃) is correct as it is the most suitable temperature for fermentationA is incorrect as at 0℃ the enzymes would not be active so not the most suitable temperature for fermentationB is incorrect as at 10℃ the enzymes would not be very active so not the most suitable temperature for fermentationD is incorrect as at 80℃ the enzymes would be denatured so not the most suitable temperature for fermentation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An explanation using either of the following linked pairs:M1 oxygen in the air would react with ethanolM2 to form ethanoic acidORM1 the fermentation/reaction/respiration needs to be anaerobicM2 ethanol would not be formed/CO2andH2O would form"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT ethanol would be oxidisedALLOW to form carboxylic acidALLOW to form vinegar"}], "answerText": "yeast", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(c)", "marks": 2, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-c3ace227-q6c", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any value or range of values between250and350℃If no unit given assume it isCelsiusACCEPT equivalent temperatures in other units provided the unit is given"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M260-70atm"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any value or range of values between60and70atmIf no unit given assume it isatmACCEPT equivalent pressures in other units provided the unit is given"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(from) orange(to) green"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "CH3COONa+1/2H2"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE bond angles scores1mark"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1for both products correctM2for correctly balanced"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW NaCH3COOACCEPT multiplesM2DEP M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "M1(temperature)300℃", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-c3ace227-q7a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT both forward and backward reactions are taking place (at same time)"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE it is a reversible reaction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "rate of the forward reaction is equal to the rate of the backward reaction scores 2 marks"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT both forward and backward reactions occur at constant rate for M2"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW the concentrations of the reactants and products remains constant scores 1 mark independently of M1 but"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT concentrations of the reactants and products are equal/the same"}], "answerText": "An explanation that links together the following two points:\nM1 reaction is taking place in both directions (at same time)\nM2 at equal rate", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7(c)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-c3ace227-q7ci", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE nitrogen burns/combusts in oxygen"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE nitrogen is oxidised"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(they form) acid rain"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT references to respiratory problems"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW a specified harmful effect of acid rain"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW references to smog"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW references to greenhouse gases/global warming/climate change"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2NO+2CO→N2+2CO2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT multiples and fractions"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "nitrogen/N2reacts with oxygen/O2(both from the air)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "8(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 8, "entryId": "ms-c3ace227-q8a", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE chlorine is dangerous/harmful/irritant"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE laboratory coats"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 60÷24000"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 0.0025(mol)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "0.0025 with no working scores 2 marks"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT 0.003 for M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "0.0025 OR answer to M2 from(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(0.0025÷4.00)×1000"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Mark CSQ on(b)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 0.625(mol/dm3)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any number of sig fig except1(unless ECF answer is exactly1 sig fig correct answer with no working throughout(b) scores2 marks"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total"}], "answerText": "An explanation using either of the following linked pairs:\nM1 use a fume cupboard\nM2 because chlorine is toxic/poisonous\nOR\nM1 wear goggles/safety glasses/gloves\nM2 because acid/bleach (may be) irritant/corrosive", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}], "icTable": null, "warnings": ["1(a): bare integer cell \"13\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1(a): bare integer cell \"6\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "2(a): bare integer cell \"3\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "orphan row before first entry: (c)", "orphan row before first entry: M1 add chlorine (solution) to potassium bromide(solution)", "orphan row before first entry: M2 (solution) turns orange", "orphan row before first entry: M3 bromine/Br2is displaced", "orphan row before first entry: IGNORE state of bromineREJECT bromideIGNORE a displacement reaction occursM3 can be scored by Br2as a product in an equation", "orphan row before first entry: M4 (therefore) chlorine is more reactive(than bromine)", "orphan row before first entry: Total", "3: bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "3: bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "orphan row before first entry: (iii)", "orphan row before first entry: Total", "4(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "4(c)(i): bare integer cell \"3\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "4(c)(i): bare integer cell \"7\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "orphan row before first entry: 5(a)(i)(ii)(iii)", "orphan row before first entry: (bonds made)4649(kJ)", "orphan row before first entry: M1 subtraction of Σ(bonds made) made and Σ(bonds broken)", "orphan row before first entry: M2 correct evaluation of the calculation shown inM1IGNORE signM3If Σ(bonds made)＞Σ(bonds broken)final answer must be negativeIf Σ(bond