ll, "paperReference": "4CH1/2CR", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "628fade2-c759-5a6a-829f-473f8ca0c149"}, "warnings": [], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about gases in the atmosphere.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The box gives the names of some gases in the atmosphere. argon carbon dioxide helium nitrogen oxygen Use gases from the box to answer the questions. Each gas may be used once, more than once or not at all.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-628fade2-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the two noble gases.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-628fade2-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the gas that is a compound.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-628fade2-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the most abundant gas in the atmosphere.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-628fade2-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the greenhouse gas.", "label": "a-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-628fade2-pa-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe the test for oxygen.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-628fade2-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-628fade2", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram represents an atom of boron.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Use information from the diagram to complete the table. The first row has been done for you.", "label": "a", "marks": 5, "partId": "q02-628fade2-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_3_1789069581356.png", "width": 199, "format": "png", "height": 2506, "sha256": "22d2ad651bc91813171be8c3c2e5a614a5ce9aaaae92df2049b1b732ae96c1de", "assetId": "img:22d2ad651bc91813171be8c3c2e5a614a5ce9aaaae92df2049b1b732ae96c1de", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000041", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789069581356.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Boron has two isotopes, boron-10 and boron-11. A sample of boron contains 18.7% of boron-10 and 81.3% of boron-11. Calculate the relative atomic mass of this sample of boron.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-628fade2-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["relative atomic mass"]}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069581323.png", "width": 901, "format": "png", "height": 688, "sha256": "0961679cacdef1e61a7223aabd251e17d1dd8856e6952ff4e477e4d3a7594b24", "assetId": "img:0961679cacdef1e61a7223aabd251e17d1dd8856e6952ff4e477e4d3a7594b24", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000036", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069581323.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789069581356.png", "width": 199, "format": "png", "height": 2506, "sha256": "22d2ad651bc91813171be8c3c2e5a614a5ce9aaaae92df2049b1b732ae96c1de", "assetId": "img:22d2ad651bc91813171be8c3c2e5a614a5ce9aaaae92df2049b1b732ae96c1de", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000041", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789069581356.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-628fade2", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000040"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Crude oil is an important source of organic compounds.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows crude oil being separated into different fractions.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-628fade2-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069581385.png", "width": 1051, "format": "png", "height": 791, "sha256": "a3be92b7b88de1116a0e5df80d8e912ee5218b049e99a972e938fb04efe658bd", "assetId": "img:a3be92b7b88de1116a0e5df80d8e912ee5218b049e99a972e938fb04efe658bd", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000049", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069581385.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the process used to separate crude oil into different fractions.", "label": "a-i", "marks": 1, "partId": "q03-628fade2-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what happens to the crude oil at X.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": 1, "partId": "q03-628fade2-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe the differences between fraction B and fraction E. In your answer, refer to size of the molecules boiling point colour viscosity", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-628fade2-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Crude oil often contains sulfur as an impurity. Explain why this is a problem when using crude oil fractions as fuels.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-628fade2-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069581385.png", "width": 1051, "format": "png", "height": 791, "sha256": "a3be92b7b88de1116a0e5df80d8e912ee5218b049e99a972e938fb04efe658bd", "assetId": "img:a3be92b7b88de1116a0e5df80d8e912ee5218b049e99a972e938fb04efe658bd", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000049", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069581385.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-628fade2", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about the halogens and their compounds.", "marks": 15, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The table gives the colour and physical state at room temperature of the halogens. Complete the table by predicting the colour of astatine and the physical state of fluorine at room temperature.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-628fade2-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Chlorine gas is bubbled into a colourless solution of potassium bromide. Explain why the solution turns orange.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-628fade2-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Potassium bromide is an ionic compound. Draw diagrams to show the outer electrons in a potassium ion and in a bromide ion Include the charges on the ions.", "label": "c", "marks": 3, "partId": "q04-628fade2-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student sets up a circuit to test the electrical conductivity of water, solid sodium chloride and aqueous sodium chloride. The table shows the student''s results. Explain these results, with reference to the structure and bonding of the substances.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-628fade2-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A concentrated aqueous solution of sodium chloride is electrolysed using graphite electrodes. Chlorine is formed at the positive electrode (anode).", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-628fade2-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give an ionic half-equation for the formation of chlorine at the positive electrode.", "label": "e-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-628fade2-pe-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why this ionic half-equation represents an oxidation reaction.", "label": "e-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-628fade2-pe-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which substance is formed at the negative electrode (cathode)? A hydrogen B oxygen C sodium D water", "label": "e-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-628fade2-pe-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-628fade2", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000065", "e000070", "e000073"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about the reactions of carboxylic acids.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Carboxylic acids react with solutions of metal carbonates.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-628fade2-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the chemical equation for the reaction of ethanoic acid, $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C O O H} $ with potassium carbonate solution. $2 \\mathrm {C H} _ {3} \\mathrm {C O O H} + \\mathrm {K} _ {2} \\mathrm {C O} _ {3} \\rightarrow$", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-628fade2-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what you would see in this reaction.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-628fade2-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The ester, ethyl ethanoate, can be prepared by reacting ethanol with ethanoic acid. This is the method for the preparation. mix equal amounts of ethanoic acid and ethanol in a boiling tube add a few drops of concentrated sulfuric acid place the boiling tube in a hot water bath for several minutes", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-628fade2-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the role of concentrated sulfuric acid in this reaction.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-628fade2-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why the mixture is heated in a water bath rather than directly with a Bunsen burner flame.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-628fade2-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State how you would know that ethyl ethanoate has formed.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-628fade2-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Another ester, methyl propanoate, can be prepared by reacting methanol with propanoic acid.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-628fade2-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw the displayed formulae of methanol, propanoic acid and the ester, methyl propanoate. methyl propanoate", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-628fade2-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of the other product of this reaction.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-628fade2-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give one use of esters.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-628fade2-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-628fade2", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000099"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "When a bottle of wine is left open for several days, some of the ethanol in the wine turns to ethanoic acid, $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C O O H} $", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "A scientist uses a titration method to investigate how much ethanoic acid is formed if a bottle of white wine is left open for one week. She uses this method. fill a burette with the white wine and record the reading add $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution to a conical flask add a few drops of phenolphthalein indicator to the flask swirl the flask continuously while adding wine from the burette add the wine drop by drop near the end point record the reading at the end point", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the piece of apparatus that would be most suitable for measuring the $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why red wine would not be suitable to use for this investigation.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why she swirls the flask continuously.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why she adds the wine drop by drop near the end point.", "label": "a-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pa-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the burette readings at the start and end of one of the titrations. Use the readings to complete the table. Give your values to the nearest 0.05 $ cm^{3}. $", "label": "b", "marks": 3, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069581532.png", "width": 221, "format": "png", "height": 504, "sha256": "a0474725cdc8a3987a3ff9635d36fb13d413d4766cdf4d9e8266692dcaba5d0d", "assetId": "img:a0474725cdc8a3987a3ff9635d36fb13d413d4766cdf4d9e8266692dcaba5d0d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000118", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069581532.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069581547.png", "width": 221, "format": "png", "height": 450, "sha256": "f746a947f6ec7b8d81d5445a2822749ecd543e766739eeaf801105bccc1cda3a", "assetId": "img:f746a947f6ec7b8d81d5445a2822749ecd543e766739eeaf801105bccc1cda3a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000119", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069581547.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The scientist repeats the titration four more times. The table shows her results for these four titrations. Concordant results are those within $ 0. 2 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of each other.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Add ticks ( $ \\surd $ ) to the table to show the concordant results.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use your ticked results to calculate the mean (average) volume of wine added.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Another scientist repeats the titration with a different bottle of white wine that has been left open for a week. The equation for the reaction that occurs in this titration is $\\mathrm {C H} _ {3} \\mathrm {C O O H} + \\mathrm {N a O H} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C H} _ {3} \\mathrm {C O O N a} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ The mean volume of wine added is $ 1 9. 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3}. $", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution is $ 0. 0 5 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3}. $ Calculate the amount, in moles, of NaOH in $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution. $\\mathrm {a m o u n t o f N a O H} =$", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Deduce the amount, in moles, of $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C O O H} $ in $ 1 9. 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of the wine. $\\mathrm {a m o u n t o f C H _ {3} C O O H} =$", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the concentration, in mol/dm $ ^{3} $ , of $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C O O H} $ in the wine. concentration of $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C O O H}= $ $\\mathrm {m o l / d m ^ {3}}$ $(\\mathrm {T o t a l f o r Q u e s t i o n 6 = 1 5 m a r k s})$", "label": "d-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-628fade2-pd-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069581532.png", "width": 221, "format": "png", "height": 504, "sha256": "a0474725cdc8a3987a3ff9635d36fb13d413d4766cdf4d9e8266692dcaba5d0d", "assetId": "img:a0474725cdc8a3987a3ff9635d36fb13d413d4766cdf4d9e8266692dcaba5d0d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000118", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069581532.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069581547.png", "width": 221, "format": "png", "height": 450, "sha256": "f746a947f6ec7b8d81d5445a2822749ecd543e766739eeaf801105bccc1cda3a", "assetId": "img:f746a947f6ec7b8d81d5445a2822749ecd543e766739eeaf801105bccc1cda3a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000119", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069581547.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q06-628fade2", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000123", "e000126"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Hydrogen gas can be produced by reacting a mixture of methane and steam in the presence of a nickel catalyst. The reaction conditions are a temperature of $ 7 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ and a pressure of 5 atmospheres. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {C H} _ {4} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {C O} (\\mathrm {g}) + 3 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\quad \\Delta H = + 2 0 6 \\mathrm {k J / m o l}$", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "What does the symbol $ \\rightleftharpoons $ represent?", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-628fade2-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The mixture of methane and steam is heated to a temperature greater than $ 7 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ but the pressure is kept at 5 atmospheres. Predict the effect of this change on the yield of hydrogen at equilibrium, giving a reason for your answer.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-628fade2-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The mixture of methane and steam is kept at the same temperature of $ 7 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ but the pressure is increased to more than 5 atmospheres. Predict the effect of this change on the yield of hydrogen at equilibrium, giving a reason for your answer.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-628fade2-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the volume, in $ \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ of hydrogen gas at rtp that is produced when 10 tonnes of methane gas completely react with steam.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-628fade2-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q07-628fade2", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000152"]}], "paperTotal": 70, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 5, "2": 7, "3": 8, "4": 15, "5": 11}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "Summer 2019", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_2CR_msc_20190822", "canonicalDocumentId": "71e00b60-e568-55e2-b8c1-8b68f8f02787"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-71e00b60-q1", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "argon and helium"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW Ar and He"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "carbon dioxide"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW CO2If both name and formula given both must be correct"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "nitrogen"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW N2IGNORE N"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iv)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "carbon dioxide"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW CO2If both name and formula given both must be correct"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "relights a glowing spill/splint"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1Total 5"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 5, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-71e00b60-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1s22s22p1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Sum of masses multiplied by percentages"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Division by 100 to give final answer"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(18.7x10)+(81.3x11)OR1081.3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M210.8ORanswer fromM1divided by100"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT1080and1081"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT10.81and10.813"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct answer without working scores2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "11 without working scores0"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "11 with correct working scores1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total7"}], "answerText": "atomic number5\nmass number11\nnumber of neutrons6\ngroup in the Periodic table that contains boron3\nperiod in the Periodic table that contains boron2\nelectronic configuration of an atom of boron2,3", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-71e00b60-q3", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "fractional distillation"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW fractionating/fractionation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "crude oil/it is heated/vapourised"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW boiled"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE evaporates"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A description which refers to the following four points"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT reverse arguments for E"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 B contains larger/longer molecules"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 B has a higher boiling point"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW E is more volatile"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE melting point"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 B has a darker colour"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW arison giving specific colours e.g B is orange and E is pale yellow"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4 B is more viscous/has greater viscosity"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW E is more runny"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "MAX 2 marks if no reference to fractions E or B in the answer?"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An planation which links the following two points"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 (when sulfur burns) sulfur dioxide/SO2 is formed"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW sulfur trioxide/SO3"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE sulfur oxides"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 causes acid rain"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW a specified harmful effect of acid rain"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT references to causing/acerbating respiratory problems"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW greenhouse gas/causes global warming/imate change"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 8"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-71e00b60-q4a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTvery dark grey"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An planation linking the following two pointsM1bromine/Br2is formed/displaced/producedM2as chlorine is more reactive(than bromine)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECTbromide for bromineACCEPTbromine/Br2shown as the productin an equationIGNOREstate of bromineREJECTbromide/chloride"}], "answerText": "M1fluorine- gasM2astatine- black", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(c)", "marks": 3, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-71e00b60-q4c", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any combination of dots and crosses."}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE inner shells even if incorrect"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 correct structure of bromide ion"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Br-"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 charges on both ions correct (with or without square brackets)."}], "answerText": "M1 correct structure of potassium ion\nK+", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(d)", "marks": 5, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-71e00b60-q4d", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWwater is a covalent ound"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPTwater does not contain any free ions/electrons or delocalised electrons"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWsodium chloride is an ionic ound/contains ions"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECTmention of atoms/molecules/intermolecular forces in Na forM3only"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4subsumesM3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECTelectrons being unable to move forM4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECTreference to electrons conducting electricity in aqueous sodium chloride forM5"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNOREreference to ions carrying charge/current"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(e)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2-→2+2e(-)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "electrons are lost(by chloride ions/-)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW2-2e→2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPToxidation number of chlorine increases(by1)/changes from-1to0"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECTchlorine loses electrons"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNOREreferences to gain of oxygen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1"}], "answerText": "An planation linking the following five points\nM1water is covalently bonded/has a simple molecular structure\nM2water does not contain any free (moving) charged parti es(so does not conduct electricity)\nM3sodium chloride has a giant ionic structure/has an ionic lattice structure/is ionically bonded\nM4the ions are in fixed positions/cannot move(so does not conduct electricity)\nM5in solution/aqueous sodium chloride the ions are free to flow/move(so the solution does conduct electricity)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5", "marks": 2, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-71e00b60-q5", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "$2\\text{CH}_{3}\\text{COOH}+\\text{K}_{2}\\text{CO}_{3}\\rightarrow 2\\text{CH}_{3}\\text{COOK}+\\text{CO}_{2}+\\text{H}_{2}\\text{O}$"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW multiples"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 2CH_{3}\\text{COOK}$"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT 2CH_{3}\\text{COO}^{-}\\text{K}^{+}$"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 CO_{2}+\\text{H}_{2}\\text{O}$"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW 2KCH_{3}\\text{COO}$"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii) effervescence / fizzing / bubbles"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If M1 not awarded any numbers before CO_{2}+\\text{H}_{2}\\text{O} can be ignored and M2 can be awarded."}, {"kind": "note", "text": "For both marks to be awarded the equation must be correctly balanced"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(acts as a) catalyst"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE carbon dioxide/gas given off/evolved/formed /produced"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii) ethanol is flammable / might catch fire / might ignite"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT ethyl ethanoate /the mixture /it is flammable /might catch fire /might ignite"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii) ester has) sweet / fruity / distinctive smell"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW liquid (ester) floats on top of mixture OWTTE"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(c)(i)", "marks": 3, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-71e00b60-q5ci", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Penalise missing bond between O and H once only"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If incorrect number of carbon atoms in alcohol and or acid allow ECF for structure of ester formed from their alcohol and acid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT H2O"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "food flavourings/perfumes"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any correct use"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "e.g. in cosmetics/making soaps/making detergents/solvents"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(for paints/varnishes)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 11"}], "answerText": "H\nH-C-O-H\nH\nH-C-C-C\nH H O-H\nwater", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6", "marks": 1, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-71e00b60-q6", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "pipette"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "red wine would mask the colour of the indicator / difficult to see colour change (at end point)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT indicator and red wine are a similar colour OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "to mix the contents (of the flask so that they can react) OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT to ensure the colour change is permanent OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW to speed up the reaction/ to ensure late reaction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iv)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "so as not to add more wine than is needed (for late reaction)/ so as not to overshoot the end point OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT to find the act/precise point of neutralisation"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE to obtain an accurate reading"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "MAX2 if final and initial burette readings are reversed."}, {"kind": "note", "text": "MAX2 if readings not given to 2 decimal places."}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW ECF for M3 on correct subtraction of M1-M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "final burette reading in cm3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "22.70"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "initial burette reading in cm3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2.15"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "volume of wine added in cm3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "20.55"}], "answerText": "(a)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7", "marks": 1, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-71e00b60-q7", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "reversible reaction"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to equilibrium"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 yield increases"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT more hydrogen produced"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to Le Chatelier e.g. an increase in temperature favours the forward reaction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 yield decreases"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT less hydrogen produced"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW parti es"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT atoms"}], "answerText": "(a)\nALLOW the reaction goes both ways\nALLOW the reaction can go forwards and backwards\nM2(equilibrium shifts to the right as the forward) reaction is endothermic\nM2 dep on M1 correct or missing\nM2(equilibrium shifts to the left as) fewer moles/molecules(of gas) on lhs/more moles/molecules(of gas) on rhs OWTTE\nIGNORE references to Le Chatelier e.g. an increase in pressure favours the side with fewer moles\nM2 dep on M1 correct or missing", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7(c)", "marks": 4, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-71e00b60-q7c", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Mark consequentially forM2,M3andM4."}, {"kind": "note", "text": "45,000,000 without working scores 3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct answer in standard form without working scores 4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Common answers4.5 x 104(3)45,000(2)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "4.5 x 101(3)45(2)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1.5 x 107(3)15,000,000(2)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "NOTE even if working is incorrect e.g. division by 24 instead of multiplicationM4can still be awarded for correct conversion to standard form"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 9"}], "answerText": "calculate the amount, in moles, of metha