e-d4c9-53bb-853c-ff85f12faff9"}, "warnings": ["unclassified heading: TOTAL FOR PAPER = 70 MARKS"], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about elements, compounds and mixtures.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name the element that burns with a lilac flame.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-b1b5d37e-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the technique used to separate the mixture of colours in black ink.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-b1b5d37e-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The box gives the names of some substances. Choose substances from the box to answer these questions.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-b1b5d37e-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the compound.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-b1b5d37e-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the mixture.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-b1b5d37e-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the non-metal element that is a solid at room temperature.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-b1b5d37e-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-b1b5d37e", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Crude oil is a mixture of hydrocarbons.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name the process used to separate crude oil into fractions.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-b1b5d37e-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give one use of the kerosene fraction.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-b1b5d37e-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "One of the hydrocarbons in the refinery gas fraction is an alkane with the structural formula $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C H_{2} C H_{2} C H_{3}} $", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-b1b5d37e-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of this alkane.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-b1b5d37e-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the relative molecular mass $ ( M_{\\mathrm{r}}) $ of this alkane.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-b1b5d37e-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "One of the alkanes in the gasoline fraction has the displayed formula", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-b1b5d37e-pd", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064136615.png", "width": 553, "format": "png", "height": 229, "sha256": "a98d85b04820ef9d75dcd67e77bab5dcc4d566a681364e75c4bdbd6e1a04fc72", "assetId": "img:a98d85b04820ef9d75dcd67e77bab5dcc4d566a681364e75c4bdbd6e1a04fc72", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000039", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064136615.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Determine the molecular formula of this alkane.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-b1b5d37e-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the general formula for the alkanes.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-b1b5d37e-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Catalytic cracking is used to convert long-chain alkanes into shorter-chain alkanes.", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-b1b5d37e-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the catalyst used in catalytic cracking.", "label": "e-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-b1b5d37e-pe-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why it is necessary to convert long-chain alkanes into shorter-chain alkanes.", "label": "e-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-b1b5d37e-pe-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Catalytic cracking also produces alkenes. $ \\mathrm{C}_{1 1} \\mathrm{H}_{2 4} $ can undergo cracking to give pentane $ \\left( \\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{1 2} \\right) $ and two different alkenes. Complete the equation for this cracking reaction. $\\mathrm {C} _ {1 1} \\mathrm {H} _ {2 4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C} _ {5} \\mathrm {H} _ {1 2} +$", "label": "f", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-b1b5d37e-pf", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064136615.png", "width": 553, "format": "png", "height": 229, "sha256": "a98d85b04820ef9d75dcd67e77bab5dcc4d566a681364e75c4bdbd6e1a04fc72", "assetId": "img:a98d85b04820ef9d75dcd67e77bab5dcc4d566a681364e75c4bdbd6e1a04fc72", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000039", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064136615.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-b1b5d37e", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about copper and its compounds.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Copper is a metal used for electrical wiring. Explain why copper is a good conductor of electricity.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-b1b5d37e-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "This apparatus is used to investigate the electrolysis of copper(II) sulfate solution with graphite electrodes. Copper forms at the negative electrode and oxygen forms at the positive electrode.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-b1b5d37e-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064136643.png", "width": 1504, "format": "png", "height": 570, "sha256": "cb647935c190ebabd8b62e186a62fdd006feb2834ec3cf219270b454bd7e3714", "assetId": "img:cb647935c190ebabd8b62e186a62fdd006feb2834ec3cf219270b454bd7e3714", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000054", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064136643.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what would be observed at each electrode. negative electrode positive electrode ..", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-b1b5d37e-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The ionic half-equation for the reaction at the negative electrode is $\\mathrm {C u} ^ {2 +} + 2 \\mathrm {e} ^ {-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C u}$ State why this is a reduction reaction.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-b1b5d37e-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why the copper(II) sulfate solution becomes paler blue during the electrolysis. (2)", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-b1b5d37e-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When hydrated copper(II) sulfate crystals are heated, anhydrous copper(II) sulfate forms. A mass of 12.5 g of hydrated copper(II) sulfate crystals is heated in a crucible until all the water of crystallisation is removed. A mass of 8.0 g of anhydrous copper(II) sulfate forms. Show by calculation that the formula of hydrated copper(II) sulfate is $ \\mathrm{C u S O_{4}. 5 H_{2}O} $ $[ M _ {\\mathrm {r}} \\mathrm {o f C u S O} _ {4} = 1 5 9. 5 \\quad M _ {\\mathrm {r}} \\mathrm {o f H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} = 1 8 ]$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-b1b5d37e-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064136643.png", "width": 1504, "format": "png", "height": 570, "sha256": "cb647935c190ebabd8b62e186a62fdd006feb2834ec3cf219270b454bd7e3714", "assetId": "img:cb647935c190ebabd8b62e186a62fdd006feb2834ec3cf219270b454bd7e3714", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000054", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064136643.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q03-b1b5d37e", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student investigates the reaction between sodium hydroxide solution and dilute sulfuric acid. He does a titration to find the concentration of the sulfuric acid. This is his plan for the titration. There are some mistakes and omissions in his plan. rinse a conical flask with the sodium hydroxide solution use a measuring cylinder to measure out $ 2 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of the sodium hydroxide solution and add it to the conical flask add a few drops of methyl orange indicator to the conical flask rinse a burette with water and then fill it with the sulfuric acid add the acid from the burette to the conical flask until the indicator changes colour at the end-point of the titration record the final burette reading", "marks": 9, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Give the colour change of the methyl orange indicator at the end-point.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-b1b5d37e-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe four changes that the student could make to improve his plan. He finds that $ 1 6. 7 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of the dilute sulfuric acid neutralises $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution of concentration $ 0. 2 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-b1b5d37e-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student then does the titration correctly. The equation for the reaction is $2 \\mathrm {N a O H} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ Calculate the concentration, in mol/dm $ ^{3} $ , of the sulfuric acid. $\\mathrm {m o l / d m ^ {3}}$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-b1b5d37e-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["concentration of sulfuric acid"]}], "number": 4, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-b1b5d37e", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Oxygen can be prepared from hydrogen peroxide using a catalyst.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which is a correct statement about oxygen? A it burns with a squeaky pop B it relights a glowing splint C it turns blue litmus red D it turns limewater milky", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b1b5d37e-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain how a catalyst increases the rate of a reaction.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b1b5d37e-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the preparation of oxygen from hydrogen peroxide is $2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} + \\mathrm {O} _ {2}$ This equation can also be written using displayed formulae to show all the covalent bonds in the molecules. $2 H - O - O - H \\rightarrow 2 H - O - H + O = O$ The table gives the bond energies for these bonds.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b1b5d37e-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the values in the table to calculate the enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ , for the reaction. Include a sign in your answer.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b1b5d37e-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the energy level diagram to show the position of the products and the enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ , for the reaction.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-b1b5d37e-pc-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064136708.png", "width": 429, "format": "png", "height": 880, "sha256": "08c234997e6d817e4e4ac6b9a50012adda8294e14ed7da99bdb82423089ba24e", "assetId": "img:08c234997e6d817e4e4ac6b9a50012adda8294e14ed7da99bdb82423089ba24e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000100", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064136708.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064136708.png", "width": 429, "format": "png", "height": 880, "sha256": "08c234997e6d817e4e4ac6b9a50012adda8294e14ed7da99bdb82423089ba24e", "assetId": "img:08c234997e6d817e4e4ac6b9a50012adda8294e14ed7da99bdb82423089ba24e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000100", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064136708.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q05-b1b5d37e", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000097"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Ethanol, $ \\mathrm{C_{2} H_{5} O H} $ , can be manufactured from ethene and steam using a phosphoric acid catalyst. (a) (i) State the temperature and pressure used in this manufacturing process. temperature pressure ... (ii) Draw the displayed formula of ethanol.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Ethanol burns in a plentiful supply of air to form carbon dioxide and water.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b1b5d37e-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b1b5d37e-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When the air supply is limited, incomplete combustion occurs and carbon monoxide forms. State why carbon monoxide is poisonous to humans.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b1b5d37e-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When ethanol reacts with ethanoic acid, an ester forms. Give the name of this ester.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b1b5d37e-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Butanedioic acid and ethanediol react together to form a polyester and water.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b1b5d37e-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of this type of polymerisation.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b1b5d37e-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the equation. Show only one repeat unit of the polyester.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-b1b5d37e-pd-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064136728.png", "width": 1630, "format": "png", "height": 428, "sha256": "ef6bb14460c3db0f47b36fe8b0f64119d141463e0c49c3c47fa7817debfb0f62", "assetId": "img:ef6bb14460c3db0f47b36fe8b0f64119d141463e0c49c3c47fa7817debfb0f62", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000112", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064136728.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064136728.png", "width": 1630, "format": "png", "height": 428, "sha256": "ef6bb14460c3db0f47b36fe8b0f64119d141463e0c49c3c47fa7817debfb0f62", "assetId": "img:ef6bb14460c3db0f47b36fe8b0f64119d141463e0c49c3c47fa7817debfb0f62", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000112", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064136728.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-b1b5d37e", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about some Group 2 elements and their compounds.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Calcium reacts with water to produce calcium hydroxide and hydrogen gas.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b1b5d37e-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the word equation for this reaction.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b1b5d37e-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State two observations that would be made during this reaction.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b1b5d37e-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe how a pure, dry sample of the insoluble salt, barium sulfate, could be made from the two solids sodium sulfate and barium chloride.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b1b5d37e-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give an ionic equation for the reaction that occurs. Include state symbols in your equation.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b1b5d37e-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When magnesium nitrate is heated, magnesium oxide, nitrogen dioxide and oxygen form. The equation for the reaction is $2 \\mathrm {M g} \\left(\\mathrm {N O} _ {3}\\right) _ {2} (\\mathrm {s}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {M g O} (\\mathrm {s}) + 4 \\mathrm {N O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b1b5d37e-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "What is the name for this type of reaction? A addition B combustion decomposition D neutralisation", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b1b5d37e-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the total volume, in $ \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ of gas produced at rtp when 7.7 g of magnesium nitrate completely reacts. [Assume that the molar volume of a gas at rtp is $ 2 4 \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $] [ $ M_{\\mathrm{r}} $ of $ \\mathrm{M g(N O_{3})_{2}}=1 4 8 $] Give your answer to two significant figures.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-b1b5d37e-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q07-b1b5d37e", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000132"]}], "paperTotal": 70, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 5, "2": 11, "4": 9, "6": 11}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "January 2020", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_2C_msc_20200305", "canonicalDocumentId": "520f4405-753d-5882-876e-e0f577befa8b"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-520f4405-q3a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M1 electrons are delocalised"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE sea of electrons/free electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(electrons) can move/can flow/are mobile"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT cations/atoms move for both marksM2 dep on M1 or mention of electrons i.e.‘electrons move’ scores 1 mark"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 brown/pink/pink-brown solid formed"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT brown/pink coating/deposit on the electrodeALLOW red-brownREJECT orangeREJECT precipitate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 bubbles/fizzing/effervescence"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW 1 mark if both observations correct but at incorrect electrodesIGNORE gas produced/evolved/releasedIGNORE name of gas"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "copper ion(s)/Cu2+ gains electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT oxidation state of copper goes down/ goes from +2 to 0IGNORE references to loss of oxygenALLOW electrons are gainedREJECT copper/Cu gains electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An explanation that links the following two points"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 the (blue) colour is caused by copper ions/Cu2+M2 copper ions/Cu2+ are being discharged/removed from the solution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT concentration of copper ions/Cu2+ decreasesALLOW copper ions/Cu2+ form copper"}], "answerText": "An explanation that links the following two points", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(c)", "marks": 4, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-520f4405-q3c", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT alternative methods which show that the answer is 5"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 11"}], "answerText": "calculate the mass of water removedcalculate the amount in moles ofCuSO4calculate the amount in moles of waterdivide amount ofH2O by amount ofCuSO4Example calculationM1(12.5-8.0)=4.5(g)M2n(CuSO4)=8.0÷159.5=0.05(0)(mol)M3n(H2O)=4.5÷18=0.25(mol)M40.25÷0.05(0)(=5)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [{"ecf": false, "text": "calculate the mass of water removedcalculate the amount in moles ofCuSO4calculate the amount in moles of waterdivide amount ofH2O by amount ofCuSO4Example calculationM1(12.5-8.0)=4.5(g)M2n(CuSO4)=8.0÷159.