517cf287-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114223.png", "width": 253, "format": "png", "height": 241, "sha256": "b3d5552d25534a11ee69c5da1e8a3f35f25c78339027b12fae28ae115f396c92", "assetId": "img:b3d5552d25534a11ee69c5da1e8a3f35f25c78339027b12fae28ae115f396c92", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000028", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114223.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789064114227.png", "width": 249, "format": "png", "height": 241, "sha256": "6ade0d70b5dd5fe09697f643faf7cdf206a5204e62dbc6902de79aba44f7fc90", "assetId": "img:6ade0d70b5dd5fe09697f643faf7cdf206a5204e62dbc6902de79aba44f7fc90", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000030", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789064114227.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789064114246.png", "width": 248, "format": "png", "height": 242, "sha256": "6b4ca08dddf314d4ab53009573b706621748338e4f71b6ed345f8d7b78ed4dc2", "assetId": "img:6b4ca08dddf314d4ab53009573b706621748338e4f71b6ed345f8d7b78ed4dc2", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000032", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789064114246.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_4_1789064114249.png", "width": 242, "format": "png", "height": 240, "sha256": "5d4ab3987ade792dbbad84782089dad7dc8b8d5cacabd615c06f4bcc589b8fee", "assetId": "img:5d4ab3987ade792dbbad84782089dad7dc8b8d5cacabd615c06f4bcc589b8fee", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000034", "sourceName": "assets/crop_4_1789064114249.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which box represents a mixture? A 1 B 2 C 3 D 4", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-517cf287-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which two boxes represent elements? A 1 and 2 B 2 and 3 C 1 and 3 D 3 and 4", "label": "a-ii", "marks": 1, "partId": "q01-517cf287-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why Box 5 represents a compound.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-517cf287-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The Periodic Table contains all the known elements.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-517cf287-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "How are the elements arranged in the Periodic Table? A increasing mass number B increasing number of neutrons C increasing number of protons", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-517cf287-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Elements in the same group have the same number of D increasing reactivity A electrons in the outer shell B electron shells neutrons D protons", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-517cf287-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114223.png", "width": 253, "format": "png", "height": 241, "sha256": "b3d5552d25534a11ee69c5da1e8a3f35f25c78339027b12fae28ae115f396c92", "assetId": "img:b3d5552d25534a11ee69c5da1e8a3f35f25c78339027b12fae28ae115f396c92", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000028", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114223.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789064114227.png", "width": 249, "format": "png", "height": 241, "sha256": "6ade0d70b5dd5fe09697f643faf7cdf206a5204e62dbc6902de79aba44f7fc90", "assetId": "img:6ade0d70b5dd5fe09697f643faf7cdf206a5204e62dbc6902de79aba44f7fc90", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000030", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789064114227.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789064114246.png", "width": 248, "format": "png", "height": 242, "sha256": "6b4ca08dddf314d4ab53009573b706621748338e4f71b6ed345f8d7b78ed4dc2", "assetId": "img:6b4ca08dddf314d4ab53009573b706621748338e4f71b6ed345f8d7b78ed4dc2", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000032", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789064114246.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_4_1789064114249.png", "width": 242, "format": "png", "height": 240, "sha256": "5d4ab3987ade792dbbad84782089dad7dc8b8d5cacabd615c06f4bcc589b8fee", "assetId": "img:5d4ab3987ade792dbbad84782089dad7dc8b8d5cacabd615c06f4bcc589b8fee", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000034", "sourceName": "assets/crop_4_1789064114249.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-517cf287", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Chromatography is used to analyse mixtures. She places four known dyes, A, B, C and D, and the green food colouring on chromatography paper. The diagram shows the student''s apparatus at the start of her experiment. A student does a chromatography experiment to analyse the composition of green food colouring in sweets.", "marks": 9, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows that the student makes two mistakes when setting up her apparatus. State the two changes that the student should make so that her experiment works.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-517cf287-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Another student does the chromatography experiment correctly. The diagram shows her chromatogram at the end of the experiment.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-517cf287-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114284.png", "width": 725, "format": "png", "height": 1042, "sha256": "bcd63be0365033d57eba0d94da0c116439a32c91fc667a9901bec3bc24473ee5", "assetId": "img:bcd63be0365033d57eba0d94da0c116439a32c91fc667a9901bec3bc24473ee5", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000069", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114284.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain what the chromatogram shows about the composition of the green food colouring.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-517cf287-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The distance between the start line and the spot for dye C is 6.2 cm. Calculate the $ R_{f} $ value of dye C.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-517cf287-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["$ R_{\\mathrm{f}} $ value"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why dye A does not move.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-517cf287-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114271.png", "width": 1154, "format": "png", "height": 864, "sha256": "dfeb477801e8d2e534e2ffcc9a2d6714f926151010a8d48bc10baeb620e7b974", "assetId": "img:dfeb477801e8d2e534e2ffcc9a2d6714f926151010a8d48bc10baeb620e7b974", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000065", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114271.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114284.png", "width": 725, "format": "png", "height": 1042, "sha256": "bcd63be0365033d57eba0d94da0c116439a32c91fc667a9901bec3bc24473ee5", "assetId": "img:bcd63be0365033d57eba0d94da0c116439a32c91fc667a9901bec3bc24473ee5", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000069", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114284.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-517cf287", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Solutions of silver nitrate and potassium chloride react together to make the insoluble salt, silver chloride. Step 1 add $ 2 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of silver nitrate solution to a conical flask Step 2 add potassium chloride solution to the flask Step 3 filter off the silver chloride A student uses this method to prepare a sample of silver chloride.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "What term is used for this reaction? neutralisation B precipitation C redox D thermal decomposition", "label": "a", "marks": 1, "partId": "q03-517cf287-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give two more steps that will produce a pure, dry sample of silver chloride. Step 4. Step 5.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-517cf287-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Acidified silver nitrate solution is used to test for chloride ions. Give a reason why hydrochloric acid is not used to acidify silver nitrate solution.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-517cf287-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The chemical equation for the reaction between solutions of silver nitrate and potassium chloride is A student adds an excess of potassium chloride solution to $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of $ 0. 1 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ silver nitrate solution. $\\mathrm {A g N O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {K C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {A g C l} (\\mathrm {s}) + \\mathrm {K N O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q})$ Calculate the maximum mass of silver chloride, in grams, that can be produced. [M of AgCl =143.5]", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-517cf287-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-517cf287", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about the metal, lead.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why metals, such as lead, are malleable.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-517cf287-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A teacher uses this apparatus in a fume cupboard to demonstrate the electrolysis of lead(II) bromide. The lead(II) bromide is heated until it melts. When the lead(II) bromide melts, the lamp lights. One of the products of this electrolysis is lead.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-517cf287-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114321.png", "width": 1163, "format": "png", "height": 795, "sha256": "8816e5ba31bc7c34d9cff8e1c050aa23cb0111492a05568313117dd3b524c906", "assetId": "img:8816e5ba31bc7c34d9cff8e1c050aa23cb0111492a05568313117dd3b524c906", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000100", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114321.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why solid lead(II) bromide does not conduct electricity.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-517cf287-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Bromine is formed by the oxidation of bromide ions at the positive electrode. Complete the ionic half-equation for the oxidation of bromide ions. $2 \\mathrm {B r} ^ {-} \\rightarrow + \\dots \\dots$", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-517cf287-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why lead metal forms at the negative electrode.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-517cf287-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The teacher stops heating the mixture and allows it to solidify. Suggest why the lamp stays alight.", "label": "b-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-517cf287-pb-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114321.png", "width": 1163, "format": "png", "height": 795, "sha256": "8816e5ba31bc7c34d9cff8e1c050aa23cb0111492a05568313117dd3b524c906", "assetId": "img:8816e5ba31bc7c34d9cff8e1c050aa23cb0111492a05568313117dd3b524c906", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000100", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114321.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-517cf287", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about Group 1 metals and their reactions.", "marks": 13, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "When lithium is added to water, bubbles of hydrogen gas are observed.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-517cf287-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give two other observations that could be made.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-517cf287-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the test for hydrogen gas.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-517cf287-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give one observation that would be different if potassium is used instead of lithium.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-517cf287-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram represents an atom of lithium and an atom of potassium. Explain why potassium is more reactive than lithium.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": 3, "partId": "q05-517cf287-pb-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114347.png", "width": 403, "format": "png", "height": 382, "sha256": "b11c3a1f751a7191bce53227e629a2469dacf909fa7ac911d60a2ba0ec17ebd2", "assetId": "img:b11c3a1f751a7191bce53227e629a2469dacf909fa7ac911d60a2ba0ec17ebd2", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000117", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114347.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789064114360.png", "width": 663, "format": "png", "height": 617, "sha256": "55b81d82693170de93bdf8d07ff172b58c784030032fa58622cc37e8954532b9", "assetId": "img:55b81d82693170de93bdf8d07ff172b58c784030032fa58622cc37e8954532b9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000118", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789064114360.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the reaction between lithium and water is $2 \\mathrm {L i} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {I}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {L i O H} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-517cf287-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A mass of 0.500g of lithium reacts with an excess of water. Calculate the volume, in $ cm^{3} $ , of hydrogen gas produced at rtp. [molar volume of a gas at rtp=24000 $ cm^{3} $] Give your answer to three significant figures.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-517cf287-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In a reaction between lithium and water, $ 1 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of lithium hydroxide solution is formed. The lithium hydroxide solution is then completely neutralised by $ 2 4. 8 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of $ 0. 1 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l / d m}^{3} $ sulfuric acid. The equation for the neutralisation is $2 \\mathrm {L i O H} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {L i} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {I})$ Calculate the concentration, in mol/dm $ ^{3} $ , of the lithium hydroxide solution.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-517cf287-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114347.png", "width": 403, "format": "png", "height": 382, "sha256": "b11c3a1f751a7191bce53227e629a2469dacf909fa7ac911d60a2ba0ec17ebd2", "assetId": "img:b11c3a1f751a7191bce53227e629a2469dacf909fa7ac911d60a2ba0ec17ebd2", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000117", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114347.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789064114360.png", "width": 663, "format": "png", "height": 617, "sha256": "55b81d82693170de93bdf8d07ff172b58c784030032fa58622cc37e8954532b9", "assetId": "img:55b81d82693170de93bdf8d07ff172b58c784030032fa58622cc37e8954532b9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000118", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789064114360.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-517cf287", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about ethane and ethene.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Ethane can be obtained from crude oil. Describe the industrial process used to separate crude oil into fractions.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-517cf287-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the reaction between ethene gas and hydrogen gas is $\\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {6}$ The rate of this reaction can be increased by increasing the pressure.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-517cf287-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why increasing the pressure increases the rate of this reaction.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-517cf287-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The rate of this reaction can also be increased by using a catalyst. Explain how using a catalyst increases the rate of a reaction.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-517cf287-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give one other way that the rate of reaction between ethene gas and hydrogen gas can be increased.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-517cf287-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The reaction between ethene and hydrogen is exothermic. Complete the reaction profile diagram, including labels for the activation energy and the enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H. $", "label": "b-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-517cf287-pb-iv", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114396.png", "width": 971, "format": "png", "height": 658, "sha256": "6c6c36a6cfec24ebee6d146269703fcb397d8b25786b653a8ac5a546fdbc181b", "assetId": "img:6c6c36a6cfec24ebee6d146269703fcb397d8b25786b653a8ac5a546fdbc181b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000145", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114396.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The reaction between ethene and hydrogen can be represented using displayed formulae. Use the bond energies in the table to calculate the enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ in kJ/mol for this reaction.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-517cf287-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114404.png", "width": 799, "format": "png", "height": 214, "sha256": "3250e586b81dd97c95eb3d5985f1a96d02056df7d360d20c5b4ca44469f3ed2f", "assetId": "img:3250e586b81dd97c95eb3d5985f1a96d02056df7d360d20c5b4ca44469f3ed2f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000147", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114404.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114396.png", "width": 971, "format": "png", "height": 658, "sha256": "6c6c36a6cfec24ebee6d146269703fcb397d8b25786b653a8ac5a546fdbc181b", "assetId": "img:6c6c36a6cfec24ebee6d146269703fcb397d8b25786b653a8ac5a546fdbc181b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000145", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114396.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114404.png", "width": 799, "format": "png", "height": 214, "sha256": "3250e586b81dd97c95eb3d5985f1a96d02056df7d360d20c5b4ca44469f3ed2f", "assetId": "img:3250e586b81dd97c95eb3d5985f1a96d02056df7d360d20c5b4ca44469f3ed2f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000147", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114404.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q06-517cf287", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000148"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) Ethanol, $ \\mathrm{C_{2} H_{5} O H} $ , can be produced by the fermentation of glucose, $ \\mathrm{C_{6} H_{1 2} O_{6}} $ (i) Complete the equation for the fermentation of glucose. $\\mathrm {C} _ {6} \\mathrm {H} _ {1 2} \\mathrm {O} _ {6} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {5} \\mathrm {O H} + 2$ (ii) State why it is necessary for fermentation to be done in the absence of air. (iii) Explain why the temperature should not be higher than $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ (iv) When 4 mol of glucose is fermented, a mass of 55.2 g of ethanol is produced. Show that the percentage yield of ethanol is 15%. [M $ _{r} $ of C $ _{2} $ H $ _{5} $ OH=46]", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Ethanol can also be produced by the reaction between ethene and steam. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {5} \\mathrm {O H} (\\mathrm {g})$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-517cf287-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "This reaction is in dynamic equilibrium. Give two features of a reaction in dynamic equilibrium.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-517cf287-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When the equilibrium mixture is heated,the yield of ethanol decreases Explain whether the forward reaction is exothermic or endothermic.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-517cf287-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Carboxylic acids react with alcohols to form esters. The displayed formula of an ester is", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-517cf287-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114464.png", "width": 606, "format": "png", "height": 295, "sha256": "abdd86ad5f2e92068a4b5bc0e90be0e3507da8ee5f77ab4a08b6cd1796f469d9", "assetId": "img:abdd86ad5f2e92068a4b5bc0e90be0e3507da8ee5f77ab4a08b6cd1796f469d9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000164", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114464.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Carboxylic acid A and alcohol B react to produce this ester. Give the displayed formula of carboxylic acid A and of alcohol B.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-517cf287-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Indicators can be used to test for carboxylic acids. Describe a different chemical test for a carboxylic acid.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-517cf287-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064114464.png", "width": 606, "format": "png", "height": 295, "sha256": "abdd86ad5f2e92068a4b5bc0e90be0e3507da8ee5f77ab4a08b6cd1796f469d9", "assetId": "img:abdd86ad5f2e92068a4b5bc0e90be0e3507da8ee5f77ab4a08b6cd1796f469d9", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000164", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064114464.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q07-517cf287", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000167"]}], "paperTotal": 70, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 6, "2": 9, "3": 6, "4": 7, "5": 13}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "January 2020", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_2CR_msc_20200305", "canonicalDocumentId": "4faf474b-ca4e-5517-82c5-a4403f0b090e"}, "entries": [{"mcq": true, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-4faf474b-q1ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "A is not correct because box A shows an element"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not correct because box C shows an element"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because box 4 shows a compound"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C Boxes 1 and 3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is C because boxes 1 and 2 contain one type of atom only."}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not correct because box 2 shows a mixture"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not correct because box 2 shows a mixture"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because box 4 shows a compound"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 (box 5 shows) two (different) elements"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW two (different) types of atoms"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 (chemically) bonded (together)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT mixture for M1"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW (chemically) combined/joined (together)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP on mention of elements/atoms in M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C number of protons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is B because the elements in the periodic table are arranged in order of proton number"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not correct because elements are not arranged in order of mass number"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is not correct because elements are not arranged in order of the number of neutrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because elements are not arranged in order of reactivity"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A electrons in the outer shell"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "The only correct answer is A because elements in the same group of the periodic table have the same number of electrons in the outer shell."}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B Is not the correct answer because elements in the same period have the same number of shells"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C elements in the same group do not have the same number of neutrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not the correct answer because elements in the same group do not have the same number of protons"}], "answerText": "B box 2\nThe only correct answer is B because box 2 contains two different particles in the same space that are not chemically joined", "confidence": 0.85, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": "B", "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-4faf474b-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT dyes/start line should be above water level"}], "answerText": "M1 level of the water must be below the dyes/start lineM2 the start line must be drawn in pencil", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(b)(i)", "marks": 3, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-4faf474b-q2bi", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW is not pure"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(distance moved by solvent correctly measured)=9.5(cm)M2 use ofRf=distance moved by the dye C distance moved by the solventM3 evaluation ofRf"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW a tolerance of±2mmeg6.29.5ALLOW ECF fromM1eg(6.2)=0.65(3)(9.5)ALLOW1-4sig figALLOW ECF fromM2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(dyeA)is not soluble in water"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW solvent for water"}], "answerText": "2 marks for any two conclusions from(the green food colouringM1 contains(dye)B and(dye)D M2 contains an unknown dyeM3 does not containA orC M4 contains three dyesand1 mark for a correct explanation of given conclusion for the green food colouringeg(explanation forM1)because has spots at same level(asB andD)eg(explanation forM2)because has a spot at different level(fromA B C D)eg(explanation forM3)because has no spots at same level(asA andC)eg(explanation forM4)because has three spots", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": true, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-4faf474b-q3a", "guidance": [], "answerText": "B Precipitation\nThe only correct answer is B the reaction of two solutions to produce an insoluble solid is precipitation.\nA is not correct because this reaction is not neutralisation\nC is not correct because this reaction is not a redox reaction\nD is not correct because this reaction is not thermal decomposition", "confidence": 0.85, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": "B", "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(b)", "marks": 2, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-4faf474b-q3b", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW leave to dry"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW leave in a warm place"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW leave for the water to evaporate"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE dry it alone"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT hot oven or any method of direct heating eg Bunsen burner"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT references (direct or inferred) to silver chloride solution or crystallisation for M1 and M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "No M2 if solid washed after drying"}], "answerText": "M1 wash the solid with (deionised) water\nM2 suitable method of drying solid eg dry between filter papers/on paper towel/in (warm) oven/in a desiccator", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(c)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-4faf474b-q3c", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW contains Cl-"}], "answerText": "Any one from:\nM1 (hydrochloric acid/it) contains chloride ions\nM2 (hydrochloric acid/it) produces a (white) precipitate with silver nitrate\nM3 (hydrochloric acid/it) reacts with silver nitrate", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(d)", "marks": 2, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-4faf474b-q3d", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW ECF from M1"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW one or more sig fig"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correct answer without working scores 2 marks."}], "answerText": "M1 n(AgNO3orAgCl)=0.0025\nM2(mass AgCl)=0.0025x143.5=0.35(9)g", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [{"ecf": false, "text": "M1 n(AgNO3orAgCl)=0.0025\nM2(mass AgCl)=0.0025x143.5=0.35", "marks": 9, "reject": false, "ordinal": 1, "rawText": "M1 n(AgNO3orAgCl)=0.0025\nM2(mass AgCl)=0.0025x143.5=0.35", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "g", "marks": null, "reject": false, "ordinal": 2, "rawText": "g", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-4faf474b-q4a", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE layers unqualified"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT layers of molecules"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP on mention of layers/atoms/ions in M1"}], "answerText": "M1 layers of atoms/positive ions\nM2 can slide over one another", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(b)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-4faf474b-q4bi", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW ions are in fixed positions/in a lattice"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE no free ions"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT any reference to electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)M1 lead ions (are positive and) are attracted to the negative electrode/Pb2+ (ions) are attracted to the negative electrode"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW cathode for negative electrode"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iv)metal or lead connects the electrodes or completes the circuit OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW metal or lead conducts electricity"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW metal or lead allows electrons to flow"}], "answerText": "ions cannot move\n(ii)2Br-→Br2+2e-\nM2 lead ions gain electrons/Pb2+ (ions) gain electrons (to form lead)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)(i)", "marks": 2, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-4faf474b-q5ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M1(lithium) moves(on the surface)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW floats"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(lithium) gets smaller/disappears"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW dissolves"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 colourless solution forms"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE white trail forms"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT melts/turns into a ball"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW temperature increases/heat given off"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)(when mixed with air)lit spill/splint or flame gives(squeaky)pop"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "must refer to test and resultIGNORE squeaky poptest aloneALLOW burns with(squeaky)popREJECT glowingspill/splint and pop"}], "answerText": "Any two from:", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(b)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-4faf474b-q5bi", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M1more rapid bubbles/fizzing/effervescence"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2turns into a ball"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW potassium melts"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3moves more quickly"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW getssmaller/disappearsmore quickly"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4catches alight/burns/produces a flame"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE flame colour"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1potassium has more shells than lithium"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW potassium atomis bigger than lithium"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW outershell/electron is furtherfrom nucleus"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(therefore)there is less attraction between theouter shell/electron and the nucleus"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW more repulsion(from inner shell)ormore shielding(from the nuclear attraction)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW nuclear pull forthe outer shell/electronis weaker"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3so the electron in the outer shell is more easilylost"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT answersinterms of lithium forM1,M2andM3"}], "answerText": "Any one from:", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc"