umber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (ii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (iii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (ii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (iii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "June 2020", "subject": null, "duration": "Afternoon (Time: 1 hour 15 minutes)", "examDate": "Wednesday 10 June 2020", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH1/2C", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "fe714b41-8dd8-5ef9-be5a-0d69de9f1439"}, "warnings": [], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student is given a mixture of salt solution and sand. She wants to obtain pure water from the mixture.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "She separates the sand from the salt solution. Which method of separation should she use? A crystallisation B filtration C fractional distillation D simple distillation", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-fe714b41-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student then uses this apparatus to obtain pure water from the salt solution.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-fe714b41-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069643327.png", "width": 1332, "format": "png", "height": 1224, "sha256": "dad96c6b20b5beb1d33615ad0ca885da58f735e124b417676deb6f3a39e19baf", "assetId": "img:dad96c6b20b5beb1d33615ad0ca885da58f735e124b417676deb6f3a39e19baf", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000031", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069643327.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the pieces of apparatus labelled X, Y and Z.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-fe714b41-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what remains in the flask when the separation is complete.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-fe714b41-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069643327.png", "width": 1332, "format": "png", "height": 1224, "sha256": "dad96c6b20b5beb1d33615ad0ca885da58f735e124b417676deb6f3a39e19baf", "assetId": "img:dad96c6b20b5beb1d33615ad0ca885da58f735e124b417676deb6f3a39e19baf", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000031", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069643327.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-fe714b41", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "In a chromatography experiment a student uses samples of three pure food dyes blue (B), red (R) and yellow (Y). He also uses samples of four unknown substances, S, T, U and V. The student puts a small drop of each substance on the pencil line. The diagram shows the student''s chromatogram at the end of the experiment.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which of the unknown substances contains only one food dye? A substance S B substance T C substance U D substance V", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-fe714b41-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which pure food dyes are in substance V.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-fe714b41-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the $ R_{f} $ value of the yellow food dye Y. $R _ {\\mathrm {f}} =$", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-fe714b41-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State how the chromatogram suggests that the yellow food dye Y is less soluble in the solvent than the red food dye R.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-fe714b41-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069643356.png", "width": 1133, "format": "png", "height": 1081, "sha256": "a21ea780625a07ef9eeb7e96a79cca6a38b023030cffd009e3b0cd64cf13cd18", "assetId": "img:a21ea780625a07ef9eeb7e96a79cca6a38b023030cffd009e3b0cd64cf13cd18", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000039", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069643356.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-fe714b41", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) The box gives the names of some metals. calcium copper iron magnesium silver zinc (i) Identify the metal from the box that burns with a bright white flame. (ii) In the Earth, metals are found either in ores or as uncombined elements Explain which metal from the box is most likely to be found as an uncombined element.", "marks": 10, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "This is the order of reactivity of four metals. The method used to obtain a metal from its oxide depends on the reactivity of the metal. Two possible methods are Method 1 heating the metal oxide with carbon Explain which method should be used to obtain lead from lead(II) oxide, PbO Method 2 electrolysis Include an equation for the formation of lead in your answer.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-fe714b41-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069643387.png", "width": 562, "format": "png", "height": 343, "sha256": "b66e612c567e3048ea947873aa58aa346f7b92a26c608b566d71aa78673afba7", "assetId": "img:b66e612c567e3048ea947873aa58aa346f7b92a26c608b566d71aa78673afba7", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000055", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069643387.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the arrangement of the particles in a pure metal. Metals are often made into alloys to make them harder. Explain why alloys are harder than pure metals. Draw a diagram to support your answer.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-fe714b41-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069643399.png", "width": 415, "format": "png", "height": 221, "sha256": "63c0b7015d8c85e6257f69349b8906520b97a54fe3f623e8a7776075aa9d704f", "assetId": "img:63c0b7015d8c85e6257f69349b8906520b97a54fe3f623e8a7776075aa9d704f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000063", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069643399.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069643387.png", "width": 562, "format": "png", "height": 343, "sha256": "b66e612c567e3048ea947873aa58aa346f7b92a26c608b566d71aa78673afba7", "assetId": "img:b66e612c567e3048ea947873aa58aa346f7b92a26c608b566d71aa78673afba7", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000055", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069643387.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069643399.png", "width": 415, "format": "png", "height": 221, "sha256": "63c0b7015d8c85e6257f69349b8906520b97a54fe3f623e8a7776075aa9d704f", "assetId": "img:63c0b7015d8c85e6257f69349b8906520b97a54fe3f623e8a7776075aa9d704f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000063", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069643399.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-fe714b41", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Alcohols contain the functional group—OH", "marks": 15, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Give the structural formula of the alcohol that contains one carbon atom.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-fe714b41-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Ethanol $ \\left( \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{O H} \\right) $ is an alcohol that can be obtained from glucose $ \\left( \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{1 2} \\mathrm{O}_{6} \\right) $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-fe714b41-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the process that converts glucose into ethanol.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-fe714b41-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why this process is carried out in the absence of air and at a temperature below $ 4 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}。 $", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-fe714b41-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table gives information about some organic compounds in the same homologous series.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-fe714b41-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the table by giving the missing information.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-fe714b41-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the homologous series that contains these compounds.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-fe714b41-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The compounds in the table can react with alcohols to form esters. When preparing esters, a small amount of concentrated sulfuric acid is also used.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-fe714b41-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the purpose of the acid.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-fe714b41-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw the displayed formula of the ester that forms when propanoic acid reacts with ethanol.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-fe714b41-pd-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Esters have particular uses that depend on their properties. Give an example of a property and use of esters.", "label": "d-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-fe714b41-pd-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-fe714b41", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000074"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The organic compound butadiene is a colourless gas used in the manufacture of synthetic rubber for tyres. The displayed formula of butadiene is", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why butadiene is described as an unsaturated hydrocarbon.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-fe714b41-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Butadiene reacts with bromine water. State the colour change that occurs during this reaction. from", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-fe714b41-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the reaction between butadiene and bromine can be shown using displayed formulae. The table gives some bond energies. Use this information to calculate the enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ , for the reaction. Include a sign in your answer. $\\Delta H =$", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-fe714b41-pb-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069643497.png", "width": 1064, "format": "png", "height": 282, "sha256": "0aa540a8e293d171981f5b49cd3ac4c6e82c97abfb950a8ea1d9c3ffedfa983b", "assetId": "img:0aa540a8e293d171981f5b49cd3ac4c6e82c97abfb950a8ea1d9c3ffedfa983b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000090", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069643497.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A scientist does an investigation to find out if butadiene would be a good fuel. He burns a sample of butadiene gas and observes that carbon forms as black soot.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-fe714b41-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the equation to explain the scientist''s observation. $2 \\mathrm {C} _ {4} \\mathrm {H} _ {6} + 7 \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow \\dots \\dots \\mathrm {C} + 4 \\mathrm {C O} + 2 \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} + \\dots \\dots \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-fe714b41-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain how one of the products, other than carbon, may cause a problem.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-fe714b41-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the combustion of butadiene in excess oxygen is $2 \\mathrm {C} _ {4} \\mathrm {H} _ {6} + 1 1 \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow 8 \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} + 6 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ The enthalpy change for this reaction, $ \\Delta H $ , is-3446 kJ/mol. Complete the energy profile diagram for the reaction. Label the enthalpy change for this reaction, $ \\Delta H $ , and the activation energy. $2 \\mathrm {C} _ {4} \\mathrm {H} _ {6} + 1 1 \\mathrm {O} _ {2}$", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-fe714b41-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069643480.png", "width": 348, "format": "png", "height": 275, "sha256": "6e6233d823eeeda04a62fba2d403d40dd341ee17bd40ecbeb5ceb886ca1b6f7d", "assetId": "img:6e6233d823eeeda04a62fba2d403d40dd341ee17bd40ecbeb5ceb886ca1b6f7d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000084", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069643480.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069643497.png", "width": 1064, "format": "png", "height": 282, "sha256": "0aa540a8e293d171981f5b49cd3ac4c6e82c97abfb950a8ea1d9c3ffedfa983b", "assetId": "img:0aa540a8e293d171981f5b49cd3ac4c6e82c97abfb950a8ea1d9c3ffedfa983b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000090", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069643497.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q05-fe714b41", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000092"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student is provided with a bottle containing a colourless solution X. Solution X is thought to be dilute sulfuric acid of concentration 0.10 mol/dm $ ^{3}. $ The student does some experiments on samples of solution X to try to show that it is dilute sulfuric acid. The student adds a few drops of litmus to a sample of solution X. The litmus turns red.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The student knows that the products of the electrolysis of dilute sulfuric acid are hydrogen and oxygen. She carries out the electrolysis using this apparatus.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-fe714b41-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069643550.png", "width": 1121, "format": "png", "height": 887, "sha256": "20f66fd58ddfa8944d1ddc8c511abab262debb9aaa78a6da5d15370947fcb727", "assetId": "img:20f66fd58ddfa8944d1ddc8c511abab262debb9aaa78a6da5d15370947fcb727", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000115", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069643550.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why the student does not use zinc electrodes in her experiment.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-fe714b41-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what is observed at both the anode and the cathode during the electrolysis.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-fe714b41-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these tests shows that the gas formed at the cathode is hydrogen? A a glowing splint relights B a burning splint gives a squeaky pop C a burning splint goes out D limewater turns cloudy", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-fe714b41-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe a test to show that solution X contains sulfate ions.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-fe714b41-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student then does a titration to see if the concentration of the dilute sulfuric acid is $ 0. 1 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3}. $ She measures $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of potassium hydroxide solution into a conical flask, and then adds a few drops of indicator solution.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-fe714b41-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the piece of apparatus the student should use to measure $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of the potassium hydroxide solution.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-fe714b41-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The concentration of potassium hydroxide in the solution is $ 0.125 \\mathrm{~m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3}. $ Calculate the amount, in mol, of KOH in $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of this solution. $\\mathrm {a m o u n t} =$", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-fe714b41-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The equation for the reaction in the titration is $\\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} + 2 \\mathrm {K O H} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {K} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ Calculate the volume, in $ cm^{3} $ , of 0.10 mol/dm $ ^{3} $ sulfuric acid needed to neutralise $ 25.0\\mathrm{cm}^{3} $ of the potassium hydroxide solution.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-fe714b41-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["volume of sulfuric acid"]}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069643550.png", "width": 1121, "format": "png", "height": 887, "sha256": "20f66fd58ddfa8944d1ddc8c511abab262debb9aaa78a6da5d15370947fcb727", "assetId": "img:20f66fd58ddfa8944d1ddc8c511abab262debb9aaa78a6da5d15370947fcb727", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000115", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069643550.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-fe714b41", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about reactions involving gases.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Potassium carbonate reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid to produce carbon dioxide gas. The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {K} _ {2} \\mathrm {C O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {H C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {K C l} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {I}) + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ Calculate the volume, in $ cm^{3} $ of carbon dioxide gas produced when 6.9 g of potassium carbonate reacts with excess dilute hydrochloric acid. [ $ M_{\\mathrm{r}} $ of $ \\mathrm{K_{2}CO_{3}}=1 3 8 $] [molar volume of $ \\mathrm{C O_{2}} $ at rtp=24 dm $ ^{3} $]", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-fe714b41-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "This reaction involving gases is in dynamic equilibrium at a temperature of $ 2 2 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ $\\mathrm {H} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {C O} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {g}) \\quad \\Delta H = + 4 1 \\mathrm {k J / m o l}$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-fe714b41-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Predict the effect on the yield of CO(g) at equilibrium when the temperature is increased without changing the pressure. Give a reason for your answer.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-fe714b41-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Predict the effect on the yield of CO(g) at equilibrium when the pressure is increased without changing the temperature. Give a reason for your answer.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-fe714b41-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-fe714b41", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 70, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 5, "2": 7, "3": 10, "4": 15, "6": 11, "7": 7}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "November 2020", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_2C_2011_MS", "canonicalDocumentId": "a7488398-a7d0-5837-8870-085a9992379e"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1", "marks": null, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-a7488398-q1", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "B filtration is the correct answer because it will enable sand to be separated from salt solution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is not correct because crystallisation will not enable sand to be separated from salt solution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not correct because fractional distillation will not enable sand to be separated from salt solution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because simple distillation will not enable sand to be separated from salt solution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "X is a thermometer"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3 cler"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Y is a (Liebig) condenser"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Z is a beaker"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "salt"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 cler"}], "answerText": "(a)", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": null, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-a7488398-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "B is not correct because T does not only contain one dye as it produces two spots"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is not correct because U does not only contain one dye as it produces three spots"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is not correct because V does not only contain one dye as it produces two spots"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "explanation containing following points"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2grad"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(V contains) blue/B and red/R(dyes)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 because V has spots at same height as those from blue/B and red/R OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 correct measurement of distance moved by spot Y"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW 5.7-6.1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 correct measurement of distance moved by solvent"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW 8.7-9.1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 use and evaluation of"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "$R_{f}=\\underline{\\underline{distance moved by spot Y}}$ distance moved by solvent"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW 1-4 sig fig ALLOW ECF from M1 M2 only if $R_{f}<1$"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "spot from yellow food dye/Y does not move as far as spot from red food dye/R OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ORA"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1exp"}], "answerText": "A substance S is the correct answer because S only contains one dye as it produces only one spot", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)(i)", "marks": null, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-a7488398-q3ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW Mg"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "explanation including the following pointsM1 silverM2 because it is the least reactive(of the metals)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW it is very unreactive"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2 grad"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "explanation including the following pointsM1 Method 1/ heating the metal oxide/lead(II) oxide with carbonM2 (because) lead is less reactive than iron(and iron is obtained from iron oxide by carbon extraction)M3 2PbO+C→2Pb+CO2"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW carbon is more reactive than leadACCEPT reverse argumentsALLOW PbO+CO→Pb+CO2ALLOW PbO+C→Pb+CO"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3 exp"}], "answerText": "magnesium", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(c)", "marks": null, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-a7488398-q3c", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT molecules once only"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW disrupts the lattice / layers / rows(of particles/ions/atoms)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW layers cannot slide over each other"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to strength/breaking of forces/(metallic) bonds"}], "answerText": "Explanation containing the following points\nPure metal:\nM1(particles/ions/atoms are same size in a regular arrangement so) layers can easily slide over each other\nAlloy:\nM2 diagram of alloy structure showing minimum of three layers with at least one different sized circle\nM3(having different sized particles/ions/atoms) disrupts/breaks up regular arrangement OWTTE\nM4so hard(er) for layers to slide over each other", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)", "marks": null, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-a7488398-q4a", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE displayed formula"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "fermentation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1cler"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Explanation including four fromM1 fermentation/reaction/respiration needs to be anaerobicM2 because in air/aerobic conditions ethanol not producedM3 because in air/aerobic conditions CO2and H2Oare producedM4(if temperature above40℃/too high)enzymes(in yeast)become denatured/lose their structureOWTTEM5 causing fermentation/reaction to slow down/stop"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWM1 in air ethanol would react with oxygen/be oxidisedM2 in air ethanol would form ethanoic acid/carboxylic acid/vinegarALLOW reference to optimum temperature(between30℃and40℃)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "4exp"}], "answerText": "CH3OH", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(c)(i)", "marks": null, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-a7488398-q4ci", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(d)(i)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(acid acts as) a catalyst/to speed up reaction(ii)M1 ester linkageM2 rest of molecule fully correct"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to activation energy"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1grad2exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(Property:) distinctive/sweet/fruity smellM2 used in perfumes/flavourings"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW volatileALLOW any correct use eg in making soaps/in solvents(for paints/varnishes)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2exp"}], "answerText": "M1M2C3H6O2M3 butanoic acidcarboxylic(acids)", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)", "marks": null, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-a7488398-q5a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M1(unsaturated because) contains(carbon to carbon)double bond(s)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW containsC=C"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(hydrocarbon because) contains(the elements/atoms)carbon and hydrogen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT molecules"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3only"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3DEP on carbon and hydrogen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "from orange to colourless"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW yellow for orange or any combination of orange/yellowIGNORE clear"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1grad"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "calculation including following steps"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "4Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1calculation of energy involved in bond breaking in reactants"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2calculation of energy involved in bond making in products"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3evaluation of difference"}, {"kind": "ecf", "text": "ECF fromM1andM2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4correct answer and sign"}, {"kind": "example-calculation", "text": "Example calculation"}, {"kind": "note