ompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which piece of apparatus can be used to measure the volume of a liquid? A B C D", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-cb9f6de1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069646062.png", "width": 104, "format": "png", "height": 405, "sha256": "eb42fca09c6709ee36cd8af99213fb13df6145b243509fdb2535718dc1e84714", "assetId": "img:eb42fca09c6709ee36cd8af99213fb13df6145b243509fdb2535718dc1e84714", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000024", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069646062.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069646072.png", "width": 331, "format": "png", "height": 132, "sha256": "039d8e34e00cdc52f3dd2d4c32a9f8090241df70b3e2c93562fb8a018e22315c", "assetId": "img:039d8e34e00cdc52f3dd2d4c32a9f8090241df70b3e2c93562fb8a018e22315c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000026", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069646072.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789069646076.png", "width": 165, "format": "png", "height": 412, "sha256": "91ca3e3106e5de718ffbcdbfc12f74aee75a3d6bbe13c2641c0944573c736cb0", "assetId": "img:91ca3e3106e5de718ffbcdbfc12f74aee75a3d6bbe13c2641c0944573c736cb0", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000027", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789069646076.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_4_1789069646082.png", "width": 450, "format": "png", "height": 132, "sha256": "5e6c20fd08e1bc05632730b0ba7ccc1a404e61b21913ac1ebc0a273e3ad90d80", "assetId": "img:5e6c20fd08e1bc05632730b0ba7ccc1a404e61b21913ac1ebc0a273e3ad90d80", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000029", "sourceName": "assets/crop_4_1789069646082.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-cb9f6de1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000032"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Thallium, Tl, is an element in Group 3 and Period 6 of the Periodic Table. The atomic number of thallium is 81 (a) How many electrons are there in the outer shell of an atom of thallium? A 3 B 6 C 13 D 81", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "A thallium ion has a charge of 3+ How many electrons are there in this thallium ion? A 3 B 78 C 81 D 84", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-cb9f6de1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A sample of thallium contains two isotopes. The table shows the mass number and percentage abundance of each isotope in the sample.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-cb9f6de1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the number of protons and the number of neutrons in one atom of the thallium-205 isotope. number of neutrons", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-cb9f6de1-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the relative atomic mass of this sample of thallium. Give your answer to one decimal place.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-cb9f6de1-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q02-cb9f6de1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000052"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) The diagram shows a fractionating column used to separate crude oil into fractions. (i) Give a use for bitumen and a use for gasoline. use for bitumen use for gasoline (ii) Explain why bitumen is collected at the bottom of the fractionating column and gasoline is collected near the top of the fractionating column.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "There is a low demand for some of the fractions obtained from crude oil. Cracking can be used to convert these fractions into more useful substances. (i) State the conditions needed for cracking.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-cb9f6de1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Dodecane $ \\left( \\mathrm{C}_{1 2} \\mathrm{H}_{2 6} \\right) $ can be cracked to produce an alkane and two alkenes. Complete the equation by giving the formulae of the two alkenes. $\\mathrm {C} _ {1 2} \\mathrm {H} _ {2 6} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C} _ {7} \\mathrm {H} _ {1 6} +$", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-cb9f6de1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069646102.png", "width": 919, "format": "png", "height": 573, "sha256": "2d762d840a15f7d5d8379bee6bef1efffd9cdc5e0b3d768adb704733dce8e86d", "assetId": "img:2d762d840a15f7d5d8379bee6bef1efffd9cdc5e0b3d768adb704733dce8e86d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000058", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069646102.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-cb9f6de1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about some of the alkali metals and their compounds.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "When a teacher drops a small piece of sodium into a trough of cold water, she observes bubbles of gas. Give two other observations that would be made when sodium reacts with cold water.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-cb9f6de1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Lithium reacts with fluorine to form the compound lithium fluoride.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-cb9f6de1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-cb9f6de1-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a test to show that lithium fluoride contains lithium ions.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-cb9f6de1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw diagrams to show the arrangement of the electrons in a lithium ion and in a fluoride ion. Include the charge on each ion.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-cb9f6de1-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the electronic configurations of sodium and potassium. Explain, in terms of their electronic configurations, why potassium is more reactive than sodium.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-cb9f6de1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-cb9f6de1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000075", "e000077"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about the metal aluminium.", "marks": 10, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a labelled diagram to represent the structure and bonding in a metal.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-cb9f6de1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why a metal conducts electricity.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-cb9f6de1-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Aluminium is used to make cans for drinks. Give two properties of aluminium that make it suitable for this use.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-cb9f6de1-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069646141.png", "width": 213, "format": "png", "height": 419, "sha256": "696626d2989a537782886d6806de241ffac1a3e3f610aa37ecff21712469a7a4", "assetId": "img:696626d2989a537782886d6806de241ffac1a3e3f610aa37ecff21712469a7a4", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000084", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069646141.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Aluminium is extracted from aluminium oxide $ \\mathrm{(A l_{2} O_{3})} $ by electrolysis. The electrolyte is aluminium oxide dissolved in molten cryolite.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-cb9f6de1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why aluminium cannot be extracted by heating aluminium oxide with carbon.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-cb9f6de1-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Aluminium is produced at the negative electrode. The ionic half-equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {A l} ^ {3 +} + 3 \\mathrm {e} ^ {-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {A l}$ State why this is a reduction reaction.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-cb9f6de1-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the ionic half-equation for the reaction at the positive electrode. $\\mathrm {O} ^ {2 -} \\rightarrow$", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-cb9f6de1-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069646141.png", "width": 213, "format": "png", "height": 419, "sha256": "696626d2989a537782886d6806de241ffac1a3e3f610aa37ecff21712469a7a4", "assetId": "img:696626d2989a537782886d6806de241ffac1a3e3f610aa37ecff21712469a7a4", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000084", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069646141.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-cb9f6de1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student wants to prepare sodium chloride crystals from sodium hydroxide solution and dilute hydrochloric acid. He does a titration to find the volume of dilute hydrochloric acid needed to neutralise the sodium hydroxide solution. This is his method. add $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution to a conical flask add a few drops of phenolphthalein indicator to the conical flask titrate the solution with the hydrochloric acid", "marks": 13, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Name a suitable piece of apparatus that the student should use to measure $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-cb9f6de1-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the colour of the phenolphthalein indicator in sodium hydroxide solution and in hydrochloric acid. colour in sodium hydroxide solution colour in hydrochloric acid...", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-cb9f6de1-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why universal indicator is never used in a titration.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-cb9f6de1-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student finds that $ 2 1. 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of hydrochloric acid is needed to neutralise $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-cb9f6de1-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe what the student should do next to prepare a pure solution of sodium chloride.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-cb9f6de1-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe how the student could obtain dry crystals of sodium chloride from the pure sodium chloride solution.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-cb9f6de1-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student needs $ 2 1. 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of hydrochloric acid to neutralise $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution of concentration $ 0. 8 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3}. $ The equation for the reaction is $\\mathrm {N a O H} + \\mathrm {H C l} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N a C l} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ Calculate the concentration, in mol/dm $ ^{3} $ , of the hydrochloric acid. $\\mathrm {m o l / d m ^ {3}}$", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-cb9f6de1-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-cb9f6de1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) Ethanol, $ \\mathrm{C_{2} H_{5} O H} $ , can be oxidised to produce ethanoic acid, $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C O O H} $ , by heating it with potassium dichromate(VI). (i) Name one other reactant needed for this reaction to occur. (ii) Which colour change occurs during this reaction? orange to colourless A colourless to green B green to orange D orange to green", "marks": 16, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "When ethanol is burned in air, complete combustion can occur. The equation for this reaction is $\\mathrm {C} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {5} \\mathrm {O H} + 3 \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} + 3 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ This equation can also be written using displayed formulae to show all the covalent bonds in the molecules. The table gives the bond energies for these bonds.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-cb9f6de1-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069646172.png", "width": 1043, "format": "png", "height": 217, "sha256": "6b2a7b1f4583f04ee9647496b8caa5e642fa460067d261494824a689926b2c7b", "assetId": "img:6b2a7b1f4583f04ee9647496b8caa5e642fa460067d261494824a689926b2c7b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000127", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069646172.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069646172.png", "width": 1043, "format": "png", "height": 217, "sha256": "6b2a7b1f4583f04ee9647496b8caa5e642fa460067d261494824a689926b2c7b", "assetId": "img:6b2a7b1f4583f04ee9647496b8caa5e642fa460067d261494824a689926b2c7b", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000127", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069646172.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-cb9f6de1", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000129", "e000130"]}], "paperTotal": 70, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 5, "3": 8, "4": 11, "5": 10, "6": 13, "7": 16}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "November 2020", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_2CR_2011_MS", "canonicalDocumentId": "afea9828-beed-5934-ab5a-9d931a781d9e"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": null, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-afea9828-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW evaporating dish/crystallising dish"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW(weighing)scale(s)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW weighing machine"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is incorrect as a test tube cannot measure a volume of liquid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is incorrect as an evaporating basin cannot measure a volume of liquid"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is incorrect as a balance measures mass not volume"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1Comp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 5"}], "answerText": "A test tube/boiling tube\nB evaporating basin\nC measuring cylinder\nD(top-pan)balance", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": null, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-afea9828-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B78A is incorrect as there are not 3 electrons in a thallium ionC is incorrect as 81 is the number of electrons in a thallium atom not a thallium ionD is incorrect as there are not 84 electrons in a thallium ion"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1Comp"}], "answerText": "A3B is incorrect as there are not 6 electrons in the outer shell of a thallium atomC is incorrect as there are not 13 electrons in the outer shell of a thallium atomD is incorrect as 81 is the total number of electrons in a thallium atom not the number in the outer shell", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(c)(i)", "marks": null, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-afea9828-q2ci", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT eighty-one"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(number of neutrons)124"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT one hundred and twenty-four"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "calculate sum of mass numbers multiplied by percentage abundances"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "divide answer by 100"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "give answer to one decimal place"}, {"kind": "example-calculation", "text": "Example calculation"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(203x30.8)+(205x69.2)OR20438.4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT4,5or6sigfig"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M220438.4÷100OR204.384"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT5or6sigfig(203x0.308)+(205x0.692)OR204.384withorwithoutworkingscoresM1andM2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3204.4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Correctanswerto1d.p.withorwithoutworkingscores3marks"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total7"}], "answerText": "M1(number of protons)81", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)(i)", "marks": null, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-afea9828-q3ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW other suitable uses"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(gasoline) petrol/fuel for cars/vehicles"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW other suitable uses e.g. fuel for cooking"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An explanation that links the following two points"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 column is cooler near the top than at the bottom ORA"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT column cool near the top and hot at the bottom"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT temperature decreases up the column ORA"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 gasoline has a lower boiling point than bitumen(so is collected nearer the top) ORA"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT gasoline has a low boiling point(so is collected near the top) and bitumen has a high boiling point(so is collected near the bottom)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 alumina/silica(catalyst)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT Al2O3/SiO2/aluminium oxide/silicon dioxide/aluminosilicate(s)/zeolite(s)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2600-700(℃)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT range or any value within the range"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C12H26-->C7H16+C2H4+C3H6"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT correct temperatures in other units"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1C2H4M2C3H6"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT answers in either order"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total8"}], "answerText": "M1(bitumen)(surfacing) roads/(surfacing) roofs", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)", "marks": null, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-afea9828-q4a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M1(sodium) floats/moves on surface(of water)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(sodium) melts/forms a ball"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3(sodium) gets smaller/disappears"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW dissolves"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4(sodium forms) white trail"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to flame/heat released/temperature increasesIGNORE fizzing/effervescence"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Li+F2→2LiF"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW multiples or fractionsIGNORE state symbols even if incorrect"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 flame testM2 red(flame)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW description of flame testALLOW crimson/scarletREJECT orange-red/brick red"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 correct electron arrangement of lithium ion"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any combination of dots and crossesIGNORE empty second shell"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "3Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 correct electron arrangement of fluoride ion"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Inner electron shell required to score M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 correct charges on both ions(with or without brackets)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 not dep on M1 and M2 correct"}], "answerText": "Any two of the following observations.", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(c)", "marks": null, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-afea9828-q4c", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M1 the outer electron is further from the nucleus in potassium / potassium has more shells/ potassium has larger atomic radius ORA"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW potassium atom is bigger than a sodium atom"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 there is more shielding (by the inner shells) in potassium ORA"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 there is less attraction between the outer electron and the nucleus in potassium ORA"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "outer electron needs to be mentioned at least once in the answer for full marks"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4 (so outer) electron (in potassium) more easily lost ORA"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Max 2 if no mention of outer electron in the answer"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 11"}], "answerText": "An explanation that links three of the following four points", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)(i)", "marks": null, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-afea9828-q5ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Max 1 if no labelsMinimum requirement for 2 marks is + signs on atoms and electrons labelled or shown as e-"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An explanation that links the following two pointsM1 delocalised electronsM2 flow/are mobile/move/are free to move"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE free electrons/sea of electronsM2 dep on mention of electronsAny mention of ions/atoms moving scores 0"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Any two of the following propertiesM1 low densityM2 does not react with drinkM3 malleable"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW lightweightIGNORE lightIGNORE less denseALLOW does not corrode/non-toxicIGNORE does not rustALLOW easy to bend/easy to shapeIGNORE references to costIGNORE can be recycledIGNORE any irrelevant properties e.g. high melting/boiling point/good conductor/ductile"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Exp"}], "answerText": "A labelled diagram showingM1 at least three rows of at least three cations/atoms in a regular arrangementM2 surrounded by (delocalised) electronsExample of diagram", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(c)(i)", "marks": null, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-afea9828-q5ci", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW aluminium is too reactive/too high in the reactivity series"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "electrons are gained (by aluminium ion/Al3+)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT oxidation state of aluminium/Al is decreased/changes from+3to0REJECT aluminium/Al gains electronsIGNORE references to loss of oxygen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2O2-→O2+4e(-)M1 correct formulae of productsM2 balancing of correct formulae"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT multiples and fractionsM2 dep on M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 10"}], "answerText": "aluminium/it is more reactive/higher in the reactivity series than carbon ORA", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": null}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6", "marks": null, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-afea9828-q6", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "pipette"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1(colour in NaOH) pink"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT magenta"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW red"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2Grad"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(colour in HCl) colourless/no colour"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE clear"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1 mark for two correct colours in the wrong order"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "There is no clear (colour change at the) end point/difficult to determine which shade of green is pH 7 OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW it has a range of colours"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "1Exp"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)", "confidence": 0.5, "markPoints":