y": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M3volume of $ \\mathrm{H_{2} S O_{4}}=\\frac{(0.01875\\times1000)}{(0.600)}=31.25(\\mathrm{c m}^{3}) $", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (d)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M1 heat the solution", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M2 to evaporate some of the water/ to form a saturated solution/ to crystallisation point", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M3 leave the solution to cool / leave the solution for (more) crystals to form", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M4 filter off the crystals", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: M5 suitable method of drying the crystals", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "7: bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "7: bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "7: bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "7(d)(i): bare integer cell \"3\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": null, "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH1/2CR", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "35fdc8b0-ffff-5f0d-a81b-100139963e77"}, "warnings": [], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The diagram shows the sub-atomic particles in an atom of an element.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of each of the sub-atomic particles labelled P, Q and R.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-35fdc8b0-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of the part of the atom labelled S.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-35fdc8b0-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the name of this element.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-35fdc8b0-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069705326.png", "width": 879, "format": "png", "height": 683, "sha256": "6e4514b366231b35c98264dbdef1cae7871c7c24b80bae11fa7290c4dd98a5fe", "assetId": "img:6e4514b366231b35c98264dbdef1cae7871c7c24b80bae11fa7290c4dd98a5fe", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000024", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069705326.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-35fdc8b0", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A potassium permanganate crystal is placed in a beaker of water. After several days a coloured solution forms.", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Give the names of the two processes that cause the coloured solution to form.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-35fdc8b0-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The formula of potassium permanganate is $ \\mathrm{KMnO_{4}} $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-35fdc8b0-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "How many different types of atom are in $ \\mathrm{KMnO_{4}} $? A 3 B 4 C 6 D 7", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-35fdc8b0-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the relative formula mass $ ( M_{r} ) $ of $ KMnO_{4} $", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-35fdc8b0-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Potassium permanganate can be used as an oxidising agent. State what is meant by the term oxidising agent.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-35fdc8b0-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069705335.png", "width": 598, "format": "png", "height": 274, "sha256": "def49c70164ba40bbcbba4cbc0e483d4f6e2a3338f993ab956fe7a810ac1e66a", "assetId": "img:def49c70164ba40bbcbba4cbc0e483d4f6e2a3338f993ab956fe7a810ac1e66a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000030", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069705335.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-35fdc8b0", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about alkanes.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these is the molecular formula of an alkane? A $ C_{2} H_{5} $ B $ C_{4} H_{10} $ C $ C H_{2} C H_{2} $ D $ C H_{3} C H_{2} C H_{3} $", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-35fdc8b0-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these has the same empirical formula and molecular formula? A $ \\mathrm{C H_{2}} $ B $ \\mathrm{C_{2} H_{6}} $ C $ \\mathrm{C_{3} H_{8}} $ D $ \\mathrm{C_{4} H_{10}} $", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-35fdc8b0-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In the presence of ultraviolet radiation, methane reacts with bromine to form bromomethane and hydrogen bromide.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-35fdc8b0-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the name of this type of reaction.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-35fdc8b0-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-35fdc8b0-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "One mole of an alkane burns completely in oxygen. The equation represents the reaction. $\\mathrm {a l k a n e} + x \\mathrm {O} _ {2} \\rightarrow y \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} + z \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ The numbers x,y and z are used to balance the equation.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-35fdc8b0-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The complete combustion of one mole of the alkane produces 220g of carbon dioxide and 108g of water. Calculate the values of y and z. $[ M _ {\\mathrm {r}} \\mathrm {o f C O} _ {2} = 4 4 \\quad M _ {\\mathrm {r}} \\mathrm {o f H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} = 1 8 ]$", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-35fdc8b0-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Determine the molecular formula of the alkane and the value of x. $x =$", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-35fdc8b0-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["molecular formula"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When an alkane burns in a limited supply of air, incomplete combustion occurs. Explain why incomplete combustion of an alkane could be harmful to humans.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-35fdc8b0-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q03-35fdc8b0", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) The diagram represents the structure of copper metal. Explain three properties of copper that make it a suitable metal to use in electrical wiring.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the electrolysis of copper(II) sulfate solution, using graphite electrodes. Copper forms at the negative electrode and oxygen forms at the positive electrode.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-35fdc8b0-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069705381.png", "width": 1268, "format": "png", "height": 523, "sha256": "dae552b84532838cdd7b4ef8b93ae448aea227f5ab59d1ef51f3adb76a839094", "assetId": "img:dae552b84532838cdd7b4ef8b93ae448aea227f5ab59d1ef51f3adb76a839094", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000073", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069705381.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the formula of the copper ion and the formula of the sulfate ion in copper(II) sulfate. copper ion sulfate ion", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-35fdc8b0-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what would be seen at the positive electrode.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-35fdc8b0-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a test for oxygen.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-35fdc8b0-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give an ionic half-equation for the formation of oxygen at the positive electrode.", "label": "b-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-35fdc8b0-pb-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest why the copper(II) sulfate solution contains some $ \\mathrm{O H}^{-} $ ions.", "label": "b-v", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-35fdc8b0-pb-v", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069705365.png", "width": 890, "format": "png", "height": 206, "sha256": "c91d662f2499df6168d591f480b33ca7aa29b5ec385e0017b6e2c6dd670bb270", "assetId": "img:c91d662f2499df6168d591f480b33ca7aa29b5ec385e0017b6e2c6dd670bb270", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000070", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069705365.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069705381.png", "width": 1268, "format": "png", "height": 523, "sha256": "dae552b84532838cdd7b4ef8b93ae448aea227f5ab59d1ef51f3adb76a839094", "assetId": "img:dae552b84532838cdd7b4ef8b93ae448aea227f5ab59d1ef51f3adb76a839094", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000073", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069705381.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-35fdc8b0", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about alcohols, carboxylic acids and esters.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Ethanol can be manufactured by the fermentation of a solution of glucose.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-35fdc8b0-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write a word equation for this reaction.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-35fdc8b0-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State the substance that needs to be added for the reaction to occur.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-35fdc8b0-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State two conditions needed for this reaction.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-35fdc8b0-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In the presence of an acid catalyst, ethanoic acid is heated with butanol to form an ester.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-35fdc8b0-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which of these is the formula of the ester? $\\mathrm {C H} _ {3} \\mathrm {C O O C} _ {3} \\mathrm {H} _ {7}$ B $ \\mathrm{C H_{3} C O O C_{4} H_{9}} $ C $ \\mathrm{C_{2} H_{5} C O O C_{4} H_{9}} $ D $ \\mathrm{C_{3}H_{7}COOC_{2}H_{5}} $", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-35fdc8b0-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State how you would know that an ester has formed.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-35fdc8b0-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give one use of an ester.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-35fdc8b0-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Aspirin is a compound used to reduce pain. Aspirin contains a carboxylic acid functional group and an ester functional group.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-35fdc8b0-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what is meant by the term functional group.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-35fdc8b0-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "This is the structural formula of aspirin. Draw a circle around the carboxylic acid functional group.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-35fdc8b0-pc-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069705438.png", "width": 551, "format": "png", "height": 137, "sha256": "5daf58390ac8f888935bc7cf87da4bf222ab18d0e15da4f0d2019ce160936c82", "assetId": "img:5daf58390ac8f888935bc7cf87da4bf222ab18d0e15da4f0d2019ce160936c82", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000100", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069705438.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Aspirin has this percentage composition by mass. $C = 60.00 \\% \\quad H = 4.44 \\% \\quad O = 35.56 \\%$ Show by calculation that the empirical formula of aspirin is $ \\mathrm{C_{9} H_{8}O_{4}} $", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-35fdc8b0-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069705438.png", "width": 551, "format": "png", "height": 137, "sha256": "5daf58390ac8f888935bc7cf87da4bf222ab18d0e15da4f0d2019ce160936c82", "assetId": "img:5daf58390ac8f888935bc7cf87da4bf222ab18d0e15da4f0d2019ce160936c82", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000100", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069705438.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q05-35fdc8b0", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A student uses this method to do a titration. use a measuring cylinder to obtain $ 2 5 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution transfer the solution to a conical flask add a few drops of universal indicator to the flask fill a burette with dilute sulfuric acid and record the initial burette reading add the acid to the flask, swirling the flask continuously add the acid slowly near the end-point record the final burette reading at the end-point The student repeats the titration until at least two concordant results are obtained.", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State what is meant by concordant results.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-35fdc8b0-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain two improvements to the student''s method so that more accurate results are obtained.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-35fdc8b0-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student makes the improvements and repeats the titration. The sulfuric acid has a concentration of $ 0. 6 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3}. $ The sodium hydroxide solution has a concentration of $ 1. 5 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3}. $ This is the equation for the reaction. $2 \\mathrm {N a O H} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ Calculate the volume, in $ cm^{3} $ , of sulfuric acid that the student needs to completely react with $ 2 5. 0 \\, \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of the sodium hydroxide solution.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-35fdc8b0-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["volume of sulfuric acid"]}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The student plans to obtain pure dry crystals of hydrated sodium sulfate. They add the calculated volume of sulfuric acid to $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of the sodium hydroxide solution to form sodium sulfate solution. Describe what the student should do to obtain pure dry crystals of hydrated sodium sulfate from the solution.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-35fdc8b0-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-35fdc8b0", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "In the presence of an iron catalyst, nitrogen reacts with hydrogen to form ammonia. The reaction conditions used are a temperature of $ 4 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ and a pressure of 200 atmospheres. This is the equation for the reaction. $\\mathrm {N} _ {2} + 3 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm {N H} _ {3} \\quad \\Delta H = - 9 2 \\mathrm {k J / m o l}$", "marks": 15, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State what the symbol $ \\rightleftharpoons $ represents.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-35fdc8b0-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the reason for using a catalyst.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-35fdc8b0-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The reaction mixture is kept at a pressure of 200 atmospheres, but the temperature is increased to $ 5 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ Explain the effect of this change on the yield of ammonia at equilibrium.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-35fdc8b0-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The reaction mixture is kept at a temperature of $ 4 5 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ , but the pressure is increased to 300 atmospheres. Explain the effect of this change on the yield of ammonia at equilibrium.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-35fdc8b0-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw an energy level diagram for the reaction between nitrogen and hydrogen Include the reactants, products and $ \\Delta H $ in your diagram.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-35fdc8b0-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "At the start of the reaction, $ 4 8 \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ of nitrogen is added to $ 1 2 0 \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ of hydrogen at rtp. $\\mathrm {N} _ {2} + 3 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\mathrm {N H} _ {3}$ [molar volume of any gas at rtp=24 dm $ ^{3} $]", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-35fdc8b0-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Show by calculation that the nitrogen is in excess.", "label": "d-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-35fdc8b0-pd-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The yield of ammonia at equilibrium is 20%. Calculate the volume, in $ \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ of ammonia formed from $ 1 2 0 \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ of hydrogen.", "label": "d-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-35fdc8b0-pd-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069705507.png", "width": 126, "format": "png", "height": 37, "sha256": "761e41f5b31b70deb672d14d29ef987b40b8bbf6cfadcf7d343e7d95ecfee835", "assetId": "img:761e41f5b31b70deb672d14d29ef987b40b8bbf6cfadcf7d343e7d95ecfee835", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000148", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069705507.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["volume of ammonia"]}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_2_1789069705507.png", "width": 126, "format": "png", "height": 37, "sha256": "761e41f5b31b70deb672d14d29ef987b40b8bbf6cfadcf7d343e7d95ecfee835", "assetId": "img:761e41f5b31b70deb672d14d29ef987b40b8bbf6cfadcf7d343e7d95ecfee835", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000148", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789069705507.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-35fdc8b0", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 70, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 5, "2": 5, "4": 11, "6": 12, "7": 15}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "Summer 2022", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_2CR_2206_MS", "canonicalDocumentId": "dd78b694-8b04-536f-ab00-c2ad60c723dc"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-dd78b694-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Answers can be in either order"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE dilution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(Mr=39+55+16x4=)158"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(something that) gives oxygen (to another substance)/takes/gains electrons (from another substance) OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW oxygen donor"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW electron acceptor"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW (something that) causes another substance to be oxidised/to lose electrons OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW (something that) is reduced (in a reaction)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW increases the oxidation number (of another species)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OWTTE"}], "answerText": "M1 dissolving\nM2 diffusion\nB is incorrect as there are not 4 different types of atoms in KMnO4\nC is incorrect as there are not 6 different types of atoms in KMnO4", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-dd78b694-q3ai", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "C $ C_{3}H_{8}$"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is incorrect as $ CH_{2} $ is not a molecular formula but is an empirical formula of an alkene"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B is incorrect as the empirical formula of $ C_{2}H_{6} $ is $ CH_{3} $ D is incorrect as the empirical formula of $ C_{4}H_{10} $ is $ C_{2}H_{5} $"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "substitution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "$ CH_{4}+Br_{2}\\rightarrow CH_{3}Br+HBr $"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW multiples"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE state symbols even if incorrect"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 y(=220÷44)=5"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 z(=108÷18)=6"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW ECF in(ii) from incorrect values of y and z in(i) to score M1 the answer must be the formula of an alkane"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(d)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 $ C_{5}H_{12} $"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 x=8"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW ECF in(ii) from incorrect values of y and z in(i) to score M1 the answer must be the formula of an alkane"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An explanation including one of the following pairs"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 dep on M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 carbon monoxide/CO is produced"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT prevents blood from carrying oxygen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 (carbon monoxide) is toxic/poisonous/reduces the capacity of the blood to carry oxygen OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW correct explanation in terms of haemoglobin e.g. prevents haemoglobin from carrying oxygen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 carbon/soot"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 causes asthma/bronchitis/respiratory problems OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 10"}], "answerText": "B $ C_{4}H_{10}$\nA is incorrect as $ C_{2}H_{5} $ is not the molecular formula of an alkane\nC is incorrect as $ CH_{2}CH_{2} $ is the structural formula of an alkene\nD is incorrect as $ CH_{3}CH_{2}CH_{3} $ is the structural formula of an alkane", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4(a)", "marks": 5, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-dd78b694-q4a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "reason must be linked to correct property"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 conducts electricity"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to cost/reactivity/hardness/strength/shiny"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(because the) delocalised electrons can move/flow (through structure)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "If any mention of ions/atoms moving cannot score M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 malleable/ductile"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW explanations of malleable/ductile"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4(because) layers of cations/atoms"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW sheets/rows"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M5 layers/cations/atoms can slide/slip/move over each other"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M6 high melting point"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE high boiling point"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M7(because) strong (electrostatic) attraction between cations and delocalised electrons"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW giant metallic lattice/strong bonds between cations and delocalised electrons/strong metallic bonds"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Max 4 if any mention of intermolecular forces/covalent/ionic bonding"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "copper ion Cu2+ sulfate ion SO42-"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW Cu2+ SO42-"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "effervescence/bubbles/fizzing"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE oxygen/gas"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "relights a glowing splint"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "2H2O→O2+4H++4e(-)OR4OH-→O2+2H2O+4e(-)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1all formulae correct"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2balancing of correct formulae"}], "answerText": "An explanation of properties that includes five of the following points", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5", "marks": 1, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-dd78b694-q5", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "glucose $\\rightarrow$ ethanol + carbon dioxide"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT fully correct chemical equation"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE yeast"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "yeast"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE zymase/enzymes"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 30℃"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any value between 20 and 40 inclusive"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE yeast"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE room temperature"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 anaerobic conditions / absence of air/oxygen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "B CH3COOC4H9"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A is incorrect as this is propyl ethanoate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "C is incorrect as this is butyl propanoate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D is incorrect as this is ethyl butanoate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "sweet/fruity/distinctive smell OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "perfumes/(food) flavourings/solvents"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE (food) colourings"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT any other appropriate use eg emulsifiers"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(atom/group of atoms in a compound that) determines its chemical properties/reactions OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW (atom/group of atoms that) determines which homologous series a compound is in OWTTE"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii) circle around O"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "II"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "-C-O-H"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-dd78b694-q6a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT other values if less than 0.2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "An explanation that links two of the following pairs of points"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1a pipette should have been used instead of measuring cylinder/to measure the sodium hydroxide solution"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2a pipette is more precise/a measuring cylinder is less precise"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3methyl orange/phenolphthalein/litmus should have been used instead of universal indicator"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4methyl orange/phenolphthalein/litmus would give a clear/sharp colour change/end-point/universal indicator would not give a clear/sharp colour change/end-point"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M5use white tile(under conical flask)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M6easier to see colour change(at end point)OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWa pipette is more accurate/a measuring cylinder is less accurate"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW pipette would give exactly25cm3"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW universal indicator would give a range of colours"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW methyl orange/phenolphthalein/litmus only have two colours/are one colour in acid and one in alkali(colours given for a named indicator do not need to be correct)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW easier to see colour change with methyl orange/phenolphthalein/litmus"}], "answerText": "(results that are) within 0.2(0)(cm3) of each other", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [{"ecf": false, "text": "(results that are) within 0.2", "marks": 0, "reject": false, "ordinal": 1, "rawText": "(results that are) within 0.2", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "(cm3) of each other", "marks": null, "reject": false, "ordinal": 2, "rawText": "(cm3) of each other", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7", "marks": 1, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-dd78b694-q7", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "reversible (reaction)/reaction that goes both ways OWTTE"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to equilibrium"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "increases the rate of reaction/speeds up the reaction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT to increase yield"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW to reach equilibrium faster"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE provides an alternative pathway with lower activation energy"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 yield decreases"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE references to rate and Le Chatelier throughout"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 (forward) reaction is exothermic/backward reaction is endothermic"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT (when temperature increased equilibrium shifts in) endothermic direction which is (from right) to left"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 yield increases"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP M1 correct or missing"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 there are fewer moles (of gas) on the right-hand side/product side / there are more moles (of gas) on the left-hand side/there are 4 moles of reactants and 2 moles of product/ OWTTE"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW molecules for moles"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP M1 correct or missing"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7(c)", "marks": 3, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-dd78b694-q7c", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE any horizontal axis/labelsIGNORE any attempts at including activation energy"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 horizontal line labelled with N2+3H2"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW nitrogen and hydrogen"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 horizontal line showing 2NH3in correct position"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW ammoniaIf not balanced or words reactants and products on horizontal lines in correct position scores1 out of2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 vertical line in correct position and labelled ΔH/-92(kJ/mol)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT double headed arrow or arrow pointing from reactants level to products levelREJECT arrow pointing from products level to reactants levelIf endothermic reaction can scoreM1 and M3"}], "answerText": "Energy N2+3H2ΔH2NH3", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7(d)(i)", "marks": 3, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-dd78b694-q7di", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT alternative methods"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "correct answer without working scores3"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW ECF from(i)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW3.3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "use of3.3gives79.2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "use of79.2gives15.84"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW ECF fromM2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT120dm3of hydrogen gives2x120÷3OR80(dm3of ammonia)forM1andM2"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWmax2for use of48dm3of nitrogen giving96dm3of ammonia and final answer of19.2(dm3)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M15mol of hydrogen gives3.33mol of ammonia"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2volume of ammonia(if100%conversion)=3.33x24OR80(dm3)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3(volume of ammoniaif20%conversion)=\\frac{(20\\times80)}{100}=16.0(dm3)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total15"}], "answerText": "M1amount of nitrogen=(48÷24=)2(mol)\nM2amount of hydrogen=(120÷24=)5(mol)\nM32.5mol of hydrogen to1mol of nitrogen(which is less than3so nitrogen is in excess)OR2mol of nitrogen needs6mol of hydrogen(so not enough hydrogen to react with the nitrogen)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}], "icTable": null, "warnings": ["orphan row before first entry: 1(a)(i)(ii)", "orphan row before first entry: Q proton", "orphan row before f