": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-50d1855f", "answerPrompts": ["(3)"], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000028", "e000030"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The table shows properties of four substances, A, B, C and D. A 800 1465 no yes B 327 1749 yes yes C 232 573 no no D 3550 4830 no no", "marks": 5, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Use information from the table to identify these substances.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-50d1855f-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which substance could be a metal? A B C D", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-50d1855f-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which substance could be diamond? A B C D", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-50d1855f-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which substance is a gas at 600 $ ^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}? $ A B C D", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-50d1855f-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "One of the substances in the table is a compound with the formula $ \\mathrm{C_{10}H_{16}N_{2}O_{3}S} $", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-50d1855f-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the number of different elements in $ C_{1 0} H_{1 6} N_{2} O_{3} S $", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-50d1855f-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Determine the number of atoms in a molecule of $ C_{1 0} H_{1 6} N_{2} O_{3} S $", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-50d1855f-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-50d1855f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000036"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about soluble salts and insoluble salts.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Which pair of solutions produces an insoluble salt when mixed? A sodium sulfate and potassium nitrate B potassium carbonate and calcium nitrate C sodium chloride and ammonium nitrate D sodium hydroxide and potassium sulfate", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-50d1855f-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When solutions of lead nitrate and sodium sulfate are mixed, one product is solid lead sulfate. This is the equation for the reaction. $\\mathrm {P b} \\left(\\mathrm {N O} _ {3}\\right) _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {P b S O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {N a N O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q})$ Describe how a pure, dry sample of solid lead sulfate can be obtained from the mixture.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-50d1855f-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The table gives the solubility of a salt in water at six different temperatures.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-50d1855f-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Plot the points on the grid.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-50d1855f-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a curve of best fit.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-50d1855f-pc-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303788.png", "width": 1093, "format": "png", "height": 1674, "sha256": "160c160734df3248428acb4f6ad0c399d5949f38a440c8fa9c9c7012a52091a8", "assetId": "img:160c160734df3248428acb4f6ad0c399d5949f38a440c8fa9c9c7012a52091a8", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000072", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303788.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A saturated solution of the salt in 100g of water is cooled from $ 9 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ to $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ Use your graph to determine the mass of salt that will crystallise. Show your working on the graph.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-50d1855f-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303788.png", "width": 1093, "format": "png", "height": 1674, "sha256": "160c160734df3248428acb4f6ad0c399d5949f38a440c8fa9c9c7012a52091a8", "assetId": "img:160c160734df3248428acb4f6ad0c399d5949f38a440c8fa9c9c7012a52091a8", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000072", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303788.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q03-50d1855f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000069"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Fractional distillation is used to separate crude oil into fractions. The diagram shows a fractionating column and the fractions obtained from crude oil.", "marks": 13, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Describe how crude oil is separated into fractions in the fractionating column.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-50d1855f-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a use for kerosene and a use for bitumen. kerosene bitumen", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-50d1855f-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Some fractions obtained from crude oil are cracked to form alkenes.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-50d1855f-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe what is meant by cracking.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-50d1855f-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Ethene is obtained by cracking. This is the displayed formula of ethene. Explain why ethene is described as an unsaturated hydrocarbon.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-50d1855f-pb-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303823.png", "width": 222, "format": "png", "height": 199, "sha256": "f05ebf2e9f0ec6deacfd36938325252f77a8a8fd0c73c764235f811b6d0caf6e", "assetId": "img:f05ebf2e9f0ec6deacfd36938325252f77a8a8fd0c73c764235f811b6d0caf6e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000084", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303823.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe a test to show that ethene is unsaturated.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-50d1855f-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303811.png", "width": 1080, "format": "png", "height": 593, "sha256": "52c2e0b50229bc0d328a65757e7dc3291ea635a3d7d4a3197d0ddc59ba66322d", "assetId": "img:52c2e0b50229bc0d328a65757e7dc3291ea635a3d7d4a3197d0ddc59ba66322d", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000076", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303811.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303823.png", "width": 222, "format": "png", "height": 199, "sha256": "f05ebf2e9f0ec6deacfd36938325252f77a8a8fd0c73c764235f811b6d0caf6e", "assetId": "img:f05ebf2e9f0ec6deacfd36938325252f77a8a8fd0c73c764235f811b6d0caf6e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000084", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303823.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-50d1855f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about metals.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why metals are malleable.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-50d1855f-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Sodium metal is extracted by the electrolysis of molten sodium chloride. Sodium metal forms at the negative electrode and chlorine gas forms at the positive electrode. The diagram represents this electrolysis.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-50d1855f-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303841.png", "width": 1368, "format": "png", "height": 516, "sha256": "48579ebec0637b813b964c0ee5143af96ba28269a345dff35047d93650fd3f3e", "assetId": "img:48579ebec0637b813b964c0ee5143af96ba28269a345dff35047d93650fd3f3e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000094", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303841.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why molten sodium chloride conducts electricity.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-50d1855f-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain how sodium metal forms at the negative electrode.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-50d1855f-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write an ionic half-equation for the formation of chlorine gas at the positive electrode.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-50d1855f-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why sodium metal does not form in the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of sodium chloride.", "label": "b-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-50d1855f-pb-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Copper can be produced by reacting an oxide of copper with methane. The diagram shows the apparatus used. The oxide of copper is heated until the reaction is complete. The table shows the results. Use the results to show that this oxide has the empirical formula CuO [for Cu, $ A_{\\mathrm{r}}=6 3. 5 $ for O, $ A_{\\mathrm{r}}=1 6 $]", "label": "c", "marks": 3, "partId": "q05-50d1855f-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303862.png", "width": 1416, "format": "png", "height": 494, "sha256": "00528650f3853cfa3e78b159f7f4ab938c4da895739909790871f9f6aff2d0fe", "assetId": "img:00528650f3853cfa3e78b159f7f4ab938c4da895739909790871f9f6aff2d0fe", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000101", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303862.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789064303873.png", "width": 121, "format": "png", "height": 38, "sha256": "4f1e1e85433518934038e99941b67cc4411f4f65c7a2eb69ff647eafe753b712", "assetId": "img:4f1e1e85433518934038e99941b67cc4411f4f65c7a2eb69ff647eafe753b712", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000107", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789064303873.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303841.png", "width": 1368, "format": "png", "height": 516, "sha256": "48579ebec0637b813b964c0ee5143af96ba28269a345dff35047d93650fd3f3e", "assetId": "img:48579ebec0637b813b964c0ee5143af96ba28269a345dff35047d93650fd3f3e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000094", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303841.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303862.png", "width": 1416, "format": "png", "height": 494, "sha256": "00528650f3853cfa3e78b159f7f4ab938c4da895739909790871f9f6aff2d0fe", "assetId": "img:00528650f3853cfa3e78b159f7f4ab938c4da895739909790871f9f6aff2d0fe", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000101", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303862.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_3_1789064303873.png", "width": 121, "format": "png", "height": 38, "sha256": "4f1e1e85433518934038e99941b67cc4411f4f65c7a2eb69ff647eafe753b712", "assetId": "img:4f1e1e85433518934038e99941b67cc4411f4f65c7a2eb69ff647eafe753b712", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000107", "sourceName": "assets/crop_3_1789064303873.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q05-50d1855f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000104"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "When ethanoic acid reacts with ethanol in the presence of concentrated sulfuric acid, one of the products is ester A. This is the equation for the reaction.", "marks": 13, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a circle around the functional group in ethanoic acid.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-50d1855f-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the displayed formula of ester A.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-50d1855f-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name ester A.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-50d1855f-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The reaction mixture is kept in a sealed container until dynamic equilibrium is reached. State what is meant by the term dynamic equilibrium.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-50d1855f-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "During the reaction, the number of moles of ethanoic acid in the reaction mixture decreases, but the number of moles of concentrated sulfuric acid does not change.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-50d1855f-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why the number of moles of concentrated sulfuric acid does not change.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-50d1855f-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student does a titration to find the accurate volume of sodium hydroxide solution needed for complete neutralisation. The student starts by using a pipette to transfer $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of the reaction mixture to a conical flask. Describe how the student should complete the titration.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-50d1855f-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303892.png", "width": 1182, "format": "png", "height": 318, "sha256": "4d45522a4c583e1a4e0dc1e4b0e2ab38f4b9ebee6a7178db7eb87070adf0aae1", "assetId": "img:4d45522a4c583e1a4e0dc1e4b0e2ab38f4b9ebee6a7178db7eb87070adf0aae1", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000111", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303892.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-50d1855f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about hydrazine, $ \\mathrm{N}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{4} $", "marks": 13, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "This is the displayed formula for a molecule of hydrazine. Complete the dot-and-cross diagram for hydrazine.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-50d1855f-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303915.png", "width": 223, "format": "png", "height": 195, "sha256": "d9fc485176365538eb631d2368fa873cf1b6bd36ef8ad746ec4ee8027e1c2f4e", "assetId": "img:d9fc485176365538eb631d2368fa873cf1b6bd36ef8ad746ec4ee8027e1c2f4e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000126", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303915.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789064303919.png", "width": 895, "format": "png", "height": 647, "sha256": "d92d09a08f358711be03a230f4cf9cde6570b402b0dd37f487d1716d97ed333a", "assetId": "img:d92d09a08f358711be03a230f4cf9cde6570b402b0dd37f487d1716d97ed333a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000128", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789064303919.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "This is the equation for the decomposition of hydrazine. $3 \\mathrm {N} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm {N H} _ {3} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {N} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ The equation can be shown using displayed formulae. The table gives the relevant bond energies.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-50d1855f-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303943.png", "width": 1023, "format": "png", "height": 718, "sha256": "9a9d674bd31ce9fe753b0fea55a17abe47c50fab0e93f0ab50bbe13b88de0dad", "assetId": "img:9a9d674bd31ce9fe753b0fea55a17abe47c50fab0e93f0ab50bbe13b88de0dad", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000132", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303943.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the data in the table to calculate the total energy needed to break all the bonds in the reactants.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-50d1855f-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Use the data in the table to calculate the total energy released when all the bonds in the products are made.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-50d1855f-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ , in kJ/mol, for the reaction.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-50d1855f-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain, in terms of bonds broken and bonds made, why this reaction is exothermic.", "label": "b-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-50d1855f-pb-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A sample of hydrazine is completely decomposed. This is the equation for the decomposition of hydrazine. $3 \\mathrm {N} _ {2} \\mathrm {H} _ {4} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightarrow 4 \\mathrm {N H} _ {3} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {N} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ The products of the decomposition are bubbled through $ 1 1 0 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of water. The ammonia completely dissolves in the water, but nitrogen is insoluble in water. The nitrogen has a volume of $ 1 5 7 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ at room temperature and pressure (rtp). Calculate the concentration, in mol/dm $ ^{3} $ , of the ammonia solution. Give your answer to 3 significant figures. [for a gas at rtp,molar volume = 24000 $ cm^{3} $]", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-50d1855f-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303915.png", "width": 223, "format": "png", "height": 195, "sha256": "d9fc485176365538eb631d2368fa873cf1b6bd36ef8ad746ec4ee8027e1c2f4e", "assetId": "img:d9fc485176365538eb631d2368fa873cf1b6bd36ef8ad746ec4ee8027e1c2f4e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000126", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303915.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789064303919.png", "width": 895, "format": "png", "height": 647, "sha256": "d92d09a08f358711be03a230f4cf9cde6570b402b0dd37f487d1716d97ed333a", "assetId": "img:d92d09a08f358711be03a230f4cf9cde6570b402b0dd37f487d1716d97ed333a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000128", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789064303919.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789064303943.png", "width": 1023, "format": "png", "height": 718, "sha256": "9a9d674bd31ce9fe753b0fea55a17abe47c50fab0e93f0ab50bbe13b88de0dad", "assetId": "img:9a9d674bd31ce9fe753b0fea55a17abe47c50fab0e93f0ab50bbe13b88de0dad", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000132", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789064303943.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-50d1855f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000134"]}], "paperTotal": 70, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 7, "2": 5, "4": 13, "6": 13, "7": 13}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "January 2023", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": "P71894A", "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_2C_MS_2301", "canonicalDocumentId": "36681d06-86ad-5e7d-9d9d-43c97e6d34e9"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1", "marks": 3, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-36681d06-q1", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "name of the part of this atom labelled A nucleus"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "number of the group that contains this element2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "number of the period that contains this element3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(species X has) three more protons than electrons/three fewer electrons than protons"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW (the atom which formed species X has) lost three electrons"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE refs to Group 3"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 same number of protons/both have 17 protons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 different number of neutrons/one has two neutrons more/one has 18 neutrons and the other has 20 neutrons"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE al