ing", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "7: bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "7: bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (b)(i)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (ii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (c)(i)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: (ii)", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}, {"detail": "orphan row before first entry: Total 14", "source": "MS_WARNING", "msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "warning", "questionNumber": null}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "June 2023", "subject": null, "duration": "Morning (Time: 1 hour 15 minutes)", "examDate": "Tuesday 13 June 2023", "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH1/2CR", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "8569da15-3df7-50f0-bc3d-cde09e9a533c"}, "warnings": ["Q7: incomplete MCQ option set folded into stem"], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) The table lists three subatomic particles. Complete the table by giving the missing information.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the position of some elements in the Periodic Table. Use the Periodic Table on page 2 to help you answer this question.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-8569da15-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "How are elements arranged in the Periodic Table? A increasing atomic number B increasing melting point C increasing reactivity D increasing relative atomic mass", "label": "b-i", "marks": 1, "partId": "q01-8569da15-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Which statement is correct about the position of phosphorus, P, in the Periodic Table? A Group 2 and Period 5 B Group 3 and Period 5 C Group 5 and Period 2 D Group 5 and Period 3", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-8569da15-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain which of the four elements in the diagram is least reactive.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-8569da15-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-8569da15", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000026", "e000028"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The table gives some information about the halogens, fluorine, chlorine, bromine and iodine.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the table by giving the missing information.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8569da15-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Describe a test to show that a colourless liquid is an alkene.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8569da15-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student is given an aqueous solution of chlorine and an aqueous solution of sodium iodide. The student mixes the two solutions. Explain the colour change that occurs.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-8569da15-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-8569da15", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000044"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) The box gives the names of some metals. Use words from the box to answer these questions. (i) Identify the metal that burns with a bright white flame. (ii) Explain which metal is most likely to be found in the Earth as an uncombined element.", "marks": 6, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Steel is an alloy of iron and carbon. The diagram shows how the particles are arranged in steel.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8569da15-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069758460.png", "width": 999, "format": "png", "height": 540, "sha256": "40aa5fd26d45777fbd0fa1bf29c0b28c83268ecf3f5c857982354c8b21d05c6e", "assetId": "img:40aa5fd26d45777fbd0fa1bf29c0b28c83268ecf3f5c857982354c8b21d05c6e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000058", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069758460.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State what is meant by the term alloy.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8569da15-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why an alloy is less malleable than a pure metal.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-8569da15-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069758460.png", "width": 999, "format": "png", "height": 540, "sha256": "40aa5fd26d45777fbd0fa1bf29c0b28c83268ecf3f5c857982354c8b21d05c6e", "assetId": "img:40aa5fd26d45777fbd0fa1bf29c0b28c83268ecf3f5c857982354c8b21d05c6e", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000058", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069758460.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-8569da15", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000052"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) The diagram shows a fractionating column used to separate crude oil into fractions. (i) Give one use for refinery gases and one use for bitumen. refinery gases bitumen (ii) Give a reason why refinery gases rise to the top of the column. (iii) State what must happen to the crude oil before it is pumped into the column.", "marks": 9, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "There is a low demand for some of the hydrocarbons obtained from crude oil. Catalytic cracking can be used to convert low-demand hydrocarbons into more useful products.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8569da15-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the conditions needed for cracking.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8569da15-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The cracking of tetradecane is shown in the equation. $\\mathrm {C} _ {1 4} \\mathrm {H} _ {3 0} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {C} _ {3} \\mathrm {H} _ {6} + \\mathrm {C} _ {8} \\mathrm {H} _ {1 8}$ Explain why there is a high demand for both of the products.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q04-8569da15-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 4, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069758476.png", "width": 930, "format": "png", "height": 642, "sha256": "5c683e5f71adaff69cd3ec5a975faa2b99b40aa80f02aacaac11dcb050e93f21", "assetId": "img:5c683e5f71adaff69cd3ec5a975faa2b99b40aa80f02aacaac11dcb050e93f21", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000063", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069758476.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-8569da15", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This question is about the insoluble salt, lead(II) bromide. Lead(II) bromide can be made by a precipitation reaction. This is the equation for the reaction. $\\mathrm {P b} \\left(\\mathrm {N O} _ {3}\\right) _ {2} (\\mathrm {a q}) + 2 \\mathrm {K B r} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {P b B r} _ {2} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {K N O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q})$", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Describe how solutions of lead nitrate and potassium bromide can be used to make a pure, dry sample of lead(II) bromide.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8569da15-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A solution containing 0.150 mol of lead(II) nitrate is reacted with an excess of potassium bromide solution. A mass of 49.6 g of pure, dry lead(II) bromide is produced. Show, by calculation, that the percentage yield of lead(II) bromide is 90.1%. [for $ \\mathrm{P b B r_{2}} $ $ M_{\\mathrm{r}}=3 6 7 $]", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8569da15-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A student investigates the change in electrical conductivity as dilute lead(II) nitrate solution is added to dilute potassium bromide solution. This is the student''s method. Step 1 add $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of potassium bromide solution to a beaker Step 2 measure the electrical conductivity of the solution Step 3 add $ 1 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of lead(II) nitrate solution to the beaker Step 4 stir the mixture Step 5 measure the electrical conductivity of the mixture Repeat steps 3,4 and 5 until a total of $ 5 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of lead(II) nitrate solution has been added. The table shows the student''s results.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8569da15-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Plot the student''s results on the grid.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8569da15-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a line of best fit, ignoring the anomalous result.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8569da15-pc-ii", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069758502.png", "width": 1354, "format": "png", "height": 1140, "sha256": "9480a31b4e6516e399a61ba28e5fff9702d35457a3e4f287305aa9044e2b26bd", "assetId": "img:9480a31b4e6516e399a61ba28e5fff9702d35457a3e4f287305aa9044e2b26bd", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000094", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069758502.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain the shape of the graph.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8569da15-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Suggest a mistake the student could have made to cause the anomalous result.", "label": "c-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8569da15-pc-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Further $ 1 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ volumes of lead(II) nitrate are added to the beaker so the lead(II) nitrate is in excess. Predict what will happen to the conductivity of the mixture when the lead(II) nitrate is in excess.", "label": "c-v", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8569da15-pc-v", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the electrolysis of molten lead(II) bromide, $ \\mathrm{P b B r}_{2} $ This is the ionic half-equation for the formation of bromine at electrode A. $2 \\mathrm {B r} ^ {-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {B r} _ {2} + 2 \\mathrm {e} ^ {-}$ Give a reason why this half-equation shows oxidation.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-8569da15-pd", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069758513.png", "width": 586, "format": "png", "height": 484, "sha256": "1cdb42d22af185dcb06b9bf3cff9b98ffa904e6f07685719e284e48fb6867b0a", "assetId": "img:1cdb42d22af185dcb06b9bf3cff9b98ffa904e6f07685719e284e48fb6867b0a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000100", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069758513.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069758502.png", "width": 1354, "format": "png", "height": 1140, "sha256": "9480a31b4e6516e399a61ba28e5fff9702d35457a3e4f287305aa9044e2b26bd", "assetId": "img:9480a31b4e6516e399a61ba28e5fff9702d35457a3e4f287305aa9044e2b26bd", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000094", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069758502.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069758513.png", "width": 586, "format": "png", "height": 484, "sha256": "1cdb42d22af185dcb06b9bf3cff9b98ffa904e6f07685719e284e48fb6867b0a", "assetId": "img:1cdb42d22af185dcb06b9bf3cff9b98ffa904e6f07685719e284e48fb6867b0a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000100", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069758513.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q05-8569da15", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000091"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "(a) Describe the forces of attraction in metallic bonding.", "marks": 15, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "When a small piece of potassium is added to water, bubbles of hydrogen gas are observed.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-8569da15-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the test for hydrogen gas.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-8569da15-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give two other observations that would be made.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-8569da15-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why lithium is less reactive than potassium. Refer to atomic structure in your answer.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-8569da15-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "This is the equation for the reaction between sodium and water. $2 \\mathrm {N a} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {I}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {N a O H} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ A mass of 0.750g of sodium is reacted with an excess of water. Calculate the volume of hydrogen gas produced, in $ cm^{3} $ , at room temperature. [molar volume of hydrogen at rtp = 24000 $ cm^{3} $] [for Na, $ A_{\\mathrm{r}}=2 3 $] Give your answer to three significant figures. $\\mathrm {v o l u m e o f h y d r o g e n} =$", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-8569da15-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "This is the equation for the reaction between sodium hydroxide and sulfuric acid. $2 \\mathrm {N a O H} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N a} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} + 2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O}$ A volume of $ 2 5. 0 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of sodium hydroxide solution is completely neutralised by $ 1 6. 3 \\mathrm{c m}^{3} $ of $ 0. 0 5 0 0 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ sulfuric acid. Calculate the concentration of the sodium hydroxide solution in mol / dm $ ^{3}. $ $\\mathrm {m o l} / \\mathrm {d m} ^ {3}$", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-8569da15-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-8569da15", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Methanol is made by the reaction between hydrogen and carbon monoxide. This is the equation for the reaction. $2 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) + \\mathrm {C O} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm {C H} _ {3} \\mathrm {O H} (\\mathrm {g}) \\quad \\Delta H = - 1 1 9 \\mathrm {k J / m o l}$ A mixture of hydrogen and carbon monoxide is left until dynamic equilibrium is reached.", "marks": 14, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Give two characteristics of a reaction at dynamic equilibrium.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8569da15-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give a reason why adding a catalyst does not affect the yield of methanol.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8569da15-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The temperature of the reaction mixture is decreased at constant pressure Explain the effect of this change on the yield of methanol.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8569da15-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The pressure of the reaction mixture is increased at constant temperature. Explain the effect of this change on the yield of methanol.", "label": "a-iv", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8569da15-pa-iv", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "This equation shows the displayed formulae for the reactants and product. The table gives the bond energies for the bonds in the reactants and product.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8569da15-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069758543.png", "width": 719, "format": "png", "height": 216, "sha256": "cd302479a425752fc6639a012059c4ad22ad0e6566b1ac54e758fccdf6e0be65", "assetId": "img:cd302479a425752fc6639a012059c4ad22ad0e6566b1ac54e758fccdf6e0be65", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000131", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069758543.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Show that the molar enthalpy change, $ \\Delta H $ , for the reaction is $ -1 1 9 \\mathrm{k J} / \\mathrm{m o l}. $", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8569da15-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why this reaction is exothermic.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8569da15-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the displayed formula of an ester that is made from methanol and a carboxylic acid.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8569da15-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069758551.png", "width": 581, "format": "png", "height": 302, "sha256": "cd21c6fb3128e50a573bd80464e7e13f4ca99f1f5e4466c4d18c9f93a1efd684", "assetId": "img:cd21c6fb3128e50a573bd80464e7e13f4ca99f1f5e4466c4d18c9f93a1efd684", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000137", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069758551.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw a circle around the functional group of the ester.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8569da15-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Give the displayed formula of the carboxylic acid used to make this ester.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-8569da15-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069758543.png", "width": 719, "format": "png", "height": 216, "sha256": "cd302479a425752fc6639a012059c4ad22ad0e6566b1ac54e758fccdf6e0be65", "assetId": "img:cd302479a425752fc6639a012059c4ad22ad0e6566b1ac54e758fccdf6e0be65", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000131", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069758543.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789069758551.png", "width": 581, "format": "png", "height": 302, "sha256": "cd21c6fb3128e50a573bd80464e7e13f4ca99f1f5e4466c4d18c9f93a1efd684", "assetId": "img:cd21c6fb3128e50a573bd80464e7e13f4ca99f1f5e4466c4d18c9f93a1efd684", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000137", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789069758551.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q07-8569da15", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000133"]}], "paperTotal": 70, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 6, "2": 7, "3": 6, "4": 9, "6": 15, "7": 14}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "Summer 2023", "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": "P71953A", "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": "4CH1_2CR_2306_MS", "canonicalDocumentId": "0647b12b-43e7-5728-8be9-61999a85d2fb"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-0647b12b-q1a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "All 4 correct=(2)2 or 3 correct=(1)0 or 1 correct=(0)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Subatomic particle"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Relative mass"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Relative charge"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "proton"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "+1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "neutron"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "A increasing atomic numberB is incorrect as elements are not arranged in order of increasing melting pointC is incorrect as elements are not arranged in order of increasing reactivityD is incorrect as elements are not arranged in order of increasing relative atomic mass"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "D Group 5 Period 3A is incorrect as phosphorus is not in Group 2 and Period 5B is incorrect as phosphorus is not in Group 3 and Period 5C is incorrect as phosphorus is not in Group 5 and Period 2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 Xe/xenonM2 because it has a full outer shell(of electrons)/8 electrons in the outer shell"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 dep on M1IGNORE refs to noble gas"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 6"}], "answerText": "", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-0647b12b-q2a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "Colour"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "pale green/yellow"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "green"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "red-brown"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "grey"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 bromine water/bromine solutionM2 turns colourless/decolourised"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW aqueous bromineIGNORE any starting colourM2 is dep on mention of bromine in M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 (mixture) turns (from colourless) to brownM2 iodine/ $I_{2}$ is displacedOR(chlorine reacts with iodide ions) to produce/form iodineM3 (because) chlorine is more reactive (than iodine)"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW red-brown/orange-brownREJECT iodideIGNORE a displacement reaction occursM2 can be scored by $I_{2}$ as a product in a balanced equation or by a word equationALLOW reverse argumentREJECT iodide, except if already penalised in M2"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 7"}], "answerText": "Physical state at room temperature\ngas\ngas\nliquid\nsolid", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-0647b12b-q3", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "magnesium"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 gold"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE silver"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 is the least reactive"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 DEP on M1"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW it is (the most) unreactive"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(an alloy is) a mixture of metals"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(an alloy is) a mixture of a metal and another metal"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW (an alloy is) a mixture of a metal and carbon"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT references to compounds / combining of metals"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2", "marks": 2, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-0647b12b-q2", "guidance": [{"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW lattice / layers / rows of atoms are disrupted / distorted / are less regular / are irregular"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 6"}], "answerText": "(ii)\nM1 the regular arrangement of atoms is distorted / disrupted / disturbed OWTTE\nOR\ncarbon atoms are smaller than iron atoms / because iron atoms are larger than carbon atoms\nM2 (therefore) it is more difficult for the atoms/layers to slide over one another\nALLOW carbon and iron atoms are of different sizes\nALLOW the atoms are not the same size / the atoms are different sizes\nIGNORE references to the strength of metallic bonds", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4", "marks": 2, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-0647b12b-q4", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "refinery gases:(fuel for) heating/cookingbitumen:tar/road surfacing/road building/roofing"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW bottled gasALLOW roads"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "any one from:refinery gases have the low(est) boiling pointORrefinery gases do not condense in the column"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW they are the most volatileREJECT refs to melting point"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "it is heated/vaporised"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE any temperatures givenALLOW boiled"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 temperature 600-700℃M2 catalyst alumina/silica/zeolites/aluminium oxide/silicon dioxide"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW any temperature in the rangeIGNORE pressures"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "an explanation containing any three of the following points:M1 alkenes/propene/C3H6can be used to make(addition) polymers/plastics"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW used to make poly(propene)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(because) they have double bonds/areunsaturatedM3 shorter alkanes/octane/C8H18are used as fuels/petrolM4(because) they have lower boiling points/are more flammable"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW to make alcohols/propanol"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "Total 9"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)\nIGNORE used as fuels", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "5(a)", "marks": 4, "number": 5, "entryId": "ms-0647b12b-q5a", "guidance": [{"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE volumes"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE heating at this stage"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT an indication that solids are mixed for M1"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 filter (the solid lead bromide)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3 wash (using deionised water)"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE any washing before filtering"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4 method of drying"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "eg leave to dry / dry in an oven / leave in a warm place / dry with filter paper"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT direct heating of final product for M4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT if solid is washed again after drying for M4"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 0.150×367 OR 55.05g"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 (49.6÷55.05)×100"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "OR"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW 0.135"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 (49.6÷367) OR 0.1351"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 (0.1351÷0.150)×100"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW a final answer of 90% by either method"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT (49.6÷0.15)=330.67 (330.67÷367)×100 for both marks"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)(i)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "all 6 points plotted ± half a square"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "straight line of best fit ignoring the anomalous result at volume=20cm3"}], "answerText": "M1 mix / react the two solutions (together)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "6(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 6, "entryId": "ms-0647b12b-q6a", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT(electrostatic attraction between positive) nuclei of (metal) atoms"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2(and) delocalised electrons"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT any references to ionic bonding/sharing of electrons/intermolecular forces for both marks"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(b)(i)(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(squeaky) pop with lighted splint/lit with a(Bunsen) flame"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNOREjust‘burns with a squeaky pop’"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "any two from:"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT use of glowing splint"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1lilac/ purple flame"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW flame"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2potassium melts/turns into a ball"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT other colours"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(c)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3potassium moves on the surface"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW floats"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M4potassium gets smaller"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW potassium disappears/dissolves"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNOREfizzing/bubbles etc"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "an explanation linking the following points:"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1lithium has fewer shells than potassium"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW lithium has smaller atoms than potassium"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW(outer shell) electron in lithium is closer to the nucleus"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW correct electron configurations"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2outer shell/outer electron in lithium is more strongly attracted to the nucleus"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT‘fewer outer shells’"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M3(so in lithium the outer shell) electron is less easily lost"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOWouter shell/outer electron in lithium is less shielded(by inner shells)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "ACCEPT valence electron"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW reverse argument throughout for potassium"}], "answerText": "M1(electrostatic attraction between positive ions", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "7", "marks": 2, "number": 7, "entryId": "ms-0647b12b-q7", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "M1 the forward and reverse reactions occur at the same rate"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 so the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW so the moles of reactants and products remain constant"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "REJECT so the concentrations of reactants and products are the same"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(ii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "a catalyst increases the rate of (both) the forwards and the reverse reaction equally"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW has the same effect on the rate of forward and reverse reaction"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iii)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 yield increases"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 the (forward) reaction is exothermic"}, {"kind": "allow", "text": "ALLOW the reverse reaction is endothermic"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE any references to Le Chatelier''s Principle (moves/shifts)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 dep on M1 correct or missing"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "(iv)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M1 yield increases"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 there are more moles of (gaseous) reactants than products/there are fewer (gaseous) moles on the right hand side/there are 3 moles (of gas) on the left and 1 mole (of gas) on the right ORA"}, {"kind": "ignore", "text": "IGNORE any references to Le Chatelier''s Principle (moves/shifts)"}, {"kind": "note", "text": "M2 dep on M1 correct or missing"}], "answerText": "(a)(i)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}], "icTable": null, "warnings": ["1(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1(a): bare integer cell \"0\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "1(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "2(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "2(a): bare integer cell \"3\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "3: bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "3: bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "4: bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "4: bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "4: bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "4: bare integer cell \"3\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "5(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "5(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "5(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "orphan row before first entry: (iii)", "orphan row before first entry: REJECT directly proportional", "orphan row before first entry: ACCEPT negative correlation between volume of lead(II) nitrate and electrical conductivity", "orphan row before first entry: ALLOW the conductivity increases when the volume of lead(II) nitrate decrease", "orphan row before first entry: M2 there are fewer ions in the mixture", "orphan row before first entry: (iv)", "orphan row before first entry: REJECT the student added less /too little lead(II) nitrate solution", "orphan row before first entry: (v)", "orphan row before first entry: (d)", "orphan row before first entry: Total 13", "6(a): bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "6(a): bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "6(a): bare integer cell \"3\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "orphan row before first entry: (d)", "orphan row before first entry: (e)", "orphan row before first entry: Total15", "7: bare integer cell \"1\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "7: bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "7: bare integer cell \"2\" skipped (rubric/rowspan ambiguity)", "orphan row before first entry: (b)(i)", "orphan row before first entry: (ii)", "orphan row before first entry: (c)(i)", "orphan row before first entry: (ii)", "orphan row before first entry: Total 14"], "paperTotal": null, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "questionTotals": {}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-ms-v1"}', '{"findings": [{"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 6, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "1"}, {"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 7, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "2"}, {"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 6, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "3"}, {"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 9, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "4"}, {"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 15, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "6"}, {"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": 14, "severity": "qp-only", "questionNumber": "7"}, {"msMarks": null, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "identity-mismatch", "questionNumber": "paper"}], "msPaperTotal": null, "qpPaperTotal": 70, "mismatchCount": 1, "paperTotalConflict": false}', NULL, '2026-09-13 13:03:38.68267+00');
INSERT INTO public.glm_ocr_bridge_records VALUES ('0894e557-84ae-4802-9b2f-f1f5549dc1de', '3a6b72b8-077b-4cb0-af32-ac9603696a15', 'glm-ocr-v1', '3c53d76f-fd56-5fb4-b3ec-5d9f61260668', '30157e99-1a53-5173-a833-5d25f9762f16', 'ec852bc6-8839-42a6-8aad-7e3ec729395e', '89ea9fa4-ae13-4633-bbaa-87bae12c1ef1', '6a9a10e439ec605a25bf38146db8871eb65bb1d55b9099d53077fc59930a12a0', '0588a3f20148d1e39f4ecfca209ad4c5cf51496dd55cf946027cabcc6d954615', 'glm-ocr-qp-v1+glm-ocr-ms-v1', 'OK', '[{"detail": "Q1: QP total 4 vs MS total 4 (match)", "source": "RECONCILIATION", "msMarks": 4, "qpMarks": 4, "severity": "match", "questionNumber": "1"}, {"detail": "Q2: QP total 7 vs MS total 7 (match)", "source": "RECONCILIATION", "msMarks": 7, "qpMarks": 7, "severity": "match", "questionNumber": "2"}, {"detail": "Q3: QP total 7 vs MS total 7 (match)", "source": "RECONCILIATION", "msMarks": 7, "qpMarks": 7, "severity": "match", "questionNumber": "3"}, {"detail": "Q4: QP total 6 vs MS total 6 (match)", "source": "RECONCILIATION", "msMarks": 6, "qpMarks": 6, "severity": "match", "questionNumber": "4"}, {"detail": "Q5: QP total 8 vs MS total 8 (match)", "source": "RECONCILIATION", "msMarks": 8, "qpMarks": 8, "severity": "match", "questionNumber": "5"}, {"detail": "Q6: QP total 11 vs MS total 11 (match)", "source": "RECONCILIATION", "msMarks": 11, "qpMarks": 11, "severity": "match", "questionNumber": "6"}, {"detail": "Q9: QP total 12 vs MS total 12 (match)", "source": "RECONCILIATION", "msMarks": 12, "qpMarks": 12, "severity": "match", "questionNumber": "9"}, {"detail": "Q7: QP total unknown vs MS total 9 (ms-only)", "source": "RECONCILIATION", "msMarks": 9, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "ms-only", "questionNumber": "7"}, {"detail": "Q8: QP total unknown vs MS total 6 (ms-only)", "source": "RECONCILIATION", "msMarks": 6, "qpMarks": null, "severity": "ms-only", "questionNumber": "8"}]', '{"paper": {"board": "Edexcel", "session": "Specimen 2017", "subject": null, "duration": "Time: 1 hour 15 minutes", "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": "4CH1/2C", "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "3c53d76f-fd56-5fb4-b3ec-5d9f61260668"}, "warnings": [], "questions": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The table shows some properties of four substances, P, Q, R and S. Use the information in the table to answer the following questions. You may use each letter once, more than once or not at all. Choose a substance that:", "marks": 4, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "is a solid at $ 3 0 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ A substance P B substance Q C substance R D substance S", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-3c53d76f-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "is a liquid at $ 2 5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}. $ A substance P B substance Q C substance R D substance S", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-3c53d76f-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "is an ionic compound. A substance P B substance Q C substance R A substance P D substance S B substance Q", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-3c53d76f-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "is a metal. C substance R D substance S", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q01-3c53d76f-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 1, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q01-3c53d76f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000023"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "This is a method used to measure the solubility of a solid in water: add an excess of solid to some water in a boiling tube and stir measure the temperature of the saturated solution formed weigh an empty evaporating basin pour some of the saturated solution into the evaporating basin weigh the basin and contents heat the evaporating basin to remove all of the water weigh the evaporating basin and remaining solid.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The table shows the results of an experiment using this method. Calculate the mass of solid obtained and the mass of water removed. mass of water = ... g", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-3c53d76f-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "In another experiment, at a different temperature, the mass of solid obtained is 10.5 g and the mass of water removed is 16.8 g. Calculate the solubility of the solid, in g per 100 g of water, at this temperature. solubility = g per 100 g of water", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-3c53d76f-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "If the evaporating basin is heated too strongly some of the solid decomposes to form a gas. Explain how this strong heating would affect the value of the calculated solubility of the solid.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q02-3c53d76f-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 2, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q02-3c53d76f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000056"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Astatine, bromine, chlorine, fluorine and iodine are all halogens. They are found in Group 7 of the Periodic Table.", "marks": 7, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Predict which halogen has the lightest colour.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3c53d76f-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name a halogen that is a solid at room temperature.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3c53d76f-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Bromine can be obtained from the bromide ions in sea water. Chlorine is bubbled into sea water. The chlorine oxidises the bromide ions to bromine atoms. The bromine atoms then form bromine molecules.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3c53d76f-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the equation to show how bromine atoms are formed from bromide ions. $\\mathrm {C l} _ {2} + \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\mathrm {B r} ^ {-} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {C l} ^ {-} + \\dots \\dots \\dots \\dots \\mathrm {B r}$", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3c53d76f-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why this reaction is described as the oxidation of bromide ions.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3c53d76f-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write an equation to show how bromine atoms form bromine molecules.", "label": "c-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3c53d76f-pc-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Boron and fluorine form a covalent compound that has the molecular formula $ \\mathrm{B F}_{3} $ Draw a dot-and-cross diagram to show the arrangement of the outer electrons in a molecule of $ \\mathrm{B F}_{3} $ Use crosses (X) to represent the outer electrons of boron. Use dots (•) to represent the outer electrons of fluorine.", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q03-3c53d76f-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 3, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q03-3c53d76f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "A technician finds a white solid in an unlabelled beaker. She knows that the solid is one of the following substances: potassium chloride potassium sulfate sodium chloride sodium sulfate. Describe how the technician can use chemical tests to identify the solid in the beaker.", "marks": 6, "parts": [], "number": 4, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q04-3c53d76f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Zinc metal is obtained from sulfide ores. The most common ore of zinc is sphalerite, which contains zinc sulfide (ZnS) and a small amount of cadmium sulfide (CdS). The stages involved in the extraction of zinc from sphalerite are: Stage1 Sphalerite is strongly heated in air. $2 \\mathrm {Z n S} (\\mathrm {s}) + 3 \\mathrm {O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {Z n O} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {S O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ $2 \\mathrm {C d S} (\\mathrm {s}) + 3 \\mathrm {O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm {C d O} (\\mathrm {s}) + 2 \\mathrm {S O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ Stage 2 The mixture of oxides is reacted with sulfuric acid. $\\mathrm {Z n O (s)} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {Z n S O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {I})$ $\\mathrm {C d O (s)} + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {S O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C d S O} _ {4} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O (l)}$ Stage 3 Zinc dust is added to the solution containing zinc sulfate and cadmium sulfate to remove the cadmium ions. $\\mathrm {C d} ^ {2 +} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {Z n} (\\mathrm {s}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {C d} (\\mathrm {s}) + \\mathrm {Z n} ^ {2 +} (\\mathrm {a q})$ Stage 4 The solid cadmium is filtered off and the pure zinc sulfate solution is electrolysed.", "marks": 8, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "State how the reaction in stage 3 shows that zinc is more reactive than cadmium.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-3c53d76f-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "During the electrolysis in stage 4, zinc is deposited on the cathode. Write an ionic half-equation for the reaction that occurs.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-3c53d76f-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Complete the ionic half-equation for the reaction occurring at the anode. $\\dots \\dots H _ {2} O \\rightarrow \\dots \\dots H ^ {+} + O _ {2} + \\dots \\dots e ^ {-}$", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-3c53d76f-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain how the pH of the solution surrounding the anode changes during the electrolysis.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-3c53d76f-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Zinc is mixed with copper to make the alloy brass. Explain why brass is harder than pure copper.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q05-3c53d76f-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 5, "figures": [], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q05-3c53d76f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "The mineral nesquehonite is a form of hydrated magnesium carbonate. The formula, $ \\mathrm{M g C O_{3}. x H_{2} O} $ , shows that nesquehonite contains water of crystallisation. When a sample of nesquohonite is heated gently, the water of crystallisation is given off and anhydrous magnesium carbonate is left. Six students are each given a sample of nesquehonite of mass 6.1 g. The students heat their samples for different times. The samples are then allowed to cool before being reweighed. The table shows their results.", "marks": 11, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "Plot these results on the grid provided. The last two points have been plotted for you. Draw a straight line of best fit for the points you have plotted. mass of sample after heating / g", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3c53d76f-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789104627947.png", "width": 1242, "format": "png", "height": 1075, "sha256": "cb7bc2b762b4f4ff3fd2258f5cdf146177dc99c2f54bd28343dda7e1a62fa989", "assetId": "img:cb7bc2b762b4f4ff3fd2258f5cdf146177dc99c2f54bd28343dda7e1a62fa989", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000113", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104627947.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Predict the mass of sample remaining after heating for 2.5 minutes. Show on the graph how you obtain your answer.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3c53d76f-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why the last two masses in the table are exactly the same.", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3c53d76f-pc", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "A sample of nesquehonite contains 1.68 g of $ \\mathrm{M g C O_{3}} $ and 1.08 g of $ \\mathrm{H_{2}O}. $ Calculate the value of x in the formula $ \\mathrm{M g C O_{3}. x H_{2}O} $ $ [ M_{r} $ of $ \\mathrm{M g C O_{3}} $ = 84; $ M_{r} $ of $ \\mathrm{H_{2}O}=18] $", "label": "d", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3c53d76f-pd", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "When anhydrous magnesium carbonate is heated strongly, it decomposes. The equation for the reaction is: $\\mathrm {M g C O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {s}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {M g O} (\\mathrm {s}) + \\mathrm {C O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g})$ Calculate the volume of carbon dioxide formed in $ cm^{3} $ , at rtp, when 4.2 g of anhydrous magnesium carbonate are completely decomposed. $ [ M_{r} $ of $ MgCO_{3}=84] $ [Assume that the molar volume at rtp of carbon dioxide is $ 24\\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ ]", "label": "e", "marks": null, "partId": "q06-3c53d76f-pe", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 6, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789104627947.png", "width": 1242, "format": "png", "height": 1075, "sha256": "cb7bc2b762b4f4ff3fd2258f5cdf146177dc99c2f54bd28343dda7e1a62fa989", "assetId": "img:cb7bc2b762b4f4ff3fd2258f5cdf146177dc99c2f54bd28343dda7e1a62fa989", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000113", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104627947.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q06-3c53d76f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": ["e000110"]}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Ammonia is manufactured on a large scale and is used to make fertilisers such as ammonium nitrate $ \\mathrm{(N H_{4} N O_{3})}. $", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The first stage in the manufacture of ammonium nitrate is to react ammonia gas with oxygen gas. $4 \\mathrm {N H} _ {3} (\\mathrm {g}) + 5 \\mathrm {O} _ {2} (\\mathrm {g}) \\rightleftharpoons 4 \\mathrm {N O} (\\mathrm {g}) + 6 \\mathrm {H} _ {2} \\mathrm {O} (\\mathrm {g}) \\quad \\Delta H = - 9 0 7 \\mathrm {k J / m o l}$ The reaction is carried out at a pressure of about 10 atm and at a temperature of $ 8 0 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ , in the presence of a catalyst. If the mixture is left for long enough in a sealed container, the reaction reaches a position of dynamic equilibrium. Graph A shows how the percentage of nitrogen monoxide (NO) in the equilibrium mixture varies with temperature at constant pressure. Graph B shows how the percentage of nitrogen monoxide (NO) in the equilibrium mixture varies with pressure at constant temperature.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3c53d76f-pa", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789104627956.png", "width": 616, "format": "png", "height": 426, "sha256": "e31f6d20fc3918cb05c953cd62ac3668e81d126388832d2717574e7d7c2c5e72", "assetId": "img:e31f6d20fc3918cb05c953cd62ac3668e81d126388832d2717574e7d7c2c5e72", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000128", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104627956.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789104627963.png", "width": 613, "format": "png", "height": 419, "sha256": "7d58eb8cfa8ea281df4441f44bc0a1077e5d5b6a43615f6f6689f6c5017d603c", "assetId": "img:7d58eb8cfa8ea281df4441f44bc0a1077e5d5b6a43615f6f6689f6c5017d603c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000129", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789104627963.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why the percentage of NO at equilibrium decreases in each case.", "label": "a-i", "marks": 4, "partId": "q07-3c53d76f-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.8, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why the use of a catalyst has no effect on the position of equilibrium in a reversible reaction.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3c53d76f-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The second stage in the manufacture of ammonium nitrate is to convert the nitrogen monoxide into nitric acid. The nitric acid is then reacted with concentrated aqueous ammonia as shown in this equation. $\\mathrm {H N O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q}) + \\mathrm {N H} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm {N H} _ {4} \\mathrm {N O} _ {3} (\\mathrm {a q})$", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3c53d76f-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State, in terms of proton transfer, why this reaction is classified as an acid-base reaction.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3c53d76f-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Calculate the volume of $ 1 4. 8 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ aqueous ammonia that is required to exactly neutralise $ 1 5 0 \\mathrm{d m}^{3} $ of a solution of nitric acid of concentration $ 1 5. 8 \\mathrm{m o l} / \\mathrm{d m}^{3}. $", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q07-3c53d76f-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": ["volume of aqueous ammonia"]}], "number": 7, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789104627956.png", "width": 616, "format": "png", "height": 426, "sha256": "e31f6d20fc3918cb05c953cd62ac3668e81d126388832d2717574e7d7c2c5e72", "assetId": "img:e31f6d20fc3918cb05c953cd62ac3668e81d126388832d2717574e7d7c2c5e72", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000128", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104627956.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789104627963.png", "width": 613, "format": "png", "height": 419, "sha256": "7d58eb8cfa8ea281df4441f44bc0a1077e5d5b6a43615f6f6689f6c5017d603c", "assetId": "img:7d58eb8cfa8ea281df4441f44bc0a1077e5d5b6a43615f6f6689f6c5017d603c", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000129", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789104627963.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q07-3c53d76f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Polymers can be classified as addition polymers or condensation polymers.", "marks": 0, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "An addition polymer can be formed from the monomer $ \\mathrm{C H_{2}=C H C l} $", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-3c53d76f-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name this monomer.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-3c53d76f-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the addition polymer formed from this monomer.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-3c53d76f-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "The diagram shows the repeat unit of a different addition polymer. Draw the displayed formula of the monomer used to make this polymer.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-3c53d76f-pb", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789104627968.png", "width": 202, "format": "png", "height": 219, "sha256": "1a5e74df0a07d3b21424a872ff7aceb81e47ae0e56ddade1a184b585b48aa17a", "assetId": "img:1a5e74df0a07d3b21424a872ff7aceb81e47ae0e56ddade1a184b585b48aa17a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000143", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104627968.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Polyesters are condensation polymers. The structures of two monomers that are used to make a polyester are: HO—CH $ _{2} $ CH $ _{2} $ —OH", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-3c53d76f-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_2_1789104627975.png", "width": 492, "format": "png", "height": 144, "sha256": "8f30695fc8a4675d35db84475ebc5780de3da7e0c66d64d1dec326d849f9541f", "assetId": "img:8f30695fc8a4675d35db84475ebc5780de3da7e0c66d64d1dec326d849f9541f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000147", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789104627975.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Draw the structure of the repeat unit of the polyester formed from these two monomers.", "label": "c-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-3c53d76f-pc-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Identify the small molecule formed when these two monomers form the polyester.", "label": "c-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q08-3c53d76f-pc-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 8, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789104627968.png", "width": 202, "format": "png", "height": 219, "sha256": "1a5e74df0a07d3b21424a872ff7aceb81e47ae0e56ddade1a184b585b48aa17a", "assetId": "img:1a5e74df0a07d3b21424a872ff7aceb81e47ae0e56ddade1a184b585b48aa17a", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000143", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104627968.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_2_1789104627975.png", "width": 492, "format": "png", "height": 144, "sha256": "8f30695fc8a4675d35db84475ebc5780de3da7e0c66d64d1dec326d849f9541f", "assetId": "img:8f30695fc8a4675d35db84475ebc5780de3da7e0c66d64d1dec326d849f9541f", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000147", "sourceName": "assets/crop_2_1789104627975.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.45, "marksKnown": false, "questionId": "q08-3c53d76f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "stem": "Grapes contain glucose that can be fermented to make ethanol.", "marks": 12, "parts": [{"qwc": false, "text": "The grapes are collected and crushed to produce an aqueous solution containing glucose. Yeast is added to the solution and the mixture is left to ferment at a temperature of about $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ in the absence of air.", "label": "a", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-3c53d76f-pa", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State why yeast is added.", "label": "a-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-3c53d76f-pa-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why another organic compound may also be formed if fermentation is carried out in the presence of air.", "label": "a-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-3c53d76f-pa-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Explain why $ 3 0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} $ is considered to be an optimum temperature for this reaction.", "label": "a-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-3c53d76f-pa-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Ethanol can also be manufactured by reacting ethene with steam.", "label": "b", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-3c53d76f-pb", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Write the chemical equation for this reaction.", "label": "b-i", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-3c53d76f-pb-i", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Name the type of reaction that occurs.", "label": "b-ii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-3c53d76f-pb-ii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "State two conditions used for this reaction in industry.", "label": "b-iii", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-3c53d76f-pb-iii", "figures": [], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}, {"qwc": false, "text": "Grapes also contain esters. The formula of an ester is shown. Deduce the name of the carboxylic acid and the alcohol that can react together to make this ester. carboxylic acid alcohol..", "label": "c", "marks": null, "partId": "q09-3c53d76f-pc", "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789104627986.png", "width": 772, "format": "png", "height": 180, "sha256": "4c6fd732c3f70b94ff5844110eb43c1d2dcf292ab59f0eb900f5d004799405d7", "assetId": "img:4c6fd732c3f70b94ff5844110eb43c1d2dcf292ab59f0eb900f5d004799405d7", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000163", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104627986.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "confidence": 0.55, "answerPrompts": []}], "number": 9, "figures": [{"url": "assets/crop_1_1789104627981.png", "width": 311, "format": "png", "height": 379, "sha256": "31ffd836a4c2c7b9a2ed6a02e1d570f22d49f42d7c8354ff6fa8748cf0b047d4", "assetId": "img:31ffd836a4c2c7b9a2ed6a02e1d570f22d49f42d7c8354ff6fa8748cf0b047d4", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000152", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104627981.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}, {"url": "assets/crop_1_1789104627986.png", "width": 772, "format": "png", "height": 180, "sha256": "4c6fd732c3f70b94ff5844110eb43c1d2dcf292ab59f0eb900f5d004799405d7", "assetId": "img:4c6fd732c3f70b94ff5844110eb43c1d2dcf292ab59f0eb900f5d004799405d7", "mimeType": "image/jpeg", "elementId": "e000163", "sourceName": "assets/crop_1_1789104627986.png", "availability": "available", "formatMismatch": true}], "options": [], "section": null, "confidence": 0.6, "marksKnown": true, "questionId": "q09-3c53d76f", "answerPrompts": [], "numberingStyle": "space", "tableElementIds": []}], "paperTotal": 70, "schemaVersion": "1.0", "sectionTotals": {}, "questionTotals": {"1": 4, "2": 7, "3": 7, "4": 6, "5": 8, "6": 11, "9": 12}, "reviewRequired": true, "extractionMethod": "glm-ocr-qp-v1", "frontMatterFigures": []}', '{"paper": {"board": null, "session": null, "subject": null, "duration": null, "examDate": null, "logNumber": null, "qualification": null, "paperReference": null, "publicationCode": null, "canonicalDocumentId": "30157e99-1a53-5173-a833-5d25f9762f16"}, "entries": [{"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q1a", "guidance": [], "answerText": "A", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(b)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q1b", "guidance": [], "answerText": "C", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(c)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q1c", "guidance": [], "answerText": "B", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "1(d)", "marks": 1, "number": 1, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q1d", "guidance": [], "answerText": "A", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(a)", "marks": 2, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q2a", "guidance": [], "answerText": "·(mass of solid)5.3(g)(1)\n·(mass of water)20.9(g)(1)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [{"ecf": false, "text": "·(mass of solid)5.3(g)", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 1, "rawText": "·(mass of solid)5.3(g)", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "·(mass of water)20.9(g)", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 2, "rawText": "·(mass of water)20.9(g)", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(b)", "marks": 2, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q2b", "guidance": [], "answerText": "·(10.5÷16.8)×100(1)\n·62.5(grams of solid per100g of water)(1)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [{"ecf": false, "text": "·(10.5÷16.8)×100", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 1, "rawText": "·(10.5÷16.8)×100", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "·62.5(grams of solid per100g of water)", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 2, "rawText": "·62.5(grams of solid per100g of water)", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "2(c)", "marks": 3, "number": 2, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q2c", "guidance": [], "answerText": "An explanation that links together the following three points:\n·the gas will escape(1)\n·the mass of solid remaining will be less(than it should be)(1)\n·the value of the calculated solubility will be lower(than it should be)(1)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [{"ecf": false, "text": "An explanation that links together the following three points:\n·the gas will escape", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 1, "rawText": "An explanation that links together the following three points:\n·the gas will escape", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "·the mass of solid remaining will be less(than it should be)", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 2, "rawText": "·the mass of solid remaining will be less(than it should be)", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "·the value of the calculated solubility will be lower(than it should be)", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 3, "rawText": "·the value of the calculated solubility will be lower(than it should be)", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(a)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q3a", "guidance": [], "answerText": "Fluorine", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(b)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q3b", "guidance": [], "answerText": "Iodine OR astatine", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(c)(i)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q3ci", "guidance": [], "answerText": "Cl2+2Br-→2Cl-+2Br", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(c)(ii)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q3cii", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "accept oxidation number(of bromine) increasesaccept oxidation number(of bromine) changes from-1to0"}], "answerText": "(they are) Losing electrons", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(c)(iii)", "marks": 1, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q3ciii", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "acceptBr+Br=Br2"}], "answerText": "2Br→Br2", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "3(d)", "marks": 2, "number": 3, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q3d", "guidance": [], "answerText": "A diagram that shows:all three bonding pairs correct(1)all non-bonding pairs(1)Example", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [{"ecf": false, "text": "A diagram that shows:all three bonding pairs correct", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 1, "rawText": "A diagram that shows:all three bonding pairs correct", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "all non-bonding pairs", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 2, "rawText": "all non-bonding pairs", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "Example", "marks": null, "reject": false, "ordinal": 3, "rawText": "Example", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}], "correctOption": null, "marksCellSource": "label row"}, {"mcq": false, "qwc": false, "label": "4", "marks": 6, "number": 4, "entryId": "ms-30157e99-q4", "guidance": [{"kind": "note", "text": "accept any combination of hydrochloric acid/nitric acid and barium chloride/barium nitrate"}], "answerText": "A description that makes reference to the following six points:\nTest for cation:\n• do a flame test(1)\n• if flame is yellow then cation is sodium(1)\n• if flame is lilac then cation is potassium(1)\nTest for anion:\n• dissolve solid in water(1)\nEITHER\n• add(dilute nitric acid and)aqueous silver nitrate(1)\n• if(white) precipitate forms the anion is chloride/if no precipitate forms then anion is sulfate(1)\nOR\n• add(dilute hydrochloric acid and)aqueous barium chloride(1)\n• if(white) precipitate forms the anion is sulfate/if no precipitate forms then anion is chloride(1)", "confidence": 0.75, "markPoints": [{"ecf": false, "text": "A description that makes reference to the following six points:\nTest for cation:\n• do a flame test", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 1, "rawText": "A description that makes reference to the following six points:\nTest for cation:\n• do a flame test", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "• if flame is yellow then cation is sodium", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 2, "rawText": "• if flame is yellow then cation is sodium", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "• if flame is lilac then cation is potassium", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 3, "rawText": "• if flame is lilac then cation is potassium", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "Test for anion:\n• dissolve solid in water", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 4, "rawText": "Test for anion:\n• dissolve solid in water", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "EITHER\n• add(dilute nitric acid and)aqueous silver nitrate", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 5, "rawText": "EITHER\n• add(dilute nitric acid and)aqueous silver nitrate", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "• if(white) precipitate forms the anion is chloride/if no precipitate forms then anion is sulfate", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 6, "rawText": "• if(white) precipitate forms the anion is chloride/if no precipitate forms then anion is sulfate", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": []}, {"ecf": false, "text": "", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 7, "rawText": "OR\n• add(dilute hydrochloric acid and)aqueous barium chloride", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "alternatives": ["• add(dilute hydrochloric acid and)aqueous barium chloride"]}, {"ecf": false, "text": "• if(white) precipitate forms the anion is sulfate/if no precipitate forms then anion is chloride", "marks": 1, "reject": false, "ordinal": 8, "rawText": "• if(white) precipitate forms the anion is sulfate/if no precipitate forms then anion is chloride", "anyTwoFrom": false, "dependentOn": [], "